Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Claire Bailey-Ross claire.bailey-ross@port.ac.uk University of Portsmouth, United Kingdom
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Andrew Beresford a.m.beresford@durham.ac.uk Durham University, United Kingdom
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Daniel Smith daniel.smith2@durham.ac.uk Durham University, United Kingdom
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Claire Warwick c.l.h.warwick@durham.ac.uk Durham University, United Kingdom
look	V;NFIN	look	How do people look at and experience art?
focus	V;NFIN	focus	Which elements of specific artworks do they focus on?
have	V;NFIN	have	Do museum labels have an impact on how people look at artworks?
involve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	involving	The viewing experience of art is a complex one, involving issues of perception, attention, memory, decision-making, affect, and emotion.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	Thus, the time it takes and the ways of visually exploring an artwork can inform about its relevance, interestingness, and even its aesthetic appeal.
describe	V;PRS;SG;3	describes	This paper describes a collaborative pilot project focusing on a unique collection of 17th Century Zurbarán paintings.
preserve	V;PST;V.PTCP	preserved	The Jacob cycle at Auckland Castle is the only UK example of a continental collection preserved in situ in purpose-built surroundings.
focus	V;PST;V.PTCP	focused	While studies of the psychology of art have focused on individual works and distinctions between representative / non-representative topics, no work has been completed on the aesthetic appreciation of collections or of devotional themes.
report	V;PRS;1;PL	report	In this paper, we report upon the novel insights eye-tracking techniques have provided into the unconscious processes of viewing the unique collection of Zurbarán artworks.
assess	V;NFIN	assess	The purpose of this pilot study was to assess the effects of different written interpretation on the visual exploration of artworks.
discuss	V;NFIN	discuss	We will discuss the potential implications of these techniques and our understanding of visual behaviours on museum and gallery practice.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	brings	The project brings together established research strengths in Spanish art history, experimental psychology, digital humanities, and museum studies to explore, using eye-tracking techniques, aesthetic reactions to digital representations of the individual Zurbarán artworks as well as the significance of the collection as a whole.
develop	V;PRS;SG;3	develops	Our experience of art develops from the interaction of several cognitive and affective processes; the beginning of which is a visual scan of the artwork.
regard	V;PRS;V.PTCP	regarding	When regarding an artwork, a viewer gathers information through a series fixations, interspersed by rapid movements of the eye called saccades.
determine	V;PST;V.PTCP	determined	The direction of saccades is determined by an interaction between the goals of the observer and the physical properties of the different elements of the scene (e.g. colour, texture, brightness etc).
study	V;PRS;V.PTCP	studying	Importantly, studying eye movements offers an insight that does not depend on the participants’ beliefs, memories or subjective impressions of the artwork.
highlight	V;PST;V.PTCP	highlighted	Previous eye tracking research has highlighted the potential to transform the ways we understand visual processing in the arts (see for example Brieber 2014; Binderman et al., 2005) and at the same time offers a direct way of studying several important factors of a museum visit (Filippini Fantoni et al., 2013; Heidenreich & Turano 2011; Milekic 2010).
preserve	V;PST;V.PTCP	preserved	It is the only cycle to be preserved in purpose-built surroundings in the UK, and one of very few of its kind in the world.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It has a long history in scholarship (Baron & Beresford 2014), but many key aspects of its production and significance have not yet been fully understood.
use	V;PST;1;PL	used	In this study we used eye-tracking in the first stage of exploring audience experience of the extensive Spanish art collections of County Durham, of which the 13 Zurbarán artworks (there are actually only 12 Zurbarán artworks, the 13th Benjamin, is a copy by Arthur Pond) are a key part of, to investigate the ways in which audiences look at Spanish art, how aesthetic experience is evaluated and whether audiences can be encouraged to approach art in different ways.
investigate	V;PST;SG;3	investigated	This pilot project primarily investigated how participants visually explore artworks and provides new insights into the potential eye-tracking has to transform the ways we understand visual processing in arts and culture and at the same time offer a direct way of studying several important factors of a museum visit, namely to assess the effects of label characteristics on visitor visual behaviour.
play	V;PRS;PL;3	play	Tenured and tenure-track university faculty play a special role in determining the speed and direction of scientific progress, both directly through their research and indirectly through their training of new researchers.
establish	V;PRS;PL;3	establish	Past studies establish that each of these efforts is strongly and positively influenced through various forms of faculty diversity, including ethnic, racial, and gender diversity.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	As an example, research shows that greater diversity within a community or group can lead to improved critical thinking [1] and more creative solutions to complex tasks [2, 3] by pairing together individuals with unique skillsets and perspectives that complement and often augment the abilities of their peers.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	Additionally, diversity has been shown to produce more supportive social climates and effective learning environments [4], which can facilitate the mentoring of young scientists.
quantify	V;PRS;V.PTCP	quantifying	Despite these positive effects, however, quantifying the impact of diversity in science remains exceedingly difficult, due in large part to a lack of comprehensive data about the scientific workforce.
measure	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Measuring	Measuring the composition and dynamics of a scientific workforce, particularly in a rapidly expanding field like computer science, is a crucial first step toward understanding how scholarly research is conducted and how it might be enhanced.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	For many scientific fields, however, there is no central listing of all tenure-track faculty, making it difficult to define a rigorous sample frame for analysis.
vary	V;PRS;PL;3	vary	Further, rates of adoption of services like GoogleScholar and ResearchGate vary within, and across disciplines.
establish	V;NFIN	establish	For instance, gender representation in computing is an important issue with broad implications [5], but without a full census of computing faculty, the degree of inequality and its possible sources are difficult to establish [6].
organize	V;PST;V.PTCP	organized	Some disciplines, like political science, are organized around a single professional society, whose membership roll approximates a full census [7].
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	Most fields, on the other hand, including computer science, lack a single all-encompassing organization and membership information is instead distributed across many disjoint lists, such as web-based faculty directories for individual departments.
assemble	V;PRS;V.PTCP	assembling	Because assembling such a full census is difficult, past studies have tended to avoid this task and have instead used samples of researchers [8 – 11], usually specific to a particular field [12 – 16], and often focused on the scientific elite [17, 18].
have	V;NFIN	have	Although useful, such samples are not representative of the scientific workforce as a whole and thus have limited generalizability.
assemble	V;PST;SG;3	assembled	One of the largest census efforts to date assembled, by hand, a nearly complete record of three academic fields: computer science, history, and business [19].
shed	V;PST;V.PTCP	shed	This data set has shed considerable light on dramatic inequalities in faculty training, placement, and scholarly productivity [6, 19, 20].
evolve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	evolving	But, this data set is only a single snapshot of an evolving and expanding system and hence offers few insights into the changing composition and diversity trends within these academic fields.
document	V;PRS;SG;3	documents	In computer science, the Computing Research Association (CRA) documents trends in the employment of PhD recipients through the annual Taulbee survey of computing departments in North America (cra.org/resources/taulbee-survey).
provide	V;NFIN	provide	Such surveys can provide valuable insight into trends and summary statistics on the scientific workforce but suffer from two key weaknesses.
inject	V;NFIN	inject	First, surveys are subject to variable response rates and the misinterpretation of questions or sample frames, which can inject bias into fine-grained analyses [21, 22].
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Second, aggregate information provides only a high-level view of a field, which can make it difficult to investigate causality [23].
wash	V;PST;V.PTCP	washed	For example, differences in recruitment and retention strategies across departments will be washed out by averaging, thereby masking any insights into the efficacy of individual strategies and policies.
present	V;PRS;1;PL	present	Here, we present a novel system, based on a topical web crawler, that can quickly and automatically assemble a full census of an academic field using digital data available on the public World Wide Web.
adapt	V;PST;V.PTCP	adapted	This system is efficient and accurate, and it can be adapted to any academic discipline and used for continuous collection.
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	collecting	The system is capable of collecting census data for an entire academic field in just a few hours using off-the-shelf computing hardware, a vast improvement over the roughly 1600 hours required to do this task by hand [19].
assemble	V;PRS;V.PTCP	assembling	By assembling an accurate census of an entire field from online information alone, this system will facilitate new research on the composition of academic fields by providing access to complete faculty listings, without having to rely on surveys or professional societies.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	This system can also be used longitudinally to study how the workforce’s composition changes over time, which is particularly valuable for evaluating the effectiveness of policies meant to broaden participation or improve retention of faculty.
apply	V;PST;V.PTCP	applied	Finally, applied to many academic fields in parallel, the system can elucidate scientists’ movement between different disciplines and relate those labor flows to scientific advances.
benefit	V;NFIN	benefit	In short, many important research questions will benefit from the availability of accurate and frequently-recollected census data.
organize	V;PST;V.PTCP	organized	Our study is organized as follows.
begin	V;PRS;1;PL	begin	We begin by detailing the design and implementation of our web crawler framework.
present	V;PRS;1;PL	present	Next, we present the results of our work in two sections.
demonstrate	V;PRS;SG;3	demonstrates	The first demonstrates the validity and utility of the crawler by collecting census data for the field of computer science and comparing it to a hand-curated census, collected in 2011 [19].
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	The second provides an example of the type of research enabled by our system and uses the 2011 and 2017 censuses to investigate the “leaky pipeline” problem in faculty retention.
witness	V;PST;V.PTCP	witnessed	Throughout the second half of the 20th century, we have witnessed the return of institutions to economic analysis.
contribute	V;PST;V.PTCP	contributed	The awarding of the Nobel Prize in Economics to its main representatives (Douglass North, Ronald Coase, Oliver Williamson, and Elinor Ostrom) has contributed to its greater recognition.
analyze	V;PST;V.PTCP	analyzed	The two main notions of this economic approach are the concepts of transaction costs and institutions, analyzed by Coase and North, respectively.
argue	V;PRS;SG;3	argues	Coase [13,14] argues that economic transactions involve costs, and where these costs outweigh the gains, the exchange will not take place.
define	V;PST;SG;3	defined	For its part, North defined the institutions as the “rules of the game”: they determine the structure of the economy, establish incentives for economic behavior, and affect social interaction [12].
determine	V;PRS;PL;3	determine	Thus, institutions also determine the level of uncertainty to which individuals are subject, stimulating or discouraging transactions.
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	A viable economy requires an institutional structure that reduces existing uncertainty and guarantees property rights.
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	In other words, it is critical that economic agents believe that their property rights will not be taken away by other public or private actors [15].
achieve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Achieving	Achieving these objectives requires the creation of inclusive institutions, which guarantee the right to private property, incorporate an impartial legal system, and promote a society based on equality of conditions [16].
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	Of course, the state takes a lead role in promoting this type of institution.
protect	V;NFIN	protect	The state must protect property rights, provide public services, and ensure a sound judiciary.
impose	V;PRS;V.PTCP	imposing	It is responsible also for imposing order and promoting a climate of cooperation among agents, penalizing all violators of contracts [17].
establish	V;PRS;SG;3	establishes	It is society that establishes, through political processes, the type of economic institutions that are adopted.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	If there are disagreements about the policies to be implemented, then the group that wins the political game will make the final decision.
adopt	V;PRS;SG;3	adopts	On the one hand, a society that adopts policies promoting inclusive political institutions will develop pluralist and centralized institutions.
guarantee	V;PRS;PL;3	guarantee	Inclusive institutions guarantee the right to private property, to an impartial legal system and promote a society based on equal conditions.
benefit	V;PRS;PL;3	benefit	These institutions benefit not only the elites, but society as a whole.
implement	V;PRS;V.PTCP	implementing	The consequences of implementing inclusive institutions are reflected in increased activity, productivity, and economic growth.
implement	V;PST;V.PTCP	implemented	On the other hand, if extractive political institutions are implemented, then power will be consolidated within a small number of groups.
extract	V;PRS;SG;3	extracts	In governments with extractive policies, the group in power usually extracts resources from the rest of the population for its own enrichment and well-being.
oppose	V;PRS;PL;3	oppose	Another feature of this type of government is that powerful interest groups oppose increased pluralism because it typically results in the loss of their privileges.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Those in power have little interest in their power devolving to a greater number of agents, as would occur under political institutions that were more pluralistic [16].
follow	V;PRS;SG;3	follows	It follows that the role of the state is essential.
restrict	V;PRS;V.PTCP	restricting	The institutional structure of a state and its constitution are responsible for restricting predatory action—even by the state itself—and for creating rules that benefit the community [18].
reflect	V;NFIN	reflect	Moreover, compliance mechanisms must reflect ex ante and ex post standards [12].
happen	V;PRS;SG;3	happens	But what happens when property rights are not respected?
exhibit	V;PRS;SG;3	exhibits	What if it is the state itself that exhibits predatory behavior?
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	All these questions have a place in this case analysis.
present	V;PRS;SG;3	presents	The expropriation of YPF is a case in which the Argentine State presents predatory behavior, encouraged by an extractive institutional framework.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	If the decisions taken by the state are not based on the general interest and, in their execution, do not respect property rights, the number of transactions will be reduced, which will result in unfavorable economic performance.
act	V;PRS;SG;3	acts	In a scenario with insecure property rights, asymmetric information, and a judicial system that acts as a lax enforcement mechanism, as discussed in the following sections, the FDI is difficult to attract.
understand	V;NFIN	understand	In order to understand policymaking and its effects, one must have adequate knowledge of the focal country’s institutional framework.
originate	V;PST;SG;3	originated	The country’s unequal economic growth originated in the colonial era and reflects how the Spanish metropolis influenced the establishment of extractive institutions [7].
achieve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Achieving	Achieving political order would require the government to limit its actions and guarantee the rights of citizens—neither of which has yet occurred in Argentina.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	Enjambment takes place when a syntactic unit is broken up across two lines of poetry (Domínguez Caparrós, 2000: 103), giving rise to different stylistic effects (e.g. increased emphasis on elements of the broken-up phrase, or contrast between those elements), or creating double interpretations for the enjambed lines (García-Paje, 1991).
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	In Spanish poetry, the syntactic configurations under which enjambment takes place have been described extensively, and detailed studies on the use of enjambment by individual authors exist (see Martínez Cantón, 2011 for an overview) including, among others Quilis (1964), Domínguez Caparrós, (2000), Paraíso, (2000), Spang (1983) for a description of enjambment, and Alarcos (1966), Senabre (1982), Luján (2006), Martínez Fernández (2010) for case-studies on a single author.
identify	V;NFIN	identify	However, a larger-scale study to identify enjambment across hundreds of authors spanning several centuries, enabling distant reading (Moretti, 2013), was not previously available.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given that need, we have developed software, based on Natural Language Processing, that automatically identifies enjambment in Spanish, and applied it to a corpus of approx. 3750 sonnets by ca. 1000 authors, from the 15th to the 19th century.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	First, the literature shows a debate about which specific syntactic units can be considered to trigger enjambment, if split across two lines, and whether lexical and syntactic criteria are sufficient to identify enjambment.
permit	V;PRS;SG;3	permits	Second, the stylistic effects that enjambment permits are also an object of current research (Martínez Fernández, 2010).
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	collecting	Systematically collecting large amounts of enjambment examples provides helpful evidence to assess scholars’ current claims, and may stimulate novel analyses.
complement	V;PRS;SG;3	complements	Finally, our study complements Navarro’s (2016) automatic metrical analyses of Spanish Golden Age sonnets, by covering a wider period and focusing on enjambment.
structure	V;PST;V.PTCP	structured	The abstract is structured thus:
provide	V;PRS;1;PL	provide	First we provide the definition of enjambment adopted.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	Then, our corpus and system are described, followed by an evaluation of the system.
discuss	V;PST;V.PTCP	discussed	Finally, findings on enjambment in our diachronic sonnet corpus are discussed.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	The project’s website provides details omitted here for space reasons, including samples for the corpus, results, and other details.
match	V;PRS;PL;3	match	Syntactic and metrical units often match in poetry.
break	V;PST;V.PTCP	broken	However, this trend has been broken since antiquity for various reasons (Parry (1929) on Homer, or Flores Gómez (1988) on early classical poetry).
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	In Spanish tradition, enjambment (in Spanish, "encabalgamiento") is considered to take place when a pause suggested by poetic form (e.g. at the end of a line or across hemistichs) occurs between strongly connected lexical or syntactic units, triggering an unnatural cut between those units.
perform	V;PST;SG;3	performed	Quilis (1964) performed poetry reading experiments, proposing that the following strongly connected elements give rise to enjambment, should a poetic-form pause break them up:
break	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Breaking	Lexical enjambment: Breaking up a word.
translate	V;PST;1;PL	translated	We translated "lexical enjambment" from Quilis’s terms "encabalgamiento léxico" or "tmesis".
bound	V;PST;V.PTCP	bounded	Phrase-bounded enjambment: Within a phrase, breaking up sequences like "noun + adjective", "verb + adverb", "auxiliary verb + main verb", among others.
translate	V;PST;1;PL	translated	We translated "phrase-bounded enjambment" from "encabalgamiento sirremático".
head	V;PRS;V.PTCP	heading	Cross-clause enjambment: Between a noun antecedent and the pronoun heading the relative clause that complements the antecedent.
translate	V;PST;1;PL	translated	We translated "cross-clause enjambment" from Quilis’s "encabalgamiento oracional".
include	V;PRS;SG;3	includes	The project site includes Quilis’s complete list of syntactic environments that can trigger enjambment, as well as the types identified by our system.
note	V;PST;SG;3	noted	Besides the enjambment types above, Spang (1983) noted that if a subject or direct object and their related verbs occur in two different lines of poetry, this can also feel unusual for a reader, even if the effect is less pronounced than in the environments identified by Quilis.
differentiate	V;NFIN	differentiate	To differentiate these cases from enjambment proper, Spang calls these cases "enlace", translated here as "expansion".
gather	V;NFIN	gather	Quilis (1964) was the only author so far to gather recitation-based experimental evidence on enjambment.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	His typology is still considered current, and was adopted by later authors, although complementary enjambment typologies have been proposed, as Martínez Cantón (2011) reviews.
identify	V;PRS;SG;3	identifies	Our system identifies Quilis’ types, besides Spang’s expansion cases.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Agents can rationally choose between experts only if the criteria that distinguish genuine experts from charlatans are common sense or widely known:
satisfy	V;NFIN	satisfy	if agents are to satisfy the epistemic conditions on responsibility, they must know what kinds of knowledge they must utilize to guide their selection of sources (on pain of infinite regress).
identify	V;PST;V.PTCP	identified	In fact, many, if not all, the markers of expertise identified by philosophers enjoy widespread recognition.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	The fact that these criteria are widely known, however, offers an opportunity to those who would use them for deception, witting or unwitting.
assess	V;PST;V.PTCP	assessed	Since expertise must be assessed through indirect markers, to mimic the markers of expertise is to mimic expertise [17].
live	V;PRS;1;PL	live	We live in an epistemic environment that is heavily and deliberately polluted by agents who use mimicry and other methods as a means of inflating their pretense to expertise.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	This fact, together with the fact that such deception is widely known to occur, reduces ordinary people’s trust in expert authority and diminishes their capacity to distinguish reliable from unreliable sources.
deceive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	deceiving	For instance, those with an interest in deceiving the general public may set up parallel institutions that ostensibly guarantee expertise, taking advantage of the ways in which these parallel institutions mimic legitimate institutions to ensure that people are taken in.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are some egregious examples of this practice in the field of health care.
set	V;PST;SG;3	set	For example, a small number of doctors set up the American College of Pediatricians (ACPeds) to advocate socially conservative viewpoints related to child health care.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	Such an organization is surely permissible, but it has had the unfortunate (and likely intended) effect of muddying debates in the public forum by misleading people into thinking that the college speaks for the pediatric profession at large.
issue	V;PST;SG;3	issued	Thus, when ACPeds issued a statement condemning gender reassignment surgery in 2016 [21], many people mistook the organization’s political beliefs for the consensus view among United States pediatricians — although the peak body for pediatric workers, the American Academy of Pediatrics, has a much more positive view of gender dysphoria [22].
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	Insofar as the larger organization, with a broader membership base, can be expected to reflect a wider range of expert opinions and a higher degree of expertise, it is reasonable to give its views greater weight than those of the smaller organization.
allow	V;PRS;SG;3	allows	When ACPeds allows or encourages the impression that it speaks for the profession, it introduces an epistemic pollutant.
involve	V;PST;SG;3	involved	A yet more egregious example of such pollution involved collaborative efforts by pharmaceutical companies and the publishing giant Elsevier to produce publications mimicking peer-reviewed journals in the interest of promoting the companies’ commercial products [23].
hope	V;PST;PL;3	hoped	The companies hoped to leverage the prestige of Elsevier with these fake journals to endow their promotional “research” with an air of reliability.
uncover	V;PST;V.PTCP	uncovered	When the deceit was uncovered, however, the effect was just the opposite: the legitimacy of the published findings was not enhanced through their publication by Elsevier, but rather the legitimacy of Elsevier’s publications — and, by extension, all academic journals — was diminished through their dissemination of deceptive and commercially interested research.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	More recently, institutions of academic expertise have been subject to a large and growing outbreak of so-called predatory journals — journals that will publish almost anything for a fee.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Once again, this phenomenon has the effect of making peer-reviewed journals appear less legitimate.
work	V;PRS;PL;3	work	At times, even those who work in academia may be unsure of a particular journal’s legitimacy, and there are genuine borderline cases.
appear	V;PRS;SG;3	appears	For example, the Frontiers contingent of journals appears legitimate — at least to me — despite the fact that authors are expected to pay a publication fee. 8
appear	V;PRS;PL;3	appear	Yet some Frontiers journals appear to have engaged in bad behavior, whether for profit or for some other motive.
publish	V;PST;PL;3	published	Frontiers in Public Health controversially published articles linking vaccines and autism [24] and questioning the link between HIV and AIDS [25].
decide	V;PST;SG;3	decided	Whether due to this behavior or not, Jeffrey Beall decided to add the publisher to his influential (but now sadly unavailable) list of questionable journals [26].
surround	V;PRS;V.PTCP	surrounding	The controversy surrounding Beall’s decision indicates how difficult it is to make such judgments — even for professionals.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	If academics with expertise in relevant fields have difficulty assessing whether particular journals or particular publishers are legitimate, one cannot reasonably expect ordinary people to make such judgments.
lower	V;PST;V.PTCP	lowered	If their confidence in scientific findings is lowered across the board as the result of such epistemic pollution, one can hardly blame them.
discount	V;NFIN	discount	Since conflicts of interest are a reason to discount expertise, it is incumbent on me to note that I have published in Frontiers journals on several occasions.
stem	V;NFIN	stem	Epistemic pollution may stem not only from counterfeit institutions of knowledge production but also from bad behavior by legitimate institutions. 9
result	V;NFIN	result	For example, pollution may result from attempts to game the systems put in place to track expertise.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider institutions with a credentialing function, such as universities, bar associations, or peer review bodies.
exist	V;NFIN	exist	These institutions do not exist solely to credential experts.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	They have other functions, and these functions may come into conflict, creating pressures to inflate credentials.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	For example, universities have a financial incentive to inflate the expertise of their academic staff, thereby increasing their rankings, bringing in grant money, and attracting students.
assess	V;PRS;PL;3	assess	Systems that assess expertise can be manipulated, and many cases of such manipulation exist — take the recent example by the University of Malaysia, which attempted to boost metrics by urging its faculty to cite one another [28].
investigate	V;NFIN	investigate	For this reason, institutions may also be slow to investigate accusations of fraud, and they may try to keep their discoveries in-house to protect their reputations.
involve	V;PRS;PL;3	involve	Cognitive games involve a number of different games working aspects of human cognition, while proposing the intersection between the sets of concepts, fun and cognition, for the improvement of cognitive functions.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	The attention is the main point made in this study, since it is fundamental to the learning process and be recurring complaint among parents and teachers in schools.
suggest	V;PRS;PL;3	suggest	With respect to the contributions of digital games to improvement of cognitive processes, researchers suggest that regular practice has a significant influence on improving the performance related to basic visual skills (Li, Polat, Scalzo, & Bavelier, 2010); on the ability to perceive objects simultaneously (Dye & Bavelier, 2010; Feng, Spence, & Pratt, 2007); and on the ability to do more than one task at the same time (Boot, Kramer, Simons, Fabiani, & Gratton, 2008).
investigate	V;PRS;PL;3	investigate	Other studies specifically investigate the use of digital games in the school context and suggest potential for digital game use to improve of student's attention span at preschool age (Rueda, Checa, & Cómbita, 2012), to improve overall intelligence capacity of elementary school children (Miller & Robertson, 2010), and to better performance of working memory ability (Klingberg et al., 2005; Thorell, Lindqvist, Nutley, Bohlin, & Klingberg, 2009).
consider	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Considering	Considering the importance of the proper functioning of attention, because of its involvement in the regulation of thoughts and emotions, maintaining the performance of this process is very important, especially in school, where the child must acquire content in an environment full of countless distractors.
focus	V;PRS;SG;3	focuses	The study in question focuses on the attention, proposing and evaluation in the context of the classroom.
suggest	V;PRS;SG;3	suggests	Thus, it suggests the use of digital games in an integrated way the school activities in the classroom.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	The games have features like increasing challenges, rules that establish what can and cannot be done, and involvement of the player in the quest to gain skills and win the game (Kirriemuir & McFarlane, 2004; Prensky, 2005).
aim	V;PRS;1;PL	aim	We aim to investigate the contributions of the use of a system that integrates cognitive digital games to a database, of the Escola do Cérebro, for monitoring and improvement of cognitive skills, highlighting the attention.
involve	V;PRS;PL;3	involve	The games involve challenges and rules involving the exercise of cognitive functions, especially the working memory, attention and capacity of solving problems.
combine	V;PRS;SG;3	combines	The study combines qualitative and quantitative approaches.
collect	V;PRS;SG;3	collects	It collects the data based on the observation of the proposed interventions as well as interviews conducted with participating teachers and students to identify their perceptions of digital games’ contributions to the learning process.
perform	V;PST;1;PL	performed	Furthermore, before and after the implementation of the intervention, we performed a D2 Test of attention that measures selective and sustained attention, as well as visual scanning accuracy and speed.
consist	V;PST;SG;3	consisted	The intervention consisted in the use of the Escola do Cérebro, using tablets in the classroom, daily for a period of five weeks.
consist	V;PST;SG;3	consisted	The sample consisted of 71 students of the Application School of Basic Education, Federal University of Santa Catarina, aged 7 and 9 years old (M = 7.64 ± 1.12), which were divided into two groups: participant and control.
participate	V;PST;SG;3	participated	The first (n = 31) participated in the intervention, the control group (n = 40) was only evaluated using the test before and after the same time interval of interventions.
integrate	V;PRS;SG;3	integrates	The Escola do Cérebro is a platform that integrates seven digital games into a database.
allow	V;PRS;SG;3	allows	The application allows visualization of the player's performance and offers the possibility of monitoring by teachers.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Students have their scores measured by four variables: time, speed, stability and accuracy.
perform	V;PST;V.PTCP	performed	A statistical analysis was performed based on the application of the paired t-test on the difference of the overall score obtained in the test before and after the intervention in the two groups.
obtain	V;PST;V.PTCP	obtained	The difference in the results obtained from the application of D2 Test of attention before and after was statistically significant (p < 0.05), the participant group had mean and standard deviation 60.23 (64.75) respectively, while the control group was 20.00 (42.65).
indicate	V;PRS;SG;3	indicates	The result indicates significant improvement in the performance of the sustained attention in the test, as well as a high dispersion, which reveals a variation in relation to the performance.
participate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	participating	In addition, students participating in the interview reported a preference for games that involve problem solving, recognize the need to plan actions in relation to their importance for the game and for daily activities, and realize improvements in the ability to sustain attention.
observe	V;PST;PL;3	observed	The teachers observed changes after the intervention, emphasizing the greater persistence and involvement in school activities, and in some students, improvement in the ability to sustain attention.
conclude	V;PRS;1;PL	conclude	From this, we conclude that an intervention based on cognitive digital games offers contributions to the learning process and improvement of sustained attention.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	A fundamental tenet of linguistic science is that the sound of a word has a purely arbitrary connection to the word's meaning [1], [2].
connect	V;PST;V.PTCP	connected	Thus, the sound of the word dog in English is connected to the concept ‘dog’ by historical accident and not by any natural connection; roughly the same concept is just as well denoted in French by chien, in German by hund, and in Japanese by inu.
have	V;NFIN	have	But it is not that a word can have just any vocal sound.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	While the possibility space for sound systems of the world's language is enormous, any given language makes use of only a restricted portion of the possible sounds [3], [4].
follow	V;PRS;SG;3	follows	It follows from these two basic principles – the ‘arbitrariness of the sign’, and the ‘selectiveness of particular sound systems’ – that the words that exist in the world's languages should sound quite different from each other, and that the likelihood that there are universal words is extremely small.
present	V;PRS;1;PL	present	But in this study we present a striking exception to this otherwise robust rule.
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	From a systematic comparison of 10 spoken languages from 5 continents we find evidence suggesting that a word like ‘Huh?’ – used as a ‘repair initiator’ when, for example, one has not clearly heard what someone just said [5], [6] – is a universal word.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are two distinct claims being made here: 1. that Huh? is universal, and 2. that Huh? is a word.
show	V;PRS;1;PL	show	In support of the first claim, we show that the similarities in form and function of an interjection with the specific function of repair initiation are very much greater across languages than chance coincidence would admit.
find	V;PRS;1;PL	find	In fact the variation in form in unrelated languages across the globe is about the same as the variation we find in the way any regular word (e.g., dog) is pronounced across dialects of English.
show	V;PRS;1;PL	show	In support of the second claim, we show that Huh? meets the criteria of a word in the sense of being a conventional lexical sign which must be learnt.
presume	V;PST;V.PTCP	presumed	Thus, in contrast to what has been presumed for interjections in general [7], [8] and for huh? in particular [9], [10], we find that this item is linguistic in nature rather than being a mere grunt or non-lexical sound.
show	V;PRS;1;PL	show	We show that the form is locally calibrated in ways that show it fitting within different language systems.
address	V;PRS;1;PL	address	Finally, we address the question of why all languages should have such a word and why its form should be so similar across languages.
observe	V;PRS;1;PL	observe	We observe that this item fulfils a crucial need shared by all languages – the efficient signalling of problems of hearing and understanding – and we propose that its form is constrained by selective pressures in a conversational environment that is essentially the same in all languages.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider a case from English [10]:
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	After speaker G makes a statement, speaker E utters the interjection huh?.
follow	V;PST;V.PTCP	followed	This is followed by a repetition of the original statement by G.
initiate	V;PST;V.PTCP	initiated	The technical term for this type of sequence is “open other-initiated repair”:
initiate	V;PST;V.PTCP	initiated	repair is initiated not by the speaker of the first turn but by the other participant (“other-initiated”), and the repair initiator signals that there is a problem, but it leaves open what the problem is (“open”) [11].
show	V;PRS;PL;3	show	Extracts 2 and 3 show structurally identical sequences in two other languages: Siwu, a Kwa language spoken in Ghana, and Lao, a Tai-Kadai language spoken in Laos.
show	V;PRS;PL;3	show	These examples show that it is possible to identify the same conversational structure in unrelated languages.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	Essentially, this method gives us a natural control over conversational data, making possible systematic comparison across languages [12], [13].
initiate	V;PST;V.PTCP	initiated	Sequences of other-initiated repair have been identified in every spoken language investigated so far [14], [15], and as the examples show, the interjection in the pivotal turn can be remarkably similar.
lead	V;PRS;SG;3	leads	This leads to the question driving our study:
compile	V;PRS;V.PTCP	compiling	By compiling data from published literature we found that in thirty-one languages around the world, the interjection for other-initiated repair appears to be strongly similar (Figure 1).
study	V;NFIN	study	The most reliable way to study a conversational interjection is by examining cases of actual use.
collect	V;PST;1;PL	collected	Therefore we collected data from recordings of naturally occurring informal conversations in a sample of 10 languages from 5 continents, varying fundamentally in terms of phonology, word structure, and grammar (languages 1–10 in Figure 1).
study	V;PST;1;PL	studied	For optimal comparability, we studied the exact same conversational environment across languages: that of other-initiated repair (OIR), in which one participant produces a turn at talk, the other then signals some trouble with this turn, and finally the first produces a next turn which aims to solve the trouble, usually by means of repetition and/or modification.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	In some languages the interjection, or an item similar to it, was also found in other sequential environments, for instance to mark surprise or to pursue a response.
derive	V;PST;V.PTCP	derived	Such alternative (and probably derived) uses provide insight in possible paths of semantic change, but we exclude them here to make sure we are comparing like with like.
hypothesize	V;PST;1;PL	hypothesized	We hypothesized that the children, who were over the age of six at the time of collection, would have adult-like SI interpretations.
expect	V;PST;1;PL	expected	We expected that the use of a training session and then the format of the question would guide participants towards evaluating pragmatic felicity over providing truth value judgments, and would result in adult-like interpretations.
posit	V;PST;1;PL	posited	Further, we posited that if our initial hypothesis was not supported, and in fact there was some non-adult-like performance, that there would be significant variability among the conditions, with cardinal numbers having the highest performance and some the weakest.
seem	V;IMP;2	seem	On the surface, it does seem as if there is variability and perhaps a larger sample size or more items could lead to a significant finding (see limitations below), but at least for this study, the only significant finding was the difference between cardinal numbers and some.
observe	V;PST;V.PTCP	observed	Significant differences were not observed between all and cardinal numbers or all and some.
limit	V;PST;V.PTCP	limited	This study is limited in its power and generalizability.
serve	V;PRS;SG;3	serves	It serves as a reminder of the work that needs to be completed for the cross-linguistic study of SI in particular and acquisition more generally.
include	V;PST;V.PTCP	included	Bantu languages are not included in studies of acquisition often enough despite the numerous languages in this linguistic grouping and the millions of people using them.
need	V;PRS;PL;3	need	Future studies need to include more participants and more items to increase power.
include	V;NFIN	include	Future studies should also include a larger age range of children to document the age at which adult-like performance emerges.
result	V;PST;V.PTCP	resulted	Related cross-cultural studies have resulted in insufficient statistical power, but interesting trends (e.g., Nedwick, 2014).
increase	V;PST;V.PTCP	increased	Follow-up studies with increased stimuli and participants are needed.
consider	V;NFIN	consider	In a future study, it is also important to consider if additional training in the difference between reporting on felicity and truth value judgments would impact results, or if more naturalistic experimental conditions (e.g., demonstrating actions using real objects instead of two-dimensional depictions) would be beneficial.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	Previous studies in this region of sub-Saharan Africa have found evidence of cultural differences in testing behavior (Hein, Reich, Marks, Thuma, & Grigorenko, 2016).
make	V;NFIN	make	The current study is too small to make strong conclusions with regard to cultural differences and experimental methods;
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	however, in the study by Hein and colleagues it was found that children responded more or less frequently based on factors such as stimuli type.
include	V;PST;V.PTCP	included	In the current study, the responses from two children were not included because they responded the same way to every item through the training and all test items.
answer	V;PST;PL;3	answered	More specifically, they answered “yes” that the puppet described the pictures well.
understand	V;PST;PL;3	understood	It is possible that these two children understood the task and really did believe that the puppet did not produce any poor descriptions, and in this regard, are not yet adult-like in their SI interpretations.
say	V;PST;PL;3	said	This is unlikely as they said that even the first training item was said well.
attempt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	attempting	It is also possible though that these two children were attempting to please the data collectors, or be polite, and that “yes” was in some ways a default answer to be provided when having to respond verbally in a test context.
improve	V;NFIN	improve	Additional training with the methodology could improve outcomes.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	The puppets used were picked specifically for this task with careful attention to their appearance.
become	V;PST;PL;3	became	Upon arrival at the school, however, it became clear that the children were not accustomed to playing with puppets and that the data collection would be a novel experience for them.
prove	V;NFIN	prove	Further exploration with greater cardinal number ranges could also prove interesting.
participate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	participating	Numbers through five in the participating communities are most often expressed with native Chitonga words while numbers greater than five are typically indicated using English borrowings.
include	V;PST;V.PTCP	included	The cardinal numbers included in this study were only one through five in order to avoid additional item variability, but larger numbers should be included in future research.
limit	V;PST;V.PTCP	limited	Albeit limited, these results provide valuable insight into SI interpretation by Chitonga-speaking children and demonstrate that pragmatic inference acquisition likely follows the order identified in previous research, but appears to be completed at a later age in this language.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	This interesting combination of findings – expected hierarchy of difficulty, but differing age of acquisition – is an important addition to our growing cross-linguistic knowledge of SI and could be the result of language-specific differences in the use of SI lexical items or methodological differences.
bust	V;PST;V.PTCP	busted	Utilization of busted CFL in developing cheap and efficient segmented compact LED bulbs
survive	V;NFIN	survive	Today’s generation will not survive a day without the help of lighting.
depend	V;PRS;SG;3	depends	In fact, someone’s productivity, particularly at night, depends on the presence of a good lighting and it seems that it is a daily necessity.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	Lighting takes a large part on the consumption of household electrical energy particularly in the Philippines.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are different type of lighting bulbs used at home can affect the overall lighting consumption.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Nowadays, most commonly and widely used bulb in the household is the Compact Fluorescent Light (CFL).
contain	V;PRS;PL;3	contain	However, the main problem of CFL is the mercury they contain.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	In response to the said problem, this project study gives solution to the problem of the society concerning environment, health and safety as well energy conservation, by developing a segmented compact light-emitting diode (SCLED) bulb from busted CFL that are efficient, economical, and does not contain toxic chemicals.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	Based	Based on the results of the 2011 Household Energy Consumption Survey (HECS), electricity remains as the most common source of energy used by households particularly in the Philippines.
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	About 87 percent of 21.0 million households used electricity from March to August 2011.
lead	V;PRS;V.PTCP	leading	As of 2016, the demand for electricity in the residential is leading among the different sectors.
group	V;PST;V.PTCP	grouped	It is often grouped depending on its uses that are distributed for heating and cooling, lighting, operating appliances etc [1]
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	Now that the world is in the age where lighting seems to be a daily necessity, typical homes as shown in figure 1, consume nearly 27 percent of the energy used today: making lighting as the major source of electricity consumption.
play	V;PRS;SG;3	plays	Lighting plays a large part on the consumption of household electrical energy; it consumes about 18% of the total generated electricity of total energy consumption in residential sectors as shown in figure 2 [2].
estimate	V;PST;V.PTCP	Estimated	Estimated electricity use in residential sector
improve	V;PRS;SG;3	improves	The use of correct and appropriate type of light bulb improves the efficiency of energy usage.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are three general types of lighting that are widely used in the household nowadays: the (light-emitting diode) LED bulb, (compact fluorescent light) CFL bulb and the incandescent bulb.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	But each of them has their perks and perils [3].
end	V;PRS;PL;3	end	Unfortunately, most of the time, people in the residential end up choosing a lamp based on it is price rather than its efficiency and this cause us to pay for our lighting more than necessary.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Today, CFLs are the most used lighting sources in the household.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using CFLs will allow people to decrease their energy consumption; also it is a good start to decrease greenhouse emissions.
contain	V;PRS;PL;3	contain	However, the main problem of CFLs is the mercury they contain.
contain	V;PRS;V.PTCP	containing	When products and wastes containing mercury are improperly disposed of, mercury is released into the air, ground or water.
break	V;PRS;SG;3	breaks	It is persistent in the environment; it never breaks down nor goes away.
emit	V;PRS;SG;3	emits	In addition to the harmful effects of mercury is that it emits Ultraviolet (UV) Radiation.
interact	V;PRS;SG;3	interacts	This UV radiation interacts with the chemicals on the inside of the bulb to generate light.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	The acute and chronic effects are the normal responses of the skin to UVR; acute reactions considered will be erythema (sunburn) and vitamin D production.
discuss	V;PST;V.PTCP	discussed	Skin aging and skin cancer will be discussed as those reactions produced by prolonged or repeated UVR exposure [4].
compare	V;PST;V.PTCP	compared	In response to the aforementioned situation where the three types of lights are compared by their efficiency and effectiveness as light sources, the proponents conducted this study.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Since the CFLs are the most commonly used light bulbs in the residential and has a great compatibility to LED in terms of design and materials, the proponents conducted a study about innovating and recycling CFL into a more efficient and environment friendly LED light bulb.
test	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Testing	Design of Mutation Operators for Testing Geographic Information Systems
present	V;PST;V.PTCP	Presented	Presented at the 2nd XoveTIC Congress, A Coruña, Spain, 5–6 September 2019.
propose	V;PRS;1;PL	propose	In this article, we propose the definition of specific mutation operators for testing Geographic Information Systems.
describe	V;PRS;1;PL	describe	We describe the process for applying the operators and generating mutants, and present a case study where these mutation operators are applied to two real-world applications.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	Mutation-based testing [1] is a test technique that involves artificially introducing errors into a System Under Test (SUT).
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	A mutant is a copy of the system in which a change has been done that, in most cases, will lead to a behaviour different than expected.
generate	V;PST;V.PTCP	generated	The different mutants are generated automatically by the application of mutation operators.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	In the state of the art, we have found mutation operators, both general purpose and specific to different technologies, languages and paradigms [2–9].
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	However, these operators are not adequate when trying to test software features associated with specific domains.
propose	V;PRS;1;PL	propose	In this article, we propose mutation operators specific to the domain of Geographic Information Systems (GIS) applications.
reproduce	V;PRS;PL;3	reproduce	These operators reproduce programming errors that are litely to occur during the development of this type of applications.
present	V;PRS;1;PL	present	In addition, we present the implementation of these operators and as proof of concept we apply these operators to two real-world GIS applications and we generate the mutants.
design	V;PRS;V.PTCP	designing	As a previous step to designing the mutation operators, we analyzed the main technologies used specifically in the development of GIS, and we identified typical errors a programmer can introduce during the development.
formalize	V;PST;V.PTCP	formalized	These errors were formalized into mutation operators.
apply	V;NFIN	apply	In order to apply these operators to a SUT, we rely on Java reflection and aspect-oriented programming.
allow	V;PRS;SG;3	allows	Reflection allows us to obtain the list of classes and methods of the SUT, so the user can decide the methods to wish the operators will be applied.
capture	V;PRS;1;PL	capture	Later, we capture information about the methods of the SUT to be mutated, together with the information of the mutation operators that were already defined.
generate	V;PRS;1;PL	generate	From these data, we generate the mutation operator, in the form of on aspect, which will then be possible to interweave with the SUT which generates a mutant of the SUT.
describe	V;PRS;1;PL	describe	Next, we describe the definition of two operators and two cases of application on real-world GIS applications.
exchange	V;PRS;SG;3	exchanges	It exchanges the coordinate system of a geometry, so it does not match the coordinate system that is being used in the user interface.
simulate	V;PRS;SG;3	simulates	It simulates the error of not checking that the coordinate system is correct.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	The error is introduced by directly modifying the coordinate system of geometry when recovering the wrapping of the figure.
simplify	V;PST;V.PTCP	simplified	Listing 1: A simplified definition of the ChangeCoordSys Operator.
apply	V;PST;V.PTCP	applied	This operator was applied to a mobile technology GIS application.
allow	V;PRS;SG;3	allows	This application allows registering places of interest for the user.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	These areas of interest are called Geofences.
determine	V;PST;V.PTCP	determined	A Geofence is determined by a geographical location expressed in terms of latitude, longitude, and a radius around that location.
create	V;PRS;V.PTCP	creating	By creating a Geofence with an erroneous location from its central location, the device will receive incorrect location notifications.
see	V;NFIN	see	As a result, the user will see in the application’s map viewer the Geofences drawn in erroneous zones (Figure 1).
introduce	V;PRS;SG;3	introduces	It introduces errors in the processing of geometries, manipulating the result of the operations that carry out the verification of different topological restrictions between geometries, such as intersects, covers or overlap.
simplify	V;PST;V.PTCP	simplified	Listing 2: A simplified definition of the BooleanPolygonConstraint Operator.
test	V;NFIN	test	To test this operator it was applied to a land reparcelling system.
reunify	V;NFIN	reunify	The objective of the land reparcelling is to reunify the lands of an owner to facilitate their exploitation.
affect	V;PST;V.PTCP	affected	In this application, the result of the operation between two polygons has been affected.
cause	V;PRS;SG;3	causes	This error causes the incorrect display of the resulting geometry that should be drawn in the user interface after the operation applied to the two initial geometries (Figure 2).
exist	V;PRS;V.PTCP	existing	In existing proposals, we can find both generic and specific mutation operators.
cover	V;NFIN	cover	However, these are not adequate to cover errors in particular domains.
define	V;PST;V.PTCP	defined	We have defined new operators specific to the GIS domain and a way to apply them to a SUT.
test	V;PST;V.PTCP	tested	In addition, we have tested the operators defined in two GIS applications.
intend	V;PRS;1;PL	intend	As future work, we intend to extend this approach to other domains, as well as to use the developed operators for the automatic improvement of sets of test cases.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	Replication studies are considered by many to play a fundamental role in any scientific endeavor.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	When using the same materials and procedures as a previous study, replication studies serve to test the reliability of the previous study’s findings.
alter	V;PRS;V.PTCP	altering	When altering specific methodological or participant characteristics of a previous study, they serve to test generalizability of the earlier findings under different conditions.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	One indication of the importance of replication is found in the 50 or more calls for replication research in the field of second language (L2) research alone (see references for 50 calls and commentaries in Appendix S1 in the Supporting Information online): from Santos (1989) through Polio and Gass 1997 to very recent proposals for specific replication studies, such as Vandergrift and Cross 2017 and even a book-length treatment (Porte, 2012).
promote	V;NFIN	promote	Beyond these calls, efforts to actively promote and facilitate replication studies have also emerged.
establish	V;PST;V.PTCP	established	For example, the Instruments for Research into Second Languages (IRIS) repository (http://www.irisdatabase.org) was established in 2011 and holds, at the time of writing, over 3,800 materials that can be used for replication, among other purposes, in L2 research (Marsden & Mackey, 2014; Marsden, Mackey, & Plonsky, 2016).
establish	V;PST;V.PTCP	established	The Open Science Framework (https://osf.io), also established in 2011, provides a web infrastructure to facilitate collaboration and has been used for large replication efforts in psychology (e.g., Open Science Collaboration, 2015), which continue to make waves in academia (Laws, 2016; Lindsay, 2015; Martin & Clarke, 2017) and the general media (Baker, 2015; Devlin, 2016).
see	V;NFIN;2	see	In some fields, a flourishing metascience, that is, the scientific study of science (see Munafò et al., 2017), has included syntheses assessing the quantity and nature of replication efforts, for example, in education (Makel & Plucker, 2014 and in psychology (Makel et al., 2012).
perceive	V;PST;V.PTCP	perceived	The driving force behind this battery of calls, commentaries, infrastructure, and metascience is a perceived crisis in the state of replication research.
underpin	V;PRS;V.PTCP	underpinning	The severe concerns underpinning the alleged crisis have several dimensions relating to: (a) the (small) amount of published replication research; (b) the (poor) quality of replication research; and (c) the (lack of) reproducibility, which refers to the extent to which findings can (not) be reproduced in replication attempts that have been undertaken.
speak	V;PRS;PL;3	speak	These concerns speak to the very core of science, raising fundamental questions about the validity and reliability of our work.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	Indeed, some commentators have called replication the “gold standard” of research evidence (Jasny, Chin, Chong, & Vignieri, 2011, p. 1225) and a “linchpin of the scientific process” (Let’s replicate, 2006, p. 330).
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	In the field of L2 research, given the importance of replication and the 50 calls for replication in L2 research that we identified, one might expect a substantial number of published replication studies by now.
perceive	V;PST;V.PTCP	perceived	However, a perceived lack of prestige, excitement, and originality of replication plagues L2 research (Porte, 2012), as it does other disciplines (Berez-Kroeker et al., 2017; Branco, Cohen, Vossen, Ide, & Calzolari, 2017; Chambers, 2017; Schmidt, 2009), and these perceptions are thought to have caused, at least in part (directly or indirectly), alleged low rates and a poor quality of published replication studies.
establish	V;PST;V.PTCP	established	However, a systematic metascience on replication research has not yet been established in the field of L2 research, leaving a poor understanding of the actual number and nature of replication studies that have been published.
begin	V;PRS;SG;3	begins	The current study begins to address this gap through narrative and systematic reviews.
consider	V;PRS;SG;3	considers	The narrative review considers challenges in replication research and is largely informed by commentaries and metascience from psychology, given that the cognitive and social subdomains of psychology are highly influential in L2 research, and also from education, another key sister discipline.
organize	V;PST;V.PTCP	organized	The narrative review is organized around four broad themes: (a) the quantity of replication research, (b) the nature of replication research, (c) the relationship between initial and replication studies, and (d) the interpretation and extent of reproducibility of the findings of initial studies.
gain	V;NFIN	gain	To gain insight into these issues in the context of L2 research, the systematic review provides a synthesis of L2 studies in journal articles that self-labeled as replications.
determine	V;PST;V.PTCP	determined	The research questions and methods of the systematic review were largely determined by the narrative review but also emerged through the design and piloting of the coding instrument.
offer	V;PRS;1;PL	offer	Finally, we offer further discussion and 16 recommendations for future replication work that draw on our narrative and systematic reviews and on our experience of carrying out multisite (Morgan-Short et al., 2018) 1 and single site (Faretta-Stutenberg & Morgan-Short, 2011; Marsden, Williams, & Liu, 2013; McManus & Marsden, 2017; Morgan-Short, Heil, Botero-Moriaty, & Ebert, 2012) replications.
start	V;PRS;1;PL	start	We start from the widely agreed premise that testing the reproducibility of findings should have an essential role in the testing and refinement of theory, at least for hypothesis-testing epistemologies that seek to ascertain generalizability and for other epistemologies in which constructs are deemed to be definable and observable.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	Thus, our overall aim is to provide conceptual clarification and an empirical base for future discussion and production of replication studies, with a view to improving the amount and quality of L2 replication research.
control	V;PRS;SG;3	controls	Salinity is one of the most important marine parameters, which controls many processes such as physical circulations, biogeochemistry dynamics from regional to global ocean [1,2].
accumulate	V;PST;V.PTCP	accumulated	Although drifters and buoys, together with cruises, have accumulated large amount in-situ water salinity data in different regions, it is still difficult to monitor global ocean salinity with high temporal-spatial resolution by the in situ measurements.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	In the past decade, with the successful launch of the soil moisture and ocean salinity (SMOS) satellite by the European Space Agency (ESA) [3], the Aquarius/SAC-D satellite [4], and the soil moisture active passive (SMAP) satellite [5,6] by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), global sea surface salinity (SSS) observations from space have become possible and a significant improvement has been made to understand the ocean dynamics and climate change.
demonstrate	V;PST;V.PTCP	demonstrated	The early concept of remote sensing of SSS has been demonstrated in the late 1970s with observations by Skylab [7] and two airborne L-band radiometry experiments [8,9].
scan	V;PST;V.PTCP	scanned	At the end of 1990, two airborne microwave interferometers, the electronically scanned thinned array radiometer (ESTAR) and the scanning low-frequency microwave radiometer (SLFMR), successfully produced SSS maps in coastal areas in agreement with in-situ measurements with an accuracy of about 1 psu.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	Based	Based on many experiments, the L-band is evidenced as the optimal frequency for remote sensing of SSS, which has been adopted by SMOS, Aquarius/SAC-D and SMAP.
measure	V;PST;V.PTCP	measured	However, the sensitivity of satellite measured brightness temperature to SSS is quite low.
polarize	V;PST;V.PTCP	polarized	For example, the sensitivity of vertically polarized brightness temperature to SSS variation is 0.4 to 0.8 K/psu for different observing angles and sea surface temperatures (SST), and it is only 0.2 to 0.6 K/psu for the horizontal polarization brightness temperature [10].
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	Thus, remote sensing of SSS requires a highly accurate retrieval model.
accept	V;PST;V.PTCP	accepted	It is widely accepted that the corrections of the sea surface and atmospheric effects are essential for remote sensing of SSS, since these effects could alter the value of sensor-measured brightness temperature and introduce errors into the SSS retrieval process.
hamper	V;NFIN	hamper	Besides the atmospheric effects, the increasing of sea surface emissivity due to the sea surface roughness and foam effects is the main source of error, which could significantly hamper the accuracy of SSS retrieval [11].
study	V;PST;V.PTCP	studied	Over the past decades, the correction for sea surface roughness effects were studied based on the in-situ and airborne measurements; for example, the experiments made from a tower [12], wind and salinity experiments (WISE) [13,14], airborne Passive-Active L-band Sensor (PALS) campaign [15] and Combined Airborne Radio instruments for Ocean and Land Studies (CAROLS) campaigns [16,17].
develop	V;PST;V.PTCP	developed	Many rough surface emission models have also been developed based on the theoretical and empirical methods.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Among these models, the small-slope approximation/small perturbation model (SSA / SPM) [18,19,20,21], two scale model (TSM) [22,23,24] and empirical/semi-empirical models [25,26] have been widely used by the research community and implemented in different satellite data processing systems.
correct	V;PST;V.PTCP	corrected	As the foam effect is significant at high wind speed conditions (above a threshold of 12 m/s) due to strong wave breaking, it has been corrected by numerous models; for example, the semi-empirical models [27,28] and radiative transfer equation (RTE) based models [29,30], which were developed to estimate the foam covered sea surface emissivity.
develop	V;PST;V.PTCP	developed	Although many theoretical and empirical models have been developed, some problems are still unsolved.
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	For example, the TSM originally proposed to estimate brightness temperature at higher frequencies, uses the sea surface wave spectrum by multiplying a factor of 2.
apply	V;PST;V.PTCP	applied	However, whether this modification can be applied to L-band is still unclear, and the choice of cutoff wavenumber is arbitrary and needs to be clarified in the L-band.
downwell	V;PRS;V.PTCP	downwelling	Moreover, the sea surface reflection of downwelling atmospheric emission is another contribution to satellite-observed brightness temperature [31], thus the determination of the cutoff wavenumber is required not only for sea surface emission but also for reflection.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	In addition, the widely used empirical models decouple the wind effect from SSS and SST effects, which means that the surface emission is due to a perfectly flat sea surface and the wind-roughened sea surface.
roughen	V;PST;V.PTCP	roughened	The wind-roughened sea surface is associated with the increased brightness temperature (due to sea surface roughness effect), which induces regional biases when applied to different areas [32].
hamper	V;PST;V.PTCP	hampered	Furthermore, the satellite measurements of SSS are hampered by the effect of radio frequency interference (RFI) in offshore areas of China (i.e., Bohai sea, Yellow sea and East China sea), which causes a large amount of data to be discarded [33].
need	V;PRS;SG;3	needs	Thus, the compatibility of these models in coastal area of China needs to be assessed and tested in order to achieve higher accuracies of SSS from the space-borne observations.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	We have shown that the addition of an artificial tail during ontogeny can produce postural and locomotory changes in chickens, consistent with the posture and kinematics inferred for non-avian dinosaurs [5], [6], [11].
produce	V;PST;SG;3	produced	The posterior displacement of the CoM produced a more vertically oriented femur during standing (femur in experimental animals was 40% more vertical than control subjects), and increased femoral retraction and decreased knee flexion during walking.
indicate	V;PRS;PL;3	indicate	These results indicate a shift from the standard bird, knee-driven bipedal locomotion to a more hip-driven locomotion, typical of crocodilians (the only other extant archosaur group), mammals, and hypothetically, bipedal non-avian dinosaurs.
attribute	V;PST;V.PTCP	attributed	These postural and kinematics changes cannot be attributed to an increased weight as subjects of the control-weight group did not show the same changes as the experimental group.
show	V;PST;PL;3	showed	In fact, the control-weight subjects showed a more horizontally oriented femur during walking with respect to the control group, similar to that observed in Carrano and Biewener's experimental subjects [7].
conclude	V;PRS;1;PL	conclude	Therefore, we conclude that the location of the CoM can be a key factor in defining limb posture and kinematics.
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	It has been proposed that the relative mass of the CFL can be used as a proxy to estimate the relative importance of femoral retraction during locomotion in extinct bipedal dinosaurs [8].
show	V;PRS;PL;3	show	Our data show that for a given CFL mass, femoral retraction can be greatly affected by the location of the CoM and limb postures.
modulate	V;PST;V.PTCP	modulated	Furthermore, limb retraction can be markedly modulated with speed [5], suggesting caution when using simple morphological parameters to estimate functional relationships.
produce	V;NFIN	produce	Differences in limb orientation can produce substantial differences in loading regimes on limb bones.
indicate	V;PRS;SG;3	indicates	The orientation of each limb element to the ground reaction force (GRF) indicates the relative contribution of axial and bending forces to external bone loading: a bone perpendicular to the GRF is expected experience greater bending forces than one parallel to the GRF.
adapt	V;PRS;SG;3	adapts	Because bone adapts to its loading environment [19], [20], [21], geometric information from limb bones, such as lengths and cross-sectional geometry, are expected to reflect differences in loading regimes and consequently in behavior and locomotor patterns [22], [23].
suggest	V;PST;V.PTCP	suggested	In this framework, scaling differences in femoral geometry between non-avian theropods and birds have been suggested to be the result of postural differences between these groups [6], [23].
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Birds have relatively shorter, stouter femora than non-avian theropods, presumed to be associated with more horizontal orientation.
suggest	V;PRS;PL;3	suggest	Experimental manipulations of femoral orientation in chickens suggest that torsional loads increase as the femur becomes more horizontal [7] supporting the idea that postural differences could be reflected in differences in limb cross-sectional geometry.
test	V;NFIN	test	To test if the postural differences observed in this study produced changes in limb morphology, we measured the length and mid-shaft cross-sectional properties of the femur in all our individuals.
find	V;PST;1;PL	found	However, we found no differences in cross-sectional femoral geometry among groups.
consider	V;PRS;V.PTCP	considering	Maybe this is not surprising considering that a recent study analyzing the relationship between posture and femur cross-sectional properties failed to find differences between birds and non-avian theropods [24], suggesting that simple morphological correlates of limb posture should be used with caution.
tend	V;PST;SG;3	tended	Interestingly, femur length tended to be greater in the experimental group than in both the control-weight and the control group (by 4 and 7%, respectively), although not signifcant.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	Longer limbs are expected to experience larger bending and torsional moments, so the fact that experimental animals had longer femora suggests that limb verticalization reduces these moments by orienting the bone more parallel to the GRF line of action.
support	V;NFIN	support	If this were the case, it would support the idea that non-avian theropods have relatively thinner femora than extant birds because of postural differences [6].
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	The present study was inspired by Carrano & Biewener [7] but our results differed markedly from theirs.
suggest	V;PRS;1;PL	suggest	We suggest that the different outcomes are due to the distinct rearing and exercising conditions used in each study, in addition to the different artificial tails used.
live	V;PST;PL;3	lived	First, our experimental subjects lived in a large enclosure under conditions that allowed them to exercise all day long.
house	V;PST;V.PTCP	housed	In Carrano & Biewener's study, experimental chickens were housed individually in smaller cages and were only allowed to exercise 20 minutes per day, 3 days per week, from the 6th to the 12th week.
attach	V;PST;V.PTCP	attached	Second, in their study, a lead mass was attached at the distal end of the experimental tail, probably generating excessive displacement of the CoM.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	During avian evolution, the loss of the CFL and reorganization of the pelvic musculature [5], [13] could have made birds unable to properly carry a postacetabular mass equivalent to that carried by non-avian theropods [5], [6].
attempt	V;PST;1;PL	attempted	In our experimental setup, we attempted to more closely mimic non-avian theropod tail morphology, in which mass is distributed through a distally tapering tail.
reduce	V;PST;1;PL	reduced	In addition, we reduced the total tail mass to 15% body mass from the 20% body mass used by Carrano and Biewener.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	Thus, our study seems to have generated a more gradual and less pronounced change in the moment of inertia produced by the artificial tail, allowing experimental subjects to adjust to the posterior mass by adopting a more vertical position of the femur while standing.
resemble	V;PRS;SG;3	resembles	Interestingly, the femur kinematics during walking in our control-weight group resembles the results reported in the experimental subjects of Carrano and Biewener.
suggest	V;PRS;SG;3	suggests	This suggests that their results could be partially explained as a response to the increased loading rather than to the displacement of the CoM.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Due to the phylogenetic relatedness, extant birds have been used to inform functional aspects of non-avian dinosaur locomotion.
make	V;PRS;PL;3	make	However, substantial differences in hindlimb morphology between these groups make difficult to assess the validity of inferences obtained from such studies.
propose	V;PST;V.PTCP	proposed	It has even been proposed that, due to functional convergence, mammals might be a better system to study bipedal dinosaur locomotion [7], [23], but the results reported here show that important aspects of non-avian theropod locomotion can be experimentally recreated in modern birds.
use	V;PRS;SG;3	uses	One caveat, however, is that our approach uses tail reduction as the mechanism for CoM displacement despite it has been recently shown that the evolutionary change in CoM position was driven instead by forelimb enlargement [8].
mean	V;NFIN	mean	Nonetheless, this does not mean that tail reduction had no effect on CoM displacement, but that it was not the most important factor.
have	V;NFIN	have	Ideally we would have increased tail mass and reduced pectoral limb mass but, unfortunately, this is not experimentally feasible.
argue	V;PRS;1;PL	argue	We argue that our experimental approach, although not perfect, was effective in displacing the CoM and recreating locomotor patterns expected in non-avian theropods.
expect	V;PRS;1;PL	expect	Thus, we expect that careful phenotypic manipulation of extant birds can open new avenues of experimental investigation into unexplored facets of dinosaur locomotor mechanics and energetics, providing a more nuanced understanding of the relationship between form and function in dinosaur evolution.
say	V;NFIN	say	It is no exaggeration to say that HBO’s Game of Thrones is more than just a television series or a successful brand:
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	it is a transmedia system in the sense first used by Marsha Kinder (1991) and popularized by Henry Jenkins (2006), in which media-hopping networks of intertextualities extend the “storyworld” of an original production.
span	V;PRS;V.PTCP	spanning	Now spanning six seasons and 60 episodes, with an average global viewership (from its most recent season) of 25.1 million viewers per episode (Shepherd, 2016), it has spawned five video games, a graphic novel adaptation, several companion books, two rap albums, a 28-city orchestral tour, a wide variety of tabletop games, toys, merchandise and mobile apps, and countless podcasts, fanfics and other fan-based creations.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given the volume of content this represents, it is easy to forget that the television series itself is an adaptation of a book series with a pre-existing fandom.
represent	V;PRS;SG;3	represents	As such, the Game of Thrones storyworld represents a remarkably rich and challenging environment for fans old and new, who must negotiate an increasingly complex network of paratexts and intertexts in order to fully engage with its narratives.
represent	V;PRS;PL;3	represent	In this sense, fans of the series represent an emerging model for cultural consumption that should be carefully explored.
exemplify	V;PST;V.PTCP	exemplified	Transmedia systems, like that exemplified by Game of Thrones, are becoming increasingly prevalent (e.g., Star Wars, Harry Potter, The Walking Dead, the Marvel Cinematic Universe, etc); these systems demonstrate, in microcosm, the global challenge of managing the fire-hose flow of information in contemporary postdigital society.
access	V;PRS;PL;3	access	The study of how people, as fans, access and manage information within a transmedia system provides valuable insight that contributes not only to practitioners and scholars of the media industry, but to the wider context of cultural studies, by offering findings on this new model of the fan as consumer and information-user.
define	V;PRS;V.PTCP	defining	For us, as digital humanists, defining the “transmedia fan” is of particular relevance as we seek to understand contemporary social and cultural transformations engendered by digital technologies.
define	V;PRS;V.PTCP	defining	As a first step in defining the “transmedia fan”, the current project undertakes a comparative discourse analysis of online conversations of Game of Thrones fans.
adapt	V;PST;V.PTCP	adapted	One of the most dramatic plot developments in the source material (Martin, 2000) was adapted to the screen in the penultimate episode of the third season, “The Rains of Castamere” (Benioff & Weiss, 2013).
anticipate	V;PST;V.PTCP	anticipated	Readers of the book series had long anticipated and dreaded the events of the “Red Wedding”, while fans of the show unfamiliar with Martin’s narrative were largely taken unawares by the pivotal episode.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Since the television series’ inception, writers at The AV Club have written two critical reviews for each episode:
document	V;PRS;PL;3	document	What results are two completely separate reviews of “The Rains of Castamere” which in turn document the fans’ reactions to the episode in the form of user comment threads:
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	one comment thread where fans were expected to be shocked by the outcome of the episode and one comment thread where fans had hotly anticipated it.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	As a pilot project, the current work takes the content of both comment threads — a corpus of approximately 5,600 comments — and analyzes each thread separately using a qualitative coding method aligned with constructivist grounded theory (Charmaz, 2006).
emerge	V;PRS;SG;3	emerges	Through this analysis, a categorization of themes emerges illustrating tactics for negotiating intertexts and paratexts unique to each group of fans.
fall	V;PRS;PL;3	fall	These themes fall under two broad categories:
reason	V;PST;SG;3	reasoned	negotiation (i.e., emotional responses) and tactical negotiation (i.e., cognitive, or reasoned responses).
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	A comparison of categories and sub-categories between both groups provides preliminary findings to support an emergent model, or models, of the “transmedia fan”.
represent	V;PRS;SG;3	represents	The present research represents a first step in exploring the impact of transmedia systems, as exemplified by Game of Thrones, through the study of fans.
pose	V;PST;V.PTCP	posed	The question posed by this research is, fundamentally, an examination of how the problem of “access” is framed in postdigital society from the perspective of the consumer.
explore	V;NFIN	explore	Future research should explore the negotiation tactics observed in transmedia fans using the principles of De Certeau’s (1984) everyday life practice, in order to extend its application to the broader context of modern-day consumers.
contribute	V;NFIN	contribute	The current study will contribute to the development of further qualitative and quantitative research that will more clearly define the information behaviors of the transmedia fan.
remember	V;PST;V.PTCP	remembered	He is particularly remembered for his applications of mathematics to mechanics, especially fluid mechanics, and for his pioneering work in probability and statistics.
commemorate	V;PST;V.PTCP	commemorated	His name is commemorated in the Bernoulli's principle, a particular example of the conservation of energy, which describes the mathematics of the mechanism underlying the operation of two important technologies of the 20th century: the carburetor and the airplane wing.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Daniel Bernoulli was born in Groningen, in the Netherlands, into a family of distinguished mathematicians.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	The Bernoulli family came originally from Antwerp, at that time in the Spanish Netherlands, but emigrated to escape the Spanish persecution of the Huguenots.
move	V;PST;SG;3	moved	After a brief period in Frankfurt the family moved to Basel, in Switzerland.
discover	V;NFIN	discover	Daniel was a son of Johann Bernoulli (one of the "early developers" of calculus) and a nephew of Jacob Bernoulli (who" was the first to discover the theory of probability").
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He had two brothers, Niklaus and Johann II.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	Daniel Bernoulli was described by W. W. Rouse Ball as "by far the ablest of the younger Bernoullis".
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	He is said to have had a bad relationship with his father.
enter	V;PRS;V.PTCP	entering	Upon both of them entering and tying for first place in a scientific contest at the University of Paris, Johann, unable to bear the "shame" of being compared Daniel's equal, banned Daniel from his house.
plagiarize	V;PST;SG;3	plagiarized	Johann Bernoulli also plagiarized some key ideas from Daniel's book Hydrodynamica in his own book Hydraulica which he backdated to before Hydrodynamica.
carry	V;PST;SG;3	carried	Despite Daniel's attempts at reconciliation, his father carried the grudge until his death.
school	V;PRS;V.PTCP	schooling	Around schooling age, his father, Johann, encouraged him to study business, there being poor rewards awaiting a mathematician.
refuse	V;PST;SG;3	refused	However, Daniel refused, because he wanted to study mathematics.
give	V;PST;SG;3	gave	He later gave in to his father's wish and studied business.
ask	V;PST;SG;3	asked	His father then asked him to study in medicine, and Daniel agreed under the condition that his father would teach him mathematics privately, which they continued for some time.
study	V;PST;SG;3	studied	Daniel studied medicine at Basel, Heidelberg, and Strasbourg, and earned a PhD in anatomy and botany in 1721.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	He went to St. Petersburg in 1724 as professor of mathematics, but was very unhappy there, and a temporary illness in 1733 gave him an excuse for leaving St. Petersburg.
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	He returned to the University of Basel, where he successively held the chairs of medicine, metaphysics, and natural philosophy until his death.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	In May, 1750 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	His earliest mathematical work was the Exercitationes (Mathematical Exercises), published in 1724 with the help of Goldbach.
point	V;PST;SG;3	pointed	Two years later he pointed out for the first time the frequent desirability of resolving a compound motion into motions of translation and motion of rotation.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	His chief work is Hydrodynamica, published in 1738;
resemble	V;PRS;SG;3	resembles	it resembles Joseph Louis Lagrange's Mécanique Analytique in being arranged so that all the results are consequences of a single principle, namely, conservation of energy.
follow	V;PST;V.PTCP	followed	This was followed by a memoir on the theory of the tides, to which, conjointly with the memoirs by Euler and Colin Maclaurin, a prize was awarded by the French Academy: these three memoirs contain all that was done on this subject between the publication of Isaac Newton's Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica and the investigations of Pierre-Simon Laplace.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	Bernoulli also wrote a large number of papers on various mechanical questions, especially on problems connected with vibrating strings, and the solutions given by Brook Taylor and by Jean le Rond d'Alembert.
try	V;PST;PL;3	tried	Together Bernoulli and Euler tried to discover more about the flow of fluids.
want	V;PST;PL;3	wanted	In particular, they wanted to know about the relationship between the speed at which blood flows and its pressure.
investigate	V;NFIN	investigate	To investigate this, Daniel experimented by puncturing the wall of a pipe with a small open ended straw and noted that the height to which the fluid rose up the straw was related to fluid's pressure in the pipe.
measure	V;PRS;V.PTCP	measuring	Soon physicians all over Europe were measuring patients' blood pressure by sticking point-ended glass tubes directly into their arteries.
discover	V;PST;SG;3	discovered	It was not until about 170 years later, in 1896 that an Italian doctor discovered a less painful method which is still in use today.
measure	V;PRS;V.PTCP	measuring	However, Bernoulli's method of measuring pressure is still used today in modern aircraft to measure the speed of the air passing the plane; that is its air speed.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Taking	Taking his discoveries further, Daniel Bernoulli now returned to his earlier work on Conservation of Energy.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	It was known that a moving body exchanges its kinetic energy for potential energy when it gains height.
realise	V;PST;SG;3	realised	Daniel realised that in a similar way, a moving fluid exchanges its kinetic energy for pressure.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Mathematically this law is now written: ½ ρ u 2 + P = constant where P is pressure, ρ is the density of the fluid and u is its velocity.
increase	V;PRS;SG;3	increases	A consequence of this law is that if the velocity increases then the pressure falls.
exploit	V;PST;V.PTCP	exploited	This is exploited by the wing of an aeroplane which is designed to create an area above its surface where the air velocity increases.
push	V;PST;V.PTCP	pushed	The pressure in this area is lower than that under the wing, so the wing is pushed upwards by the relatively higher pressure under the wing.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	Born	Born in Tianjin with ancestry in Changzhou, Jiangsu province, Chao went to the United States with a Boxer Indemnity Scholarship in 1910 to study mathematics and physics at Cornell University, where he was a classmate and lifelong friend of Hu Shih, the leader of the New Culture Movement.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	He then became interested in philosophy, and earned a Ph.D. in philosophy from Harvard University in 1918 with a dissertation entitled "Continuity: Study in Methodology".
turn	V;PST;V.PTCP	turned	Already in college his interests had turned to music and languages.
speak	V;PST;SG;3	spoke	He spoke German and French fluently and some Japanese, and he had a reading knowledge of ancient Greek and Latin.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	He served as Bertrand Russell's interpreter when Russell visited China in 1920.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	In his My Linguistic Autobiography, he wrote of his ability to pick up a Chinese dialect quickly, without much effort.
possess	V;PST;SG;3	possessed	Chao possessed a natural gift for hearing fine distinctions in pronunciation that was said to be "legendary for its acuity", enabling him to record the sounds of various dialects with a high degree of accuracy.
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	He returned to China in 1920, marrying the physician Yang Buwei there that year.
attend	V;PST;V.PTCP	attended	The ceremony was simple, rather than the noisy traditional wedding, attended only by Hu Shih and one other friend.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	Hu's account of it in the newspapers made the couple a model of modern marriage for China's New Culture generation.
teach	V;PST;SG;3	taught	Chao taught mathematics at Tsinghua University and, one year later, returned to the United States to teach at Harvard.
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	He again returned to China in 1925, teaching at Tsinghua, and beginning a survey of the Wu dialects in 1926.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	He began to conduct linguistic fieldwork throughout China for the Institute of History and Philology of Academia Sinica from 1928 onwards.
collaborate	V;PST;SG;3	collaborated	During this period of time, he collaborated with Luo Changpei and Li Fang-Kuei, the other two leading Chinese linguists of his generation, to edit and render into Chinese Bernhard Karlgren's monumental Etudes sur la Phonologie Chinoise (published in 1940).
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	He left for the US in 1938, and resided there afterwards.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	In 1945, he served as president of the Linguistic Society of America, and a special issue of the society's journal Language was dedicated to him in 1966.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	He became an American citizen in 1954.
teach	V;PST;SG;3	taught	From 1947 to 1960, he taught at the University of California at Berkeley, where in 1952, he became Agassiz Professor of Oriental Languages.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Both Chao and Yang were known for their good senses of humor, he particularly for his love of subtle jokes and language puns: they published a family history entitled, Life with Chaos: the autobiography of a Chinese family.
invite	V;PST;V.PTCP	invited	Late in his life, he was invited by Deng Xiaoping to return to China in 1981.
return	V;PST;PL;3	returned	Previously at the invitation of Premier Zhou En-Lai, Chao and his wife returned to China in 1973 for the first time since the 1940s.
visit	V;PST;SG;3	visited	He visited China again between May and June in 1981 after his wife died in March the same year.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	He died in Cambridge, Massachusetts.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	His third daughter Lensey, born in 1929, is a children's book author and mathematician.
identify	V;PST;V.PTCP	identified	Pierre Charles L'Enfant (French: [pjɛʁ ʃɑʁl lɑ̃fɑ̃]; August 2, 1754 – June 14, 1825), self-identified as Peter Charles L'Enfant while living in the United States, was a French-born American military engineer who designed the basic plan for Washington, D.C. (capital city of the U.S.) known today as the L'Enfant Plan (1791).
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	L'Enfant was born in Paris, France on August 2, 1754, the third child and second son of Pierre L'Enfant (1704 – 1787), a painter with a good reputation in the service of King Louis XV of France, (1710 - 1774, reigned 1715 - 1774), and Marie L'Enfant, the daughter of a minor official at court.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	In 1758, his brother Pierre Joseph died at the age of six, and Pierre Charles became the eldest son.
study	V;PST;SG;3	studied	He studied art at the Royal Academy in the Louvre, as well as with his father at the Royal Academy of Painting and Sculpture.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	He left school in France to enlist in the American Revolutionary War on the side of the rebelling colonials.
recruit	V;PST;V.PTCP	recruited	L'Enfant was recruited by Pierre Augustin Caron de Beaumarchais to serve in the American Revolutionary War in the United States.
arrive	V;PST;SG;3	arrived	He arrived in 1777 at the age of 23, and served as a military engineer in the Continental Army with Major General Lafayette.
commission	V;PST;V.PTCP	commissioned	He was commissioned as a captain in the Corps of Engineers on April 3, 1779 to rank from February 18, 1778.
identify	V;PST;SG;3	identified	Despite his aristocratic origins, L'Enfant closely identified with the United States, changing his first name from Pierre to Peter when he first came to the rebelling colonies in 1777.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	L'Enfant served on General George Washington's staff at Valley Forge.
commission	V;PST;SG;3	commissioned	While there, the Marquis de Lafayette commissioned L'Enfant to paint a portrait of Washington.
wound	V;PST;V.PTCP	wounded	He was wounded at the Siege of Savannah on October 9, 1779.
recover	V;PST;SG;3	recovered	He recovered and became a prisoner of war at surrender of Charleston, South Carolina on May 12, 1780.
exchange	V;PST;V.PTCP	exchanged	He was exchanged in November 1780 and served on General George Washington's staff for the remainder of the American Revolution.
promote	V;PST;V.PTCP	promoted	L'Enfant was promoted by brevet to Major in the Corps of Engineers on May 2, 1783, in recognition of his service to the cause of American liberty.
discharge	V;PST;V.PTCP	discharged	He was discharged when the Continental Army was disbanded in December 1783.
design	V;PST;SG;3	designed	After the war, L'Enfant designed the badge of the Society of the Cincinnati, an organization of former officers of the Continental Army, shaped as an eagle, at the request of Washington.
send	V;PST;V.PTCP	sent	He was sent to France to have insignias made for members of the Society, a group of veterans of the war.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Following	Following the American Revolutionary War, L'Enfant established a successful and highly profitable civil engineering firm in New York City.
achieve	V;PST;SG;3	achieved	He achieved some fame as an architect by redesigning the City Hall in New York for the First Congress of the United States (See: Federal Hall).
design	V;PST;SG;3	designed	He also designed furniture and houses for the wealthy as well as coins and medals, including the insignia of the Society of the Cincinnati.
reside	V;PRS;PL;3	reside	He was also a friend of Alexander Hamilton, and some of their correspondances from 1790 to 1801 now reside in the National Archives.
initiate	V;PST;V.PTCP	initiated	While L'Enfant was in New York City, he was initiated into Freemasonry.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	His initiation took place on April 17, 1789, at Holland Lodge No. 8, F&AM, which the Grand Lodge of New York F&AM had chartered in 1787.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	L'Enfant took only the first of three degrees offered by the Lodge and did not progress further in Freemasonry.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	He received his Ph.D. in Linguistics from the University of Michigan in 1961.
spend	V;PST;SG;3	spent	Fillmore spent ten years at The Ohio State University and a year as a Fellow at the Center for Advanced Study in the Behavioral Sciences at Stanford University before joining Berkeley's Department of Linguistics in 1971. [1]
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	A three–day conference was held at UC Berkeley in celebration of his 80th birthday in 2009. [2]
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	Fillmore received the 2012 Lifetime Achievement Award of the Association for Computational Linguistics. [3]
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	He died in 2014. [4]
spend	V;PST;SG;3	spent	Fillmore spent three years in the U.S. Army stationed in Japan, where he intercepted coded Russian conversations on short-wave radio and taught himself Japanese. [2]
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Following	Following his discharge, he taught English at a Buddhist girls' school while also taking classes at Kyoto University.
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	He returned to the US, receiving his doctorate at the University of Michigan and then teaching at The Ohio State University in Columbus.
embed	V;PRS;V.PTCP	embedding	In 1963, his seminal article The position of embedding transformations in a Grammar introduced the transformational cycle.
apply	V;NFIN	apply	The central idea is to first apply rules to the smallest applicable unit, then to the smallest unit containing that one, and so on. [5]
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	By 1965, Fillmore had come to acknowledge that semantics plays a crucial role in grammar. [6]
publish	V;PST;SG;3	published	In 1968, he published his theory of Case Grammar (Fillmore 1968), which highlighted the fact that syntactic structure can be predicted by semantic participants.
have	V;NFIN	have	An action can have an agent, a patient, purposes, locations, and so on.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	These participants were called "cases" in his original paper, but later came to be known as semantic roles or thematic relations, which are similar to theta roles in generative grammar.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Following	Following his move to the University of California, Berkeley, in 1971, this theory eventually evolved into a broader cognitive linguistic theory called Frame Semantics (1976).
involve	V;PST;SG;3	involved	A commercial event, for instance, crucially involved elements such as a seller, a buyer, some good, and some money.
express	V;PST;V.PTCP	expressed	In language, such an event can be expressed in a variety of different ways, e.g. using the verb 'to sell' or the verb 'to buy'.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to frame semantics, meaning is best studied in terms of the mental concepts and participants in the minds of the speaker and addressee.
deliver	V;PST;V.PTCP	delivered	Around the same time, Fillmore's Santa Cruz Lectures on Deixis, delivered in 1971 and published in 1975, contributed to establishing the field of linguistic pragmatics, which studies the relationship between linguistic form and the context of utterance. [7] [8]
illuminate	V;PST;SG;3	illuminated	In all of this research, he illuminated the fundamental importance of semantics, and its role in motivating syntactic and morphological phenomena.
generalize	V;PST;V.PTCP	generalized	His collaboration with Paul Kay and George Lakoff was generalized into the theory of Construction Grammar.
aim	V;PST;SG;3	aimed	This work aimed at developing a complete theory of grammar that would fully acknowledge the role of semantics right from the start, while simultaneously adopting constraint-based formalisms as popular in computer science and natural language processing.
build	V;PST;SG;3	built	This theory built on the notion of construction from traditional and pedagogical grammars rather than the rule-based formalisms that dominate most of generative grammar.
notice	V;PST;V.PTCP	noticed	One of Fillmore's most widely noticed works of the time (with Paul Kay and Cathy O'Connor) appeared in 'Language' in 1988 as "Regularity and Idiomaticity in Grammatical Constructions: The Case of Let Alone".
highlight	V;PST;SG;3	highlighted	Their paper highlighted the merits of such a theory of by focusing on the 'let alone' construction.
develop	V;PST;SG;3	developed	Over time, construction grammar developed into a research area of its own, and a number of variants have been proposed over the years by different researchers.
recognize	V;PST;V.PTCP	recognized	Fillmore is now widely recognized as one of the founders of cognitive linguistics.
begin	V;PRS;SG;3	begins	The first chapter of “Cognitive Linguistics” by Cruse and Croft (2004), for instance, begins with a summary of Fillmore's work.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	Fillmore served as President of the Linguistic Society of America in 1991 and was awarded an honorary doctorate from the University of Chicago in 2000. [7]
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	His legacy continues with his many notable students, including Adele Goldberg, Laura Michaelis, Christopher Johnson, Miriam R. L. Petruck, Len Talmy, and Eve Sweetser.
teach	V;PST;SG;3	taught	In the 1990s, Fillmore taught classes in computational lexicography at the University of Pisa, where he met Sue Atkins, who was conducting frame-semantic analyses from a lexicographic perspective.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	In their subsequent discussions and collaborations, Fillmore came to acknowledge the importance of considering corpus data. [2]
discuss	V;PST;PL;3	discussed	They discussed the "dictionary of the future", in which every word would be linked to example sentences from corpora. [7] [9]
retire	V;PST;SG;3	retired	After 23 years at the University of California, Berkeley, Fillmore retired in 1994 and joined Berkeley's International Computer Science Institute. [6]
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	There, he started a project called FrameNet, an on-line structured description of the English lexicon implementing much of what he had earlier proposed more theoretically in his theory of Frame semantics, while implementing the idea of emphasizing example sentences from corpora.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	In FrameNet, words are described in terms of the frames they evoke.
gather	V;PST;V.PTCP	gathered	Data is gathered from the British National Corpus, annotated for semantic and syntactic relations, and stored in a database organized by both lexical items and Frames.
review	V;PST;SG;3	reviewed	Siméon Poisson reviewed Galois' paper on equation theory and declared it "incomprehensible".
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	Galois returned to mathematics after his expulsion from the École Normale, although he continued to spend time in political activities.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	After his expulsion became official in January 1831, he attempted to start a private class in advanced algebra which attracted some interest, but this waned, as it seemed that his political activism had priority. [4] [7]
ask	V;PST;SG;3	asked	Siméon Poisson asked him to submit his work on the theory of equations, which he did on 17 January 1831.
declare	V;PST;SG;3	declared	Around 4 July 1831, Poisson declared Galois' work "incomprehensible", declaring that "[Galois'] argument is neither sufficiently clear nor sufficiently developed to allow us to judge its rigor"; however, the rejection report ends on an encouraging note: "We would then suggest that the author should publish the whole of his work in order to form a definitive opinion." [16]
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	While Poisson's report was made before Galois' July 14 arrest, it took until October to reach Galois in prison.
react	V;PST;SG;3	reacted	It is unsurprising, in the light of his character and situation at the time, that Galois reacted violently to the rejection letter, and decided to abandon publishing his papers through the Academy and instead publish them privately through his friend Auguste Chevalier.
ignore	V;NFIN	ignore	Apparently, however, Galois did not ignore Poisson's advice, as he began collecting all his mathematical manuscripts while still in prison, and continued polishing his ideas until his release on 29 April 1832, [12] after which he was somehow talked into a duel. [8]
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	Galois' fatal duel took place on 30 May. [17]
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	What is known is that five days before his death, he wrote a letter to Chevalier which clearly alludes to a broken love affair. [7]
suggest	V;PRS;SG;3	suggests	Some archival investigation on the original letters suggests that the woman of romantic interest was a Mademoiselle Stéphanie-Félicie Poterin du Motel, [18] the daughter of the physician at the hostel where Galois stayed during the last months of his life.
copy	V;PST;V.PTCP	copied	Fragments of letters from her, copied by Galois himself (with many portions, such as her name, either obliterated or deliberately omitted), are available. [19]
hint	V;PRS;PL;3	hint	The letters hint that Mlle. du Motel had confided some of her troubles to Galois, and this might have prompted him to provoke the duel himself on her behalf.
support	V;PST;V.PTCP	supported	This conjecture is also supported by other letters Galois later wrote to his friends the night before he died.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	Galois' cousin, Gabriel Demante, when asked if he knew the cause of the duel, mentioned that Galois "found himself in the presence of a supposed uncle and a supposed fiancé, each of whom provoked the duel."
exclaim	V;PST;SG;3	exclaimed	Galois himself famously exclaimed: "I am the victim of an infamous coquette and her two dupes." [12]
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	Much more detailed speculation based on these scant historical details has been interpolated by many of Galois' biographers (most notably by Eric Temple Bell in Men of Mathematics), such as the frequently repeated speculation that the entire incident was stage-managed by the police and royalist factions to eliminate a political enemy. [14]
name	V;PRS;SG;3	names	As to his opponent in the duel, Alexandre Dumas names Pescheux d'Herbinville, [13] who was actually one of the nineteen artillery officers whose acquittal was celebrated at the banquet that occasioned Galois' first arrest. [20]
involve	V;PST;V.PTCP	involved	However, Dumas is alone in this assertion, and if he were correct it is unclear why d'Herbinville would have been involved.
speculate	V;PST;V.PTCP	speculated	It has been speculated that he might have been du Motel's "supposed fiancé" at the time (she ultimately married someone else), but no clear evidence has been found supporting this conjecture.
give	V;PRS;PL;3	give	On the other hand, extant newspaper clippings from only a few days after the duel give a description of his opponent (identified by the initials "L. D.") that appear to more accurately apply to one of Galois' Republican friends, most probably Ernest Duchatelet, who was imprisoned with Galois on the same charges. [21]
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given the conflicting information available, the true identity of his killer may well be lost to history.
convince	V;PST;V.PTCP	convinced	Whatever the reasons behind the duel, Galois was so convinced of his impending death that he stayed up all night writing letters to his Republican friends and composing what would become his mathematical testament, the famous letter to Auguste Chevalier outlining his ideas, and three attached manuscripts. [22]
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Mathematician Hermann Weyl said of this testament, "This letter, if judged by the novelty and profundity of ideas it contains, is perhaps the most substantial piece of writing in the whole literature of mankind."
pour	V;PRS;V.PTCP	pouring	However, the legend of Galois pouring his mathematical thoughts onto paper the night before he died seems to have been exaggerated. [7]
outline	V;PST;SG;3	outlined	In these final papers, he outlined the rough edges of some work he had been doing in analysis and annotated a copy of the manuscript submitted to the Academy and other papers.
bury	V;PST;V.PTCP	buried	Évariste Galois was buried in a common grave and the exact location is still unknown.
shoot	V;PST;V.PTCP	shot	Early in the morning of 30 May 1832, he was shot in the abdomen, [17] abandoned by his opponents and seconds, and was found by a passing farmer.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	He died the following morning [17] at ten o'clock in the Hôpital Cochin (probably of peritonitis), after refusing the offices of a priest.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	His funeral ended in riots. [17]
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	There were plans to initiate an uprising during his funeral, but during the same time frame the leaders heard of General Jean Maximilien Lamarque's death, and the rising was postponed without any uprising occurring until 5 June.
notify	V;PST;V.PTCP	notified	Only Galois' younger brother was notified of the events prior to Galois' death. [23]
cry	V;IMP;2	cry	(Don't cry, Alfred!
need	V;PRS;SG;1	need	I need all my courage to die at twenty!)
bury	V;PST;V.PTCP	buried	On 2 June, Évariste Galois was buried in a common grave of the Montparnasse Cemetery whose exact location is unknown. [17] [15]
erect	V;PST;V.PTCP	erected	In the cemetery of his native town – Bourg-la-Reine – a cenotaph in his honour was erected beside the graves of his relatives. [24]
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Matthew William Goode (born 3 April 1978) is an English character actor.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	He made his screen debut in 2002 with ABC's TV film feature Confessions of an Ugly Stepsister.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	His breakthrough role was in the romantic comedy, Chasing Liberty (2004), for which he received a nomination at Teen Choice Awards for Choice Breakout Movie Star - Male.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	He then appeared in a string of supporting roles in films like Woody Allen's Match Point (2005), the German - British romantic comedy Imagine Me and You (2006), and the period drama Copying Beethoven (2006).
garner	V;PST;V.PTCP	garnered	He's garnered praise for his performance as Charles Ryder in Julian Jarrold’s adaptation of Evelyn Waugh's Brideshead Revisited (2008), and as Ozymandias in the American neo-noir superhero film Watchmen (2009), based on DC Comics' limited series of the same name.
star	V;PST;SG;3	starred	He then starred in romantic comedy Leap Year (2010) and Australian drama Burning Man (2011), the latter earning him a nomination for Best Actor at the Film Critics Circle of Australia Awards.
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	Other notable film roles include The Lookout (2007), A Single Man (2009), Cemetery Junction (2010), Stoker (2013), Belle (2013), The Imitation Game (2014) and Self/less (2015).
appear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	appearing	As well as appearing in films, Goode has appeared in numerous television shows.
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	His most notable television roles include Henry Talbot in the final season of historical period drama Downton Abbey, and Finley "Finn" Polmar in the CBS legal drama The Good Wife.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He also had a lead role in the critically acclaimed British mini-serial Dancing on the Edge, as music journalist Stanley Mitchell, for which he earned a nomination for Best Actor in a Miniseries or a Television Film at the Satellite Awards.
portray	V;PST;SG;3	portrayed	In 2017, Goode portrayed Antony Armstrong-Jones, 1st Earl of Snowdon in the Netflix biographical drama series The Crown, for which he received a Primetime Emmy Award nomination for Outstanding Guest Star in a Drama Series. [2]
star	V;PRS;SG;3	stars	He currently stars in Sky One's fantasy-romance series, A Discovery of Witches, as Professor Matthew Clairmont and is filming Matthew Vaughn's Kingsman prequel, The King's Man.
marry	V;PST;PL;3	married	They married in 2014. [17] [18] [19]
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	They have two daughters, Matilda Eve Goode [3] (b. March 2009) and Teddie Eleanor Rose Goode (b. September 2013), and a son, Ralph Goode (b. August 2015).
play	V;PST;SG;3	played	Goode played Peter Lynley, the brother of Inspector Lynley in the BBC production Inspector Lynley Mysteries: A Suitable Vengeance and co-starred in the TV film Confessions of an Ugly Stepsister, based on the Gregory Maguire novel of the same name and William Shakespeare's play The Tempest.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In 2004, Goode made his American film debut opposite Mandy Moore in the romantic comedy Chasing Liberty.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Goode was born in Exeter, Devon.
grow	V;PST;SG;3	grew	He grew up in the village of Clyst St. Mary, near Exeter. [3]
educate	V;PST;V.PTCP	educated	Goode was educated at Exeter School, an independent school in Exeter, Devon, [4] followed by the University of Birmingham and London's Webber Douglas Academy of Dramatic Art.
descend	V;PST;V.PTCP	descended	He was descended from a family of Aberdeenshire, holders of the small estate of Auchleuchries, near Ellon.
connect	V;PST;V.PTCP	connected	The family was connected with the noble branch of Haddo.
rise	V;PST;SG;3	rose	As a result of his distinguished service for Sweden, Poland and Russia he rose in ranks from trooper to full general, and became principal advisor and close friend of Tsar Peter the Great.
assume	V;PST;SG;3	assumed	Gordon assumed the additional Christian name of Leopold when confirmed as a Roman Catholic shortly before his death.
bring	V;PST;V.PTCP	brought	Gordon was brought up and remained a lifelong Roman Catholic, at a time when the Church was being persecuted in Scotland, which had become Calvinist.
enter	V;PST;SG;3	entered	After an education at the parish schools of Cruden and Ellon, at age of fifteen he entered the Jesuit college at Braunsberg, East Prussia, then part of Poland; however, his character did not tolerate well the strict and sombre way of life at the school, and he soon decided to return home.
change	V;PST;SG;3	changed	He changed his mind, however, before re-embarking on the journey back to Scotland, and after journeying on foot in several parts of what is today Germany, in 1655 he enlisted at Hamburg into the military service of Sweden.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	In the course of the next five years he served alternately for Poland and Sweden and was taken prisoner by both.
wound	V;PST;V.PTCP	wounded	At the Battle of Chudnov in 1660, Gordon was wounded. [1]
hear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hearing	Upon hearing of the Stuart restoration, Gordon left the Polish service, but found himself unable to obtain military employment in Scotland or England. [1]
join	V;PST;SG;3	joined	In 1661, after further experiences as a soldier of fortune, he joined the Russian army under Tsar Aleksei I, and in 1665 was sent on a special mission to England.
distinguish	V;PST;SG;3	distinguished	After his return he distinguished himself in several wars against the Turks and Tatars in southern Russia.
dislike	V;PST;SG;3	disliked	Gordon disliked the Russian service, complaining of the corruption and venality of Russian officials, which left him, in his own words, "almost at wits end with vexation". [2]
promote	V;PST;V.PTCP	promoted	However, in recognition of his service he was promoted to major-general in 1678, was appointed to the high command at Kiev in 1679, and in 1683 was made lieutenant-general.
find	V;PST;PL;3	found	He was not the only Scottish soldier in the Tsar's service; his fellow-Scots Paul Menzies and Alexander Livingston also found themselves in Russia fleeing religious intolerance or seeking adventure.
visit	V;PST;SG;3	visited	Gordon visited England and Scotland in 1686.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	In 1687 and 1689 he took part in expeditions against the Tatars in the Crimea, being made a full general.
break	V;PST;SG;3	broke	Later in 1689 a revolution broke out in Moscow, and with the troops under his command Gordon virtually decided events in favour of Peter the Great, [3] and against the Regent, Tsarevna Sophia Alekseyevna.
confide	V;PST;SG;3	confided	Consequently, he was for the remainder of his life in high favour with the Tsar, who confided to him the command of his capital during his absence from Russia.
play	V;PST;SG;3	played	In 1696, Gordon's design of a "moveable rampart" played a key role in helping the Russians take Azov. [3]
allege	V;PST;V.PTCP	alleged	Gordon's alleged tomb at Vvedenskoye Cemetery in Moscow.
secure	V;PRS;V.PTCP	securing	One of Gordon's greatest achievements was securing permission from the Tsars to establish the first Roman Catholic church and school in Muscovy, of which he remained the main benefactor, and headed the Catholic community in Russia until his death.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	For his services his second son James, brigadier of the Russian army, was created Count of the Holy Roman Empire in 1701.
employ	V;PST;SG;3	employed	The Tsar employed him in organizing his army according to the West European system; and raised him to the rank of full general.
visit	V;PST;V.PTCP	visited	At the end of his life the Tsar, who had visited Gordon frequently during his illness, was with him when he died, and with his own hands closed his eyes.
bury	V;PST;V.PTCP	buried	Originally buried in the Foreign Quarter, his remains were reinterred at Azov in 1877, with an inscription in German which mistakenly gives his rank as Oberst, Colonel.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	General Gordon left behind him a uniquely detailed diary of his life and times, written in English.
preserve	V;PST;V.PTCP	preserved	This is preserved in manuscript in the Russian State Military Archive in Moscow.
edit	V;PST;V.PTCP	edited	An incomplete and faulty German translation, edited by Dr Moritz Posselt (Tagebuch des Generals Patrick Gordon) was published, the first volume at Moscow in 1849, the second at St Petersburg in 1851, and the third at St Petersburg in 1853; and Passages from the Diary of General Patrick Gordon of Auchleuchries (1635 – 1699), was printed, under the editorship of Joseph Robertson, for the Spalding Club, at Aberdeen, Scotland, 1859.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	A new full scholarly edition of Gordon's Diary in English was published by the Centre for Irish and Scottish Studies in Aberdeen (6 vols, 2009 - 2016), as well as its translation into Russian.
marry	V;PST;V.PTCP	married	His daughter was married firstly to the German-Russian Colonel Rudolf Strasburgh, and then from 1699 or 1700 to his kinsman in the Russian service Alexander Gordon of Auchintoul, author of "The History of Peter the Great, Emperor of Russia".
live	V;PST;SG;3	lived	She lived until 1739. [4]
receive	V;NFIN	receive	She was the first woman to receive the Pritzker Architecture Prize, in 2004.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	She received the UK's most prestigious architectural award, the Stirling Prize, in 2010 and 2011.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	In 2012, she was made a Dame by Elizabeth II for services to architecture, and in 2015 she became the first and only woman to be awarded the Royal Gold Medal from the Royal Institute of British Architects.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	She was described by The Guardian of London as the 'Queen of the curve', who "liberated architectural geometry, giving it a whole new expressive identity."
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	Her major works include the aquatic centre for the London 2012 Olympics, Michigan State University's Broad Art Museum in the US, and the Guangzhou Opera House in China.
present	V;PST;V.PTCP	presented	Some of her designs have been presented posthumously, including the statuette for the 2017 Brit Awards, and many of her buildings are still under construction, including the Al Wakrah Stadium in Qatar, a venue for the 2022 FIFA World Cup.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Hadid was born on 31 October 1950 in Baghdad, Iraq, to an upper-class Iraqi family.
co-found	V;PST;SG;3	co-founded	He co-founded the left-liberal al-Ahali group in 1932, a significant political organisation in the 1930s and 1940s.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	He served as minister of finance after the overthrow of the monarch after the 1958 Iraqi coup d'état for the government of General Abd al-Karim Qasim.
attend	V;PST;SG;3	attended	In the 1960s Hadid attended boarding schools in England and Switzerland.
study	V;PST;SG;3	studied	Hadid studied mathematics at the American University of Beirut before moving, in 1972, to London to study at the Architectural Association School of Architecture.
study	V;PST;SG;3	studied	There she studied with Rem Koolhaas, Elia Zenghelis and Bernard Tschumi.
describe	V;PST;SG;3	described	Her former professor, Koolhaas, described her at graduation as "a planet in her own orbit."
describe	V;PST;SG;3	described	Zenghelis described her as the most outstanding pupil he ever taught.
call	V;PST;1;PL	called	"We called her the inventor of the 89 degrees.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	She had spectacular vision.
explode	V;PRS;V.PTCP	exploding	All the buildings were exploding into tiny little pieces."
recall	V;PST;SG;3	recalled	He recalled that she was less interested in details, such as staircases.
draw	V;PST;SG;3	drew	"The way she drew a staircase you would smash your head against the ceiling, and the space was reducing and reducing, and you would end up in the upper corner of the ceiling.
care	V;NFIN	care	She couldn’t care about tiny details.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	when it came to the joinery she knew we could fix that later.
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	Her fourth-year student project was a painting of a hotel in the form of a bridge, inspired by the works of the Russian suprematist artist Kazimir Malevich
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Ichiyō Higuchi (樋口 一葉 Higuchi Ichiyō, May 2, 1872 – November 23, 1896) was a pen name of Japanese author Natsu Higuchi (樋口 奈津 Higuchi Natsu), also known as Natsuko Higuchi (樋口 夏子 Higuchi Natsuko).
specialize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Specializing	Specializing in short stories, she was one of the first important writers to appear in the Meiji period (1868 - 1912) and Japan's first prominent woman writer of modern times.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	She wrote relatively little as a result of living a brief life — she died at 24 — but her stories had a large impact on Japanese literature and she is still appreciated by the Japanese public today.
become	V;NFIN	become	Efforts to become a writer
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	She, her mother, and younger sister made ends meet by doing needlework, washing, and other jobs.
see	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeing	In 1892, after seeing the success of a classmate, Kaho Tanabe, who wrote a novel, Higuchi decided to become a novelist to support her family.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	writing	Nevertheless, her initial efforts at writing fiction were in the form of a short story, a form to which she would remain true.
meet	V;PST;SG;3	met	In 1891 she met her future advisor who would help, she assumed, this poet-turned-fiction-writer and connect her with editors: Tosui Nakarai.
fall	V;PST;SG;3	fell	She fell in love with him right away, not knowing that, at 31, he had a reputation as a womanizer.
realize	V;NFIN	realize	Nor did she realize that he wrote popular literature which aimed to please the general public and in no way wished to be associated with serious literature.
return	V;NFIN	return	Her mentor did not return her passionate, if discreet, love for him, and instead treated her as a younger sister.
become	V;NFIN	become	This failed relationship would become a recurrent theme in Higuchi's fiction.
get	V;PST;SG;3	got	Eventually, she got the break she was so eager for:
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	her first stories were published in a minor newspaper under her pen name, Ichiyo Higuchi.
suffer	V;PST;PL;3	suffered	The stories from this first period (1892 – 94) suffered from the excessive influence of Heian poetry.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	Higuchi felt compelled to demonstrate her classical literary training.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	The plots were thin, there was little development of character and they were loaded down by excessive sentiment, especially when compared to what she was writing concurrently in her diary.
develop	V;PRS;V.PTCP	developing	But she was developing rapidly.
appear	V;PRS;PL;3	appear	Several of her trademark themes appear; for example, the triangular relationship among a lonely, beautiful, young woman who has lost her parents, a handsome man who has abandoned her (and remains in the background), and a lonely and desperate ragamuffin who falls in love with her.
repeat	V;PST;SG;3	repeated	Another theme Higuchi repeated was the ambition and cruelty of the Meiji middle class.
signal	V;PST;SG;3	signaled	The story "Umoregi" ("In Obscurity") signaled Higuchi's arrival as a professional writer.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	It was published in the prestigious journal Miyako no Hana in 1892, only nine months after she had started writing in earnest.
notice	V;PST;V.PTCP	noticed	Her work was noticed and she was recognized as a promising new author.
abandon	V;PST;PL;3	abandoned	In 1893, Higuchi, her mother and her sister abandoned their middle class house and, with a grim determination to survive, moved to a poor neighborhood where they opened a stationery store that before long failed.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	living	Her experience living in this neighborhood would provide material for several of her later stories, especially "Takekurabe", (literally," Comparing heights"; "Child's Play" in the Robert Lyons Danly translation; also called "Growing Up" in the Edward Seidensticker translation.)
mark	V;PST;V.PTCP	marked	The stories of her mature period (1894 – 96) were not only marked by her experience living near the red-light district and greater concern over the plight of women, but also by the influence of Ihara Saikaku, a 17th-century writer, whose stories she had recently discovered.
lie	V;PST;SG;3	lay	His distinctiveness lay in great part in his acceptance of low-life characters as worthwhile literary subjects.
add	V;PST;SG;3	added	What Higuchi added was a special awareness of suffering and sensitivity.
belong	V;PRS;PL;3	belong	To this period belong "Ōtsugomori" ("On the Last Day of the Year"), "Nigorie" ("Troubled Waters"), "Wakare-Michi" ("Separate Ways"), "Jūsan'ya" ("The Thirteenth Night") and "Takekurabe" ("Child's Play").
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	The last two are considered her best work.
spread	V;PST;SG;3	spread	With these last stories her fame spread throughout the Tokyo literary establishment.
visit	V;PST;V.PTCP	visited	In her humble home she was visited by other writers, students of poetry, admirers, the curious, critics, and editors requesting her collaboration.
stop	V;PST;SG;3	stopped	But between constant interruptions and frequent headaches, Higuchi stopped writing.
catch	V;PST;V.PTCP	caught	As her father and one of her brothers had before her, she had caught tuberculosis.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	She died on November 23, 1896, at the age of twenty-four.
adorn	V;PRS;SG;3	adorns	Higuchi's likeness adorns the Japanese 5000 yen banknote as of fall 2004, becoming the third woman to appear on a Japanese banknote, after Empress Jingū in 1881 and Murasaki Shikibu in 2000.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Her best-known stories have been made into movies.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	Born	Born: June 11, 1988 (age 30)
