bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Brock Wyatt Holt (born June 11, 1988) is an American professional baseball second baseman, shortstop and outfielder for the Boston Red Sox of Major League Baseball (MLB).
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	While primarily used as an infielder, he has started in and played at every position for the Red Sox, except for pitcher and catcher.
list	V;PST;V.PTCP	Listed	Listed at 5 feet 10 inches (1.78 m) and 180 pounds (82 kg), Holt bats left-handed and throws right-handed.
play	V;PST;SG;3	played	He first played in MLB for the Pittsburgh Pirates in 2012, but has played for the Red Sox every season since.
graduate	V;PST;SG;3	graduated	Holt graduated in 2006 from Stephenville High School in Texas.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In 2003, his freshman year, he made the District 8-4A All-District baseball team despite batting just .227, and shared the league's Newcomer of the Year award. [1]
bat	V;PRS;V.PTCP	batting	Holt was a second-team All-District 8-4A inclusion his sophomore year, batting .324 for the season. [2]
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	In 2005, Holt was named defensive player of the year in District 6-4A, [3] and he earned all-district honors in 2006, his senior year. [4]
attend	V;PST;SG;3	attended	Holt attended Navarro College in 2007 and 2008. [5]
transfer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	transferring	After transferring to Rice University in 2009, he posted a .348 batting average with 12 home runs and 43 RBIs in 59 games for the Rice Owls.
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	He also led the Owls with 67 runs scored and shared the team lead with 11 stolen bases.
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	In the same season, he was named both to the All-NCAA Regional Team and the All-Silver Glove Trophy Series team. [6]
draft	V;PST;V.PTCP	drafted	Holt was drafted by the Pittsburgh Pirates with the club's ninth round selection in the 2009 Major League Baseball Draft, one pick before his Rice teammate Ryan Berry. [7]
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	Holt began his professional career with the Pittsburgh Pirates' short season, Class-A affiliate, the State College Spikes, before advancing to the High-A Bradenton Marauders in 2010.
cut	V;PST;V.PTCP	cut	His 2010 season was cut short due to injury to his medial collateral ligament, which required surgery. [8]
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	He began the 2011 season with the Pirates' Double-A affiliate, the Altoona Curve.
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	On July 13, 2011, Holt was named the MVP of the Eastern League All-Star Game after hitting a two-run homer in the ninth inning. [9]
open	V;PST;SG;3	opened	Holt opened 2012 with Altoona and later joined Triple-A Indianapolis Indians.
promote	V;PST;V.PTCP	promoted	He was promoted to the Pittsburgh Pirates from the minors as the major league rosters expanded on September 1.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	Holt made his Major League debut on September 1, 2012.
hit	V;PST;V.PTCP	hit	At the time of his promotion, he had hit a .322 average in 102 games with Altoona and .432 in 24 games for Indianapolis. [10]
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	On September 4, against the Houston Astros, Holt became only the second Pirates player since 1900 to have a four-hit game within his first four career games in the majors.
have	V;NFIN	have	The only other Pirates player to have a four-hit game so early in his career was Jack Merson, who went 4-for-5 in his second major league game on September 15, 1951. [11]
bat	V;PST;SG;3	batted	In 24 games, Holt batted .292 with a triple and 3 RBI.
trade	V;PST;V.PTCP	traded	On December 26, 2012 he was traded to the Boston Red Sox (along with Joel Hanrahan) for Jerry Sands, Stolmy Pimentel, Mark Melancon, and Iván DeJesús, Jr.. [12]
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	Holt started the 2013 season with the Triple-A Pawtucket Red Sox.
debut	V;PST;SG;3	debuted	He debuted for Boston after getting called up on July 6 in Anaheim.
play	V;PST;SG;3	played	In 2013, Holt played in 26 games, hitting .203 with 11 RBI.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	Holt received his first championship ring when the Red Sox won the World Series over the St. Louis Cardinals in six games, despite not playing in the postseason.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	Holt again started 2014 in Triple-A, but was called up May 17 after an injury to Will Middlebrooks and hit leadoff for the rest of the season.
hit	V;PST;SG;3	hit	On May 31, 2014, against the Tampa Bay Rays at Fenway Park, Holt hit his first Major League home run.
allow	V;NFIN	allow	On June 8, 2014, in order to allow Xander Bogaerts to play 3rd base, Holt moved to left field.
impress	V;PST;SG;3	impressed	He immediately impressed, making a sensational catch on the warning track to rob Ian Kinsler of a hit.
drive	V;PST;SG;3	drove	On July 9, 2014, against the Chicago White Sox, Holt drove in Daniel Nava in the bottom of the 9th inning for the first walk-off hit of his career. [13]
start	V;PRS;V.PTCP	starting	On June 1, 2015, while starting at first base for the first time in his career, Holt went 4-for-4 with four doubles, a walk, and two RBI against the Tampa Bay Rays to win 4 to 0. [14]
start	V;PST;V.PTCP	started	By the end of the season, Holt had started games at every position except pitcher and catcher. [15]
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Jerome (/ dʒəˈroʊm /; Latin: Eusebius Sophronius Hieronymus; Greek: Εὐσέβιος Σωφρόνιος Ἱερώνυμος; c. 347 – 30 September 420) was a Latin Catholic priest, confessor, theologian, and historian, commonly known as Saint Jerome.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	He was born at Stridon, a village near Emona on the border of Dalmatia and Pannonia. [3] [4] [5]
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	He is best known for his translation of most of the Bible into Latin (the translation that became known as the Vulgate), and his commentaries on the Gospels.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	The protégé of Pope Damasus I, who died in December of 384, Jerome was known for his teachings on Christian moral life, especially to those living in cosmopolitan centers such as Rome.
focus	V;PST;SG;3	focused	In many cases, he focused his attention on the lives of women and identified how a woman devoted to Jesus should live her life.
stem	V;PST;SG;3	stemmed	This focus stemmed from his close patron relationships with several prominent female ascetics who were members of affluent senatorial families. [7]
recognise	V;PST;V.PTCP	recognised	Jerome is recognised as a saint and Doctor of the Church by the Catholic Church, the Eastern Orthodox Church, the Lutheran Church, and the Anglican Communion. [8]
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Eusebius Sophronius Hieronymus was born at Stridon around 347 AD. [9]
speak	V;NFIN	speak	He was of Illyrian ancestry, [10] although his ability to speak the Illyrian languages causes controversy.
baptize	V;PST;V.PTCP	baptized	He was not baptized until about 360 - 366, when he had gone to Rome with his friend Bonosus of Sardica (who may or may not have been the same Bonosus whom Jerome identifies as his friend who went to live as a hermit on an island in the Adriatic) to pursue rhetorical and philosophical studies.
study	V;PST;SG;3	studied	He studied under the grammarian Aelius Donatus.
learn	V;PST;SG;3	learned	There Jerome learned Latin and at least some Greek, [11] though probably not the familiarity with Greek literature he would later claim to have acquired as a schoolboy. [12]
engage	V;PST;SG;3	engaged	As a student in Rome, Jerome engaged in the superficial escapades and sexual experimentation of students there, which he indulged in quite casually but for which he suffered terrible bouts of guilt afterwards. [13] [better source needed]
appease	V;NFIN	appease	To appease his conscience, he would visit on Sundays the sepulchres of the martyrs and the Apostles in the catacombs.
remind	V;NFIN	remind	This experience would remind him of the terrors of hell:
find	V;NFIN	find	Often I would find myself entering those crypts, deep dug in the earth, with their walls on either side lined with the bodies of the dead, where everything was so dark that almost it seemed as though the Psalmist's words were fulfilled, Let them go down quick into Hell. [14]
enter	V;PRS;V.PTCP	entering	Here and there the light, not entering in through windows, but filtering down from above through shafts, relieved the horror of the darkness.
find	V;PST;SG;2	found	But again, as soon as you found yourself cautiously moving forward, the black night closed around and there came to my mind the line of Vergil, "Horror ubique animos, simul ipsa silentia terrent". [15] [16]
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	Jerome used a quote from Virgil — "On all sides round horror spread wide; the very silence breathed a terror on my soul" [17] — to describe the horror of hell.
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	Jerome initially used classical authors to describe Christian concepts such as hell that indicated both his classical education and his deep shame of their associated practices, such as pederasty which was found in Rome. [citation needed]
convert	V;PST;V.PTCP	converted	Although initially skeptical of Christianity, he was eventually converted. [18]
travel	V;PST;SG;3	travelled	After several years in Rome, he travelled with Bonosus to Gaul and settled in Trier where he seems to have first taken up theological studies, and where, for his friend Tyrannius Rufinus, he copied Hilary of Poitiers' commentary on the Psalms and the treatise De synodis.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Next came a stay of at least several months, or possibly years, with Rufinus at Aquileia, where he made many Christian friends.
accompany	V;PST;SG;3	accompanied	Some of these accompanied Jerome when about 373, he set out on a journey through Thrace and Asia Minor into northern Syria.
stay	V;PST;SG;3	stayed	At Antioch, where he stayed the longest, two of his companions died and he himself was seriously ill more than once.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	During one of these illnesses (about the winter of 373 - 374), he had a vision that led him to lay aside his secular studies and devote himself to God.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	He seems to have abstained for a considerable time from the study of the classics and to have plunged deeply into that of the Bible, under the impulse of Apollinaris of Laodicea, then teaching in Antioch and not yet suspected of heresy.
seize	V;PST;V.PTCP	Seized	Seized with a desire for a life of ascetic penance, Jerome went for a time to the desert of Chalcis, to the southeast of Antioch, known as the "Syrian Thebaid", from the number of eremites inhabiting it.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	During this period, he seems to have found time for studying and writing.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	He made his first attempt to learn Hebrew under the guidance of a converted Jew; and he seems to have been in correspondence with Jewish Christians in Antioch.
copy	V;PST;V.PTCP	copied	Around this time he had copied for him a Hebrew Gospel, of which fragments are preserved in his notes, and is known today as the Gospel of the Hebrews, and which the Nazarenes considered to be the true Gospel of Matthew. [19]
translate	V;PST;SG;3	translated	Jerome translated parts of this Hebrew Gospel into Greek. [20]
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Jenna Nicole Mourey (born September 15, 1986 [1] [2]), better known by her pseudonym Jenna Marbles, is an American YouTube personality, vlogger, comedian and actress.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	As of March 2019, her channel has approximately 3.1 billion video views and 19.5 million subscribers, [3] and is the 86th most subscribed channel on YouTube and the eighth-most-popular channel operated by a woman. [4]
have	V;NFIN	have	Marbles is the first social media star to have a wax figure displayed in Madame Tussauds Museum, located in New York City. [5]
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Marbles was born and raised in Rochester, New York, where she graduated from Brighton High School in 2004.
hold	V;PRS;SG;3	holds	Her father is a chemist who holds several patents and she has one older brother. [6]
move	V;PST;SG;3	moved	She then moved to Boston, where she attended Suffolk University.
obtain	V;PST;SG;3	obtained	At Suffolk she obtained a Bachelor of Science in Psychology, and later attended Boston University for a Master of Education in Sport Psychology and Counseling. [7]
share	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sharing	In the summer of 2010, Marbles was sharing a three-bedroom apartment in Cambridge, Massachusetts.
pay	V;PST;SG;3	paid	She paid $ 800 rent by working various jobs, like bartending, working at a tanning salon, blogging, and go-go dancing at nightclubs. [8]
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	That year, Marbles started her career with Barstool Sports, where she wrote for their female-oriented site StoolLaLa.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	She left Barstool Sports in 2011. [9]
upload	V;PRS;SG;3	uploads	Marbles uploads a new video to her YouTube channel every Wednesday/Thursday.
release	V;PST;V.PTCP	released	One video, released in 2010 and entitled "How To Trick People Into Thinking You're Good Looking" was viewed over 5.3 million times in its first week. [10] [11]
Avoid	V;NFIN	Avoid	Her video "How To Avoid Talking To People You Don't Want To Talk To "was featured in articles by The New York Times and ABC News in August 2011.
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	In the video, she stated, "I'm sick and tired of guys thinking that just because I showed up at a club or a dance or a bar, that I want to have their genitalia touching my backside"; [12] [13] the video had approximately 36.2 million views as of March 2018. [14]
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	The pseudonym "Jenna Marbles "comes from her mother's complaint that "Mourey", their legal surname, pulled up only Jenna's videos on Google.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	Jenna's mother was unemployed at the time that Jenna's first video went viral, and was concerned that their content might put off potential employers. [7]
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	The name "Marbles" comes from her dog "Mr. Marbles".
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	As of January 2019, Marbles's YouTube channel had about 19 million subscribers and 2.9 billion video views. [15] [16]
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	Marbles appeared as Eve in season 2 of Epic Rap Battles of History, episode 13, "Adam vs. Eve" [17]
play	V;PST;SG;3	played	She played a banana in The Annoying Orange episode "Fake n' Bacon".
play	V;PST;SG;3	played	She played Miley Cyrus in a "Wrecking Ball" - inspired segment of YouTube Rewind 2013.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	On January 30, 2014, she appeared in season four of Ridiculousness.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	Marbles appeared as herself in Smosh: The Movie. [18]
unveil	V;PST;SG;3	unveiled	In 2015, Marbles unveiled a wax figure of herself at Madame Tussauds New York.
immortalize	V;PST;V.PTCP	immortalized	She was the first ever YouTube star to be immortalized at Madame Tussauds New York and her figure is the first selfie-taking wax figure at this location. [19]
professionalize	V;PST;V.PTCP	professionalized	Marbles has also professionalized her business affairs by expanding her business and bringing in people to help, including, a personal assistant, a business manager, and her mother (the chief executive officer). [8]
do	V;PRS;SG;3	does	Marbles does a weekly podcast with her boyfriend Julien Solomita called "The Jenna Julien Podcast" (formerly called "The Jenna and Julien Podcast").
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	Topics include stories of growing up, conspiracy theories, YouTubers and other general topics.
feature	V;PRS;PL;3	feature	They sometimes feature other YouTubers as guests on their podcast, including Shane Dawson. [20]
release	V;PST;SG;3	released	Marbles released a brand of dog toys called Kermie Worm & Mr. Marbles.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	The toys' appearances are based upon her real dogs. [7]
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	She has also created items with some of her most memorable quotes printed on them (e.g. "what are this").
host	V;PRS;SG;3	hosts	Marbles also hosts a weekly pop countdown on SiriusXM Hits 1 called "YouTube 15". [21] [22]
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	In 2016, Marbles became an executive producer for Maximum Ride, a film based on the series of novels of the same name by James Patterson. [23]
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	In 2015, Marbles became a full-time vegan. [24] [25]
live	V;PRS;PL;3	live	Marbles has been in a relationship with fellow YouTuber Julien Solomita since 2013, and the two live together in California with their four dogs, Marbles, Kermit, Peach and Bunny, who regularly appear in her videos. [26]
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	Born	Born 23 January 1928 Paris, France
die	V;PST;SG;3	Died	Died 31 July 2017 (aged 89) Paris, France
marry	V;PST;V.PTCP	m.	Spouse(s) Jean-Louis Richard (m. 1949; div. 1964) William Friedkin (m. 1977; div. 1979)
win	V;PST;SG;3	won	She won the Cannes Film Festival Award for Best Actress for Seven Days ... Seven Nights (1960), the BAFTA Award for Best Foreign Actress for Viva Maria! (1965), and the César Award for Best Actress for The Old Lady Who Walked in the Sea (1992).
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	She was also the recipient of several lifetime awards, including a BAFTA Fellowship in 1996, Cannes Golden Palm in 2003 and César Award in 2008.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	Moreau made her theatrical debut in 1947, and established herself as one of the leading actresses of the Comédie-Française.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	She began playing small roles in films in 1949, with impressive performances in the Fernandel vehicle Meurtres? (Three Sinners, 1950) and alongside Jean Gabin as a showgirl/gangster's moll in the film Touchez pas au grisbi (1954).
achieve	V;PST;SG;3	achieved	She achieved prominence as the star of Elevator to the Gallows (1958), directed by Louis Malle, and Jules et Jim (1962), directed by François Truffaut.
continue	V;PST;SG;3	continued	Most prolific during the 1960s, Moreau continued to appear in films into her 80s.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Moreau was born in Paris, the daughter of Katherine (née Buckley), a dancer who performed at the Folies Bergère (d. 1990), and Anatole-Désiré Moreau, a restaurateur (d. 1975). [1] [2]
convert	V;PST;SG;3	converted	Moreau's father was Catholic and her mother, originally a Protestant, converted to Catholicism upon marriage. [2]
move	V;PST;SG;3	moved	When a young girl," the family moved south to Vichy, spending vacations at the paternal ancestral village of Mazirat, a town of 30 houses in a valley in the Allier.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	"It was wonderful there", Moreau said.
split	V;PST;V.PTCP	split	During the World War II, the family was split, and Moreau lived with her mother in Paris.
lose	V;PST;SG;3	lost	Moreau ultimately lost interest in school at age 16, and after attending a performance of Jean Anouilh's Antigone, found her calling as an actor.
study	V;PST;SG;3	studied	She later studied at the Conservatoire de Paris.
separate	V;PST;PL;3	separated	Her parents separated permanently while Moreau was at the conservatory and her mother, "after 24 difficult years in France, returned to England with Jeanne's [6] sister, Michelle." [6]
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In 1947, Moreau made her theatrical debut at the Avignon Festival.
debut	V;PST;SG;3	debuted	She debuted at the Comédie-Française in Ivan Turgenev's A Month in the Country [6] and, by her 20s, was already one of leading actresses in the theatre's troupe. [2]
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	After 1949, she began appearing in films with small parts but continued primarily active in the theatre for several years — a year at the Théâtre National Populaire opposite among others Gérard Philipe and Robert Hirsch, then a breakout two years in dual roles in The Dazzling Hour by Anna Bonacci, then Jean Cocteau's La Machine Infernale and others before another two-year run, this time in Shaw's Pygmalion. [6]
appear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	appearing	From the late 1950s, after appearing in several successful films, she began to work with the emerging generation of French film-makers.
follow	V;PST;V.PTCP	followed	Elevator to the Gallows (1958) with first-time director Louis Malle was followed by Malle's The Lovers (Les Amants, 1959). [7]
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	Moreau went on to work with many of the best known New Wave and avant-garde directors. [2]
center	V;PST;V.PTCP	centered	François Truffaut's New Wave film Jules et Jim (1962), her biggest success internationally, is centered on her magnetic starring role. [2]
work	V;PST;SG;3	worked	She also worked with a number of other notable directors such as Michelangelo Antonioni (La notte and Beyond the Clouds), Orson Welles (The Trial, Chimes at Midnight and The Immortal Story), Luis Buñuel (Diary of a Chambermaid), Elia Kazan (The Last Tycoon), Rainer Werner Fassbinder (Querelle), Wim Wenders (Until the End of the World), Carl Foreman (Champion and The Victors), and Manoel de Oliveira (Gebo et l'Ombre).
award	V;PST;V.PTCP	awarded	In 2005, she was awarded with the Stanislavsky Award at the 27th Moscow International Film Festival. [9]
release	V;PST;SG;3	released	She released several albums and once performed with Frank Sinatra at Carnegie Hall in 1984. [2]
work	V;PST;SG;3	worked	In addition to acting, Moreau worked behind the camera as a writer, director and producer. [2]
need	V;PST;V.PTCP	needed	Her accomplishments were the subject of the film Calling the Shots (1988) by Janis Cole and Holly Dale. [citation needed]
develop	V;PST;SG;3	developed	Eugene A. Nida (November 11, 1914 – August 25, 2011) was a linguist who developed the dynamic-equivalence Bible-translation theory and one of the founders of the modern discipline of Translation Studies.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Nida was born in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma on November 11, 1914.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	He became a Christian at a young age, when he responded to the altar call at his church "to accept Christ as my Saviour."
graduate	V;PST;SG;3	graduated	He graduated summa cum laude from the University of California in 1936.
graduate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	graduating	After graduating he attended Camp Wycliffe, where Bible translation theory was taught.
minister	V;PST;SG;3	ministered	He ministered for a short time among the Tarahumara Indians in Chihuahua, Mexico, until health problems due to an inadequate diet and the high altitude forced him to leave.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	Sometime in this period, Nida became a founding charter member of Wycliffe Bible Translators, a related organization to the Summer Institute of Linguistics.
undertake	V;PST;SG;3	undertook	In 1937, Nida undertook studies at the University of Southern California, where he obtained a master's degree in New Testament Greek in 1939.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	In that same year he became interim pastor of Calvary Church of Santa Ana, California following the resignation of its founding pastor.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	Despite his conservative background, in later years Nida became increasingly ecumenical and New Evangelical (clarification needed) in his approach.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	In 1943, Nida received his Ph.D. in Linguistics from the University of Michigan.
ordain	V;PST;V.PTCP	ordained	He was ordained as a Baptist minister.
marry	V;PST;SG;3	married	He married Althea Lucille Sprague, who died in 1993.
marry	V;PST;SG;3	married	In 1997, he married María Elena Fernandez-Miranda, a lawyer and diplomatic attache.
retire	V;PST;SG;3	retired	Nida retired in the early 1980s, although he continued to give lectures in universities all around the world, and lived in Alpine, Arizona, USA; Madrid, Spain and Brussels, Belgium.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	He died in Madrid on August 25, 2011 aged 96.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	according	His Ph.D. dissertation, A Synopsis of English Syntax, was the first full-scale analysis of a major language according to the "immediate-constituent" theory.
remain	V;PST;SG;3	remained	It remained the only thorough introduction to the field for decades and is still valuable for its many examples and exercises.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	His most notable contribution to translation theory is Dynamic Equivalence, also known as Functional Equivalence.
see	V;IMP;2	see	For more information, see "Dynamic and formal equivalence."
develop	V;PST;SG;3	developed	Nida also developed the componential analysis technique, which split words into their components to help determine equivalence in translation (e.g. "bachelor" = male + unmarried).
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	This is, perhaps, not the best example of the technique, though it is the most well-known.
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	Nida's dynamic-equivalence theory is often held in opposition to the views of philologists who maintain that an understanding of the source text (ST) can be achieved by assessing the inter-animation of words on the page, and that meaning is self-contained within the text (i.e. much more focused on achieving semantic equivalence).
translate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	translating	This theory, along with other theories of correspondence in translating, are elaborated in his essay Principles of Correspondence, where Nida begins by asserting that given that "no two languages are identical, either in the meanings given to corresponding symbols or in the ways in which symbols are arranged in phrases and sentences, it stands to reason that there can be no absolute correspondence between languages.
be	V;NFIN	be	Hence, there can be no fully exact translations."
be	V;NFIN	be	While the impact of a translation may be close to the original, there can be no identity in detail.
set	V;PRS;SG;3	sets	Nida then sets forth the differences in translation, as he would account for it, within three basic factors:
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	1. The nature of the message: in some messages the content is of primary consideration, and in others the form must be given a higher priority.
give	V;NFIN	give	2. The purpose of the author and of the translator: to give information on both form and content; to aim at full intelligibility of the reader so he / she may understand the full implications of the message; for imperative purposes that aim at not just understanding the translation but also at ensuring no misunderstanding of the translation.
differ	V;PRS;PL;3	differ	3. The type of audience: prospective audiences differ both in decoding ability and in potential interest.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	brings	Nida brings in the reminder that while there are no such things as "identical equivalents" in translating, what one must in translating seek to do is find the "closest natural equivalent".
identify	V;PRS;SG;3	identifies	Here he identifies two basic orientations in translating based on two different types of equivalence:
focus	V;PRS;SG;3	focuses	F-E focuses attention on the message itself, in both form and content.
concern	V;PST;V.PTCP	concerned	Such translations then would be concerned with such correspondences as poetry to poetry, sentence to sentence, and concept to concept.
typify	V;PRS;SG;3	typifies	Such a formal orientation that typifies this type of structural equivalence is called a "gloss translation" in which the translator aims at reproducing as literally and meaningfully as possible the form and content of the original.
govern	V;PRS;V.PTCP	governing	The principles governing an F-E translation would then be: reproduction of grammatical units; consistency in word usage; and meanings in terms of the source context.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Jared Tristan Padalecki (born July 19, 1982) [1] is an American actor.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	He is best known for playing the role of Sam Winchester in the TV series Supernatural.
grow	V;PST;SG;3	grew	He grew up in Texas and rose to fame in the early 2000s after appearing on the television series Gilmore Girls as well as the films New York Minute and House of Wax.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Padalecki was born in San Antonio, Texas, to Gerald and Sherri Padalecki. [1] [2]
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	His father is of Polish descent, while his mother has German, Scottish, French, and English ancestry. [3]
win	V;PST;PL;3	won	In 1998, Padalecki and his partner Chris Cardenas won the National Forensic League national championship in Duo Interpretation. [4]
plan	V;PST;V.PTCP	planned	Although he had originally planned to attend the University of Texas after graduating from high school in 2000, Jared decided to move Los Angeles, California instead to pursue an acting career. [1]
win	V;PST;SG;3	won	Padalecki won Fox Broadcasting's 1999 "Claim to Fame Contest"; he subsequently appeared at the Teen Choice Awards, where he met an agent. [5]
cast	V;PST;V.PTCP	cast	In 2000, he was cast as Dean Forester on the television series Gilmore Girls, a role he played until 2005.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	Throughout the early 2000s he appeared in several made-for-television films, including Silent Witness, Close to Home, and the Disney Channel Original Movie A Ring of Endless Light. [6] [7]
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Padalecki had an uncredited role as a high school bully in 2003's comedy Cheaper by the Dozen, which he played after being asked by fellow actor and friend Tom Welling, who played Charlie Baker, and the director of the movie, who wanted someone larger than Charlie to pick on him.
audition	V;PST;SG;3	auditioned	Padalecki originally auditioned for Welling's role, but gave it up in order to film a pilot titled Young MacGyver which was never picked up. [8]
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	In 2004, he appeared in the Mary-Kate and Ashley Olsen comedy New York Minute as Trey Lipton, a cute boy to whom the Olsens' characters are attracted.
land	V;PST;SG;3	landed	He also landed a short role in the thriller Flight of the Phoenix alongside Dennis Quaid and Hugh Laurie.
star	V;PST;SG;3	starred	In 2005, Padalecki starred opposite Elisha Cuthbert, Chad Michael Murray, and Paris Hilton in House of Wax as Wade.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	In 2005, he appeared in Cry Wolf, another horror film, as Tom. [9]
cast	V;PST;V.PTCP	cast	That same year, Padalecki was cast as Sam Winchester on the WB series, Supernatural.
drive	V;PRS;PL;3	drive	Sam and his brother Dean (Jensen Ackles) drive throughout the United States hunting paranormal predators, fighting demons and angels, and dealing with all manner of fantasy & sci-fi genre.
own	V;PRS;SG;3	owns	It owns the title of the longest-running North American sci-fi series in history. [10]
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	In 2007, Jared served as the host of MTV's horror reality series, Room 401, which was discontinued after only eight episodes due to poor ratings. [11]
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He had the lead role in 2008's The Christmas Cottage as Thomas Kinkade, alongside acclaimed actor Peter O'Toole.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He also had the lead role in the 2009 version of Friday the 13th as Clay Miller, a character who heads out to Camp Crystal Lake in search of his sister who has gone missing. [12]
announce	V;PST;V.PTCP	announced	Padalecki's engagement to his Supernatural co-star Genevieve Cortese was announced in January 2010.
meet	V;PST;PL;3	met	They met when Cortese guest starred as Ruby on the show's fourth season.
propose	V;PST;SG;3	proposed	Padalecki proposed to her in front of their favorite painting, "Joan of Arc", by French realist Jules Bastien-Lepage at New York's Metropolitan Museum of Art in October 2009. [13] [14] [15] [16]
marry	V;PST;SG;3	married	The pair married on February 27, 2010, in Cortese's hometown of Sun Valley, Idaho.
announce	V;PST;SG;3	announced	On October 10, 2011, the couple announced that they were expecting their first child together.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Their son, Thomas Colton Padalecki, was born on March 19, 2012.
give	V;PST;SG;3	gave	On December 22, 2013, Cortese gave birth to their second son, Austin Shepherd "Shep" Padalecki.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Their daughter, Odette Elliott Padalecki, was born on March 17, 2017. [17] [18]
reside	V;PRS;SG;3	resides	The couple resides in Austin, Texas with their three children.
support	V;PST;PL;3	supported	Padalecki and Supernatural co-stars Jensen Ackles and Misha Collins supported Beto O'Rourke for the 2018 Senate election in Texas. [19]
Keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Always Keep Fighting
launch	V;PST;SG;3	launched	In March 2015, Padalecki launched his Always Keep Fighting campaign through Represent.com.
raise	V;PST;SG;3	raised	His first campaign raised funds for To Write Love On Her Arms, which supports people struggling with depression, addiction, self-injury, and suicide.
Keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	For the second campaign in the Always Keep Fighting series in April 2015, Padalecki partnered with co-star Jensen Ackles to release a shirt featuring both of their faces, to benefit their newly formed joint charitable fund.
sell	V;PST;V.PTCP	sold	Over 70,000 shirts were sold. [21] [22]
launch	V;PST;SG;3	launched	Most recently, Padalecki launched a third campaign selling a further 40,000 shirts. [23]
surprise	V;PST;PL;3	surprised	During San Diego Comic-Con 2015, more than 6,000 fans surprised Padalecki at the Supernatural panel by holding up candles for him, after having revealed his battle with depression earlier in the year. [24]
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Theodorus of Tabennese (c. 314 – 368), also known as Abba Theodorus and Theodore the Sanctified was the spiritual successor to Pachomius and played a crucial role in preventing the first Christian cenobitic monastic federation from collapsing after the death of its founder.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to hagiography, Theodorus was born into a wealthy Christian family and was well educated from a young age.
deny	V;PST;SG;3	denied	Early in life he denied the excesses of his parents, and at the age of fourteen joined a monastery in the diocese of Sne, near the modern town of Esna, Egypt.
stay	V;PST;SG;3	stayed	A brother from Theodorus’ monastery stayed with Pachomius in Tabennese while traveling and preached of the virtues of the Koinonia upon his return to Sne.
pray	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Praying	Praying and weeping, Theodorus became determined that his destiny lay with Pachomius.
deny	V;PST;V.PTCP	denied	Although initially denied passage to Tabennese by a Pachomian monk due to his wealthy background, Theodorus opted to follow the visiting monk, and his persistence (and lack of obedience) paid off.
welcome	V;PST;V.PTCP	welcomed	He was welcomed by Pachomius upon his arrival, and quickly integrated into the community at Tabennese around 328.
become	V;PRS;V.PTCP	becoming	Quickly becoming a favorite of Pachomius, Theodorus lived an enviably ascetic life in the monastery, and took on the title of the "brothers’ comforter".
assert	V;PST;SG;3	asserted	Theodorus asserted his influence openly (which resulted in reprimands from Pachomius) and generally maintained himself as a prominent figure at Tabennese for several years, despite never being given a position of power in the monastic hierarchy.
call	V;PST;SG;3	called	Finally, Pachomius called on him to preach to the brothers despite his youth, and eventually appointed him steward of Tabennese after several new monasteries had been built.
retire	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Retiring	Retiring to the monastery of Phbow, Pachomius gave Theodorus complete control over Tabennese, though recalled him from the position after witnessing several monks under Theodorus’ watch violating the monastic rules without reprimand.
fail	V;PST;V.PTCP	failed	Having failed to prove his governing ability and once more in a position no higher than when he had entered the monastery, Theodorus became a personal assistant to Pachomius and remained relatively quiet for some years.
refuse	V;PST;SG;3	refused	Despite his clear potential and popularity, Pachomius refused to grant Theodorus any kind of authority; though the old man's failing health soon changed things forever.
assist	V;PRS;V.PTCP	assisting	After Theodorus had been assisting Pachomius at Tabennese for several years following his demotion, Pachomius became very ill and seemed to be on the verge of death.
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	Pachomius had not named a successor, and several brothers pleaded with Theodorus to assert himself as head of the Koinonia when the sickly man died.
want	V;NFIN	want	Despite the assertion that Theodorus apparently did not want the rank of a father or this world's glory, he consented.
recover	V;PST;SG;3	recovered	Unfortunately for the would-be leader, Pachomius recovered, and upon hearing of Theodorus’ decision, exiled the ambitious young man.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	living	After living a life of constant weeping and prayer in solitude, Pachomius forgave Theodorus and allowed him to live among the brothers once more.
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	Normalcy returned, though never again would Pachomius fully trust his former protégé.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	In 348 Pachomius died, naming the brother Petronios as his successor rather than the clearly qualified Theodorus.
leave	V;NFIN	leave	The death of Petronios later that year would leave Apa Horsiesios in charge, and the popular Theodorus would retreat to the distant monastery of Phnoum.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	Although it seems Theodorus practiced complete submission to the new leader of the Koinonia, problems soon arose in several Pachomian monasteries that viewed Horsiesios as a weak leader.
refuse	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Refusing	Refusing to work or communicate and demanding a new leader, many of the elder monks completely abandoned any notion of obedience to Horsiesios.
fall	V;PRS;V.PTCP	falling	The federation of monasteries was falling apart, and Theodorus rushed to Tabennese to placate the rebels.
claim	V;PST;SG;3	claimed	Soon after his arrival, Theodorus was the new de facto leader, though he claimed to be only acting on behalf of Horsiesios.
administer	V;PST;SG;3	administered	For eighteen years Theodorus administered from Pachomius’ old headquarters in Phbow, using techniques far more assertive than his predecessors, such as constantly shuffling the offices and locations of the most ambitious monks.
rule	V;PST;V.PTCP	ruled	Yet in spite of this somewhat unstable shifting, the Koinonia was ruled peacefully for nearly two decades, at which point Theodorus predicted he would soon die.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Within a few months his prediction came true, and Apa Horsiesios once more took his place as the head of the communities in both title and authority. [1]
