please	V;NFIN	please	Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Both courts below agreed and Goldsmith doesn't dispute that Warhol's Prince Series can reasonably be perceived to convey a fundamentally different meaning or message from Goldsmith's photograph.
play	V;NFIN	play	The question in this case is whether that different meaning or message should play a role, any role, in the fair use analysis.
put	V;PRS;SG;3	puts	Warhol's transformative meaning puts points on the board under Factor 1 of the four-factor balancing test.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	Goldsmith and the Second Circuit say no.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	Warhol's new meaning is categorically irrelevant and can't be considered as part of Factor 1 or any other factor.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	I want to emphasize three points.
support	V;PRS;SG;3	supports	First, the precedent supports us.
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	Campbell unambiguously requires an examination of meaning or message.
reaffirm	V;PRS;SG;3	reaffirms	Google reaffirms that test and cites Warhol's Soup Cans as a paradigmatic example of when it's satisfied.
maintain	V;PRS;SG;3	maintains	Second, our approach, unlike Goldsmith's, maintains a balance between protecting artists' rights to monetize their works and encouraging new and important follow-on expression.
give	V;PRS;1;PL	give	We give follow-on artists credit for innovation at Factor 1 while recognizing that Factor 4 and the other factors will sometimes cut decisively the other way.
upend	V;PRS;SG;3	upends	Goldsmith's necessity test, by contrast, upends that balance.
banish	V;PRS;SG;3	banishes	It banishes transformative meaning from the equation altogether, and by doing so, it violates 107's text, contradicts precedent, and undermines copyright's key goal, promoting creativity for the public good.
strip	V;NFIN	strip	A ruling for Goldsmith would strip protection not just from the Prince Series but from countless works of modern and contemporary art.
make	V;NFIN	make	It would make it illegal for artists, museums, galleries, and collectors to display, sell, profit from, maybe even possess a significant quantity of works.
chill	V;NFIN	chill	It would also chill the creation of new art by established and up-and-coming artists alike.
reject	V;NFIN	reject	You should reject them.
ask	V;PRS;1;PL	ask	We ask you to reaffirm Campbell and reverse the decision below.
give	V;NFIN	give	Could you give us an example of any follow-on work that fails your test?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think a classic example would be a -- a book-to-movie adaptation.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think that would be a follow-on work.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think, if you -- if someone were to, you know, try to do that, I think that the -- the original creator, the author of the book, could very easily assert that that was not fair use and would have a winning case under Factor 4 and probably also under Factor 1.
follow	V;NFIN	follow	And, certainly, that would be a kind of classic example of a follow-on work that would not count.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, derivative works are generally in a different medium, and almost all of them, even a dramatization on -- on theater or even a motion picture or a sequel, they add something new according to your definition in your brief.
protect	V;PST;V.PTCP	protected	So why shouldn't they be protected as well according to your theory?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Yeah, I think -- I think there's a Factor 4 issue and a Factor 1 issue.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think the most obvious problem would be a Factor 4 problem for the person who's trying to copy or -- or create the movie adaptation.
read	V;PRS;SG;1	read	I read Factor 1, the purpose and character of the use, including whether such use is of a commercial nature or is for nonprofit educational purposes.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Is -- I think I have to look at a use under 1 as well.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	That factor, I think, is telling me to look at a use.
use	V;PRS;PL;3	use	So which use are you looking at?
think	V;PRS;1;PL	think	So -- so we think that both uses are directly implicated in this case.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	I know there's a significant amount of confusion between our side and the other side on this, so I'd like to try to clarify it.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	This case came about because Ms. Goldsmith contacted the foundation, asserted that -- that the -- the original Warhol works were infringing, demanded a quite substantial seven-figure sum of money, and also demanded the copyrights in the work.
file	V;PST;1;PL	filed	We then filed --
put	V;PRS;V.PTCP	putting	I'm -- I'm putting that aside.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	They can tell us whether they're claiming -- I think they're out of the statute of limitations, so they can't claim that.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	So I think the only thing they can claim under the statute is the 2016 license.
claim	V;PST;PL;3	claimed	No, respectfully, Your Honor, that's not right, because what they claimed in their -- in their brief -- in their complaint, and this is at JA 120 to 121, was that they said that we were not allowed to invoke our copyright in the works.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	That has ongoing significance because, if we --
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	So then I want to break them down.
assume	V;IMP;2	Assume	Assume that it's the creation.
understand	V;PRS;SG;1	understand	I understand your argument.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	If that's all he did, that's one thing.
let	V;IMP;2	let	But let's look at the 2016 license of Orange Prince, which is what I thought this case was about, but putting that aside, assume it's that.
assume	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Assuming	Assuming -- assuming that we're just talking about that piece of the case, the licensing use --
need	V;NFIN	need	-- even with respect to the licensing use, you'd still need to look at Factor 1, which would look at -- at the purpose and character of the use.
encompass	V;NFIN	encompass	And that would certainly encompass the fact that Warhol's use, the image that's being licensed, was transformative and created -- and -- and -- and infused a new meaning or message on top of Goldsmith's original work.
give	V;PRS;SG;1	give	That I give you -- I spot you.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	It should be considered.
do	V;NFIN	do	But then what do I do with the rest of Factor 1, the purpose and use and -- and character of the use?
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	That's up to what was made, what use was made of Orange Prince.
license	V;PST;SG;3	licensed	Goldsmith also licensed her photographs to magazines, just as Warhol's estate did.
hear	V;IMP;2	Hear	Hear ye, hear ye, hear ye.
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	All persons having any business before this honorable Supreme Court of the State of Hawaiʻi, draw nigh, give your attention and you shall be heard.
seat	V;PST;V.PTCP	seated	Please be seated.
call	V;IMP;2	call	Please call the case.
argue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	arguing	Bradley Sova, arguing for defendants' appellants, Jungwook "Wookie" Kim, arguing for plaintiffs' appellees.
broadcast	V;PST;V.PTCP	broadcast	Today's proceeding is being broadcast live on the Judiciary's YouTube channel as well as on ʻŌlelo.
like	V;NFIN	like	I'd like to welcome our substitute justices, Judge Shanlyn Park and Judge Jim Kawashima.
like	V;NFIN	like	I'd also like to welcome teachers from the Department of Education who are visiting the courthouse today and are in our courtroom.
proceed	V;NFIN	proceed	Counselor, are you both ready to proceed?
allocate	V;PST;V.PTCP	allocated	Court's allocated 30 minutes for each side.
confirm	V;NFIN	confirm	Just to confirm, Mr. Sova, you've reserved 10 minutes for rebuttal, so your initial argument will go for 20 minutes and then we'll give you a warning at that point.
have	V;NFIN	have	Mr. Kim, you'll have 30 minutes.
give	V;NFIN	give	We'll give you a five-minute warning.
proceed	V;IMP;2	proceed	Alright, Mr. Sova, please proceed.
please	V;NFIN	please	May it please the Court.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	I want to focus today on two primary issues.
protect	V;NFIN	protect	And second, what specific process is necessary to protect this interest?
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I believe that discussion will make clear that the interest here is possession of personal property and that process due is not a contested case under Chapter 91.
believe	V;PRS;1;PL	believe	As such, we believe the Circuit Court erred in finding jurisdiction over this matter.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	In general, a claim of due process right to a hearing has two steps.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	And second, if the interest is property, what specific procedures are required to protect the interest?
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	Now, something that's created a lot of confusion in this case is there are really two asserted interests here.
occupy	V;PRS;V.PTCP	occupying	First is the interest in property itself, and second is an interest in occupying and maintaining that property on public lands.
conclude	V;PST;SG;3	concluded	Now, as to the property itself, the Circuit Court correctly concluded that personal property is something that people have a due process interest in.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	The record is replete with case law that says exactly that, and I'm not here to dispute that today.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	But I want to talk about a second interest that can be seen in this record, and that is the right to occupy public land, the right to maintain personal property on public land.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	And although plaintiffs have a protected interest in their property, they don't have a protected interest in occupying public land, maintaining their property on public land, or dictating the county's management of the lands under its control.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	And plaintiffs have described their interests this way.
state	V;PST;PL;3	stated	Appellants in their notice of appeal stated they appealed from a final decision to, quote, "execute the forced eviction and vacature of people and their belongings from punitive County of Maui property."
protect	V;PRS;PL;3	protect	But this isn't an interest that the state or federal constitutions protect.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	It's not something that there is a legitimate claim of entitlement to.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	And the case law makes it clear that there is no such claim of entitlement.
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	In Lavan, the Court stated "the District Court did not recognize, and we do not now address, the existence of a constitutionally protected property right to leave possessions unattended on public sidewalks."
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	Later, the Court goes on to say, "nor does this appeal concern any purported right to use public sidewalks as storage facilities."
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	And there are many, many other case law citations that support that there is not a due process right to maintain property on public property, even if there is a due process right in possession of the property itself.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	And because this interest is not property, there's not a due process right that attaches to this interest.
afford	V;NFIN	afford	Even so, the County did afford at least some process on the issue of maintaining people and possessions on these lands.
explain	V;NFIN	explain	Can you explain to me, how is it that you're divorcing these two interests?
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, there's a conced- -- I thought I heard you concede that the the chattels are personal property is property for due process analysis.
look	V;PRS;SG;2	look	And if you look at the cases from the Ninth Circuit and elsewhere, it doesn't seem to be much doubt that we assume that it's property for due process analysis, whether it's on public sidewalk or not.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	So I'm not sure that -- about the distinction you're trying to draw, are you suggesting there's some sort of a lesser property interest here because the property, it was kept on public property?
get	V;PRS;SG;1	get	I'm not sure I get that, or how I see it's consistent with the cases that have been cited to us.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	So thank you for the question.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	Yes, there is an interest in possession of the property itself.
look	V;PST;V.PTCP	looked	And the courts that have looked at this have stated that possession of the property is the protected right, even when it's maintained on public land, even when it's maintained on lands in violation of trespass statutes.
say	V;PRS;V.PTCP	saying	So I'm not saying that the property loses protections when it's on public land.
say	V;PRS;V.PTCP	saying	What I'm saying is that there's been a separate argument that's permeated all these cases that is talking about the right to maintain the property on public land.
protect	V;PST;V.PTCP	protected	This is not necessarily a protected interest itself.
protect	V;PST;V.PTCP	protected	The protected interest is in continued possession of the property, regardless of its location.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	Is	Is there any specific case that you can point to, though, that distinguishes or sets some boundary as to placement and when it becomes occupation subject to eviction?
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	Because if you are going to separate property right in the race itself from the location of it, then I'm not clear on whether any of the cases cited actually draws that distinction.
seem	V;PRS;PL;3	seem	They seem to be primarily based on occupation that amounts to trespass or occupation using the property in effect as a proxy for trespass.
flow	V;NFIN	flow	So where does that separation flow from?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think the problem has been that in many of the cases that are cited, the municipalities have stated that there's no interest in the property because it's maintained on public land.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's no interest in the property because it's maintained on land in violation of trespass statutes.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	The distinction I'm trying to draw is that we're conflating two different issues.
protect	V;PST;V.PTCP	protected	The interest in possession of the property itself, which is constitutionally protected, and the ability to store property or maintain property on public land, which is not necessarily protected.
suggest	V;PST;V.PTCP	suggested	And that is suggested in Lavan based upon the citations I just gave.
suggest	V;PRS;V.PTCP	suggesting	So what are you suggesting would have been the proper process that is due under these circumstances to protect the property that you acknowledge the plaintiffs -- in which the plaintiffs have a property right that you acknowledge?
protect	V;NFIN	protect	The proper process to protect the property at issue is the process that been acknowledged in multiple cases, most notably deOccupy Honolulu, where there was inventorying, storage, and the ability to reclaim property.
happen	V;NFIN	happen	So that did not happen here, right?
happen	V;NFIN	happen	That did not happen.
acknowledge	V;PRS;V.PTCP	acknowledging	So you are acknowledging a due process violation here then?
concede	V;PRS;V.PTCP	conceding	I'm conceding that there may be a due process violation based upon the failure to provide those specific procedures.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	And it's our position that those specific procedures are the ones that are required by due process and not a Chapter 91 contested case here.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	So yes, there is a concession there, but not one that would rise to the level of --
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	How were the plaintiffs supposed to -- what were they supposed to do when the notice gave no notice whatsoever, as to how they were supposed to, what would happen to their property if it was confiscated?
note	V;NFIN	note	Well, I will note there was an availability of alternative procedures here.
recognize	V;PST;V.PTCP	recognized	First, all the cases, even those that have recognized the property right and the possessions have acknowledged that the property will not be implicated in the action if it's removed from the property at issue.
eliminate	V;NFIN	eliminate	That doesn't eliminate the due process right over the property itself, but it is something that we believe is mitigating here.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	Additionally, there were alternatives --
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	So what were they supposed to do?
file	V;NFIN;2	File	File a dec action?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think that would be one course of relief.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	And had that been done here, it may have stopped the sweep from moving forward at all.
contact	V;PRS;V.PTCP	contacting	So contacting and speaking to the mayor directly was not enough?
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	So there was contact with the mayor.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I believe that occurred on September 17th from the record.
speak	V;NFIN	speak	And at that time, the mayor did speak individually with Ms. Lau as well as Ms. Davis.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Writing	Writing in the Age of Distraction
license	V;PST;V.PTCP	licensed	“Distractions” by Emily boston is licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0
know	V;PRS;1;PL	know	We know that our readers are distracted and sometimes even overwhelmed by the myriad distractions that lie one click away on the Internet, but of course writers face the same glorious problem: the delirious world of information and communication and community that lurks behind your screen, one alt-tab away from your word-processor.
get	V;PST;SG;1	got	The single worst piece of writing advice I ever got was to stay away from the Internet because it would only waste my time and wouldn’t help my writing.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	This advice was wrong creatively, professionally, artistically, and personally, but I know where the writer who doled it out was coming from.
see	V;PRS;SG;1	see	Every now and again, when I see a new website, game, or service, I sense the tug of an attention black hole: a time-sink that is just waiting to fill my every discretionary moment with distraction.
co-parent	V;PRS;V.PTCP	co-parenting	As a co-parenting new father who writes at least a book per year, half-a-dozen columns a month, ten or more blog posts a day, plus assorted novellas and stories and speeches, I know just how short time can be and how dangerous distraction is.
inform	V;PST;V.PTCP	informed	It’s informed my creativity and aesthetics, it’s benefited me professionally and personally, and for every moment it steals, it gives back a hundred delights.
give	V;NFIN	give	I’d no sooner give it up than I’d give up fiction or any other pleasurable vice.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think I’ve managed to balance things out through a few simple techniques that I’ve been refining for years.
feel	V;PRS;SG;1	feel	I still sometimes feel frazzled and info-whelmed, but that’s rare.
do	V;PRS;SG;1	do	Here’s how I do it:
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	When I’m working on a story or novel, I set a modest daily goal — usually a page or two — and then I meet it every day, doing nothing else while I’m working on it.
try	V;NFIN	try	It’s not plausible or desirable to try to get the world to go away for hours at a time, but it’s entirely possible to make it all shut up for 20 minutes.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Writing	Writing a page every day gets me more than a novel per year — do the math — and there’s always 20 minutes to be found in a day, no matter what else is going on.
claim	V;PST;V.PTCP	claimed	Twenty minutes is a short enough interval that it can be claimed from a sleep or meal-break (though this shouldn’t become a habit).
do	V;NFIN	do	The secret is to do it every day, weekends included, to keep the momentum going, and to allow your thoughts to wander to your next day’s page between sessions.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try to find one or two vivid sensory details to work into the next page, or a bon mot, so that you’ve already got some material when you sit down at the keyboard.
leave	V;IMP;2	Leave	Leave yourself a rough edge
hit	V;PRS;SG;2	hit	When you hit your daily word-goal, stop.
stop	V;IMP;2	Stop	Stop even if you’re in the middle of a sentence.
sit	V;PRS;SG;2	sit	That way, when you sit down at the keyboard the next day, your first five or ten words are already ordained, so that you get a little push before you begin your work.
leave	V;PRS;PL;3	leave	Knitters leave a bit of yarn sticking out of the day’s knitting so they know where to pick up the next day — they call it the “hint.”
leave	V;PRS;PL;3	leave	Potters leave a rough edge on the wet clay before they wrap it in plastic for the night — it’s hard to build on a smooth edge.
forget	V;IMP;2	Forget	Forget advice about finding the right atmosphere to coax your muse into the room.
forget	V;IMP;2	Forget	Forget candles, music, silence, a good chair, a cigarette, or putting the kids to sleep.
have	V;NFIN	have	It’s nice to have all your physical needs met before you write, but if you convince yourself that you can only write in a perfect world, you compound the problem of finding 20 free minutes with the problem of finding the right environment at the same time.
put	V;IMP;2	put	When the time is available, just put fingers to keyboard and write.
put	V;NFIN	put	You can put up with noise/silence/kids/discomfort/hunger for 20 minutes.
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	Anything that requires you to wait for a response, even subconsciously, occupies your attention.
leap	V;PRS;SG;3	leaps	Anything that leaps up on your screen to announce something new, occupies your attention.
train	V;NFIN	train	The more you can train your friends and family to use email, message boards, and similar technologies that allow you to save up your conversation for planned sessions instead of demanding your attention right now helps you carve out your 20 minutes.
schedule	V;IMP;2	schedule	By all means, schedule a chat — voice, text, or video — when it’s needed, but leaving your IM running is like sitting down to work after hanging a giant “DISTRACT ME” sign over your desk, one that shines brightly enough to be seen by the entire world.
claim	V;NFIN	claim	I don’t claim to have invented these techniques, but they’re the ones that have made the 21st century a good one for me.
save	V;NFIN	Save	10: How to Save the Middle Class When Jobs Don’t Pay (Barnes)
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	There’s long been a notion that, because money is a prerequisite for survival and security, everyone should be assured some income just for being alive.
advance	V;PST;V.PTCP	advanced	The notion has been advanced by liberals such as James Tobin, John Kenneth Galbraith, and George McGovern, and by conservatives like Friedrich Hayek, Milton Friedman, and Richard Nixon.
embd	V;PST;V.PTCP	embedded	It’s embedded in the board game Monopoly, in which all players get equal payments when they pass Go.
agree	V;NFIN	agree	And yet, with one exception, Americans have been unable to agree on any plan that guarantees some income to everyone.
lie	V;PRS;PL;3	lie	The reasons lie mostly in the stories that surround such income.
require	V;NFIN	require	Does it require higher taxes and bigger government?
think	V;PRS;PL;3	think	Americans think dimly of all these things.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	But then, there’s the exception.
conceive	V;PST;SG;3	conceived	Jay Hammond, the Republican governor of Alaska from 1974 to 1982, was an independent thinker who conceived of, and then persuaded Alaska’s legislators to adopt, the world’s first system for paying equal dividends to everyone.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	In Hammond’s model, the money comes not from taxes but from a common resource: North Slope oil.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using proceeds from that gift of nature, the Alaska Permanent Fund has paid equal yearly dividends to every resident, including children, ranging from about $ 1,000 to over $ 3,000.
bear	V;IMP;2	Bear	(Bear in mind that a family of four collects four same-sized dividends.)
live	V;NFIN	live	While this isn’t enough to live on, it nicely supplements Alaskans’ other earnings.
pay	V;PRS;V.PTCP	paying	And paying such dividends regularly for more than thirty years has bolstered the state’s economy, reduced poverty, and made Alaska one of the least unequal states in America.
ask	V;NFIN	ask	The question Americans in the lower 48 should now ask is: Did Alaska find the right formula?
convert	V;NFIN	convert	If it can convert part of its common wealth into equal dividends for everyone, can the rest of America do the same? [...]
work	V;NFIN	Work	How Would It Work Nationally?
work	V;NFIN	work	How might a common wealth dividend system work at the national level?
distribute	V;PRS;V.PTCP	distributing	The easy part is distributing the dividends.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	As in Alaska, enrollment could be done online and payments could wired electronically at a cost of pennies per transaction.
set	V;NFIN	set	The Social Security Administration could set that up in a jiffy.
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	collecting	The harder part is collecting the revenue.
show	V;PRS;SG;1	show	In my latest book, With Liberty and Dividends For All, I show how, over time, we could generate enough revenue to pay dividends of up to $ 5,000 per person per year.
come	V;NFIN	come	Initially, a sizable chunk would come from selling a declining number of permits to dump carbon into our air.
flow	V;NFIN	flow	Later, more revenue could flow from our monetary infrastructure, our patent and copyright systems, and our electromagnetic airwaves.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider what $ 5,000 per person per year would mean.
save	V;PST;V.PTCP	saved	If a child’s dividends were saved and invested starting from birth, they’d yield enough to pay for a debt-free college education at a public university.
add	V;NFIN	add	In midlife, $ 5,000 per person would add 25 percent to the income of a family of four earning $ 80,000 a year.
boost	V;NFIN	boost	In late life, it would boost the average retiree’s Social Security benefit by about 30 percent.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	Thus, dividends from common wealth would provide a badly-needed boost for poor and middle class families during what promises to be a lasting shortage of good-paying jobs.
distinguish	V;PST;SG;3	distinguished	In his 1796 essay “Agrarian Justice,” American patriot Thomas Paine distinguished between two kinds of property: “natural property, or that which comes to us from the Creator of the universe--such as the earth, air, water … [and] artificial or acquired property, the invention of men.”
argue	V;PST;SG;3	argued	The second kind of property, Paine argued, must necessarily be distributed unequally, but the first kind belongs to everyone equally.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	And Paine went further.
propose	V;PST;SG;3	proposed	He proposed a practical way to implement that right: create a “National Fund” to pay every man and woman a lump sum (roughly $ 17,000 in today’s money) at age twenty-one, and a stipend of about $ 1,000 a month after age fiftyfive.
come	V;NFIN	come	Revenue would come from what Paine called “ground rent” paid by landowners.
show	V;PST;SG;3	showed	He even showed mathematically how this could work.
recognize	V;PST;SG;3	recognized	Presciently, Paine recognized that land, air, and water could be monetized not just for the benefit of a few but for the good of all.
see	V;PST;SG;3	saw	Further, he saw that this could be done at a national level.
apply	V;NFIN	apply	This was a remarkable feat of analysis and imagination, and it’s time to apply it broadly.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Today, Paine’s core idea — that everyone has a right to equal income from common wealth — can be applied not just to natural resources but also to the creations of society.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider, for example, the immense value created by our legal, intellectual, and financial infrastructures, the Internet, and our economy as a whole.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	This value isn’t created by single individuals or corporations; it’s created collectively and hence belongs equally to all.
distribute	V;PST;V.PTCP	distributed	In a fairer economy some of it would actually be distributed to all.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	The ideal mechanism for doing this would be common wealth dividends — simple, transparent, direct (not trickle down), built on co-ownership rather than redistribution, and politically appealing.
apply	V;PST;V.PTCP	applied	If Paine’s idea and Alaska’s model were applied at sufficient scale, the implications would be vast.
wide	V;NFIN	widen	The current tendencies of capitalism to widen inequality and devour nature would be self-corrected.
generate	V;NFIN	generate	Instead of plutocracy and climate change, our market economy would generate widely-shared, earth-friendly prosperity.
achieve	V;NFIN	achieve	And it would achieve these goals automatically, without much need for government intervention.
lead	V;PRS;SG;3	leads	Common wealth dividends could be the next step in America’s long march toward equal rights — and the game-changer that leads to a new version of capitalism.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	But first, we have to see the opportunity and demand it.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Making	Cold, Hard Harvest: Making the Case for Frozen Produce (Pandolfi)
freeze	V;PST;V.PTCP	frozen	“Close-up of frozen peas” by Marco Verch is licensed under CC BY 4.0
acquire	V;PST;SG;1	acquired	I recently acquired a hand-me-down chest freezer from a colleague, and have since been daydreaming about all the ways it is going to enhance my food life.
preserve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	preserving	From preserving berries and greens at peak freshness to caching away soups and stocks, the extra freezer space will help me spend my food dollars more strategically.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	With a little forethought, it provides an alternative to relying on distantly sourced, subpar produce in the bleak winter months.
marginalize	V;PST;V.PTCP	marginalized	Frozen foods have long been marginalized by foodies as pale, second-rate substitutes for their fresh equivalents, but those sentiments are starting to shift.
usher	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ushering	Here are a few pitches for ushering in a new age of appreciation for these undersung foot soldiers of the local food movement.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	A 2011 study by the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization examining food waste in several industrialized countries found that 40% of all food is wasted along its journey from producer to consumer.
occur	V;PRS;SG;3	occurs	What’s more, close to half of that waste occurs before it even reaches the distributor, let alone the end-consumer.
consider	V;PRS;SG;2	consider	These are wild numbers when you consider what is lost: not widgets or knick knacks, but the stuff that sustains human life and health.
attack	V;NFIN	attack	The first logical front to attack this waste problem on is pre-season crop planning.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	When there are established outlets for small-batch (i.e. 1,000 lb rather than 10,000 lb) freezing, producers can look at historical data and estimate a certain percentage of projected yield that – either because of quality or short shelf life – will not sell on the fresh market.
make	V;NFIN	make	Growers can then make arrangements with regional processors for this portion of the crop to be frozen, locking in a fair price ahead of time without the urgent pressure to move perishable inventory mid-season.
turn	V;NFIN	turn	In this way, farmers can turn potential waste into nutritious and saleable food.
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	set	Our economic system is not set up to make capital readily available to small farms and food businesses, which are often perceived as risky ventures.
wish	V;PRS;1;PL	wish	If we wish to safeguard sources of healthy food and ensure conscientious land stewardship, we need to create a framework for directing resources to the next generation of farmers and food innovators.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a well-documented multiplier effect that kicks in when dollars are spent at small independent businesses rather than national or transnational chains, with the net result that two to three times more of this “slow money” stays in local circulation.
enfranchise	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Enfranchising	Enfranchising frozen foods as bona fide culinary players in the regional foodshed expands opportunities for small and mid-size growers, offering them outlets for far more food than they can sell fresh.
tap	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Tapping	Tapping into the frozen market can create new revenue streams, allowing producers to re-invest in farm infrastructure that will help them capture more of the existing harvest and even grow their production to meet new demand.
give	V;PRS;PL;3	give	Moreover, as chefs give frozen foods a second chance, they rely less on the Just-In-Time inventory model touted by broadline distributors and can begin to redesign their kitchen spaces to accommodate more frozen storage.
spend	V;PRS;1;PL	spend	Most dollars we spend immediately subdivide into pennies that scatter all over the world.
blind	V;PRS;SG;3	blinds	Sheer geography blinds us to the real human, environmental and social consequences of our economic choices.
sourcing	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sourcing	Only by sourcing from local producers can we witness the impacts of our food choices and vote with our dollars for best practices that create a foundation for lasting regional food security.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	By food security I mean not simply access to calories, but community-wide access to healthy, real food from trusted producers who belong to the same community as the eaters they are feeding.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is an emerging scientific consensus that extreme weather events are on the rise, and such events can impact regional infrastructure for transporting food goods.
create	V;NFIN	create	Major flooding, winter storms and other natural disasters can create serious obstacles for both shipping out and bringing in food.
cause	V;PRS;PL;3	cause	Despite the temporary nature of these phenomena and the damage they cause, a pinch in the food supply has immediate effects on a community.
link	V;PST;V.PTCP	linked	More importantly, access to good food should be a basic right and its absence is linked to other types of disenfranchisement, as the plight of many food deserts shows.
freeze	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Freezing	Freezing and storing the local harvest is one way to establish reserves of good food in the interest of community resilience and improving food access for all.
sway	V;NFIN	sway	To be sure, none of these pitches will sway anyone who thinks they are just window dressing designed to make them forget the reason they spurned frozen foods in the first place: a nagging sense that they are weak stand-ins for their fresh counterparts.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	Fortunately, there is growing evidence to the contrary, as frozen foods are gaining credence and approval in the realms of both flavor and nutrition.
stand	V;PRS;V.PTCP	standing	Despite their long-standing stigma, frozen foods are shedding their bad reputation as eaters and chefs make increasingly complex decisions about where they get their food.
believe	V;PST;SG;3	believed	In a 2014 survey of food industry professionals, 75% of respondents believed there are unnecessary negative connotations attached to frozen food, a staggering 60% increase from an earlier version of the survey conducted just three years prior.
indicate	V;PRS;SG;3	indicates	Such a precipitous shift in industry opinion indicates that this is more than just a fringe idea.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to several recent studies frozen fruits and vegetables have equal or greater nutritional value than their fresh cousins.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	This is in large part because fresh produce has been shown to decline in nutrients the longer it sits between harvesting and eating, whereas frozen produce stops its nutritive shot clock the moment its temperature drops.
freeze	V;PST;V.PTCP	frozen	Hence the popularity of the term “fresh frozen,” which conveys a useful message despite its ubiquitousness as fodder for claim-tastical food marketing campaigns.
bring	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Bringing	Bringing it back home
resonate	V;PRS;PL;3	resonate	If any of these pitches resonate with you, we are fortunate to have some great options for frozen local produce in northwest Michigan.
teach	V;PRS;SG;3	teaches	One is Goodwill Industries’ Farm to Freezer project, a job training program that teaches kitchen skills by freezing small batches of local produce.
carry	V;PRS;PL;3	carry	They carry a wide range of fruits and vegetables, which are available at many area grocers.
partner	V;PST;SG;3	partnered	Oryana Natural Foods Co-Op recently partnered with Farm to Freezer and Cherry Capital Foods to pack and distribute a line of Fair Harvest berries, starting with organic strawberries grown at Ware Farm in Benzie County.
offer	V;PRS;PL;3	offer	Some area CSAs, such as Providence Farm in Central Lake, even offer frozen Michigan fruit as an optional add-on to members’ shares.
weather	V;PRS;1;PL	weather	As we weather the harsh realities of a Michigan winter, here’s to taking some time to honor the freezer and carve out a more respectable place for it in our foodshed.
license	V;PST;V.PTCP	licensed	“Motoko Kusanagi- Ghost in the Shell cosplay” by GEMMA DENISE is licensed under CC BY-NC-ND 2.0
release	V;PST;V.PTCP	released	When the anime movie Ghost in the Shell was released in 1995, the world wide web was still little more than a novelty, Microsoft was just beginning to find its GUI-feet, and artificial intelligence research was in the doldrums.
come	V;PRS;1;PL	come	Twenty-three years later, it’s even more relevant as we come to grips with advances in human augmentation, AI, and what it means to be human in a technologically advanced future.
feature	V;PRS;PL;3	feature	Ghost in the Shell is one of twelve science fiction movies that feature in a new book that grapples with the complex intersection between emerging technologies and social responsibility.
set	V;PRS;SG;1	set	In Films from the Future: The Technology and Morality of Sci-Fi Movies (from Mango Publishing), I set out to explore the emerging landscape around transformative trends in technology innovation, and the social challenges and opportunities they present.
select	V;PST;V.PTCP	selected	The movies in the book were initially selected to help tell a story of technological convergence and socially responsible innovation.
end	V;PST;PL;3	ended	But to my surprise, they ended up opening up much deeper insights into the nature of our relationship with technology.
hack	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hacking	Identity-hacking
open	V;PRS;SG;3	opens	Ghost in the Shell opens with cyborg special-operative Major Kusanagi helping track down a talented hacker — aka the “Puppet Master” — who’s re-writing people’s “ghost”, or what makes them uniquely “them”, using implanted brain-machine interfaces.
inhabit	V;PRS;SG;3	inhabits	Kusanagi inhabits a world where human augmentation is commonplace, and is almost entirely machine.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	This technological augmentation provides her and others with super-human abilities.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	But it also makes them vulnerable—especially to hackers who can effectively re-code their memories.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	This seems to be the modus operandi of the Puppet Master.
unfold	V;PRS;SG;3	unfolds	Yet as the narrative unfolds, we learn that this is not a person, but an AI developed by US security services that has escaped the leash of its handlers.
designate	V;PST;V.PTCP	designated	The Puppet Master (or “2051” as it’s formally designated) is seeking asylum from its US masters.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	But it’s also looking for meaning and purpose as a self-aware entity.
ensue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ensuing	Through the ensuing story, Ghost touches on a number of deeply philosophical questions that lie at the heart of society’s relationship increasingly powerful technologies.
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	These include what it means to be human, the value of diversity, and even the nature of death.
put	V;PST;SG;3	put	As Emily Yoshida so aptly put it in their Beginner’s Guide to the Ghost in the Shell Universe, Ghost is a “meditation on consciousness and the philosophy of the self”.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	This is where the film comes into its own as it jolts viewers out of the ruts of conventional thinking, and leads them to reflect more deeply on the potential social impacts of technologies like AI, human augmentation, and computer-brain interfaces.
navigate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Navigating	Navigating responsible brain-machine augmentation
establish	V;PST;SG;3	established	In 2016 Elon Musk established the company Neuralink to develop science fiction-like wireless brain-machine interfaces.
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	Inspired	Inspired by the neural laces of Iain M. Banks’ Culture novels, and echoing Ghost, Musk announced on Twitter that, in his opinion, “Creating a neural lace is the thing that really matters for humanity to achieve symbiosis with machines.”
illustrate	V;PRS;SG;3	illustrates	Yet as Ghost in the Shell so presciently illustrates, where you have read-write brain connections, you’re likely to have brain-hackers.
fly	V;PRS;V.PTCP	flying	It’s by no-means clear how successful Neuralink will be (the company is still largely flying under the radar).
coincide	V;PRS;SG;3	coincides	But its launch coincides with intense efforts to better-understand and control the human brain, and breakthroughs in optogenetics that could one day enable wireless machine-mind networks.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given these and similar developments, it’s not beyond the realms of possibility that someone will try and fit a subject up with an internet-connected brain interface that can write as well as read what’s going on inside their head; and that someone else will attempt to hack into it.
develop	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Developing	Developing such capabilities responsibly will require great care as scientists and others tread the fine line between “could” and “should”.
demand	V;NFIN	demand	And it’ll demand novel ways of thinking creatively about what could possibly go wrong, and how to avoid it.
illuminate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	illuminating	This is where films like Ghost are remarkably helpful in illuminating the risk-landscape around such technologies—not because they get the tech right, but because they reveal oftenhidden aspects of how people and technologies interact.
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	But Ghost’s insights go far beyond unpacking the problems of hackable brain implants.
own	V;PRS;SG;3	owns	Who owns and controls your augmented self?
plague	V;PST;V.PTCP	plagued	Throughout Ghost, Major Kusanagi is plagued by doubts of who she is.
make	V;NFIN	make	Do her cybernetic augmentations make her less human, or having less worth?
have	V;NFIN	have	And what autonomy does she have when she malfunctions, or needs an upgrade?
begin	V;PRS;V.PTCP	beginning	These are questions that are already beginning to tax developers and others in the real world.
become	V;PRS;PL;3	become	And as robotic and cyber technologies become increasingly advanced, they are only going to become harder to navigate.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In 2012, the South African athlete Oscar Pistorius made history by being the first runner to compete in the Olympic Games with two prosthetic legs.
come	V;PST;PL;3	came	His iconic racing blades came to represent the promise of technological enhancements to overcome human limitations.
stir	V;PST;PL;3	stirred	Yet they stirred up fears of them giving him an unfair advantage that led to him being barred from competing in the previous Olympics.
compete	V;PST;SG;3	competed	The same year that Pistorius successfully competed in the Olympics, the Canadian researcher Steve Mann was allegedly assaulted because his computer-augmented eye extension offended someone.
discover	V;PST;SG;3	discovered	And in 2015, patient-advocate Hugo Campos discovered he didn’t legally have access to the implanted defibrillator that kept him alive.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	These are all relatively small examples of the tension that’s growing between conventional thinking and human augmentation.
illustrate	V;PRS;PL;3	illustrate	But they illustrate how the angst that Kusanagi feels about her augmented body, and how it defines her, is already part of today’s society.
touch	V;PST;V.PTCP	touched	And we’ve barely touched the tip of this particular iceberg.
form	V;PRS;SG;3	forms	Again, this is where Ghost forms a powerful canvas on which to explore challenges that often transcend conventional thinking, and play out at the borders of our moral and ethical understanding.
watch	V;PST;V.PTCP	Watched	Watched in the right way, it can help reveal hidden truths around our relationship with the technologies we’re building, and guide us toward more socially responsible ways of developing and using it.
limit	V;PST;V.PTCP	limited	And isn’t limited to Ghost — in Films from the Future, I draw on films as diverse as Never Let Me Go and Minority Report, to Ex Machina, to tease out insights into the moral and ethical challenges and opportunities that increasingly powerful technologies present.
immerse	V;PST;V.PTCP	immersed	Having immersed myself in these movies and the technologies that inspire them, it’s clear that, if we want to ensure these trends don’t cause more problems than they resolve, we desperately need the perspectives that movies like Ghost in the Shell and others reveal.
risk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	risking	The alternative is risking losing our own “ghosts” in the drive to innovate bigger and better, without thinking about the consequences.
get	V;PRS;SG;3	Gets	28: Why Sex Gets Better in Older Age (Forbes, Eaton, and Krueger)
license	V;PST;V.PTCP	licensed	"Old Couple" by Ian MacKenzie is licensed under CC BY 2.0
age	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Aging	Aging is generally associated with improvements in our quality of life: We become more proficient in our work, learn how to manage our finances better and our bonds with loved ones deepen.
improve	V;PRS;PL;3	improve	With time and practice, most of the core domains of our lives improve as we develop skills and strategies to manage our lives with more mastery.
report	V;PST;V.PTCP	reported	An exception to this pattern is the quality of our sex lives, which has consistently been reported to deteriorate with age.
fit	V;PRS;SG;3	fits	While this fits with the messages we receive from popular culture, which tell us that sex is a young person's domain, it is somewhat at odds with the fact that older adults continue to explore and enjoy sexuality well into old age.
rate	V;PRS;PL;3	rate	They also rate sex as an important part of life.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	So, if there is no epidemic of age-related frigidity, why would sexual quality of life take a nosedive in later life?
cite	V;PRS;SG;3	cites	A common answer to this question cites declining physical health and sexual functioning with age.
decline	V;NFIN	decline	Another answer might be: The quality of our sex lives doesn't decline with age.
study	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Studying	Studying sex and aging
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a key element missing from nearly all studies of sex and aging: studying change over time.
ask	V;PRS;1;PL	ask	If we ask a group of people how satisfied they are with their sex life, and the younger people are more satisfied than the older people, does that mean that aging is responsible for this difference?
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	What if instead the apparent age difference is because people born in the 1930s have different attitudes toward sex than people who grew up after the sexual revolution of the '60's and '70's?
get	V;NFIN	get	To get to the bottom of how aging affects sexual quality of life, we analyzed patterns in longitudinal data collected from over 6,000 individuals followed over a period of 18 years, spanning ages 20-93.
speak	V;PRS;V.PTCP	speaking	In 1995, 2004 and 2013, the representative sample of English-speaking Americans completed extensive self-administered survey questionnaires in private and returned them by mail.
rate	V;NFIN	rate	A key question for our study was: How would you rate the sexual aspects of your life these days, from the worst possible situation (0) to the best possible situation (10)?
suggest	V;PST;PL;3	suggested	The basic trends in the data suggested that - without taking any other factors into account sexual quality of life declines with age.
age	V;PST;PL;3	aged	But as people in the study aged, they placed more emphasis on the quality - not quantity - of sexual encounters.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	For example, frequency of sex became less important with age, and the amount of thought and effort invested in sex became more important.
change	V;PRS;V.PTCP	changing	These changing priorities were key predictors of sexual quality of life for older adults, and appeared to buffer its decline.
match	V;PST;1;PL	matched	When we matched older and younger adults on key characteristics of their sex lives - along with sociodemographic characteristics, and mental and physical health - older adults actually had better sexual quality of life.
compare	V;PST;1;PL	compared	For example, if we compared a 40-year-old man and a 50-year-old man with the same levels of perceived control over their sex life, who invest the same amount of thought and effort in their sex life, have sex with the same frequency and had the same number of sexual partners in the past year, we would expect the 50-year-old to report better sexual quality of life.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	This is consistent with the improvement we see in other life domains with age, and highlights the benefits of life experience for sexuality as people learn more about their sexual preferences and their partners' likes and dislikes.
take	V;NFIN	take	The positive relationship between sexual quality of life and aging was strongest in the context of good-quality romantic relationships, where sexual exploration and a focus on partners' pleasure is more likely to take place.
suggest	V;PRS;PL;3	suggest	Together these findings suggest that as we age, our sexual priorities change and we develop knowledge, skills and preferences that protect against aging-related declines in sexual quality of life.
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	Since wisdom is "the quality of having experience, knowledge and good judgment," our study suggests that life experience is fostering sexual wisdom.
satisfy	V;PRS;V.PTCP	satisfying	This is great news, as a satisfying sex life has been found to be important for health and well-being, regardless of age.
predict	V;PRS;SG;3	predicts	For older adults in particular, being sexually active predicts a longer and healthier life.
know	V;PRS;1;PL	know	We now know that age-related declines in sexual quality of life are largely related to modifiable factors, so we can target sexual skills, beliefs and attitudes in clinical interventions.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given that our life expectancy continues to grow, this research highlights the opportunity to facilitate positive sexual experiences across the lifespan.
appear	V;PST;SG;3	appeared	Their article first appeared in The Conversation.
get	V;PRS;SG;3	Gets	Why Sex Gets Better in Older Age by Miri Forbes, Nicholas Eaton, and Robert Krueger is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0 International License.
believe	V;PST;SG;3	believed	Betty was thirty-eight and still believed in Santa Claus.
lean	V;PST;SG;3	leaned	She leaned against the kitchen counter, picking dried glaze from the wrinkles in the back of her hand.
dangle	V;PST;SG;3	dangled	From the wireless headset that dangled from her neck, her old marine buddies called to her with joyous profanity and the bass of digital gunfire.
stuff	V;PRS;V.PTCP	stuffing	Satellite coverage in Santa’s Village was just another Christmas miracle, like faster-than-light travel and stuffing oneself through gas fireplaces with neither explosions nor lawsuits nipping at your heels.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	Santa had said he needed to leave at eight.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	So Betty had made Christmas Eve dinner while listening to — instead of participating in — the big special ops campaign game online.
salt	V;PST;V.PTCP	salted	She’d garlic-salted the yams to the tune of Gomez unloading his Glock into a drug dealer.
paint	V;PST;V.PTCP	painted	She’d painted the ham with maple glaze while Williams punctuated Patty Smyth’s “Goodbye to You” with bursts from his assault rifle.
call	V;PST;SG;3	called	“Her boys,” as she called them, didn’t take a lot of eggnog in their rum.
spend	V;PST;V.PTCP	spent	Santa had spent his day opening envelopes from North American children and falling asleep watching the Pittsburgh-Cleveland game in the media room.
spend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	spending	Now he was in the kitchen, awake and on his way out the door, because suddenly spending fifteen minutes eating dinner with his wife had become too much of a burden on this most wonderful night of the year.
tuck	V;PST;V.PTCP	tucked	His white V-neck undershirt was tucked into his pants in front, pulled taught over his belly.
stretch	V;PST;SG;3	stretched	It stretched the neckhole halfway down to his happy trail.
find	V;PRS;V.PTCP	finding	His hands were finding their way through the armholes of his robe.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	“It’s just that there are more children in the world now,” he said.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	“And Santa’s not getting any younger, ho ho ho.”
get	V;NFIN	get	“Don’t get much older either,” Betty said.
die	V;PST;V.PTCP	died	Santa’s first wife had died centuries ago, but he always just dismissed the question of his own mortality.
know	V;PST;SG;3	knew	Betty never knew how naughty she should feel about wanting to strangle him sometimes.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	“You’re seriously going to let me eat alone again on Christmas Eve?”
think	V;IMP;2	think	“Don’t think of it like that, doll,” Santa said.
think	V;IMP;2	Think	“Think of the children.”
sling	V;PST;SG;3	slung	He slung his belt around his back, and gave it a quick tug.
shrink	V;PST;SG;3	shrunk	His waist shrunk from dangerously obese to barely jolly.
crumple	V;PST;V.PTCP	crumpled	A few crumpled bills in American currency fell out of his pocket.
bend	V;PST;SG;3	bent	Santa bent down to pick them up, mumbling about “emergency money.”
dress	V;PST;V.PTCP	dressed	“Can’t you get dressed in the bedroom like a normal person?” Betty asked.
erupt	V;PST;SG;3	erupted	Distorted shouting erupted from Betty’s headset.
imagine	V;PST;SG;3	imagined	She imagined red and yellow mosaic bursts lighting up the sunless snowscape outside, as if there was an HDTV behind the blinds instead of their kitchen window.
step	V;PST;SG;3	stepped	One of her boys probably stepped on a landmine.
guess	V;PST;SG;3	guessed	Williams, she guessed.
think	V;PST;SG;3	thought	She thought she could hear Gomez laughing.
pick	V;PST;SG;3	picked	She picked up the pans in which she’d made the potatoes and maple glaze.
put	V;PST;SG;3	put	She put them in the sink to soak.
wait	V;NFIN	wait	The gingerbread batter bowl could wait a day, even though it would take longer to clean tomorrow.
appreciate	V;PST;SG;3	appreciated	For now, she appreciated its fight to cover the ham-stink that coated the room.
chime	V;PST;SG;3	chimed	The jingle bell chimed on Santa’s cell.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	“Nutcrackers,” he said.
reach	V;NFIN	reach	“Santa can’t reach it, and it’s probably Ginny.
call	V;PRS;V.PTCP	calling	“Your secretary’s calling, and you need me to answer it?
wipe	V;PST;SG;3	wiped	Betty wiped her fingers on a hand-towel.
lift	V;PST;SG;3	lifted	She lifted her headset’s microphone to her mouth.
copy	V;PRS;PL;3	Copy	“Copy that,” Gomez said on the other end.
take	V;NFIN	take	“It’ll take that long to put Humpty back together again anyway.”
switch	V;PST;SG;3	switched	Betty switched off her headset mic.
unclip	V;PST;SG;3	unclipped	She unclipped the cell phone from the back of Santa’s girdle, and pushed the button with the green arrow, serrated to look like a double-edged diving knife.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	“Hey Ginny,” Betty said into the phone.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	“Oh hello, Mrs. Claus,” Ginny said.
call	V;IMP;2	call	“For heaven’s sake, call me Betty.”
leave	V;PST;V.PTCP	left	Has Mr. Claus left yet?”
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	“On his way,” Betty said.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	“He was just thinking of the children.
believe	V;PST;PL;3	believed	When Tyler was very young, his grandmother was his favorite person in the world because, unlike his parents, who believed that children should always be told the truth as adults understood it, she would fill in the gaps in his knowledge — Santa Claus, the Easter Bunny, God.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	His parents were always too busy and often a little too serious, but his grandmother had a sense of peace about her, a lightness that lifted his spirit.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	A few times, when Tyler’s parents were away, she took him with her to church.
remember	V;PST;SG;3	remembered	He remembered liking the singing and the colorful windows, and how safe he felt there, in that large, empty space, sitting on a hard bench next to her warmth.
die	V;PST;SG;3	died	When she died, grief overwhelmed Tyler.
grow	V;PST;SG;3	grew	But like most adults, when he grew older he could only recall the intensity of that love in childhood in an abstract way.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	He made the common error of identifying maturity with worth, and assumed that the love he had for her as a young child must have been lacking in strength and depth.
torture	V;PST;V.PTCP	tortured	For many years after her death, however, Tyler was tortured by the memory of a certain visit from her.
play	V;PRS;V.PTCP	playing	He was five or so, and they were playing some board game at the kitchen table.
swing	V;PST;SG;3	swung	As he swung his legs in his excitement, he kicked her repeatedly in the shins.
ask	V;PST;SG;3	asked	She asked him to stop, and he refused, giggling.
frown	V;PST;SG;3	frowned	When she finally frowned at him and threatened to stop playing if he didn’t stop he told her to go to Hell.
see	V;NFIN	see	In Tyler’s mind he could see her face grow taut, lose color, and then, for the only time he could remember, she began to cry.
remember	V;PST;SG;3	remembered	He also remembered his own utter confusion.
have	V;NFIN	have	His parents did not have much use for religion and so for him Hell was a word without much mystery or power.
know	V;PST;SG;3	knew	At that time he knew only vaguely that Hell was a place you did not want to go, like the dark basement or the even darker attic.
remember	V;PST;SG;3	remembered	He remembered feeling resentful that she was crying and he did not even understand why.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	Tyler felt the guilt of this memory even in his teenage years.
sum	V;PST;SG;3	summed	For him it summed up all his insecurities and fears about his own cruelty, ignorance, and the possibility that he was, in reality, not a good person.
cause	V;PST;V.PTCP	caused	The fact that he had caused someone who loved him such pain with so little effort and understanding troubled him deeply.
look	V;PST;SG;3	looked	One day Tyler looked through an old family photo album, and in it was a picture of the kitchen in the house they used to live in.
surprise	V;PST;V.PTCP	surprised	He was surprised to discover that the small kitchen contained a central island, and had no space for the table in his memory at all.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	With the discovery of that single error in his memory came a cascade of other revelations.
remember	V;PST;SG;3	remembered	Now he remembered that they always ate in the dining room, and when they did play board games, it was always on the coffee table in the living room.
cause	V;PST;V.PTCP	caused	The memory that had caused him such pain over the years could not possibly have occurred.
manufacture	V;PST;V.PTCP	manufactured	Somehow, he must have manufactured the whole scene in his imagination.
explain	V;NFIN	explain	It was not very hard to explain what really happened, he thought.
cause	V;PST;V.PTCP	caused	The death of his grandmother had probably caused in him feelings of abandonment and guilt.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	In his confusion he had taken elements from storybooks and imagined out of nothing this memory to punish himself.
occur	V;PST;V.PTCP	occurred	This was the sort of fantasy that could have occurred to any young child who lost an important relative.
cry	V;PRS;V.PTCP	crying	With that realization, the image of his grandmother crying faded in his memory and became less and less believable.
think	V;PST;SG;3	thought	Tyler thought he was very lucky to have discovered the single error in his false memory, which enabled him to reason his way into distinguishing between reality and fantasy.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	He felt that it was a coming-of-age moment.
admit	V;PST;SG;3	admitted	Nonetheless, he admitted to himself that he was a little sad also at the discovery.
lose	V;PST;SG;3	lost	When that memory lost its compelling aura of truth, it was like another part of her died with it.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	He had no name for the emptiness that remained.
watch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	watching	Everyone’s just watching the weird retarded girl except this one chubby guy who’s trying to get her to put down the lights but who’s afraid to touch her.
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	“Not supposed to touch those,” he says.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“Sorry!” I say.
frown	V;PRS;SG;3	frowns	The chubby guy frowns at me.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“Cara,” I say.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	Cara looks at me, looks at the lights.
try	V;PRS;SG;1	try	I gently try to take them.
wail	V;PRS;SG;3	wails	“No!” she wails.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“I’m sorry,” I say, “I’m her brother.
delay	V;PST;V.PTCP	delayed	She’s developmentally delayed.
wail	V;PRS;SG;3	wails	She wails, but lets me disentangle her hands.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“I’m sorry,” I say again, the concerned big brother.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	“I was just looking around and thought she was right with me, you know?
hover	V;PRS;SG;3	hovers	Chubby guy kind of hovers until I get the lights away from Cara and as soon as I put them on the shelf he grabs them and starts straightening them out and draping them back over the display.
herd	V;PRS;SG;1	herd	I herd Cara towards the front of the store, mouthing sorry at the front cashier.
smile	V;PRS;SG;3	smiles	She smiles at me.
wail	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wailing	Back out in the mall, Cara is wailing, which could start an asthma attack, so to distract her I say, “You want a cookie?”
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Mom has Cara on a diet, so of course she wants a cookie.
perk	V;PRS;SG;3	perks	She perks up the way Shelby, our Shetland Sheepdog does, when you say ‘treat.’
take	V;PRS;SG;1	take	I take her to the food court and buy her an M&M cookie and buy myself a Mountain Dew and then while she’s eating her cookie, I pull the deck of cards out of my pocket and unwrap it.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	We’ve got another fifteen minutes before we have to meet my mom.
play	V;NFIN	play	The idea is to play fish except every time you get a match you’re supposed to do what it says.
tie	V;IMP;2	Tie	Tie partner’s hands with a silk scarf.
kiss	V;IMP;2	Kiss	Kiss anywhere you like and see how long your partner can keep from moving or making any noise.
last	V;PRS;SG;3	lasts	The one who lasts the longest gets to draw an extra card.
wait	V;NFIN	wait	I can’t wait to show Toph and Len.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Cara has chocolate smeared on her mouth, but she lets me wipe her face off.
go	V;NFIN	go	“You ready to go back to see Mom?” I say.
pass	V;PRS;1;PL	pass	When we pass Spencer’s again, she stops.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Uhhh,” she says, pointing to the store.
try	V;PRS;SG;3	tries	Mom always tries to get her to say what she wants, but I know what she wants and I don’t want to fight with her.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“No,” I say.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	“Let’s go see Mom.”
crumple	V;PRS;SG;3	crumples	Cara’s face crumples up and she hunches her thick shoulders.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Uhhh,” she says, mad.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“It’s okay,” I say.
come	V;IMP;2	Come	“Come on.”
swing	V;PRS;SG;3	swings	She swings at me.
grab	V;PRS;SG;1	grab	I grab her hand and pull her behind me.
try	V;PRS;SG;3	tries	She tries to sit down, but I just keep on tugging and she follows me, gulping and wailing.
do	V;NFIN	do	“What did you do?” my mom says when she sees us.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	My mom had to buy stuff, like gym shorts for me and underwear for herself, so I told her that I’d take Cara with me while she bought her stuff.
hold	V;PRS;V.PTCP	holding	She’s holding a Dillard’s bag.
want	V;PST;SG;3	wanted	“She wanted to go in Spencer’s,” I say.
go	V;PST;1;PL	went	“We went in but she kept grabbing stuff and I had to take her out and now she’s upset.”
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Robert,” my mom says, irritated.
crouch	V;PRS;SG;3	crouches	She crouches down.
cry	V;IMP;2	cry	“Ah, Cara mia, don’t cry.”
trail	V;PRS;1;PL	trail	We trail out of the store, Cara holding Mom’s hand and sniffling.
get	V;PRS;1;PL	get	By the time we get to the car, though, Cara’s wheezing.
dig	V;PRS;SG;3	digs	Mom digs out Cara’s inhaler and Cara dutifully takes a hit.
try	V;PST;SG;1	tried	I tried it once and it was pretty dreadful.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	It felt really weird, trying to get that stuff in my lungs, and it made me feel a little buzzy but it didn’t even feel good, so it’s pretty amazing that Cara will do it.
sit	V;PRS;SG;3	sits	Cara sits in her booster seat in the back of the car, wheezing all the way home, getting worse and worse, and by the time we pull in the driveway, she’s got that white look around her mouth.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Robert,” Mom says,
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	“I’m going to have to take her to the Emergency Room.”
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“Okay,” I say and get out of the car.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	“You want to call your dad?” Mom asks.
know	V;NFIN	know	“I don’t know how long we’ll be.”
check	V;PRS;SG;3	checks	Mom checks her watch.
want	V;NFIN	want	I don’t want to call my dad who is probably with Joyce, his girlfriend, anyway.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	Joyce is always trying to be likable and it gets on my nerves after awhile — she tries way too hard.
make	V;NFIN	make	“I can just make a sandwich,” I say.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	“I want you to stay at home, then,” she says.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	“I’ve got my cell phone if you need to call.”
come	V;NFIN	come	“Can Toph and Len come over?” I ask.
sigh	V;PRS;SG;3	sighs	She sighs.
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember you have school tomorrow.”
open	V;PRS;SG;3	opens	She opens the garage door so I can get in.
stand	V;PRS;SG;1	stand	I stand there and watch her back down the driveway.
turn	V;PRS;SG;3	turns	She turns back, watching where she’s going, and she needs to get her hair done again because I can really see the gray roots.
watch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	watching	Cara is watching me through the watery glass, her mouth a little open.
wave	V;PRS;SG;1	wave	I wave good-bye.
rain	V;PST;SG;3	rained	The day it rained in the garden
fall	V;PST;PL;3	fell	The first drops of rain fell onto the parched stones from a cloudless blue sky.
disappear	V;PST;PL;3	disappeared	They disappeared immediately, drunk down into the mineral silence.
pause	V;PST;SG;3	paused	The garden paused, and everything held its breath and waited.
come	V;NFIN	come	More rain would come.
know	V;PST;SG;3	knew	Everyone knew, beyond belief or necessity that the time had come.
bounce	V;PST;PL;3	bounced	Heavy oily drops bounced from leaf to grass to dusty ground, gathering in stems and folds on the way.
hold	V;PST;SG;3	held	Still the garden held its breath.
pluck	V;PST;SG;3	plucked	A spattering of drops plucked memories from deep in the roots, shivering through shoots and tendrils, humus and stone.
marble	V;PST;PL;3	marbled	Wispy vapour trails marbled the blue surface above.
rain	V;PST;SG;3	rained	And then it rained for good.
sit	V;PST;1;PL	sat	We sat on the porch watching, no longer daring to dart out and leap around in the downfall as drops turned to torrents and the staccato tapping on the roof climaxed to a thunderous roar.
erupt	V;PST;PL;3	erupted	Pools erupted and overflowed along the edges of the lawn, cascading around the raspberry canes and over the flower beds.
rise	V;PST;PL;3	rose	Earthworms rose up from the water-laden earth where they could no longer breath and wriggled and struggled fro air on the surface, writhing and swimming for a safe haven.
burst	V;PST;SG;3	burst	Water burst over the barrels and cans standing under the eaves and gutters, flooding the pathways, carrying away all that wasn’t fixed solid and licking at the stairs to the back porch before climbing each step and lapping against the planks we were sitting on, a wooden raft surrounded by angry water and martial beat drum, drum, drumming on the roof above.
slip	V;PST;1;PL	slipped	We slipped out feet over the sides and when we felt the fish, some as big as logs, some as small as leaves, nibble at our feet, we leant over to watch them fraying and frolicking under the choppy surface.
lap	V;PST;PL;3	lapped	Waves lapped at the porch, washing away the boxes, and bowls, and the dog’s basket.
rock	V;PRS;V.PTCP	rocking	The rocking chair, the watering can, boxes of cuttings, and tied-up newspapers, all floated away as we slammed the kitchen door shut and retreated to the front room we gazed out at the river that the street had become.
smear	V;PST;V.PTCP	smeared	The sky was pewter smeared with charcoal now, and pressed down hard on the rooftops, slowly dissolving everything below.
ripple	V;PST;SG;3	rippled	When the water rippled into the hallway, we splashed our way to the staircase and then up as it nipped at our heels.
surround	V;PST;V.PTCP	surrounded	Outside the other houses were rocks surrounded by sea with mermaids swimming all around, flapping their scaly tails and hauling themselves up, to fall backwards into the crest of the oncoming waves.
swim	V;PST;PL;3	swam	And as they swam they sang, their voices born on the winds and the salt and the rain, and we could hear them singing, He is coming, He is coming.
comb	V;PST;PL;3	combed	And they combed their long hair in preparation.
wave	V;PST;1;PL	waved	We waved and called, and they laughed and played, tantalising, their seaweed locks just out of reach.
sing	V;PRS;V.PTCP	singing	With a flick of the tail they were off, still singing, He is coming, He is coming, until their voices were lost in the noise of the rain and the water.
arise	V;PST;SG;3	arose	A great whiskered head arose, then a belly, wet and sleek, long enough to wrap itself twice around the world.
ask	V;PST;1;PL	asked	“Is it him?” we asked each other, nudging elbows and climbing onto the chairs.
pull	V;PST;1;PL	pulled	We pulled off wet socks and shoes, letting them float away on the tide, before the great worm turned and the wake engulfed the creaking, cracking house.
clamber	V;PST;1;PL	clambered	We clambered up onto the roof, sliding on the tiles and clinging to chimney stacks, sheltering our eyes from the driving downpour.
fleck	V;PST;V.PTCP	flecked	The sky was black, and the stormy sea all the deepest, darkest blues and greens and greys and flecked with white where the sea-horses galloped.
break	V;PST;PL;3	broke	The waters broke making way for the gnarled head of a whale, spouting water high against the clouds before opening its whiskered maw and in a looming, booming voice called: He is coming, He is coming.
wash	V;PST;PL;3	washed	The chimneys washed away, so we scrambled onto the blue-black back encrusted with barnacles and cockles like stars in a night sky and held on tight as he rode the waves, dipping and blowing, dipping and blowing.
tug	V;PST;PL;3	tugged	The wind and the rain tugged at our hair.
rain	V;PST;SG;3	rained	And still it rained.
hear	V;PST;1;PL	heard	We heard a fearsome creaking and cracking and before our eyes a great patch of sky peeled away, deeping down in the troughs and riding the crests with torn sails set against the wind.
tar	V;PST;V.PTCP	tarred	It was a galleon with masts like a dead man’s fingers, tarred timbers groaning and a mermaid sat on the bow as he ship dipped and passed.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	He is coming, He is coming, she sang out.
hit	V;PST;SG;3	hit	And just as the wash hit us, prising us from the whale’s speckled back and down into the sea, someone threw down a knotted rope and we hung on for dear life.
haul	V;PST;PL;3	hauled	The singing sailors hauled us aboard chanting, He’s a coming-ha! He’s a coming-ha!
fall	V;PST;1;PL	fell	And we fell onto the deck like fish bursting from a net.
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	The Captain stood above us dressed in jewels and finery from a thousand pillagings.
bury	V;PST;V.PTCP	buried	I had buried my parents in their gray marble mausoleum at the heart of the city.
bury	V;PST;V.PTCP	buried	I had buried my husband in a lead box sunk into the mud of the bottom of the river, where all the riverboatmen lie.
bury	V;PST;V.PTCP	buried	And after the war, I had buried my children, all four, in white linen shrouds in the new graveyards plowed into what used to be our farmland: all the land stretching from the river delta to the hills.
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	I had one granddaughter who survived the war.
see	V;PST;SG;1	saw	I saw her sometimes: in a bright pink dress, a sparkling drink in her hand, on the arm of some foreign officer with brocade on his shoulders, at the edge of a marble patio.
look	V;PST;SG;3	looked	She never looked back at me — poverty and failure and political disrepute being all, these days, contagious and synonymous.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	The young were mostly dead, and the old men had been taken away, they told us, to learn important new things and to come back when they were ready to contribute fully.
sip	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sipping	And it was in a grandmother bar by the waterfront — sipping hot tea with rum and watching over the shoulders of dockworkers playing mah-jongg — that I first heard of the valley of giants.
laugh	V;PST;1;PL	laughed	We all laughed at the idea, except for a chemist with a crooked nose and rouge caked in the creases of her face, who was incensed.
live	V;PRS;1;PL	live	“We live in the modern era!” she cried.
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	The traveler stood up from the table.
vein	V;PST;V.PTCP	veined	Her eyes were veined with red.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	“Nonetheless,” the traveler said, and she walked out.
laugh	V;PRS;V.PTCP	laughing	They were laughing at the chemist as well as at the traveler.
find	V;NFIN	find	To find anyone still proud, anyone who believed in giants or shame, was hilarious.
find	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Finding	Finding someone even more vulnerable and foolish than we were, after everything had been taken from us — that was a delight.
follow	V;PST;SG;1	followed	But I followed the traveler, into the wet streets.
ooze	V;PST;SG;3	oozed	The smell of fish oozed from the docks.
char	V;PST;V.PTCP	charred	Here and there were bits of charred debris in the gutters.
catch	V;PST;SG;1	caught	I caught her at her door.
invite	V;PST;SG;3	invited	She invited me in for tea and massage.
smell	V;PST;SG;3	smelled	She smelled like honey that has been kept a while in a dark room, a little fermented.
scour	V;PST;SG;3	scoured	In the morning, brilliant sunlight scoured the walls and the floor, and the traveler and her pack were gone.
hurry	V;PST;SG;1	hurried	I hurried home.
survive	V;PST;V.PTCP	survived	My house had survived the war with all its brown clay walls intact, though the garden and the courtyard were a heap of blackened rubble.
pack	V;PST;SG;1	packed	I packed six loaves of flatbread, some olives, a hard cheese, one nice dress, walking clothes, my pills and glasses, a jug of wine, a can-teen of water, and a kitchen knife.
sit	V;PST;SG;1	sat	I sat in the shadow in my living room for a while, looking at the amorphous mass of the blanket I had been crocheting.
work	V;PST;PL;3	worked	That granddaughter: her parents both worked in the vineyards, and when she was a child, she would play in my courtyard in the after-noons.
scrape	V;PST;SG;3	scraped	When she scraped her knees bloody on the stones, she refused to cry.
cry	V;NFIN	cry	She would cry from frustration when the older children could do something that she couldn't — like tie knots, or catch a chicken.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	It had taken only a matter of hours for the Kethlan system to become a tumbling sea of debris; the twisted and burnt-out remnants of a once glorious Imperial nation.
come	V;NFIN	come	An empire whose costly mistakes would for many years to come echo into every corner of the known galaxy.
come	V;NFIN	come	And with those mistakes would come death to billions of innocent, unsuspecting lives.
hurtle	V;PST;SG;3	hurtled	A starfighter hurtled through the scattered metal, the pilot desperate to find a way to stop himself from joining the ever growing population of this interstellar graveyard.
try	V;PST;SG;3	tried	He tried to steady himself as he began cycling once again through the available display options for his starfighter's radar system.
continue	V;PST;SG;3	continued	His anxiety continued to grow with each passing second, every change of the screen doing nothing to abate it.
stop	V;PST;SG;3	stopped	He stopped midway through the calibration and glanced out of his cockpit.
bring	V;PST;SG;3	brought	The scene was the same as it had been a few moments ago and it brought him no comfort.
count	V;NFIN	count	He couldn't count the number of capital ships that dominated the Enemy's frontline.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	In his years of service to the Imperial Naval Forces he had never seen anything like it, not even in archive war footage.
loom	V;PST;PL;3	loomed	The enormous forms of the battleships loomed like giants atop a hill, staring down upon a tiny village below.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	Then there were the Enemy starfighters themselves: hundreds at least, swarming about like a huge wall of locusts.
know	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Knowing	Knowing that he had already been flying straight for longer than was advisable, Chalmers altered his course to attempt to throw off any pursuers.
stand	V;PRS;V.PTCP	standing	Not long before he had been standing on the flight deck of his deployment carrier, amongst the other pilots, his heart pumping in his ears, his hands sweating as he awaited the order to board his fighter.
see	V;PST;SG;3	saw	Chalmers saw his friends run forward as their names were called out, scrambling into cockpits, pulling on helmets and performing last-minute safety checks.
hide	V;PST;SG;3	hid	Though most hid it well, he was convinced they were all as nervous and scared as he was - knowing they could well be speeding only to their own deaths.
watch	V;PST;SG;3	watched	As he watched his friends' fighters hurtle down the catapult, his commanding officer had addressed the last remaining pilots still standing on the flight deck.
listen	V;IMP;2	listen	“Right, listen up,” he started.
make	V;NFIN	make	“This is where we must make our stand.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	The Enemy cannot be allowed to advance any further.
fight	V;PRS;1;PL	fight	Tonight we fight the battle for Kethlan and for the Imperium; the battle for our survival.
depend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	depending	Hundreds of millions of lives are depending on our actions here.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make them proud!”
think	V;PST;SG;3	thought	Hundreds of millions? thought Chalmers.
leave	V;PST;V.PTCP	left	Is that all that's left?
creep	V;PST;V.PTCP	crept	This day had crept ever closer as cities, planets and then entire star systems had fallen to the Enemy; to those damned Pandorans; to the Senate's mistake.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	How many of his friends had he lost over these last few terrible months?
die	V;PST;V.PTCP	died	Had they died fast or were they now suffering a fate far worse..?
continue	V;PST;SG;3	continued	As his CO continued to pump them up for the critical battle, Chalmers' head was filled with visions of row upon row of black-clad soldiers.
reside	V;PST;SG;3	resided	A white emblem resided on their right arms and left breast, an all-encompassing full black helmet upon their heads, its smooth form negating all facial features.
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	set	Two bright red oval spheres were set into an eye-level groove that ran all around, the “eyes” themselves slanted into a menacing and intimidating scowl.
turn	V;PST;SG;3	turned	One of the soldiers turned to face him, the ruby-like eyes seeming to pierce his very soul.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	He felt his hand tighten on the flight helmet he held, swallowing hard.
call	V;PST;SG;3	called	“Daniels! Peterson! Foster! Brown! Rye ...” a voice called out above the other sounds that filled the flight deck.
move	V;PST;PL;3	moved	Feet moved and Chalmers felt his stomach lurch.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	His name would be called soon.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	He felt a sense of doom.
stop	V;NFIN	stop	If the Empire could not stop the Enemy before, what hope did they have now?
grow	V;PST;V.PTCP	grown	The Enemy's power had grown exponentially and they had crushed everything in their path with harrowing little effort.
feel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	feeling	Chalmers was feeling forced to accept the truth: they were all that was left of the Imperial Naval Forces.
win	V;PST;V.PTCP	won	This was a battle that could not be won; not now, not ever.
feel	V;PST;SG;3	felt	At the sound of his name, Chalmers felt himself move robotically, his mind screaming in protest against what his legs were doing.
run	V;PST;SG;3	ran	He ran over to the waiting starfighter, threw on his helmet and begun ascending the ladder into the cockpit.
sink	V;PST;SG;3	sank	Zombie-like he sank down into the seat, watching as if from outside his body as his hands buckled him in, his fingers flip switches, press buttons, acknowledge questions and confirmations on the screens before him.
taxi	V;PST;V.PTCP	taxied	Moments later, his craft was taxied to the catapult and before long he'd found himself out in space and into the thick of battle.
realise	V;PST;V.PTCP	realised	At that moment, his worst fears had not only been altogether realised, but far exceeded.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	Brings	What the Moon Brings
hate	V;PRS;SG;1	hate	I hate the moon - I am afraid of it - for when it shines on certain scenes familiar and loved it sometimes makes them unfamiliar and hideous.
shine	V;PST;SG;3	shone	It was in the spectral summer when the moon shone down on the old garden where I wandered; the spectral summer of narcotic flowers and humid seas of foliage that bring wild and many-coloured dreams.
walk	V;PST;SG;1	walked	And as I walked by the shallow crystal stream I saw unwonted ripples tipped with yellow light, as if those placid waters were drawn on in resistless currents to strange oceans that are not in the world.
curse	V;PST;V.PTCP	cursed	Silent and sparkling, bright and baleful, those moon-cursed waters hurried I knew not whither; whilst from the embowered banks white lotos-blossoms fluttered one by one in the opiate night-wind and dropped despairingly into the stream, swirling away horribly under the arched, carven bridge, and staring back with the sinister resignation of calm, dead faces.
run	V;PST;SG;1	ran	And as I ran along the shore, crushing sleeping flowers with heedless feet and maddened ever by the fear of unknown things and the lure of the dead faces, I saw that the garden had no end under that moon; for where by day the walls were, there stretched now only new vistas of trees and paths, flowers and shrubs, stone idols and pagodas, and bendings of the yellow-litten stream past grassy banks and under grotesque bridges of marble.
whisper	V;PST;PL;3	whispered	And the lips of the dead lotos-faces whispered sadly, and bade me follow, nor did I cease my steps till the stream became a river, and joined amidst marshes of swaying reeds and beaches of gleaming sand the shore of a vast and nameless sea.
shine	V;PST;SG;3	shone	Upon that sea the hateful moon shone, and over its unvocal waves weird perfumes breeded.
see	V;PST;SG;1	saw	And as I saw therein the lotos-faces vanish, I longed for nets that I might capture them and learn from them the secrets which the moon had brought upon the night.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	But when that moon went over to the west and the still tide ebbed from the sullen shore, I saw in that light old spires that the waves almost uncovered, and white columns gay with festoons of green seaweed.
know	V;PRS;V.PTCP	knowing	And knowing that to this sunken place all the dead had come, I trembled and did not wish again to speak with the lotos-faces.
see	V;PST;SG;1	saw	Yet when I saw afar out in the sea a black condor descend from the sky to seek rest on a vast reef, I would fain have questioned him, and asked him of those whom I had known when they were alive.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	This I would have asked him had he not been so far away, but he was very far, and could not be seen at all when he drew nigh that gigantic reef.
watch	V;PST;SG;1	watched	So I watched the tide go out under that sinking moon, and saw gleaming the spires, the towers, and the roofs of that dead, dripping city.
watch	V;PST;SG;1	watched	And as I watched, my nostrils tried to close against the perfume-conquering stench of the world's dead; for truly, in this unplaced and forgotten spot had all the flesh of the churchyards gathered for puffy sea-worms to gnaw and glut upon.
hang	V;PST;SG;3	hung	Over these horrors the evil moon now hung very low, but the puffy worms of the sea need no moon to feed by.
watch	V;PST;SG;1	watched	And as I watched the ripples that told of the writhing of worms beneath, I felt a new chill from afar out whither the condor had flown, as if my flesh had caught a horror before my eyes had seen it.
tremble	V;PST;V.PTCP	trembled	Nor had my flesh trembled without cause, for when I raised my eyes I saw that the waters had ebbed very low, shewing much of the vast reef whose rim I had seen before.
see	V;PST;SG;1	saw	And when I saw that the reef was but the black basalt crown of a shocking eikon whose monstrous forehead now shown in the dim moonlight and whose vile hooves must paw the hellish ooze miles below, I shrieked and shrieked lest the hidden face rise above the waters, and lest the hidden eyes look at me after the slinking away of that leering and treacherous yellow moon.
escape	V;NFIN	escape	And to escape this relentless thing I plunged gladly and unhesitantly into the stinking shallows where amidst weedy walls and sunken streets fat sea-worms feast upon the world's dead.
hurt	V;NFIN	hurt	“It doesn’t hurt, Gram,” Renata says.
pull	V;PRS;SG;3	pulls	My sixteen-year-old daughter pulls up her T-shirt sleeve to show her bare arm, the skin summer brown and the muscle swelling slightly into smooth biceps, flawless.
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	“I had it done when I was little and see, you can’t even tell.”
sit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sitting	My mother is sitting in the little examining room at the assisted living.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	Everything is white and hospital-like but there’s no examining couch.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	There’s just a desk, a little white table with two chairs and a scale.
know	V;NFIN	know	The doctor, a woman I don’t know, is sitting in the other chair.
bewilder	V;PST;V.PTCP	bewildered	My mother is bewildered, her face turned up towards me.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	She’s got Alzheimer’s.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“It’s okay, Mom,” I say.
want	V;PRS;SG;3	wants	She wants to understand, I can see that.
explain	V;PRS;SG;1	explain	So I explain again.
let	V;NFIN	let	“It’s an implant that will let them know where you are, and how you are.
hurt	V;NFIN	hurt	It won’t hurt.”
water	V;PRS;PL;3	water	Her eyes water constantly, now.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	In the time it takes me to explain she grasps and loses the words, grasps and loses phrases as they go past.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	She looks at me and then at Renata, who is smiling, and finally submits uncomprehendingly.
wear	V;PST;V.PTCP	worn	We have worn her down.
bare	V;PRS;SG;3	bares	The doctor bares my mother’s arm, where the crepey flesh hangs loosely on the bones.
swab	V;PRS;SG;3	swabs	The doctor swabs her upper arm with antiseptic and says, “I’m going to give you something to numb it, okay?”
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	To me the doctor says in her normal voice, “It’s just a little lidocaine.”
like	V;NFIN	like	I don’t like the doctor, but I don’t know why.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	She has professional hair, lightly streaked.
dislike	V;NFIN	dislike	This is no reason to dislike her.
wince	V;PRS;SG;3	winces	My mother winces at the injection and is surprised again.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	She looks up at me, at Renata.
smile	V;PRS;V.PTCP	smiling	We are smiling, both of us.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Okay,” my mother says.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have no idea.
wait	V;PRS;1;PL	wait	We wait for a few minutes.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	My mother says, “Is it time to go?”
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	“Not yet,” I say.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	“They’re going to give you an injection.”
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“What?” my mother says.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	“They’re going to implant a chip.
help	V;NFIN	help	It will help them take care of you.”
try	V;PRS;SG;1	try	I try to say it every time as if it was the first time I said it.
want	V;NFIN	want	I don’t want to embarrass her.
swing	V;PRS;SG;3	swings	Her head swings around, from Renata to me to the doctor and then back to Renata.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“It’s okay, Gram,” Renata says.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Renata,” my mother says.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The doctor has an injection gun and while my mother is focused on my daughter, she puts it firmly against my mother’s arm and puts the chip in.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	“Oh!” says my mother.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	It’s another low-grade moment of horror, but I think about that particular time with my mother because Renata was there and we were united, she and I.
come	V;PRS;SG;1	come	I come back to it a lot.
shine	V;PRS;V.PTCP	shining	In the background, shining, is Renata, who is young and healthy and good, raising her arm to show her grandmother that the chip is nothing, nothing at all.
dream	V;PST;V.PTCP	dreamed	The last two nights I have dreamed of dogs in trouble.
dream	V;NFIN	dream	I don’t dream about Renata, although when the dreams wake me up, it’s thinking about Renata that keeps me awake.
dream	V;PST;SG;1	dreamed	The first night I dreamed of seeing a stray dog and not stopping to pick it up although it was wandering in an empty parking lot near a busy road.
come	V;NFIN	come	That was the first night Renata didn’t come home and it doesn’t take Freud to figure out what that meant.
sleep	V;PRS;PL;3	sleep	Seventeen-year-olds sleep on friends’ couches, I know.
sleep	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sleeping	Or Renata might be sleeping in her car.
dream	V;PST;SG;1	dreamed	The second night I dreamed that I was on a desert island and Sonia, our golden retriever, was with me.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	There was some horrible fate impending for Sonia and I had to kill her before something worse happened.
lay	V;PST;SG;1	laid	I laid her down on some sticks.
trust	V;PRS;SG;3	trusts	She trusts me, she’s more my dog than anyone’s, and she didn’t like it but for me she lay there.
tell	V;PST;SG;1	told	In my dream I told her, “Stay, Sonia. Stay.”
stay	V;PST;SG;3	stayed	She stayed because I held her there by looking at her, the way you can sometimes will a dog into submission.
stay	V;PST;SG;3	stayed	She stayed while I lit the fire.
hit	V;PST;SG;3	hit	And then the horror of it all hit me and I said, “Sonia, up!”
wake	V;PST;SG;1	woke	And I woke up.
come	V;NFIN	come	That was last night, the second night Renata didn’t come home.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	"Try to touch the past.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try to deal with the past.
start	V;NFIN	start	It didn't start out here.
say	V;NFIN	say	Most people would say it started with the Fireflies, but they'd be wrong.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	It ended with all those things.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	For me, it began with Robert Paglino.
bind	V;PST;V.PTCP	bound	We were fellow outcasts, bound by complementary misfortune.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	His was genetic: an uncontrolled genotype that left him predisposed to nearsightedness, acne, and (as it later turned out) a susceptibility to narcotics.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	His parents had never had him optimized.
believe	V;PST;PL;3	believed	Those few TwenCen relics who still believed in God also held that one shouldn't try to improve upon His handiwork.
repair	V;PST;V.PTCP	repaired	So although both of us could have been repaired, only one of us had been.
arrive	V;PST;SG;1	arrived	I arrived at the playground to find Pag the center of attention for some half-dozen kids, those lucky few in front punching him in the head, the others making do with taunts of mongrel and polly while waiting their turn.
watch	V;PST;SG;1	watched	I watched him raise his arms, almost hesitantly, to ward off the worst of the blows.
see	V;NFIN	see	I could see into his head better than I could see into my own; he was scared that his attackers might think those hands were coming up to hit back, that they'd read it as an act of defiance and hurt him even more.
become	V;PRS;V.PTCP	becoming	Even then, at the tender age of eight and with half my mind gone, I was becoming a superlative observer.
know	V;NFIN	know	But I didn't know what to do.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	I hadn't seen much of Pag lately.
avoid	V;PRS;V.PTCP	avoiding	I was pretty sure he'd been avoiding me.
help	V;PRS;SG;2	help	Still, when your best friend's in trouble you help out, right?
go	V;NFIN	go	Even if the odds are impossible — and how many eight-year-olds would go up against six bigger kids for a sandbox buddy? — at least you call for backup.
flag	V;PRS;PL;3	Flag	Flag a sentry.
stand	V;PST;SG;1	stood	I just stood there.
want	V;NFIN	want	I didn't even especially want to help him.
make	V;NFIN	make	That didn't make sense.
empathize	V;PST;V.PTCP	empathized	Even if he hadn't been my best friend, I should at least have empathized.
suffer	V;PST;V.PTCP	suffered	I'd suffered less than Pag in the way of overt violence; my seizures tended to keep the other kids at a distance, scared them even as they incapacitated me.
appear	V;PRS;SG;3	appears	I was no stranger to the taunts and insults, or the foot that appears from nowhere to trip you up en route from A to B.
know	V;PST;SG;1	knew	I knew how that felt.
cut	V;PST;V.PTCP	cut	But that part of me had been cut out along with the bad wiring.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	I was still working up the algorithms to get it back, still learning by observation.
tear	V;PRS;PL;3	tear	Pack animals always tear apart the weaklings in their midst.
know	V;PRS;SG;3	knows	Every child knows that much instinctively.
let	V;NFIN	let	Maybe I should just let that process unfold, maybe I shouldn't try to mess with nature.
mess	V;PST;V.PTCP	messed	Then again, Pag's parents hadn't messed with nature, and look what it got them: a son curled up in the dirt while a bunch of engineered superboys kicked in his ribs.
work	V;PST;SG;3	worked	In the end, propaganda worked where empathy failed.
think	V;NFIN	think	Back then I didn't so much think as observe, didn't deduce so much as remember — and what I remembered was a thousand inspirational stories lauding anyone who ever stuck up for the underdog.
pick	V;PST;SG;1	picked	So I picked up a rock the size of my fist and hit two of Pag's assailants across the backs of their heads before anyone even knew I was in the game.
turn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	turning	A third, turning to face the new threat, took a blow to the face that audibly crunched the bones of his cheek.
remember	V;PRS;SG;1	remember	I remember wondering why I didn't take any satisfaction from that sound, why it meant nothing beyond the fact I had one less opponent to worry about.
run	V;PST;SG;3	ran	The rest of them ran at the sight of blood.
promise	V;PST;SG;3	promised	One of the braver promised me I was dead, shouted "Fucking zombie!" over his shoulder as he disappeared around the corner.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	Three decades it took, to see the irony in that remark.
twitch	V;PST;SG;3	twitched	Two of the enemy twitched at my feet.
