see	V;IMP;2	See	See you then.
tell	V;PST;V.PTCP	told	“I have already told you of the sickness and confusion that comes with time travelling.
seat	V;PST;V.PTCP	seated	And this time I was not seated properly in the saddle, but sideways and in an unstable fashion.
cling	V;PST;SG;1	clung	For an indefinite time I clung to the machine as it swayed and vibrated, quite unheeding how I went, and when I brought myself to look at the dials again I was amazed to find where I had arrived.
record	V;PRS;SG;3	records	One dial records days, and another thousands of days, another millions of days, and another thousands of millions.
reverse	V;PRS;V.PTCP	reversing	Now, instead of reversing the levers, I had pulled them over so as to go forward with them, and when I came to look at these indicators I found that the thousands hand was sweeping round as fast as the seconds hand of a watch—into futurity.
drive	V;PST;SG;1	drove	“As I drove on, a peculiar change crept over the appearance of things.
palpitate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	palpitating	The palpitating greyness grew darker; then—though I was still travelling with prodigious velocity—the blinking succession of day and night, which was usually indicative of a slower pace, returned, and grew more and more marked.
puzzle	V;PST;SG;3	puzzled	This puzzled me very much at first.
grow	V;PST;PL;3	grew	The alternations of night and day grew slower and slower, and so did the passage of the sun across the sky, until they seemed to stretch through centuries.
brood	V;PST;SG;3	brooded	At last a steady twilight brooded over the earth, a twilight only broken now and then when a comet glared across the darkling sky.
indicate	V;PST;V.PTCP	indicated	The band of light that had indicated the sun had long since disappeared; for the sun had ceased to set—it simply rose and fell in the west, and grew ever broader and more red.
vanish	V;PST;V.PTCP	vanished	All trace of the moon had vanished.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	The circling of the stars, growing slower and slower, had given place to creeping points of light.
stop	V;PST;SG;1	stopped	At last, some time before I stopped, the sun, red and very large, halted motionless upon the horizon, a vast dome glowing with a dull heat, and now and then suffering a momentary extinction.
glow	V;PST;V.PTCP	glowed	At one time it had for a little while glowed more brilliantly again, but it speedily reverted to its sullen red heat.
perceive	V;PST;SG;1	perceived	I perceived by this slowing down of its rising and setting that the work of the tidal drag was done.
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	The earth had come to rest with one face to the sun, even as in our own time the moon faces the earth.
remember	V;PST;SG;1	remembered	Very cautiously, for I remembered my former headlong fall, I began to reverse my motion.
go	V;PST;PL;3	went	Slower and slower went the circling hands until the thousands one seemed motionless and the daily one was no longer a mere mist upon its scale.
grow	V;PST;PL;3	grew	Still slower, until the dim outlines of a desolate beach grew visible.
stop	V;PST;SG;1	stopped	“I stopped very gently and sat upon the Time Machine, looking round.
shine	V;PST;PL;3	shone	North-eastward it was inky black, and out of the blackness shone brightly and steadily the pale white stars.
grow	V;PST;SG;3	grew	Overhead it was a deep Indian red and starless, and south-eastward it grew brighter to a glowing scarlet where, cut by the horizon, lay the huge hull of the sun, red and motionless.
see	V;NFIN	see	The rocks about me were of a harsh reddish colour, and all the trace of life that I could see at first was the intensely green vegetation that covered every projecting point on their south-eastern face.
see	V;PRS;SG;3	sees	It was the same rich green that one sees on forest moss or on the lichen in caves: plants which like these grow in a perpetual twilight.
stand	V;PRS;V.PTCP	standing	“The machine was standing on a sloping beach.
stretch	V;PST;SG;3	stretched	The sea stretched away to the south-west, to rise into a sharp bright horizon against the wan sky.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	There were no breakers and no waves, for not a breath of wind was stirring.
rise	V;PST;SG;3	rose	Only a slight oily swell rose and fell like a gentle breathing, and showed that the eternal sea was still moving and living.
break	V;PST;SG;3	broke	And along the margin where the water sometimes broke was a thick incrustation of salt—pink under the lurid sky.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	There was a sense of oppression in my head, and I noticed that I was breathing very fast.
remind	V;PST;SG;3	reminded	The sensation reminded me of my only experience of mountaineering, and from that I judged the air to be more rarefied than it is now.
hear	V;PST;SG;1	heard	“Far away up the desolate slope I heard a harsh scream, and saw a thing like a huge white butterfly go slanting and fluttering up into the sky and, circling, disappear over some low hillocks beyond.
shiver	V;PST;SG;1	shivered	The sound of its voice was so dismal that I shivered and seated myself more firmly upon the machine.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Looking	Looking round me again, I saw that, quite near, what I had taken to be a reddish mass of rock was moving slowly towards me.
see	V;PST;SG;1	saw	Then I saw the thing was really a monstrous crab-like creature.
imagine	V;NFIN	imagine	Can you imagine a crab as large as yonder table, with its many legs moving slowly and uncertainly, its big claws swaying, its long antennæ, like carters’ whips, waving and feeling, and its stalked eyes gleaming at you on either side of its metallic front?
ornament	V;PST;V.PTCP	ornamented	Its back was corrugated and ornamented with ungainly bosses, and a greenish incrustation blotched it here and there.
see	V;NFIN	see	I could see the many palps of its complicated mouth flickering and feeling as it moved.
stay	V;PST;SG;3	stayed	Veronique stayed with me that night, lying next to me in my blankets and furs.
sleep	V;NFIN	sleep	She didn't sleep, I don't think.
listen	V;PRS;V.PTCP	listening	I was listening to her breath.
feel	V;PST;SG;1	felt	I felt as if I should help her sleep.
lie	V;PST;SG;1	lay	I lay there and tried to think if I should put my arm around her, but I didn't know.
want	V;NFIN	want	Maybe she didn't want to be touched.
lie	V;PST;SG;3	lay	She lay tense in the dark.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	"Are you going to be a teacher?" I asked.
laugh	V;PST;SG;3	laughed	She laughed.
get	V;PRS;SG;1	get	"If I get out of here."
wait	V;PST;SG;1	waited	I waited for her to say more, but she didn't.
get	V;NFIN	Get	"Get out of here" meant to make someone leave.
mean	V;PST;SG;3	meant	Maybe she meant if she made herself.
come	V;PRS;SG;2	come	"You come here from Earth?" I asked.
get	V;NFIN	get	To get her to talk, although I was tired of lingua and I didn't really want to think about anything.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	"My family came here from Earth," she said.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	"My father, he's an anthropologist," she said.
know	V;NFIN	know	"Do you know anthropologist?"
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	"No," I said.
study	V;PRS;SG;3	studies	"He is a person who studies the way people live.
meet	V;PST;V.PTCP	met	All the offworlders I had ever met were teachers.
wonder	V;PST;SG;1	wondered	I wondered who did all the work on Earth.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	"Because Earth had lost touch with your world, the people here are very interesting to my father," she said.
