wish	V;NFIN	wish	You might therefore wish to start right in on the first page of text that follows and only then return to this introduction.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider a similar case: a little book published in 1934 by the Surrealist artist Max Ernst called Une Semaine de Bonté.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	It was made entirely of collaged illustrations cut out of old books
sit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sitting	It was just before Easter Sunday, 1 and I was sitting at my table.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	I’d said my prayers, talking a long time as usual with my Maker and thinking about some of the great mysteries the Father of Lights had revealed to me.
make	V;NFIN	make	Now I was ready to make and to bake – only in my heart, actually – a small, perfect unleavened wafer to eat with my beloved Paschal Lamb.
blow	V;PST;SG;3	blew	All of a sudden a terrible wind blew up, so strong that I thought the hill my little house was built on would be blown apart – but I’d seen the Devil do things as bad as this before (the Devil had often tried to harm me), so I took heart and went on meditating.
feel	V;PST;SG;1	felt	Till I felt somebody touch me on the back.
frighten	V;PST;SG;3	frightened	This frightened me so that I didn’t dare turn.
try	V;PST;SG;1	tried	I tried to stay as brave and calm as a human being could under the circumstances.
feel	V;PST;SG;1	felt	I felt my coat tugged at, and tugged again, and at last I looked around.
stand	V;PST;SG;3	stood	A woman stood there, so bright and beautiful, in a sky-colored robe – a heavens covered with
begin	V;PRS;SG;3	begins	1 Christian’s story begins just before Easter, like Dante’s Divine Comedy.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	It can be seen as an allegory of Christ’s death and resurrection, though this idea produces some puzzles.
sing	V;PST;PL;3	sang	The birds sang so beautifully, the young deer skipped so happily, that it gladdened my old heart, and I couldn't help singing too ...
lie	V;PRS;V.PTCP	lying	I was awake and lying in bed next morning, looking idly at the wonderful images and inscriptions all around my room, when suddenly I heard the sound of trumpets, as if a procession were already underway.
jump	V;PST;SG;3	jumped	My page jumped out of bed as if crazed, looking more dead than alive, and you can imagine how I felt when he cried, “They're already being presented to the king!"
groan	V;NFIN	groan	I could only groan in frustration and curse my lazy bones.
dress	V;PST;V.PTCP	dressed	I got dressed, but my page was quicker than I was and ran out of the chamber to see what was what.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	He soon came back and gave me the good news that I actually hadn’t overslept; all I’d done was miss breakfast: they hadn’t wanted to wake an old man who needed his rest.
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	But now I had to get ready to go with him to the lion fountain, where most of the others were gathered.
recover	V;PST;PL;3	recovered	My spirits recovered, and as soon as I had got into my habit, I followed him to the garden I have already told about.
find	V;PST;SG;1	found	I found that the lion, in place of his sword, now held a rather large plaque.
examine	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Examining	Examining this, I could tell that it had been taken from those ancient monuments I’d seen and put here for some special reason.
fade	V;PRS;V.PTCP	fading	The inscription on it was fading away, so I should set it down here as it was then, and ask my readers to ponder it:
suffer	V;PST;V.PTCP	suffered	Prince Hermes: Now after humankind has suffered so much harm here I flow having by god’s counsel and with the help of art become a healing balm.
drink	V;IMP;2	Drink	Drink from me if you can; wash, if you like; trouble my waters if you dare.
drink	V;IMP;2	Drink	Drink, Brethren!
drink	V;IMP;2	Drink	Drink and live!
tell	V;PRS;SG;3	tells	Biologist Nick Bos tells Wikinews about 'self-medicating' ants
study	V;PRS;SG;3	studies	Nick Bos, of the University of Helsinki, studies "the amazing adaptations social insects have evolved in order to fight the extreme parasite pressure they experience".
accept	V;PST;V.PTCP	accepted	In a recently-accepted Evolution paper Bos and colleagues describe ants appearing to self-medicate.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	"I have no doubt that as time goes on, there will be more and more cases documented"
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	The team used Formica fusca, an ant species that can form thousand-strong colonies.
eat	V;PRS;SG;3	eats	This common black ant eats other insects, and also aphid honeydew.
nest	V;PRS;SG;3	nests	It often nests in tree stumps or under rocks and foraging workers can sometimes be spotted climbing trees.
infect	V;PST;V.PTCP	infected	Some ants were infected with Beauveria bassiana, a fungus.
infect	V;PST;V.PTCP	Infected	Infected ants chose food laced with toxic hydrogen peroxide, whereas healthy ants avoided it.
reduce	V;PST;SG;3	reduced	Hydrogen peroxide reduced infected ant fatalities by 15%, and the ants varied their intake depending upon how high the peroxide concentration was.
encounter	V;NFIN	encounter	In the wild, Formica fusca can encounter similar chemicals in aphids and dead ants.
report	V;PST;SG;3	reported	The Independent reported self-medicating ants a first among insects.
obtain	V;PST;SG;3	obtained	Bos obtained his doctorate from the University of Copenhagen.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	He began postdoctoral research at Helsinki in 2012.
run	V;PRS;SG;3	runs	He also runs the AntyScience blog.
aim	V;PRS;SG;3	aims	The blog aims to help address "a gap between scientists and 'the general public'."
reference	V;PRS;V.PTCP	referencing	The name is a pun referencing ants, its primary topic, science, and "non-scientific" jargon-free communication.
discuss	V;PRS;SG;3	discusses	He now discusses his work with Wikinews.
attract	V;PST;SG;3	attracted	What first attracted you to researching ants?
keep	V;PRS;V.PTCP	keeping	Me and a studymate were keeping a lot of animals during our studies, from beetles, to butterflies and mantids, to ants.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We had the ants in an observation nest, and I could just look at them for hours, watching them go about.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	This was in my third year of Biology study I think.
need	V;PST;SG;1	needed	After a while I needed to start thinking about an internship for my M.Sc. studies, and decided to write a couple of professors.
end	V;PST;SG;1	ended	I ended up going to the Centre for Social Evolution at the University of Copenhagen where I did a project on learning in Ants under supervision of Prof. Patrizia d'Ettorre.
like	V;PST;SG;1	liked	I liked it so much there I ended up doing a PhD and I've been working on social insects ever since.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	What methods and equipment were used for this investigation?
try	V;PRS;SG;1	try	I try to work on a very low budget, and like to build most of the experimental setups myself (we actually have equipment in the lab nicknamed the 'Nickinator', 'i-Nick' and the 'Nicktendo64').
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's not that much money in fundamental science at the moment, so I try to cut the costs wherever possible.
collect	V;PST;1;PL	collected	We collected wild colonies of Formica fusca by searching through old tree-trunks in old logging sites in southern Finland.
house	V;PST;1;PL	housed	We then housed the ants in nests I made using Y-tong [aerated concrete].
carve	V;NFIN	carve	It's very soft stone that you can easily carve.
carve	V;PST;1;PL	carved	We carved out little squares for the ants to live in (covered with old CD covers to prevent them escaping!).
drill	V;PST;1;PL	drilled	We then drilled a tunnel to a pot (the foraging arena), where the ants got the choice between the food with medicine and the food without.
infect	V;PST;1;PL	infected	We infected the ants by preparing a solution of the fungus Beauveria bassiana.
dip	V;PST;V.PTCP	dipped	Afterwards, each ant was dipped in the solution for a couple of seconds, dried on a cloth and put in the nest.
expose	V;PRS;V.PTCP	exposing	After exposing the ants to the fungus, we took pictures of each foraging arena three times per day, and counted how many ants were present on each food-source.
aerate	V;PST;V.PTCP	aerated	Example of aerated concrete, which provided a home for the subjects.
give	V;PST;SG;3	gave	This gave us the data that ants choose more medicine after they have been infected.
die	V;PRS;PL;3	die	The result that healthy ants die sooner when ingesting ROS [Reactive Oxygen Species, the group of chemicals that includes hydrogen peroxide] but infected ants die less was obtained in another way (as you have to 'force feed' the ROS, as healthy ants, when given the choice, ignore that food-source.)
put	V;PST;1;PL	put	For this we basically put colonies on a diet of either food with medicine or without for a while.
infect	V;PST;SG;3	infected	And afterwards either infected them or not.
count	V;PRS;1;PL	count	Then for about two weeks we count every day how many ants died.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	This gives us the data to do a so-called survival analysis.
measure	V;PST;1;PL	measured	We measured the ROS-concentration in the bodies of ants after they ingested the food with the medicine using a spectrophotometer.
add	V;PRS;V.PTCP	adding	By adding certain chemicals, the ROS can be measured using the emission of light of a certain wave-length.
measure	V;NFIN	measure	The detrimental effect of ROS on spores was easy to measure.
mix	V;PST;1;PL	mixed	We mixed different concentrations of ROS with the spores, plated them out on petridishes with an agar-solution where fungus can grow on.
count	V;PST;1;PL	counted	A day after, we counted how many spores were still alive.
consider	V;NFIN	consider	How reliable do you consider your results to be?
get	V;PST;1;PL	got	The results we got are very reliable.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We had a lot of colonies containing a lot of ants, and wherever possible we conducted the experiment blind.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	This means the experimenter doesn't know which ants belong to which treatment, so it's impossible to influence the results with 'observer bias'.
extrapolate	V;NFIN	extrapolate	It is hard to extrapolate to other ants, as different species lead very different lives.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews President of the International Brotherhood of Magicians
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	October is National Magic Month in the United States.
speak	V;PST;SG;3	spoke	Wikinews spoke with William Evans, president of the International Brotherhood of Magicians, about the current state of magic and what its future looks like in the world of entertainment.
involve	V;PST;V.PTCP	involved	For how long have you been involved in performing / studying magic?
start	V;PST;SG;1	started	I am 61 now so I really started when I was about 10 years old even though I had seen and worked with some basic tricks a few years earlier.
remember	V;PRS;SG;1	remember	I remember going into Hollywood Magic and wanting that big red box with the dragons on it and I didn't even know what it did.
sell	V;NFIN	sell	The magician behind the counter was wise enough not to sell it to me, but instead sold me two books which I still own today - "Scarne on Card Tricks" and "Scarne on Magic Tricks".
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	That started me out on books and I have amassed quite a few since then.
think	V;NFIN	think	Do you think magic is alive as a viable form of entertainment today?
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are more magicians working today than ever before.
be	V;NFIN	be	There will always be an audience for good magicians doing good magic.
help	V;PST;V.PTCP	helped	Has the internet helped or hurt magic?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think it has helped draw younger people to magic as a hobby, but I think it has hurt the ability of young magicians to think because so many rely on the internet for everything and they aren't reading the books.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have to have knowledge of the history of magic and what has been done before.
see	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeing	Moreover, seeing someone perform on the internet takes away the use of our senses, originality and imagination that reading and thinking about magic can produce.
become	V;NFIN	become	Anyone can become a monkey; it takes serious study to become a magician.
travel	V;NFIN	travel	Do you travel much in your current role?
travel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	traveling	Yes, I am traveling quite a bit.
induct	V;PST;V.PTCP	inducted	After I was inducted as International President in Phoenix in July, I have been to the Jeff McBride Experience in Eureka Springs, Arkansas, Magic Live in Las Vegas, The Midwest Magic Jubilee in St. Louis, the TAOM in Dallas, the British Ring Convention in Buxton, England and the Magic Circle in London.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	I am going to Las Vegas and Los Angeles next month, Italy in November, back to Las Vegas in December, Mexico in January, Magi-Fest in February, the Winter Carnival of Magic in Tennessee in March, Eureka Springs Cavalcade of Magic in March, and the FFFF in April.
display	V;PRS;V.PTCP	displaying	File photo of magician David Gish displaying magic props for a young child.
credit	V;PST;V.PTCP	credited	At the age of 40 he was credited with revolutionizing the field of modern linguistics.
describe	V;PST;V.PTCP	described	He was one of the first opponents of the Vietnam War, and is a self-described Libertarian Socialist.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	At age 80 he continues to write books; his latest book, Hegemony or Survival, was a bestseller in non-fiction.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to the Arts and Humanities Citation Index Professor Chomsky is the eighth most cited scholar of all time.
sit	V;PST;SG;3	sat	On March 13, Professor Chomsky sat down with Michael Dranove for an interview in his MIT office in Cambridge, Massachusetts.
want	V;PST;SG;1	wanted	I just wanted to know if you had any thoughts on recent NATO actions and the protests coming up at the 60th NATO conference, I know you’re speaking at the counter-conference.
give	V;PRS;SG;1	give	Could be I give so many talks I can’t remember.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	On the NATO conference, well I mean the obvious question is why should NATO exist?
ask	V;NFIN	ask	In fact you can ask questions about why it should ever have existed, but now why should it exist.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean the theory was, whether you believe it or not, that it would be a defensive alliance against potential Soviet aggression, that’s the basic doctrine.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	Well there’s no defense against Soviet aggression, so whether you believe that doctrine or not that’s gone.
collapse	V;PST;SG;3	collapsed	When the Soviet Union collapsed there had been an agreement, a recent agreement, between Gorbachev and the U.S government and the first Bush administration.
agree	V;PST;SG;3	agreed	The agreement was that Gorbachev agreed to a quite remarkable concession: he agreed to let a united Germany join the NATO military alliance.
destroy	V;PST;V.PTCP	destroyed	Now it is remarkable in the light of history, the history of the past century, Germany alone had virtually destroyed Russia, twice, and Germany backed by a hostile military alliance, centered in the most phenomenal military power in history, that’s a real threat.
agree	V;PST;SG;3	agreed	Nevertheless he agreed, but there was a quid pro quo, namely that NATO should not expand to the east, so Russia would at least have a kind of security zone.
agree	V;PST;PL;3	agreed	And George Bush and James Baker, secretary of state, agreed that NATO would not expand one inch to the east.
propose	V;PST;SG;3	proposed	Gorbachev also proposed a nuclear free weapons zone in the region, but the U.S wouldn’t consider that.
collapse	V;PST;SG;3	collapsed	Okay, so that was the basis on which then shortly after the Soviet Union collapsed.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Well, Clinton came into office what did he do?
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	Well one of the first things he did was to back down on the promise of not expanding NATO to the east.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	Well that’s a significant threat to the Soviet Union, to Russia now that there was no longer any Soviet Union, it was a significant threat to Russia and not surprisingly they responded by beefing up their offensive capacity, not much but some.
rescind	V;PST;PL;3	rescinded	So they rescinded their pledge not to use nuclear weapons on first strike, NATO had never rescinded it, but they had and started some remilitarization.
predict	V;PST;V.PTCP	predicted	With Bush, the aggressive militarism of the Bush administration, as predicted, induced Russia to extend further its offensive military capacity; it’s still going on right now.
propose	V;PST;SG;3	proposed	When Bush proposed the missile defense systems in Eastern Europe, Poland and Czechoslovakia, it was a real provocation to the Soviet Union.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean that was discussed in U.S arms control journals, that they would have to regard as a potential threat to their strategic deterrent, meaning as a first strike weapon.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	And the claim was that it had to do with Iranian missiles, but forget about that.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take say on Obama, Obama’s national security advisor James Jones former Marine commandant is on record of favoring expansion of NATO to the south and the east, further expansion of NATO, and also making it an intervention force.
explain	V;PST;V.PTCP	explained	And the head of NATO, Hoop Scheffer, he has explained that NATO must take on responsibility for ensuring the security of pipelines and sea lanes, that is NATO must be a guarantor of energy supplies for the West.
want	V;NFIN	want	Well that’s kind of an unending war, so do we want NATO to exist, do we want there to be a Western military alliance that carries out these activities, with no pretense of defense?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Well I think that’s a pretty good question; I don’t see why it should, I mean there happens to be no other military alliance remotely comparable — if there happened to be one I’d be opposed to that too.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	So I think the first question is, what is this all about, why should we even be debating NATO, is there any reason why it should exist?
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	We’ve seen mass strikes all around the world, in countries that we wouldn’t expect it.
think	V;NFIN	think	Do you think this is a revival of the Left in the West?
think	V;NFIN	think	Or do you think it’s nothing?
tell	V;NFIN	tell	It’s really hard to tell.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean there’s certainly signs of it, and in the United States too, in fact we had a sit down strike in the United States not long ago, which is a very militant labor action.
sit	V;NFIN	Sit	Sit down strikes which began at a significant level in the 1930’s were very threatening to management and ownership, because the sit down strike is one step before workers taking over the factory and running it and kicking out the management, and probably doing a better job.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	So that’s a frightening idea, and police were called in and so on.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	Well we just had one in the United States at the Republic Windows and Doors Factory, it’s hard to know, I mean these things are just hard to predict, they may take off, and they may take on a broader scope, they may fizzle away or be diverted.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews New York bar owner on Santorum cocktail
interview	V;PST;SG;3	interviewed	Wikinews interviewed one of the owners of a New York City bar about a popular new politically-themed cocktail drink called Santorum.
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	The beverage was inspired by the santorum neologism coined in advice columnist Dan Savage's column Savage Love in response to comments made by former Pennsylvania Senator Rick Santorum about homosexuality;
vote	V;PST;PL;3	voted	Savage's readers voted to define santorum as; "the frothy mixture of lube and fecal matter that is sometimes the byproduct of anal sex."
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	The santorum neologism has inspired satirical forms of parody, including this political cartoon by Saturday Morning Breakfast Cereal webcomic artist Zach Weiner.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	The Pacific Standard bar is located in Brooklyn, New York, and is co-owned by Jonathan M. Stan and John-Christian G. Rauschenberg.
comment	V;PST;SG;3	commented	Stan commented on the creation of the Santorum cocktail, "When he was winning in the polls, I thought, 'OK, I'll do a Santorum'."
regard	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Regarding	Regarding how long the beverage will be made available, Stan remarked to The Brooklyn Paper, "We'll keep it around until he’s irrelevant. I hope he’s there the whole way".
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	The main ingredients of the Santorum drink include vodka of an orange citrus variety, Baileys Irish Cream, and Angostura bitters.
serve	V;PST;V.PTCP	served	It is served in a cocktail glass and topped with Godiva chocolate flakes.
price	V;PST;V.PTCP	priced	The beverage is priced at US $ 8.00, and upon an order for it, the bartender will recount for the customer the definition of the santorum neologism.
venture	V;PST;SG;3	ventured	Troy Patterson of Slate Magazine ventured over to Pacific Standard to sample the new santorum cocktail at the bar.
taste	V;PRS;V.PTCP	tasting	After tasting the beverage, Patterson observed, "My Santorum was sweet but balanced, with a subtle citrus pucker".
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	[The santorum neologism is] offensive beyond, you know, anything that any public figure or anybody in America should tolerate, and the mainstream media laughs about it. — Rick Santorum
compare	V;PST;SG;3	compared	In a 2003 interview with the Associated Press, Rick Santorum compared legalizing same-sex marriage in the United States to supporting bestiality.
select	V;PST;PL;3	selected	Readers of the Savage Love advice column selected a new definition for the Senator’s last name, and Savage created a website SpreadingSantorum.com to promulgate the spread of the phenomenon.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	The term became a prominent result in searches online, and gained dominance on Web search engines including Google, Bing, and Yahoo!.
acknowledge	V;PST;V.PTCP	acknowledged	Rick Santorum himself has acknowledged and discussed the existence and prevalence of the santorum neologism phenomenon; he was quoted by The Canadian Press on his assessment of Google's response;
have	V;NFIN	have	"To have a business allow that type of filth to be purveyed through their website or through their system is something that they say they can't handle.
suspect	V;PRS;SG;1	suspect	I suspect that's not true."
criticize	V;PST;SG;3	criticized	Santorum criticized the response of the press to the phenomenon in a 2011 radio interview, saying, "It's offensive beyond, you know, anything that any public figure or anybody in America should tolerate, and the mainstream media laughs about it."
display	V;PRS;SG;3	displays	Pacific Standard owner, Jonathan M. Stan, displays the Santorum cocktail drink as a finished product at the bar. (2012).
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	Image; Pacific Standard, provided by the owners.
inspire	V;PST;SG;3	inspired	What inspired you to create a cocktail after the santorum neologism?
think	V;PST;1;PL	thought	Santorum the person has been in the news throughout the primary season, and we thought it would be interesting to try to create a delicious drink that mimicked the appearance of the Dan Savage meaning of "santorum."
hear	V;NFIN	hear	How did you first hear about the definition of the santorum neologism that grew out of the contest from the Savage Love advice column?
remember	V;NFIN	remember	We don't really remember.
read	V;PST;SG;3	read	Probably read about it somewhere.
take	V;NFIN	take	It's not for us to take a stand on any political issues.
leave	V;NFIN	leave	We'll leave that to the professionals.
think	V;NFIN	think	Do you think it was an appropriate form of satire for Dan Savage to popularize the definition of the santorum neologism created in his advice column?
think	V;PST;1;PL	thought	We thought it was funny.
leave	V;PRS;1;PL	leave	Whether it is appropriate or not is another thing we leave to the pros to decide.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	When was the Santorum cocktail first created?
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	What ingredients go in the Santorum cocktail?
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	How is the Santorum drink made?
shake	V;PST;V.PTCP	shaken	The ingredients are shaken and/or poured into a cocktail glass.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See the pictures.
receive	V;PST;V.PTCP	received	Your Santorum cocktail creation has already received media coverage from publications including; The Brooklyn Paper, The New York Times, Jezebel, Metro.us, EDGE on the Net, and Instinct Magazine.
think	V;NFIN	think	Did you think when you created it that the Santorum cocktail would receive this news coverage?
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	We were just trying to come up with a topical and funny new cocktail for our customers to laugh about and enjoy.
recommend	V;NFIN	recommend	What culinary dishes would you recommend that go well with the Santorum cocktail?
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	You'd probably be having the cocktail at dessert time, so something sweet; ice cream or pie.
suppose	V;NFIN	suppose	How many times do you suppose you’ve served it at your establishment since its creation?
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	The drink was mildly popular for the last few months, but of course has become a great deal more popular since getting all this publicity.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have no way to estimate how many times we've served it overall, but we're now pouring around ten a night.
come	V;NFIN	come	How did you come to be involved with this discovery
get	V;PST;SG;3	got	Frank Rack got in touch after they returned from Antarctica in hopes that I could help with an identification on the anemone.
learn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	learning	What was your first reaction upon learning there was an undiscovered ecosystem under the ice in the Ross Sea?
amaze	V;PST;V.PTCP	amazed	I was amazed and really excited.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think to say it was unexpected is inaccurate, because it implies that there was a well-founded expectation of something.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	The technology that Frank and his colleagues are using to explore the ice is so important because, given our lack of data, we have no reasonable expectation of what it should be like, or what it shouldn't be like.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's a return trip planned hopefully for 2015, with both biologists and ANDRILL geologists.
intend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	intending	Are you intending to go there yourself?
love	V;NFIN	love	I would love to.
go	V;NFIN	go	But I am also happy to not go, as long as someone collects more animals on my behalf!
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	What I want to do with the animals requires new material preserved in diverse ways, but it doesn't require me to be there.
enhance	V;NFIN	enhance	Although I am sure that being there would enhance my understanding of the animals and the system in which they live, and would help me formulate more and better questions about the anemones, ship time is expensive, especially in Antarctica, and if there are biologists whose contribution is predicated on being there, they should have priority to be there.
shroud	V;PST;V.PTCP	shrouded	These animals are shrouded in mystery.
intrigue	V;PRS;V.PTCP	intriguing	Some of the most intriguing questions are chemical; do they produce some kind of antifreeze, and is that orange glow in the ROV lights their own?
talk	V;IMP;2	Talk	Talk us through the difficulties encountered when trying to find answers with the specimens on hand.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	The samples we have are small in terms of numbers and they are all preserved in formalin (a kind of formaldehyde solution).
preserve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	preserving	The formalin is great for preserving structures, but for anemones, it prevents study of DNA or of the chemistry of the body.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	This means we can't look at the issue you raise with these animals.
do	V;NFIN	do	What we could do, however, was to study anatomy and figure out what it is, so that when we have samples preserved for studying e.g., the genome, transcriptome, or metabolome, or conduct tests of the fluid in the burrows or in the animals themselves, we can make precise comparisons, and figure out what these animals have or do (metabolically or chemically) that lets them live where they live.
know	V;PRS;V.PTCP	knowing	Just knowing a whole lot about a single species isn't very useful, even if that animal is as special as these clearly are - we need to know what about them is different and thus related to living in this strange way.
get	V;NFIN	get	The only way to get at what's different is to make comparisons with close relatives.
start	V;NFIN	start	We can start that side of the work now, anticipating having more beasts in the future.
suspect	V;PRS;SG;1	suspect	In terms of their glow, I suspect that it's not theirs - although luminescence is common in anemone relatives, they don't usually make light themselves.
make	V;NFIN	make	They do make a host of florescent proteins, and these may interact with the light of the ROV to give that gorgeous glow.
perform	V;NFIN	perform	What analysis did you perform on the specimens and what equipment was used?
use	V;PST;SG;1	used	I used a dissecting scope to look at the animal's external anatomy and overall body organization (magnification of 60 X).
embed	V;PST;SG;1	embedded	I embedded a few of the animals in wax and then cut them into very thin slices using a microtome, mounted the slices on microscope slides, stained the slices to enhance contrast, and then looked at those slides under a compound microscope
get	V;PST;SG;1	got	(that's how I got the pictures of the muscles etc in the paper).
use	V;PST;SG;1	used	I used that same compound scope to look at squashed bits of tissue to see the stinging capsules (= nematocysts).
compare	V;PST;SG;1	compared	I compared the things I saw under the 'scopes to what had been published on other species in this group.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	This step seems trivial, but it is really the most important part!
compare	V;PRS;V.PTCP	comparing	By comparing my observations to what my colleagues and predecessors had found, I figured out what group it belongs to, and was able to determine that within that group, it was a new species.
take	V;NFIN	take	It was three years between recovery of specimens and final publication, why did it take so long?
mean	V;PRS;SG;2	mean	You mean, how did we manage to make it all happen so quickly, right? :)
send	V;PST;SG;3	sent	It was about two years from when Frank sent me specimens to when we got the paper out.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	Some of that time was just lost time - I had other projects in the queue that I needed to finish.
figure	V;PST;1;PL	figured	Once we figured out what it was, we played a lot of manuscript email tag, which can be challenging and time consuming given the differing schedules that folks keep in terms of travel, field work, etc.
process	V;PRS;V.PTCP	processing	Manuscript review and processing took about four months.
pose	V;PST;V.PTCP	posed	What sort of difficulties were posed by the unorthodox preservatives used, and what additional work might be possible on a specimen with intact DNA?
follow	V;PST;PL;3	followed	The preservation was not unorthodox - they followed best practices for anatomical preservation.
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Having	Having DNA-suitable material will let us see whether there are new genes, or genes turned on in different ways and at different times that help explain how these animals burrow into hard ice and then survive in the cold.
belong	V;PRS;SG;3	belongs	I am curious about the population structure of the "fields" of anemones - the group to which Edwardsiella andrillae belongs includes many species that reproduce asexually, and it's possible that the fields are "clones" produced asexually rather than the result of sexual reproduction.
test	V;NFIN	test	DNA is the only way to test this.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	This map shows the location of the Ross Ice Shelf in the Antarctic, and the two known localities for E. andrillae relative to McMurdo Station
explain	V;PST;SG;3	explained	Daly explained how she obtained these images of the anemone's anatomy.
operate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Operating	Operating the business
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's a misconception in this industry.
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	Your exposure to this is probably primarily what you see in movies, scanning magazines, scanning the Internet, just snippets everywhere.
portray	V;PST;V.PTCP	portrayed	It's portrayed as though it's dark, black and evil.
wear	V;NFIN	wear	I do wear a lot of black.
look	V;PRS;SG;2	look	If you look at the rooms, there's all the accouterments you'd find in these types of places -- but more than anything, there's a lot of good feeling around here.
hire	V;PRS;1;PL	hire	We hire the opposite of what you'd think we would hire, what the average person would think we would hire.
hire	V;PRS;1;PL	hire	We hire educated, nice people.
remember	V;PRS;SG;1	remember	I remember one of our ads that I had put in said, "If you think nice girls don't do S&M," -- I forgot what the rest of it said --
think	V;IMP;2	think	"-- think again."
think	V;IMP;2	think	Yeah, "think again" or something like that.
pull	V;PST;V.PTCP	pulled	And that ad had pulled so many girls, because they -- I think they all thought that nice girls don't do this.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	And a bunch of them had called and they said, "Wow."
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	And I think I remember, a bunch of guys called because of that also.
remember	V;NFIN	remember	Do you remember?
involve	V;PST;V.PTCP	involved	What are the startup costs involved?
vary	V;PRS;SG;3	varies	It varies; look around Manhattan, look around rents, look around build-outs, look around hiring, look around advertising ...
spend	V;PRS;1;PL	spend	The single most costly thing we spend on is rent and advertising, those two together make up the bulk of what we spend.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's supplies, and there's wear and tear and maintenance, and bookkeeping and things like that that you have to do.
run	V;PRS;V.PTCP	running	The hardest thing in running this business, which is why people can't do it, is the relationship that my wife has with the gals and with the customers.
advertise	V;NFIN	advertise	Where do you advertise?
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	A lot on the web, we've used most of the local city papers, New York Press, things like that, L Magazine.
change	V;PRS;PL;3	change	Magazines constantly change their policy on it; they want it, they don't want it ...
depend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Depending	Depending on the election year --
buy	V;PRS;V.PTCP	buying	Who's buying the paper, who's selling the paper.
change	V;PRS;SG;3	changes	So it changes, so one year we got to be in the Press, but they have now stopped adult ads.
move	V;PRS;V.PTCP	moving	We're moving more towards the Internet at this point, because the Internet is a much easier, safer way for a person to look at sensitive material and not get caught with it.
hire	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Hiring	Hiring employees
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	Personality test questions given to prospective mistresses.
hire	V;PRS;1;PL	hire	Pursuant to your business question: we only hire nice people.
care	V;NFIN	care	I don't care if a girl is absolutely exquisite looking --
meet	V;PST;V.PTCP	met	My husband has met beautiful, beautiful girls --
say	V;NFIN	say	-- and he's in the end, he would say, "Well, I won't hire them."
be	V;PRS;SG;1	'm	I'm like, ‘Are you insane?
hire	V;NFIN	hire	I won't hire them.
hire	V;PRS;SG;1	hire	I hire --
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	Because I want someone who's going to treat people nicely and well.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	How can you tell whether they will or will not?
talk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	talking	Just by talking to them?
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	Having been a psychologist for a number of years gives me a leg up on it.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	And I have made him hire these girls, and ultimately he's been right
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I also have a profile they fill out for me, a mini-MMPI that I developed, so that I have an idea -- Rebecca and I put it together
know	V;NFIN	know	I don't know if you'd like to see one.
show	V;NFIN	show	I could show you.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	So I have a mini-MMPI that I --
have	V;NFIN	have	Could I have a sample, not necessarily one that's filled out, but just one that you --
show	V;NFIN	show	I'll show you.
make	V;NFIN	make	He'll make you fill one out.
hire	V;NFIN	hire	You wouldn't hire me.
hire	V;PRS;SG;2	hire	Is it only girls you hire?
reveal	V;NFIN	reveal	What would a question like "the greatest woman in the world" reveal to you?
reveal	V;NFIN	reveal	No question would reveal anything independently of all the questions together --
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	-- but I have a chart that I'll go through; the MMPI.
use	V;PRS;SG;1	use	The baseline data that I use to --
originate	V;NFIN	originate	How did you originate these questions?
make	V;NFIN	make	Where did I make them?
get	V;PRS;SG;2	get	You get a baseline from asking girls and refining profiles --
know	V;NFIN	know	I don't know, the O.J. Simpson ….
see	V;PRS;SG;1	see	Yeah, I see O.J. Simpson.
give	V;PST;SG;2	gave	I'm not sure which one you gave him, but there's a brief, brief, brief IQ test there.
ask	V;NFIN	ask	Do you ask for references?
call	V;NFIN	call	No, because I really can't call and tell them I'm yeah, so-and-so's applying here for a position.
interview	V;PST;V.PTCP	interviewed	Jack Herrick, wikiHow founder interviewed by Wikinews
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	wikiHow is a wiki-based site which aims to "build the world's largest, highest quality how-to manual."
publish	V;PST;SG;3	published	It recently published its 50,000th article, and to discuss what the site has achieved since its creation, Wikinews interviewed the site's founder, Jack Herrick.
explain	V;PRS;SG;3	explains	In the interview, Herrick explains what wikiHow is, why he decided to create it, how he grew it in size, and why he chose to use a Creative Commons license (HyperLink) for his site.
read	V;IMP;2	Read	Read below for more of the interview in full.
explain	V;NFIN	explain	For our readers who are unaware of your site, could you briefly explain what wikiHow is?
build	V;NFIN	build	wikiHow is a collaborative effort to build the world's largest, highest quality how-to manual.
produce	V;NFIN	produce	Our long term goal is to produce practical instructions on almost every topic in every language.
take	V;PRS;1;PL	take	We're quite far from our goals currently, but we take comfort in the fact that every month wikiHow gets noticeably better and larger
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We currently have just under 50000 articles and over 1000 featured articles.
hope	V;PRS;1;PL	hope	We hope that in time, we will have millions of articles with many of them having featured status.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	That said, we are realistic enough to realize that this goal will take decades to achieve.
get	V;PRS;1;PL	get	We get over 14 million unique visitors per month (source: Google Analytics), which makes us the 135th most popular site according to Quantcast.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	[Note: The interview took place just before the 50000 milestone was met]
run	V;PRS;SG;3	runs	Like Wikipedia, wikiHow is a wiki that runs on Mediawiki software.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	That said, we are quite different from Wikipedia in several respects:
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The English wikiHow has only 60 admins, compared to the 1000 + on English Wikipedia.
get	V;PRS;SG;3	gets	As a result, wikiHow is still at the size that every editor eventually gets to know other editors.
run	V;PST;V.PTCP	run	We are run as a Hybrid Organization - a for-profit company focused on achieving a social good.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	This has several advantages and disadvantages when compared to the non-profit structure at the Wikimedia Foundation.
show	V;PRS;1;PL	show	We show opt-out advertising to anonymous visitors rather than ask for donations.
fund	V;PRS;SG;3	funds	Our advertising revenue funds community meetups and has even allowed us to donate over $ 60,000 to charities such as the Wikimedia Foundation and Creative Commons.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	In addition, we've used our revenue on things like buying carbon offsets to become carbon neutral.
focus	V;PST;V.PTCP	focused	Our community culture is focused on wikiLove and civility.
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	As a result some folks believe wikiHow is a more humane and enjoyable place to work than other places online.
decide	V;NFIN	decide	Why did you decide to create wikiHow?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think that providing people with a practical education is one of the most empowering things you can offer a person.
imagine	V;IMP;2	Imagine	Imagine having detailed step-by-step instructions on how to do any activity you could imagine.
accomplish	V;NFIN	accomplish	How much more could you accomplish as an individual if you could learn any activity?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think that building a universal how-to manual would be a tremendous gift for the world.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Knowledge is power and wikiHow has the potential to make all of us a bit more powerful.
build	V;PRS;V.PTCP	building	I've been interested in building a comprehensive how-to manual long before I first envisioned wikiHow.
build	V;PRS;V.PTCP	building	My first attempt at building this resource was a website I used to run called eHow.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	eHow was and continues to be the largest and how to website in the world.
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	contains	eHow contains copyrighted content run on proprietary software.
run	V;PST;SG;1	ran	When I ran eHow, the content was professionally produced and edited.
work	V;PST;SG;3	worked	This business model worked for producing content on topics that appeal to advertisers in languages like English that have large advertising markets.
pay	V;PRS;V.PTCP	paying	However, paying people to write and edit articles ultimately means that you have to make one of two sacrifices.
sacrifice	V;PRS;SG;2	sacrifice	You either 1) sacrifice on breadth and don't produce the topics that won't interest advertisers, or 2) you sacrifice on quality and produce content that doesn't cost much to write.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	I wasn't interested in making either of these sacrifices, as I think the world's how to manual needs to be high quality AND comprehensive.
decide	V;PST;SG;1	decided	So ultimately I decided that eHow's model would not build the resource I envisioned.
discover	V;PST;SG;1	discovered	When I discovered Wikipedia, I recognized that the wiki model had the potential to build the how-to resource the world really needs.
decide	V;PST;SG;1	decided	So I decided to sell eHow and use the proceeds to build wikiHow.
decide	V;NFIN	decide	Why did you decide to use a Creative Commons license?
use	V;PRS;1;PL	use	We use a Creative Commons license to give our community the right to fork (http://www.wikihow.com/wikiHow:Right-to-Fork).
contribute	V;NFIN	contribute	In my opinion, people should be hesitant to contribute to websites where you only have the "right to leave."
think	V;PRS;PL;3	think	If wikiHow volunteers think our site is going in the wrong direction, they can take all the content and all the software and move the project elsewhere.
release	V;PRS;1;PL	release	This is possible because we release all of our software under the Open Source GPL license and release our content under a Creative Commons license.
fork	V;NFIN	fork	This right to fork guarantees that wikiHow, the company, will always serve the goal of the mission and our volunteer community.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I believe that offering this right to fork has been a key element of our success thus far.
demand	V;NFIN	demand	My hope is that over time, internet users will demand this of any site where they invest their time in a way that creates value for others.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	That said, when we started out I didn't know the difference between the GNU Free Document License and my California Driver's license.
make	V;PST;1;PL	made	As a result, we made some mistakes along the path.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	In retrospect, I think we probably should have selected the CC BY-SA license instead of CC-BY-SA-NC.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	At a minimum this would provide a helpful compatibility with other wikis and free culture projects.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	wikiHow now has millions of visitors per month.
grow	V;NFIN	grow	How did you grow the site to the size it is today?
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	In December 2008, wikiHow had over 14 million unique visitors according to Google Analytics (Full data: http://www.wikihow.com/wikiHow:Statistics).
rank	V;PRS;SG;3	ranks	Quantcast ranks wikiHow as the 135th most popular site in the US.
achieve	V;PST;SG;3	achieved	wikiHow achieved this popularity for a few reasons.
want	V;PRS;PL;3	want	People want to learn more practical skills, and for the most part the internet doesn't provide enough quality information yet.
attract	V;PST;1;PL	attracted	Second, we attracted millions of readers via the virtuous circle of wiki editing: We had some articles of mixed quality, and editors joined to improve those articles, which in turn attracted more readers.
continue	V;PRS;1;PL	continue	We continue to depend on this same virtuous cycle: More readers, means more editors, which creates better instructions, leading again to more readers.
enjoy	V;PST;V.PTCP	enjoyed	It is the same virtuous circle that Wikipedia has enjoyed.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews academic Simon Ličen about attitudes towards US Paralympics
interview	V;PST;SG;3	interviewed	On Thursday, with 110 days until the start of the 2014 Winter Paralympics in Sochi, Russia, Wikinews interviewed Assistant Professor in Educational Leadership, Sport Studies and Educational / Counseling Psychology at Washington State University Simon Ličen about attitudes in United States towards the Paralympics.
join	V;PST;V.PTCP	joined	Ličen has recently joined the Sport Management Program at Washington State University to develop its sport media and communication research and teaching contents.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	Originally from Slovenia, he served as the Director of Media and Communications of a WTA Tour event and was a member of the UNESCO Slovenian National Commission.
think	V;NFIN	think	Why do you think the Paralympic movement has so little visibility in the US compared to other countries like Australia, the United Kingdom and even Canada?
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Sports in the United States largely reaffirm existing relations of power in society.
emphasize	V;PRS;SG;3	emphasizes	It emphasizes consumerism, the belief that success always goes to people who merit it due to their abilities, dedication and qualifications, and reinforces, rather than changes, existing ideas related to gender, ethnicity and nationality.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	brings	Paralympic sport brings attention to athletes who are typically overlooked in American society because the majority of the population does not want to identify with people who are disabled.
put	V;NFIN	put	Although disability is not contagious, interest in disabled sports might put into question the masculinity of the males following it.
challenge	V;PRS;PL;3	challenge	Disabled athletes also challenge existing relations of power by displaying dedication, hard work and perseverance in different contexts than those most sports fans are accustomed to.
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	Other countries, including the ones you mention, have stronger social orientations in all aspects of society.
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	Even though legislative support may be less strong than the one provided by the Americans with Disabilities Act, many social institutions including the media are more receptive to this form of diversity.
think	V;NFIN	think	What do you think the impact will be for the Paralympic movement will be with the Games being televised live in the United States for first time?
depend	V;PRS;SG;3	depends	The impact depends on a number of aspects.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	One of them is the channels that NBC as the broadcasting rights owner for the United States will use to air the Paralympic Games on.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	Will they be shown nationally or regionally, on NBC or on any of the company’s multiple cable networks?
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	A second aspect is the parts or hours of the day the Paralympics will be shown.
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember that there is a nine-hour difference between Sochi and New York, and a 12-hour difference between Sochi and the US West Coast.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	So daytime events will be shown live in the United States in the middle of the night, and evening prime-time events will be shown — indeed live — in the morning U.S. time.
show	V;PRS;V.PTCP	showing	So showing the Paralympics live on United States television might turn out to be less glamorous than it appears.
mediate	V;PST;V.PTCP	mediated	A third important factor is the way the event will be mediated: will NBC have its best sports broadcasters following the event after having worked the Winter Olympics?
treat	V;NFIN	treat	Will they treat and announce the competitions as they do all others — for better and worse?
take	V;NFIN	take	Will they take it as an opportunity to educate viewers about disability and diversity while showing superb athletic performances without engaging in a discourse of pity?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	All in all, I think this is a terrific opportunity to improve sports coverage in a multitude of aspects; but we will have to wait until after the event to assess to what extent the broadcasters will meet these expectations.
feature	V;PRS;SG;3	features	The LibriVox website features a catalog of about a hundred completed books, including books in other languages like German, Hebrew, and Japanese.
decide	V;NFIN	decide	Why did you decide to take the acoustic fate of public domain works into your own hands, and how did you go about it?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think that a vibrant public domain is very important to a healthy world, and so I thought: here's a way to help the cause.
launch	V;PST;SG;1	launched	I launched LibriVox, emailed some friends and some podcasters who where doing literary stuff, and invited them to record a chapter of Joseph Conrad's Secret Agent.
grow	V;PST;V.PTCP	grown	Things have grown steadily since.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	By the way, AKMA was the first guy I know of to do something like this, with Lawrence Lessig's Free Culture.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	That, I think, started my thinking about this, but it took a while for the idea to crystallize.
liberate	V;PST;V.PTCP	liberated	Hugh McGuire with some books that have not yet been acoustically liberated.
record	V;NFIN	record	Do you personally record audiobooks, and did you already do so before LibriVox?
record	V;PRS;V.PTCP	recording	LibriVox was my first experience recording audiobooks.
do	V;PRS;SG;1	do	I still do the odd chapter, but i am a bit delinquent in finishing some of them these days!
be	V;PRS;SG;3	Is	Is there a particular LibriVox book which you think stands out because of the quality, the overall effort involved, its popularity, or for some other reason?
see	V;IMP;2	See	Ed.: See the LibriVox catalog for a full index.
browse	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Browsing	Browsing the catalog, I find that the quality of the spoken audio varies greatly from text to text or even from chapter to chapter.
want	V;PST;SG;3	wanted	If someone wanted to re-record a chapter because they didn't like the speaker, would you then offer two versions?
take	V;PRS;1;PL	take	We take the Wikipedia approach: if you think it should be better, please help it be so.
note	V;NFIN	note	You'll note we offer a couple of versions of some books.
celebrate	V;PRS;SG;3	celebrates	Indeed our weekly poetry project celebrates this diversity of voice: each week a new short poem is chosen and as many readers as wish to record a version, so you get 20 + versions of the same poem, a very interesting audio experience.
expire	V;PST;V.PTCP	expired	Besides works whose copyright has expired, there is an increasing number of modern books available under Creative Commons and similar licenses.
mention	V;PST;SG;2	mentioned	You mentioned Larry Lessig's Free Culture, but it also includes some out of print titles by publishers who are open to the concept, such as O'Reilly.
want	V;NFIN	want	Do you want to limit the scope of LibriVox strictly to old public domain works, or do you have plans to include recent works under reasonably permissive licenses as well?
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	We are only doing public domain works, for a number of reasons, partly to keep simplicity in our copyright situation -- public domain means its as simple as can be.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	CC makes things more complex.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Also, there are other sites, notably podiobooks.com, which are better suited to CC works than LibriVox.
find	V;PRS;1;PL	find	we find it makes things clear and easy to say: published works, public domain, and leave it at that.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	As far as I can tell, podiobooks.com features "free as in beer" content — material which you can download, but which isn't under a free content license.
work	V;NFIN	work	Instead of "public domain", wouldn't "free content" work just as well, using something like the Free Content Definition to limit the scope?
need	V;PRS;SG;3	needs	This needs more discussion obviously: we chose public domain because the texts we use are public domain and we didn't want to add new restrictions, especially not non-commercial, since already our recordings are being used for a number of commercialish projects (I can't recall any specific project names, but some pay-education sites use LV stuff).
want	V;NFIN	want	We don't want to have to give anyone permission to use our recordings, we want anyone to use them for whatever they want.
work	V;NFIN	work	Also, for the thing to work you need to have many dedicated volunteers willing to do the management (onerous) and many willing to do the reading.
attract	V;NFIN	attract	While Pride and Prejudice will attract many readers, I don't know about O'Reilly's latest tome on XML ... though I might be wrong.
interview	V;PST;SG;3	interviewed	On Saturday, Wikinews interviewed Tina McKenzie, a former member of the Australia women's national wheelchair basketball team, known as the Gliders.
retire	V;PST;SG;3	retired	McKenzie, a silver and bronze Paralympic medalist in wheelchair basketball, retired from the game after the 2012 Summer Paralympics in London.
catch	V;PST;SG;3	caught	Wikinews caught up with her in a cafe in the leafy Melbourne suburb of Preston.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	It was a tournament that I wish we'd actually gone back to more often.
play	V;PRS;SG;3	plays	Who plays in that one?
go	V;PST;1;PL	went	It's quite a large Canadian tournament, and so we went as the Gliders team.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	So we were trying to get as many international games as possible.
compete	V;NFIN	compete	Cause that's one of our problems really, to compete.
cost	V;PRS;SG;3	costs	It costs us so much money to for us to travel overseas and to compete internationally.
compete	V;NFIN	compete	And so we can compete against each other all the time within Australia but we really need to be able to ...
get	V;NFIN	get	No, it's really not, so it's really important to be able to get as a many international trips throughout the year to continue our improvement.
see	V;NFIN;2	see	Also see where all the other teams are at as well.
take	V;PST;1;PL	took	We took quite a few new girls over there back then in 2005, leading into the World Cup in the Netherlands.
captain	V;PST;SG;1	captained	No, I captained in 2010.
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	And then I had a bit of some time off in 2011.
win	V;PST;V.PTCP	won	The Gliders have never won the World Championship.
seem	V;PRS;1;PL	seem	We always seem to have just a little bit of a chill out at the World Cup.
know	V;NFIN	know	I don't know why.
win	V;PST;1;PL	won	2002 World Cup, we won bronze.
end	V;PST;1;PL	ended	Then in 2006 we ended up fourth.
play	V;PST;V.PTCP	played	It was one of the worst World Cups we've played actually.
know	V;NFIN	know	And then in 2010 we just ... I don't know what happened.
play	V;NFIN	play	We just didn't play as well as we thought we would.
come	V;PST;SG;3	Came	Came fourth.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	But you know what?
fire	V;PST;SG;3	Fired	Fired us up for the actual Paralympics.
do	V;NFIN	do	So the World Cup is ... it's good to be able to do well at the World Cup, to be placed, but it also means that you get a really good opportunity to know where you're at in that two year gap between the Paralympics.
come	V;NFIN	come	So you can come back home and revisit what you need to do and, you know, where the team's at.
talk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	talking	Unfortunately, they are talking about moving it so it will be on the year before the Paralympics.
receive	V;PST;V.PTCP	received	You have received a good deal of attention recently.
come	V;PRS;PL;3	come	Even though I'm not Michael Jackson or somebody, when people come up to me and introduce themselves and say, 'Hey Frank, my name is John,' I say, 'Hey John, my name is Frank' and they laugh.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	What goes through your head when that happens?
understand	V;PRS;SG;1	understand	I understand it.
go	V;PST;V.PTCP	gone	I've gone to readings and concerts.
look	V;PRS;SG;1	look	I look at it as human interaction.
perform	V;PST;V.PTCP	performed	Over the years I have performed in 32 countries and 40 states.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	I've been doing this professionally since I was in my twenties, and before that since I was sixteen doing little tidbit poetry readings in coffeehouses.
start	V;PST;SG;1	started	The band I started in 1993, Spoken Motion, received a lot of recognition as a spoken word band born out of the New York spoken word scene.
work	V;PST;SG;1	worked	I worked with some great musicians and performed around the world.
remember	V;PRS;SG;1	remember	I remember signing my first autograph to a kid when I was 25 years old.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	As time went on, I came out with books and CDs, and I became used to that kind of thing.
move	V;NFIN	move	To me, the ultimate feeling of success as an artist, is to move somebody enough where they thank you.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	When someone comes up and says, 'Frank, thank you, your work is great."
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	You have a long career in poetry, but as of late the attention you have garnered is for the Mets-inspired work.
feel	V;NFIN	feel	How do you feel about having a lot of your work overshadowed by the Mets work?
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Some of the greatest poetry has been born out of failure and the depths of adversity in the human experience.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	Walt Whitman, the first great American poet, wrote about the Civil War.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	He went looking for his brother, George Whitman, after he a telegram telling him his brother was injured in the South.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	When he started out his poems were about beating drums, and blow, bugle, blow.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	Then he started to see the real horrors of war.
tap	V;NFIN	tap	He was able to tap into the human condition and the situation at that time.
find	V;PST;SG;3	found	Eventually when he found his brother he had resolution.
experience	V;PST;SG;1	experienced	I experienced that kind of adversity during 9/11 being a civilian volunteer.
load	V;PST;SG;1	loaded	I loaded ferry boats in Jersey City across the river to deliver goods to Ground Zero.
turn	V;PST;SG;1	turned	I turned to Whitman to find some understanding of what is happening in the world right now.
write	V;PST;SG;1	wrote	When I wrote my 9/11-related poems, that was true adversity.
realize	V;PRS;SG;1	realize	I realize baseball is just a game.
recite	V;NFIN	recite	Can you recite a stanza that expresses how you feel right now?
quote	V;PST;SG;3	quoted	This was a piece that the Times only quoted one stanza, but it's about preparation for a battle, and being prepared to either rise to the occasion, or go down:
know	V;NFIN	know	Do you know what it's like to be chased by the Ghost of Failure while staring through Victory's door?
catch	V;PST;V.PTCP	caught	Of course you do, you're a Mets fan caught in a do-or-die moment in late September at Shea
battle	V;PST;V.PTCP	battled	As one that's battled hard through many a broken dream Let me say, "in order to rise to the occasion you must be willing to go down with the ship",
have	V;IMP;2	Have	Have no fear, no hesitation, for Winning shall be it's reward!
let	V;IMP;2	let	Don't let them get in your head!
keep	V;PST;V.PTCP	kept	you've kept it up this long
fight	V;NFIN	fight	You're a Mets fan in late September and youll fight til the glorious end
cheer	V;IMP;2	Cheer	Cheer the team today; (your boys in orange and blue) Let them hear you shout as they fight for what's mightily due
reprint	V;PST;V.PTCP	reprinted	(copyright Frank Messina; reprinted with permission)"
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Sports fans aren't known as patrons of poetry.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	Have you had interaction with "new readers' through your Mets work?
meet	V;PST;SG;1	met	This one person who I never met took a picture of me and sent it to me in an e-mail.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	The e-mail said, 'Frank, I have never bothered you during the game, but I just wanted to say thank you for your work and thank you for making some sense of the successes and failures and I wish you much success with your work.'
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	Last year in my section at the stadium I had a banner that read We Know'.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	That's all it said.
start	V;PST;PL;3	started	Then earlier this year these shirts started to come out that said, "Poet says We Know".
use	V;NFIN	use	We didn't use the banner this year, though, because we didn't know.
know	V;PST;1;PL	knew	The team wasn't so far ahead that we knew.
know	V;PST;1;PL	knew	Last year we just knew we were going to the playoffs; we knew we were going post-season.
walk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	walking	We were walking on eggshells.
stagger	V;PST;V.PTCP	staggered	She was staggered by the loss last year to the Cardinals.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Last year she came up to me during one of the games late in the season; she was so happy we were going to the post season.
clinch	V;PST;V.PTCP	clinched	By that point we had clinched it.
hand	V;PST;SG;3	handed	She handed me a shirt she bought at the stadium and she gave me a big hug.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	With tears in her eyes she said, "Thank you, Mets Poet, thank you."
make	V;NFIN	make	Moments like that must make you realize you have touched people who aren't normally touched by poetry.
open	V;PST;V.PTCP	opened	It's opened up a new fan base, so to speak.
broadcast	V;PST;V.PTCP	broadcast	For the last year SNY has broadcast footage of me with my poems, so quite a few fans known about the "Mets Poet'.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	I have never called myself that, by the way.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	The back of my jersey says "The Poet' because growing up that was my nickname.
use	V;PST;PL;3	used	My brother was a runner and they used to call him The Birdman -- Birdie -- and they called me The Poet.
coin	V;PST;SG;1	coined	It was a natural thing, but I never coined myself as 'The Mets Poet.'
write	V;PRS;SG;3	writes	How The Onion writes an issue
feature	V;PRS;SG;3	features	This exclusive interview features first-hand journalism by a Wikinews reporter.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See the collaboration page for more details.
decide	V;NFIN	decide	How do you decide on the stories?
do	V;PRS;1;PL	do	We do everything backwards here.
start	V;PRS;1;PL	start	We start with the headline and then flesh out the story, as opposed to The New York Times, which writes the issue and then has a headline editor to make it snappy.
start	V;PRS;1;PL	start	We start with a joke.
read	V;PRS;1;PL	read	We read about six hundred to eight hundred headline ideas on a Monday coming from our staff and a small group of writers outside — a contributing core.
whittle	V;PRS;1;PL	whittle	We whittle that down on a Monday to about a hundred, come in Tuesday and pick an issue and brainstorm with the whole editorial staff; all fifteen of us.
assign	V;PRS;1;PL	assign	Then we assign a headline to a specific writer to execute.
go	V;PRS;1;PL	go	We go through a number of drafts and then have the editors pick it up and assign the photojournalism aspect of it to our graphic design team, who adds the visual aspect to it.
punch	V;PRS;PL;3	punch	The editors punch it up over the last couple of days and then it goes out over the Internet and into the print edition.
have	V;NFIN	have	So you don’t have writers coming up with a story and headline, but you will instead have a team of writers choosing the best headlines and then assign it to a particular writer who was not necessarily involved?
write	V;NFIN	write	Writers will occasionally write their own headlines, but we come up with a list of 15 or 20 headline ideas, what we think will make a good story and then we assign it based upon what people’s writing strengths are.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have some people who are great at politics; some people we give all the war stuff to; someone who is in charge of the Britney Spears story of the week — the entertainment stories.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	It seems like some publications, like AM New York, always have a Britney Spears story;
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	is there anything similar with The Onion where they continually revisit a topic or person?
tend	V;PRS;1;PL	tend	We tend to target the zeitgeist more than anything.
hit	V;NFIN	hit	We’ll hit the mainstream media’s portrayal of the entertainment world as much as we’ll hit characters in the entertainment world.
attack	V;NFIN	attack	We’ll attack People Magazine’s coverage of Britney as much as Britney.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	In an interview with Terry Gross, Stephen Colbert said of his time at Second City that they had decided on not doing political humor and, in particular, hackneyed political humor such as Ted Kennedy drinking jokes.
feel	V;PST;PL;3	felt	They felt it was overdone, mean-spirited and not funny.
have	V;NFIN	have	Do your writers have similar rules of thumb?
have	V;NFIN	have	We don’t have any rules or known lines we won’t cross.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have an understanding based upon having the same writers in the back room for years, and those writers training the new writers as they come in.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is an understanding in the room.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	If it makes the room laugh, it probably ends up in the paper.
run	V;PST;1;PL	ran	One example is we ran an article a couple of years back that was, "No Jennifer Lopez News Today".
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	We weren’t going to touch on her dress, or who she was dating.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think it’s important we have an original take on those things.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think it’s similar to what Colbert said to Terry Gross.
do	V;PRS;1;PL	do	We don’t want to just traffic in the same 24 hour news cycle.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	’s	There’s a 24 hour comedy news cycle that exists on all the late night talk shows.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The Onion has a different creative process where we are not trying to hit everything in the 24 hours and on the same notes.
want	V;PRS;1;PL	want	We want an original take.
choose	V;PRS;1;PL	choose	If we choose to do something on J-Lo, it’s going to be something like that, something less obvious.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	When you are going through the headlines, is it just you guys sitting around trying to crack each other up?
kid	V;PRS;V.PTCP	kidding	Nah, I’m kidding.
imagine	V;NFIN	imagine	It’s actually more businesslike than you’d imagine.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	It’s very much like you are trying to make the room laugh, but the room has been a sort of captive audience for many many years now, so it takes a lot to make the room laugh.
hear	V;PST;V.PTCP	heard	The best analogy I’ve heard is when Rob Siegel, former editor-in-chief, likened it to wine tasting.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	It’s this quiet experience where you are trying to soak in what the joke is, have we done anything like this, is it a unique take, what are other people doing.
fall	V;PRS;V.PTCP	falling	It’s sort of like," Hmmm ... that’s hilarious. That’s really really funny" rather than people falling off their chairs.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	It’s more subdued than I think what most people would expect.
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	Yeah, what you see on Studio 60 and 30 Rock, those are scripted writers rooms.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's a lot of unfunny jokes that are told in a back room, that's why they stay and die in that back room and don’t go out in The Onion.
tell	V;PRS;V.PTCP	telling	If someone is continually telling unfunny jokes, do you eventually fire them?
make	V;NFIN	make	That’s why we are on the 10th floor to make sure they die when they get kicked out.
get	V;PRS;SG;2	get	By the time you get to be a writer for The Onion, though, the odds are you are going to succeed because we make it pretty challenging.
go	V;PRS;SG;2	go	You go through quite a bit.
demonstrate	V;PST;V.PTCP	demonstrated	You will already have demonstrated a pretty long successful record of writing stuff for us before you will be in that room on a daily basis.
wake	V;PRS;SG;3	wakes	But if somebody wakes up one morning and suddenly no longer is funny, then yeah, head first, out the door as quickly as possible, and as sadistically as possible.
take	V;NFIN	take	"The future is only peace. The problem is how long will it take and how many victims will it call for."
think	V;NFIN	think	Mr. President, as a Nobel Peace Prize recipient and as one of the fathers of the modern peace process in Israel, do you still think that there is a future to the peace process?
take	V;NFIN	take	The problem is how long will it take and how many victims will it call for.
say	V;NFIN	say	Why do I say peace?
look	V;PRS;SG;2	look	Because when you look historically, at the development of humanity, most of our lives we are living on the land.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	The history is written with red ink.
fight	V;PRS;V.PTCP	fighting	The reason for it is because people were fighting for our land, either defending it or extending it, because that was the main source.
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	The land, the natural resources, the markets, all these go together.
replace	V;PST;V.PTCP	replaced	The minute the land was replaced by science, what is there to fight about?
conquer	V;NFIN	conquer	Armies cannot conquer science.
check	V;NFIN	check	Customs cannot check what a scientist has in his mind, they can see what he has in his pocket but not what he has in his mind so it’s uncontrolled; it means that borders aren’t important and distances aren’t important.
approach	V;NFIN	approach	How do you approach the difficult challenge of talking to the Palestinians when, in the end, they don’t want Israel to exist.
come	V;NFIN	come	How do you come to an understanding to make peace possible?
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	I mean, is the problem national, say between Jews and Arabs; or is it a matter of generations between an old age and a new age?
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	You see, the terrorists are protesting against modernity.
think	V;PRS;PL;3	think	They think that modernity may endanger their tradition.
hate	V;PRS;PL;3	hate	They are simply afraid and hate modernity.
consider	V;PRS;PL;3	consider	They consider modernity as their enemy, but then they have two problems.
exist	V;NFIN	exist	First of all, can they exist on tradition?
have	V;NFIN	have	Sooner or later they will have to enter the new age.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	All the talks about nationalities, etcetera, well, the new age has very little patience for history.
become	V;PRS;V.PTCP	becoming	History is becoming more and more irrelevant.
feel	V;NFIN	feel	How do you feel about that?
distinguish	V;PRS;SG;1	distinguish	Well, I distinguish between two histories, the spiritual and the material.
grow	V;NFIN	grow	The history of values is okay because wisdom is ageless; it doesn’t grow old, like material.
tell	V;IMP;2	Tell	Tell me, what events is today important such as how many elephants Hannibal had on the Alps, when you can have helicopters?
bother	V;NFIN	bother	Why should I bother my children with all this nonsense?
have	V;NFIN	have	What sort of a nose did Cleopatra have?
know	V;NFIN	know	God, I don’t know!
invite	V;NFIN	invite	You can invite people to war over noses, but nobody will go to fight for noses any more.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	On the other hand, there are already machines that can replace our memory.
bother	V;NFIN	bother	Why should I bother my child with memory when he can buy a computer that will remember everything you asked him to remember?
wane	V;PRS;V.PTCP	waning	The waning importance of history
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Isn’t the answer to that question that wise decisions are made with a basis from memory?
forget	V;IMP;2	Forget	Forget memory.
look	V;NFIN	Look	Look, the new age is unprecedented.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	When something is unprecedented, it means it doesn’t have a past, doesn’t have a history.
orient	V;PST;V.PTCP	oriented	It’s totally oriented on the future.
dwell	V;PRS;SG;3	dwells	And whoever dwells in the past, doesn’t understand the future because the past is full of prejudices, of commitments.
arrest	V;PRS;SG;3	arrests	It arrests us.
say	V;PRS;SG;2	say	And then you say you won’t commit a mistake, so you’ll commit new mistakes.
matter	V;NFIN	matter	It doesn’t matter.
say	V;PRS;SG;1	say	`` I say brains is the greater producer of wealth, not oil.
see	V;NFIN	see	It’s limitless, and you’ll see that the GNP of Israel is very close to the Saudis’.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	So they are 3 times larger than us and they have all the oil in the world.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have brains."
eclipse	V;NFIN	eclipse	— Peres, on whether the Arab states' oil wealth will eclipse Israel's prominence in the region.
forget	V;PRS;PL;3	forget	What about the adage, “Those who forget history are doomed to repeat it”?
make	V;NFIN	make	So they will make new mistakes.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Mistake is inevitable as long as there are human beings.
repeat	V;NFIN	repeat	But you cannot repeat mistakes because the world is not built on repetition; it’s built on mutation.
think	V;NFIN	think	Don’t you think Darfur is repetition?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think Darfur is, again, the last, or among the last battles between old and new.
fight	V;PRS;V.PTCP	fighting	What are they fighting for?
kill	V;PRS;V.PTCP	killing	What are they killing killing killing over?
carry	V;NFIN	carry	They don’t carry futures.
belong	V;PRS;SG;3	belongs	It belongs to a past.
have	V;NFIN	have	It doesn’t have a moment.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	I am sure that the reasons for war are over, even though still there are wars which are an inertia from the past, a continuation that doesn’t make sense.
answer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	answering	I’m answering your question.
enable	V;NFIN	enable	The problem is how to enable the whole world to enter the new future, including the Arabs.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	And there are already Arabs who did it.
look	V;IMP;2	Look	Look at Turkey, who is knocking on the doors of the united Europe.
say	V;PRS;PL;3	say	They say you can be Muslim and modern.
interview	V;PRS;SG;3	interviews	Wikinews interviews Israeli mathematician and writer Aner Shalev
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Aner Shalev is an Israeli mathematician and writer born in 1958 in Kibbutz Kinneret, Tiberias.
interview	V;PST;V.PTCP	interviewed	He was interviewed by Wikinews.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	What are you going to talk about here in Levico?
call	V;PST;SG;1	called	I called it Character, walks and words.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a whole topic which is called asymptotic groups theory.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are groups which express the symmetry of some structures.
study	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Studying	Studying groups is a bit like studying symmetry.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	It's very beautiful I think.
do	V;PRS;SG;2	do	And then when you do it in an asymptotic way is like you don't look at the small details.
look	V;PRS;SG;2	look	You look at the general patterns.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	And there is another topic which is very important in mathematics which is called representation theory.
talk	V;NFIN	talk	So I will mainly talk about how to use a representation theory to solve all kind of problems and conjectures in asymptotic groups theory.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Why did you choose to study mathematics?
show	V;PST;SG;3	showed	When I was ten my father already showed me some stuff in mathematics and the beauty and the imagination was quite apparent.
like	V;PRS;SG;1	like	I also like the freedom in mathematics.
study	V;PRS;SG;2	study	If you study physics or chemistry then you should describe the real world.
build	V;NFIN	build	But in mathematics you can build your own structures.
walk	V;NFIN	walk	You can walk in worlds created by the imagination of people.
commit	V;PST;V.PTCP	committed	You're not committed to the real world.
create	V;NFIN	create	You can create worlds, you can study them.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think it's a combination of the beauty, of the imagination, of the freedom.
dislike	V;PRS;PL;3	dislike	Many people, and many students, dislike math.
think	V;NFIN	think	Why do you think it is so?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	First of all I think maybe they have bad teachers, which is a problem.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	You have to have very good teachers in mathematics if you really want to enjoy and to succeed in it.
think	V;PRS;PL;3	think	Probably some people mainly think about the scientific part of it – you have to be accurate - and they see less these elements of the freedom, and the imagination, and creation.
like	V;NFIN	like	Maybe they don't like the precise nature of mathematics.
talk	V;PRS;SG;1	talk	Also when I talk mathematics I notice the language is very important in mathematics and I actually notice that many times people fail in mathematics because they don't know how to write a composition.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	They have ideas but they cannot formulate them in the right way.
find	V;PRS;SG;1	find	Actually I find many connections between mathematics and literature because almost all the thing I told about mathematics are also seen in literature and in writing because when you write you can describe the real world, but you can also create worlds of fiction.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	So you have imagination, you have total freedom, you can invent characters, you can invent all kind of development and surprises and this kind of end of story or another.
know	V;NFIN	know	So in a way people often don't know how to combine the two fields.
look	V;NFIN	look	It could look like almost the opposite: literature is kind of arts and mathematics is science but they also see a lot in common and there where mathematician who were writers.
write	V;PST;SG;2	wrote	You also wrote two collections of short stories and a novel.
begin	V;NFIN	begin	Why did you begin to write?
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	I was trying to interpret my dreams.
start	V;PST;SG;1	started	I started to write down my dreams.
notice	V;PST;SG;1	noticed	And then I noticed that I cheat a little bit when I write them down.
make	V;PRS;SG;1	make	Sometimes I make them more pretty then they were actually.
document	V;PRS;V.PTCP	documenting	So instead of documenting the dreams it gradually became also like adding creational elements, and I think that somehow through writing my dreams I gradually came to writing, also poems, but mainly stories.
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	I'm a little bit inspired by dreams but when eventually I started to write more seriously I was more interested in structure which also .. a bit like mathematics in a way .. for example my first book is called Opus 1 .. a collection of four long stories with musical structures.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	The first part is called Legato and the second part was called Staccato.
experiment	V;PRS;V.PTCP	experimenting	It was a bit experimenting connections between the language and the music and the psychology of people.
make	V;PST;SG;1	made	In the second book I made another experiment.
call	V;PST;SG;1	called	It's a book of opening, Overtures I called it, and there is no end to the stories.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are seventy beginning of stories without ends.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	In the novel Dark Matters I think what interested me in the structure was two thing: simple narrative and email.
keep	V;PRS;SG;3	keeps	It keeps changing between narrative and email.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	Another thing was two different time scapes: one goes very slow and one goes very fast and they almost kind of meet in the end.
interest	V;PST;SG;3	interested	... love story and romance and surprises and tragedies and all this but also this structure interested me a lot.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Why did you choose this particular structure?
tell	V;PRS;SG;1	tell	When I tell stories I don't like that everything will be clear from the beginning.
like	V;PRS;SG;1	like	I like that something will be a bit of secret, or even unreliable narrator: someone tells something and first you trust him but then you read more and you become thrilled by everything.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think by using emails on the one hand which are more the voice of the woman Eva and narrative which is more the perspective of the man Adam, I kind of confront them, their different perspective and then kind somehow you can see the distortions in the way the story is told.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think that in this kind of structure there is a very gradual clarification of the relationship and the forth going on.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep your eyes peeled for cosmic debris: Andrew Westphal about Stardust @ home
collect	V;PST;SG;3	collected	Stardust is a NASA space capsule that collected samples from comet 81P/Wild (also known as "Wild 2) in deep space and landed back on Earth on January 15, 2006.
decide	V;PST;V.PTCP	decided	It was decided that a collaborative online review process would be used to "discover" the microscopically small samples the capsule collected.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	The project is called Stardust @ home.
distribute	V;PST;V.PTCP	distributed	Unlike distributed computing projects like SETI @ home, Stardust @ home relies entirely on human intelligence.
interview	V;PST;SG;3	interviewed	Wikinews interviewed him for May's Interview of the Month (IOTM) on May 18, 2006.
conduct	V;PST;V.PTCP	conducted	As always, the interview was conducted on IRC, with multiple people asking questions.
know	V;NFIN	know	Some may not know exactly what Stardust or Stardust @ home is.
explain	V;NFIN	explain	Can you explain more about it for us?
launch	V;PST;V.PTCP	launched	Stardust is a NASA Discovery mission that was launched in 1999.
collect	V;NFIN	collect	The primary science goal of the mission was to collect a sample from a known primitive solar-system body, a comet called Wild 2 (pronounced "Vilt-two" — the discoverer was German, I believe).
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	This gives a little context.
mean	V;PRS;SG;1	mean	By "sample return" of course I mean a mission that brings back extraterrestrial material.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	I should have said above that this is the first "solid" sample return mission —
bring	V;PST;SG;3	brought	Genesis brought back a sample from the Sun almost two years ago, but Stardust is also bringing back the first solid samples from the local interstellar medium —
mention	V;NFIN	mention	I should mention parenthetically that there is a fantastic launch video — taken from the POV of the rocket on the JPL Stardust website — highly recommended — best I've ever seen — all the way from the launch pad, too.
see	V;IMP;2	see	Yes [see below].
digress	V;PRS;SG;1	digress	OK, I digress.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	The first challenge that we have before can do any kind of analysis of these interstellar dust particles is simply to find them.
know	V;NFIN	know	This is a big challenge because they are very small (order of micron in size) and are somewhere (we don't know where) on a HUGE collector — at least on the scale of the particle size — about a tenth of a square meter.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	We're right now using an automated microscope that we developed several years ago for nuclear astrophysics work to scan the collector in the Cosmic Dust Lab in Building 31 at Johnson Space Center.
handle	V;PRS;SG;3	handles	This is the ARES group that handles returned samples (Moon Rocks, Genesis chips, Meteorites, and Interplanetary Dust Particles collected by U2 in the stratosphere).
collect	V;PRS;SG;3	collects	The microscope collects stacks of digital images of the aerogel collectors in the array.
send	V;PST;V.PTCP	sent	These images are sent to us — we compress them and convert them into a format appropriate for Stardust @ home.
distribute	V;PST;V.PTCP	distributed	Stardust @ home is a highly distributed project using a "Virtual Microscope" that is written in html and javascript and runs on most browsers — no downloads are required.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using the Virtual Microscope volunteers can search over the collector for the tracks of the interstellar dust particles.
remove	V;PST;V.PTCP	removed	Aerogel slice removed with an ultrasonic blade, showing particle tracks.
anticipate	V;NFIN	anticipate	How many samples do you anticipate being found during the course of the project?
know	V;NFIN	know	The short answer is that we don't know.
know	V;PRS;1;PL	know	Here's what we know.
carry	V;PST;SG;3	carried	The Galileo and Ulysses spacecraft carried dust detectors onboard that Eberhard Gruen and his colleagues used to first detect and them measure the flux of interstellar dust particles streaming into the solar system.
cause	V;PST;V.PTCP	caused	(This is a kind of "wind" of interstellar dust, caused by the fact that our solar system is moving with respect to the local interstellar medium.)
estimate	V;PST;V.PTCP	estimated	Markus Landgraf has estimated the number of interstellar dust particles that should have been captured by Stardust during two periods of the "cruise" phase of the interplanetary orbit in which the spacecraft was moving with this wind.
estimate	V;PST;SG;3	estimated	He estimated that there should be around 45 particles, but this number is very uncertain — I wouldn't be surprised if it is quite different from that.
say	V;NFIN	say	One thing that I should say ... is that like all research, the outcome of what we are doing is highly uncertain.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a wonderful quote attributed to Einstein — "If we knew what we were doing, it wouldn't be called "research", would it?"
expect	V;PRS;1;PL	expect	We expect that the particles will be of order a micron in size.
search	V;PRS;V.PTCP	searching	When people are searching using the virtual microscope, they will be looking not for the particles, but for the tracks that the particles make, which are much larger — several microns in diameter.
switch	V;PST;1;PL	switched	Just yesterday we switched over to a new site which has a demo of the VM (virtual microscope) I invite you to check it out.
shoot	V;PST;V.PTCP	shot	The tracks in the demo are from submicron carbonyl iron particles that were shot into aerogel using a particle accelerator modified to accelerate dust particles to very high speeds, to simulate the interstellar dust impacts that we're looking for.
see	V;IMP;2	see	And that's on the main Stardust @ home website [see below]?
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	I'm not taking time for a real letter, but am venturing to send back by Roy Stinebower copies of two carbons of letters to my father.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	After a rather leisurely start, the conference has got well under way with a hurry, and everybody is keeping busy.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	Lionel and Bert and I all have lots to do.
specialize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	specializing	He is specializing more or less on Trieste, whil Bert and I cover pretty much everything else.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	Have been thinking of all of youpeople in LE, and want to be remembered to my friends.
try	V;NFIN;2	try	Will try to write a real letter before long.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	The Conference seems much busier now than it was a couple of weeks ago; after doing nothing for so long a time, they are getting down to work in the several commissions.
schedule	V;PST;V.PTCP	scheduled	In addition to the regularly scheduled commissions, there are also some meetings going on of the four Drafting Powers, in an effort to avoid long delays (especially after the completion of the Paris Conference itself).
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	While I've been pretty busy, there has still been occasional time I could get off for one thing or another.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	Last night several of us went to l' Opera to hear the Flying Dutchman, or Vaisseau Phantome, as it is called here.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Although none of the singers were particularly well-known, at least to me, the performance was good.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	I thought the stage setting particularly well done, with the one boat on the stage in the foreground and the other shown on a screen by shadow; the ghost ship made its proper disappearance, etc., in silhouette.
fill	V;PST;V.PTCP	filled	The house was filled, but not with the same sort of audience as had attended the ballet in honor of the opening of the conference last July.
have	V;PST;SG;1	had	On several occasions last week I had lunch at the Luxembourg between m etings, and then was able to walk back in the latter part of the afternoon.
serve	V;PST;V.PTCP	served	The meals served in a special restaurant for the delegates are good, and there is sometimes rather interesting company.
eat	V;PST;1;PL	ate	OnThurday Bert and I atethere with M. Shaganon, the secretary of the Legal and Drafting Commission, and found it a good opportunity to practice French, for he is one of the few around who does not insist on speaking English.
schedule	V;PST;V.PTCP	scheduled	Friday morning there was scheduled a meeting of a subcommission to draft the exact description of the French-Italian frontier in places where Italy makes smsll grants to France.
go	V;PST;1;PL	went	Thursday evening Bert and I went over the description with Otto Guthe, our geographer, on a series of maps at abaut 3 inches to a mile, rather interesting in mountain country.
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	Then on Friday the meeting was not held, after all, so we walked back from the Luxembourg.
turn	V;PST;V.PTCP	turned	As it had turned off a sunny morning, we detoured somewhat, and visited the old church of St. Severin on the left bank, and then the little church of St. Julien le Pauvre, just across the river from Notre Dame and used by the students and faculties as the University Church back in the twelfth to sixteenth centuries.
look	V;PST;1;PL	looked	We looked around in Notre Dame a while, getting good light on he stained glass windows, and also revisited the Sainte Chapelle.
stain	V;PST;V.PTCP	staind	All of the thirteenth century staind glass windows on the south side are now back in, and they are replacing those on the north side (removed, of course, for safety at the start of the war).
celebrate	V;PST;SG;3	celebrated	Friday was Jack Reinstein's birthday, and a large number of the delegation celebrated it with a cocktail party.
know	V;NFIN	know	I don't know about the quality of the drinks, but the sandwiches and nuts prepared by some from the PX supplies went very well.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Several of us had enough in the way of sandwiches so that we did not want a regular dinner afterwards, and therefore finished off at the PX Sugarbowl with American milkshakes and sundaes.
get	V;PRS;SG;3	gets	It is rather interesting how one gets to depend on the various army facilities like the PX for its weekly supplies and the Sugarbowl as almost the only source of milk products (the French "glaces" being very obviously water ices rather than icecreams).
rain	V;PRS;SG;3	rains	The weather has been better the last few days, though it usually rains at least once a day.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	On Thursday morning we had a 9 A.M. meeting at the Luxembourg, and ev yyone going over in the car spoke of how it seemed like the start of school in the fall, with a hazy Indian summer type of sunshine, and temperatures around 60.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	Today is partly clear, and I hope I may be able to get out somewhere after the meeting which begins at 11 and will r until about 2.
go	V;PST;V.PTCP	gone	I have gone over for one meeting thismorning, and found it cancelled.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	We have lost much -- too much, really, to list.
lie	V;PRS;SG;3	lies	The task that lies ahead -- to put Rapid City and the area back together -- will not be done for years.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	But it must be done.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think of the letter from a lady which said, "It is only as the days pass that we are beginning to realize what we have lost.
make	V;NFIN	make	Estimates to make our home livable range around $10,000, and this does not include such items as furniture, antiques, and loving work we have done steadily since buying the house.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	Also, we still have approximately $17,100 left on our mortgage.
do	V;NFIN	do	Unfortunately, the present Disaster Relief Act does not do the job for private homeowners and businessmen who saw their property destroyed by the flood.
face	V;PRS;V.PTCP	facing	For those facing this situation all the present law offers is a loan at 5 1/8% interest to try to rebuild.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	The best way, and I believe the fairest way, to help families and businesses who lost everything in the flood is to give them flat cash grants to rebuild.
investigate	V;PST;SG;1	investigated	I investigated this possibility carefully and found the unanimous feeling that legislation to give flat cash grants could never pass the Congress.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	I could have introduced a bill to provide grants even though it could not have passed, but I feel very strongly that to have done this would have simply raised false hopes where real help is what is needed.
decide	V;PST;SG;1	decided	For this reason I decided to introduce legislation that would lower interest rates on rebuilding loans and provide that a major portion of these loans would not have to be repaid.
win	V;PRS;V.PTCP	winning	Very frankly, winning passage of the entire package of flood relief bills I have sponsored will be an extremely difficult task.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	On the other hand, when these bills were introduced most Congressmen felt that no new legislation could be passed.
pass	V;PST;V.PTCP	passed	As of today, the House has passed one of my bills, and prospects for at least some helpful new legislation are good.
affect	V;PST;V.PTCP	affected	Because it is important that people affected by the flood disaster know exactly what new laws have been proposed, how these would affect them, and what the chances for getting these new laws really is, I would like to give you a hard, realistic appraisal of what we are trying to do, and how likely it is that we will succeed.
lower	V;NFIN	lower	1. Legislation to lower the interest rate on SBA loans:
affect	V;PST;V.PTCP	affected	SBA loans are presently available to businessmen and homeowners affected by the flood.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	They are given at 5 1/8% interest.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	The person making the loan must repay all but $2,500 of the loan and pay the 5 1/8% interest rate as well.
introduce	V;PST;SG;1	introduced	I introduced a bill to lower the interest rate on these loans to 1%.
rewrote	V;PST;SG;3	rewrote	The House Committee rewrote my bill to provide for 3% interest if the person repays the full loan, or 1% interest if he chooses to not repay $ 2,500 of the loan.
pass	V;PST;SG;3	passed	The bill then passed the House in this slightly weakened form.
permit	V;NFIN	permit	The bill would be retroactive to before the flood and would permit people who have made SBA loans at 5 1/8% interest to renegotiate those loans at the new lower interest rate.
pass	V;NFIN	pass	The chances that the Senate will pass this bill this summer are good.
increase	V;NFIN	increase	2. Legislation to increase the amount of a loan which does not have to be repaid:
repaid	V;PST;V.PTCP	repaid	Disaster loans must presently be fully repaid except for $ 2,500 which is forgiven.
take	V;PRS;SG;2	take	Thus, if you take a $ 20,000 loan to rebuild, you must repay $ 17,500 of that loan.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	I have introduced a bill which would forgive 90% of the amount of any disaster loan.
pass	V;PRS;SG;3	passes	Thus, if this bill passes, a person making a $ 20,000 loan to rebuild would have to repay only $2,000 of that amount.
pass	V;PRS;SG;3	passes	Again, the provisions of this bill, if it passes, will be available to people who have made loans under the present law.
need	V;PRS;1;PL	need	This is the bill which we need most badly.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	It would provide loans on such generous terms that nearly everyone could afford to rebuild.
pass	V;NFIN	pass	Frankly, this is also the bill which will be most difficult to pass.
open	V;NFIN	open	The House Public Works Subcommittee will open hearings on this bill in July.
increase	V;PRS;V.PTCP	increasing	I am hopeful that some legislation increasing the amount of a disaster loan which will not have to be repaid (will be "forgiven") can pass.
rate	V;NFIN	rate	But I would rate the chances of passing my 90% forgiveness bill as poor.
pass	V;PRS;V.PTCP	passing	The chances of passing some increases in the amount to be forgiven on a loan are probably fair to good.
rebuild	V;NFIN	rebuild	3. Legislation to rebuild public recreation areas:
restore	V;PST;V.PTCP	restored	Under the present law all public facilities except recreation areas will be restored by the Federal Government.
feel	V;PRS;SG;1	feel	I feel that recreation facilities, which are vital to our tourist oriented economy, should also be restored with Federal help.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	I have introduced a bill that would permit this restoration.
rate	V;NFIN	rate	I would rate chances for its passage as fair to good.
give	V;NFIN	give	This, then, is the most realistic assessment I can give of my legislative efforts to help.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	I wish I could be more optimistic, but, in fairness, I cannot.
promise	V;NFIN	promise	All I can promise is that I will push just as hard as I know how for new laws to help in every way possible.
help	V;NFIN	help	One way you might help me is to look over the laws I have proposed and write me explaining in detail how they would help you or people you know.
need	V;PRS;1;PL	need	We need specific examples of what these laws would mean to the people of Rapid City.
go	V;PRS;1;PL	go	These examples will be our best argument when we go before the Committees and the Congress.
like	V;NFIN	like	In return for your help I would like to remind you that my job, and that of my office, is to help.
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	Many of you have already written describing problems you are having with Federal agencies as a result of the flood.
urge	V;NFIN	urge	I cannot urge you too strongly to let me know whenever you feel there is anything at all I might do to assist.
reach	V;NFIN	reach	You can reach me at: 508 Cannon Office Building, Washington, D.C. 20515.
build	V;PRS;1;PL	build	The things we build -- homes, businesses, communities -- can be destroyed, true, but the human spirit is indomitable.
hit	V;PST;V.PTCP	hit	The people hit by this flood have shown that spirit, and shown it high and wide.
stay	V;IMP;2	Stay	Stay in touch,
print	V;PST;V.PTCP	printed	(Not printed at Government expense)
enhance	V;NFIN	enhance	The movement of the Soviet Union to a market economy will greatly enhance the prosperity of your citizens.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	Your economists have learned much from the experience of nations with economies based in varying degrees on free markets.
undertake	V;PST;V.PTCP	undertaken	Your plans for freely convertible currency, free trade, and enterprises undertaken and managed by individuals who receive the profit or bear the losses that result from their decisions are all highly commendable.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	But there is a danger that you will adopt features of our economies that keep us from being as prosperous as we might be.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	In particular, there is a danger that you may follow us in allowing most of the rent of land to be collected privately.
retain	V;PST;V.PTCP	retained	It is important that the rent of land be retained as a source of government revenue.
collect	V;PRS;PL;3	collect	While the governments of developed nations with market economies collect some of the rent of land in taxes, they do not collect nearly as much as they could, and they therefore make unnecessarily great use of taxes that impede their economies-- taxes on such things as incomes, sales and the value of capital.
serve	V;PRS;SG;3	serves	Social collection of the rent of land and natural resources serves three purposes.
guarantee	V;PRS;SG;3	guarantees	First, it guarantees that no one dispossesses fellow citizens by obtaining a disproportionate share of what nature provides for humanity.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Second, it provides revenue with which governments can pay for socially valuable activities without discouraging capital formation or work effort, or interfering in other ways with the efficient allocation of resources.
result	V;PRS;V.PTCP	resulting	Third, the resulting revenue permits utility and other services that have marked economies of scale or density to be priced at levels conducive to their efficient use.
arise	V;PRS;SG;3	arises	The rental value of land arises from three sources.
combine	V;PST;V.PTCP	combined	The first is the inherent natural productivity of land, combined with the fact that land is limited.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	All citizens have equal claims on the component of land value that arises from nature.
arise	V;PRS;SG;3	arises	The component of land value that arises from community growth and provision of services is the most sensible source of revenue for financing public services that raise the rental value of surrounding land.
include	V;PRS;PL;3	include	These services include roads, urban transit networks, parks, and public utility networks for such services as electricity, telephones, water and sewers.
strive	V;NFIN	strive	A public revenue system should strive to collect as much of the rent of land as possible, allocating the part of rent derived from nature to all citizens equally, and the part derived from public services to the governmental units that provide those services.
collect	V;PRS;PL;3	collect	When governments collect the increase in land value that results from the provision of services, they are able to offer services at prices that represent the marginal social cost of these services, promoting efficient use of the services and enhancing the rental value of the land where the services are available.
use	V;PRS;PL;3	use	Government agencies that use land should be charged the same rentals as others for the land they use, or services will not be adequately financed and agencies will not have adequate incentive or guidance for economizing on their use of land.
tempt	V;PST;V.PTCP	tempted	Some economists might be tempted to suggest that the rent can be collected publicly simply by selling land outright at auction.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are a number of reasons why this is not a good idea.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	First, there is so much land to be turned over to private management that any effort to dispose of all of it in a short period would result in an extreme depression in prices offered.
make	V;NFIN	make	Second, some persons who could make excellent use of land would be unable to raise money for the purchase price.
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Collecting	Collecting rent annually provides access to land for persons with limited access to credit.
enable	V;NFIN	enable	Third, subsequent resale of land would enable speculators to make large profits unrelated to any productive services they offer, resulting in needless inequity and dissatisfaction.
tend	V;NFIN	tend	Fourth, concern about future political conditions would tend to depress offers.
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Collecting	Collecting rent annually permits the citizens of future years to capture the benefits of good future public policies.
tend	V;PRS;PL;3	tend	Fifth, because investors tend to be averse to risk, general uncertainty about the future will tend to depress offers.
sidestepp	V;PST;V.PTCP	sidestepped	This risk aversion is sidestepped by allowing future rental payments to be determined by future conditions.
claim	V;PST;V.PTCP	claimed	Finally, the future rent of land can more justly be claimed by future generations than by today's citizens.
require	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Requiring	Requiring annual payments from the users of land allows each year's population to claim that year's rent.
invest	V;PST;V.PTCP	invested	While the proceeds of sales could be invested for the benefit of future generations, not collecting the money in advance guarantees the heritage of the future against political excesses.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	The attached Appendix provides a brief technical discussion of issues of the duration of rights to use land, the transfer of land, the assessment of land, social protection against the abuse and subsequent abandonment of run-down property, and redistribution among localities to adjust for differences in natural per capita endowments.
need	V;PRS;PL;3	need	While these issues need to be addressed, none of them present any insoluble problems.
keep	V;PST;V.PTCP	kept	A balance should be kept between allowing the managers of property to retain value derived from their own efforts to maintain and improve property, and securing for public use the naturally inherent and socially created value of land.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	Users of land should not be allowed to acquire rights of indefinite duration for single payments.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	For efficiency, for adequate revenue and for justice, every user of land should be required to make an annual payment to the local government, equal to the current rental value of the land that he or she prevents others from using.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	sure enough we had creative days these past two days.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Frankly I think it was one of their biggest mistakes; to make them be so soon after vacation.
catch	V;NFIN	catch	I wasn't creative at all, but did catch up on my sleep.
forget	V;PST;SG;1	forgot	I forgot to get two more checks from you.
arrive	V;PST;SG;3	arrived	The grapefruit apparently arrived before vacation, but smart Mrs. Bonde put them in the big kitchen refrig.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	I've already had 5 out of the 9.
wish	V;PRS;PL;3	wish	wish i could have more?!
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	I've been thinking that $ 20 - $ 25 per month would be fine.
give	V;NFIN	give	Why don't you just give me $ 130 in a lump or send me a monthly check.
prefer	V;PRS;SG;1	prefer	I prefer the latter.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We had discussion groups about the hand book and certain situations.
wish	V;PRS;SG;1	wish	I really wish they'd waited for the creative days; in the middle of Feb. I would have been ready for them, and would have done something very creative.
do	V;PST;SG;1	did	I did the picture of the family and I'm so mad ‘cuz only 1 came out.
understand	V	under	I can't under stand it.
know	V;NFIN	know	I still don't know how to work that camera.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It all has to do with the speed and shutter settings.
get	V;NFIN	get	Hopefully I'll get more on the next roll.
believe	V;NFIN	believe	I can't believe we've got achievements. today.
hear	V;PST;SG;1	heard	I heard from Adelle that already 25 have been accepted at Wesleyan, that leaves 75 more, I already know of 6 kids ten times more competent than I, who are applying.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	gotta go now.
give	V;IMP;2	give	give my love to all, say "hullo" to Wila.
love	V;PRS;PL;3	Love	Love ya.
send	V;PST;V.PTCP	sent	P.S. Slacks are on the way, sent yesterday.
start	V;PST;SG;1	started	I just started my Eng. project on Sunday and have been working like mad to get some or all of it done.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	Academic probabition list came out yesterday, I'm not on it.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	gotten	So far in Math I've gotten a B & B- and we had a take home test Tues. nite.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	After working so hard and long on it, I hope to get a good mark.
do	V;PST;SG;1	did	I did a French comp. the other nite.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Arosa said it was terrible, I just can’t write comps., so I’ve got a confer. this after.
get	V;PST;SG;1	got	Oh crum, I just got back my Math test and got a C.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	I'm so mad, after working so hard, I could scream!
ask	V;PST;SG;1	asked	I asked the guy, who really helped me at the Hum. Relations Confer, to the Prom.
think	V;NFIN	think	I really am Crazy, I don't think Mike will come, but I hope so.
ask	V;NFIN	ask	If he doesn't then I'll probably ask Dan Kent or Craig Ashley.
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	I'm supposed to go to the Winter Carnival this Sat. with Ed Davison, but haven't seen or heard from him since Sunday when he walked me back from Vespers.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	I said, "Hey, write sometime" & he said, “Yeah, sometime.".
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	I've got to find out what's coming off, because he said if he got back from his swimming meet at a reasonable hour then we'd go.
go	V;PST;SG;1	went	I went to the Catholic mass, yesterday morning.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Sue said since she was getting up & would probably wake me, would I like to go.
get	V;PST;1;PL	got	We got up at 5:55 and stumbled into our clothes.
hurry	V;PST;1;PL	hurried	We and 2 other girls hurried to church by 6:30.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	I was completely lost 'cuz the service wasn't in the Misalette.
get	V;PST;1;PL	got	But we all got our ashes.
stay	V;PST;PL;3	stayed	Mine were so dark and they stayed on till I washed my face last nite late.
remark	V;PST;PL;3	remarked	It's funny how so many people remarked, "How Oh what a good girl you are, you went to church."
notice	V;PST;1;PL	noticed	On the way back Sue Cleveland & I noticed cars at the Epis. church so we decided to have commonion.
get	V;PST;1;PL	got	We got back to school at 7:40 and didn't have breakfast.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	I've given up all extras & fattening foods for lent.
starve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	starving	Oh, I'm starving!
write	V;IMP;2	write	Please write soon.
get	V;PST;SG;3	Got	Got your note today, many thanks, it was good to hear from you.
do	V;NFIN	do	What did you do with duck?
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	What am I going to do about that coed school in the north?
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There aren’t very many good ones around, (that I could get into).
send	V;NFIN	send	We can’t send stuff UPS because it’s out of Boston and for some reason it’s too much trouble.
send	V;PST;SG;1	sent	So ^ Today I sent a box of stuff home regular (parcel post) mail.
put	V;PST;SG;3	put	Mrs. B put the postage on (80 ¢) but I just know it’s not going to be enough.
start	V;PRS;PL;3	start	My exams start on Monday, Eng, French, then Math (M.-Wed)
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	Icky news for me – Brendan (my love of this month) is going to Antioch and they start June 30.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	He’s trying to go the next session; Sept 29th, but won’t hear for another week or two.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	I’m going to keep my fingers crossed, I was really planning on seeing him this summer.
hate	V;PRS;SG;1	hate	I hate to ask this, but … could you send me a measly five bucks (only 5 smackers) so I can get home.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	At this point I have just barely enough to get home, but I want to get something for Mlle. Arosa and then as always there’ll be other petty expenses.
tell	V;IMP;2	tell	Also tell Nance to cash the check if she hasn’t already, it’s fouling me up.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	I hope it stays nice, so I can study all morning outside.
say	V;IMP;2	Say	Say “hello” to all for me.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See you soon!
spend	V;PST;SG;1	spent	I spent the entire day at picnics + beach parties at P.A., but I'll try to make a little bit of sense.
meet	V;PRS;V.PTCP	meeting	My First experiance with the 4-room (as it was this yr.) was meeting my ROOMATES RUTH RASER, SALLY BROWNING AND karen HO, FROM Schenectady N.Y., MYSTIC CONN. and HONG-KONG respectively.
go	V;PST;PL;3	went	Things went on well for a while, but it takes some getting used to.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Then around November Ruth had to move to a single because of bad grades- that's one thing about this room, it can really hurt your grades - she moved upstairs to the 3rd floor, where there are 3 singles so the four-room became a 3 room for a while, until January, when the 3rd floor was condemned by some shitty building inspector.
live	V;PST;V.PTCP	lived	Dory Streett who had lived upstairs in a single became my new commate.
exist	V;PST;V.PTCP	existed	We've all existed so far but we've been damn lucky, and have also worked damned hard.
talk	V;NFIN;2	talk	First of all don't talk back to Mrs. G. and you'll have a good year.
have	V;NFIN	have	Adele, the maid, is so dumb that you'll have loads of fun just giving her gas.
carry	V;PRS;PL;3	carry	voices from the 4-room carry a lot farther than you think- you can tell this by listening from the bathroom.
worry	V;NFIN	worry	So be quiet, but don't worry - Mrs. G never reports you to Core for being noisy or staying up, only for things like cutting Church or required crap.
take	V;PRS;PL;3	take	Unless they take them out this summer, you will notice 2 hooks in the ceiling of the outer porch.
use	V;PST;1;PL	used	Sally + I, whose beds were out on the porch used them for hanging flashlights over our beds for studying (just tie them to used nylons or SOMEthing)
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	IF you have A SMALL STUDY LAMP you DO N'T EVEN NEED TO WORRY ABOUT THIS.
report	V;PST;V.PTCP	REPORTED	THE ONLY DANGER ABOUT AFTER LIGHTS STUDYING IS BEING REPORTED BY THE VICTOR OR PINKERTON GUARDS WHO PATROL THE CAMPUS TO PROTECT US FROM "TOWNIES"
leave	V;IMP;2	LEAVE	NEVER EVER LEAVE THE WINDOWS OPEN DURING SPRING TERM without SCREENS, or you'll find a BEE 'S NEST IN YOUR BED LIKE SALLY DID THIS SPRING.
bitch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	BITCHING	WELL, ONE of MY ROOMATES is BITCHING LIKE CRAZY 'CAUSE she's "TIRED," so I CA N'T GO ON MUCH LONGER - WE 'RE HAVING A PARTY IN HERE TOMORROW NIGHT (The last night of school) so I guess I should try to get to sleep tonight too.
remember	V;IMP;2	remember	so just remember to give this room all you can and remember that it can't be all that bad.
have	V;IMP;2	Have	Have a great 4-room experience and a fantastic prep year- it's supposed to be the best year to have, so TAKE ADVANTAGE.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	TRYING	I 'M TRYING FOR FRENCH HOUSE NEXT YEAR, AND I HOPE I'll make it, cause I hate ABBEY HOUSE.
hate	V;PRS;SG;1	HATE	(I ALSO HATE MRS. ABBOT, HOUSEMOTHER IN FRENCH HOUSE, BUT THAT 'S LIFE)
say	V;PRS;SG;3	SAYS	So, LIKE EVERYBODY SAYS, COME ON UP, AND VISIT, I 'M ROOMING with RUTH AGAIN
get	V;PRS;PL;3	GET	IF THINGS GET DESPERATE, I 'M WILLING TO SELL MY GREEK + ROMAN HISTORY NOTEBOOK CONTAINING SECTIONS AND MAPS + NOTES FOR A SUITABLE PRICE.
consider	V;IMP;2	CONSIDER	CONSIDER THIS OFFER LATER WHEN MISS MUCK DEMANDS 30 SECTIONS IN ONE NIGHT.
get	V;PST;SG;1	GOT	(I GOT AN A- and a B!)
fix	V;PRS;V.PTCP	fixing	I am fixing up my new home here.
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	set	It is a one-room house, but a large room, and set in two blocks of grounds with an artesian well.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have to do some phoneering, but I find that I like it.
like	V;PRS;SG;3	likes	I am the happiest I have been in the last ten years, irregardles of whether Scribners likes the novel or not.
chop	V;PRS;V.PTCP	chopping	I am up every morning at five oclock chopping down weeds and planting flowers and things.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	That is why I have been so long getting to my machine to write letters.
go	V;PRS;SG;1	go	I go to bed happily tired and swear that I will write you a letter first thing in the morning.
feed	V;PRS;SG;1	feed	But the birds, which I feed and who have begun to collect here already in large numbers, wake me up clamoring for their breakfast, and I dash out and place stale bread, etc. and watch the many colors and many behaviors of my feathered friends.
light	V;PST;SG;3	lit	Less than an hour ago, a male cardinal lit on the porch no more than five feet from me and complained that there was no more food outside, so I hurried to put some out.
open	V;PST;SG;1	opened	Then just as I opened up the machine, a great conclave of birds, led by two crows, screamed to me that some enemy was raiding a mocking-bird nest in a big oak tree, and I rushed over to find a lean and desperate cat climbing the tree, and chunked him clear off the place.
love	V;PRS;PL;3	love	Spot, and her daughter, Shag, love it here.
show	V;PRS;PL;3	show	They show it in many ways.
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	They go hunting, and so far, have scared up a cat, two rabbits and an armadilla.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	It seems that t ey are plentiful around here.
lose	V;PRS;V.PTCP	losing	From all that hoeing and what not, I am losing weight, God be praised!
take	V;PRS;SG;1	take	This day, I take out my notes and go back to work with new vigor and a clean-swept mind.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	belidve	I belidve that I can do it, and I'm giving it a very serious try this time.
have	V;NFIN	have	You will have assorted scripts in your hand very shortly.
question	V	questi	Now, you p rhaps questi n why I am putting so much into this place where I now live.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	I have a chance to buy it.
write	V;PST;SG;1	wrote	In this little house I wrote MULES AND MEN.
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	Since that time, it has come to be in a White residential neighborhood.
grow	V;PST;V.PTCP	grown	Eau Gallie has grown with the Jet Base, and not far away, near Cocoa, the Guided Missile Base, and many people stationed here are buying and building.
go	V;NFIN	go	He must go slow about selling it to me, waiting on public reaction.
begin	V;PRS;V.PTCP	beginning	It is beginning to be favorable to me.
stop	V;PST;V.PTCP	stopped	Several of my neighbors have stopped in the last week and remarked on the improvements I have made.
look	V;PST;SG;3	looked	It looked like a jungle three weeks ago, and it took a strong heart and an eye on the future for me to move in when I arrived.
run	V;PST;V.PTCP	run	The place had run down so badly.
mean	V;PST;V.PTCP	meant	In what was meant to be a compliment, I have been told twice, "You don't live like the majority of your people. You like things clean and orderly around you."
see	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeing	The manager of the local dairy, seeing me setting out flowers as I cleared, surprised me with a load of compost as a present Saturday.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	The grounds have great possibilities, and I really would love to own it.
remove	V;NFIN	remove	I still must remove tons of junk, old tin cans and bottles from the premises.
feel	V;PRS;SG;3	feels	Somehow, this one spot on earth feels like home to me.
intend	V;PST;V.PTCP	intended	I have always intended to come back here.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	That is why I am doing so much to make a go of it.
play	V;PRS;V.PTCP	playing	No loud radios and record-playing to irritate me, and great oaks and palms around the place.
hope	V;NFIN	hope	I do hope that other person that Burroughs Mitchell had reading my script was not Roy Ottley.
see	V;NFIN	see	You can see why I would not want a Negro writer to be handling it.
think	V;PST;V.PTCP	thought	Since none of the others have thought to do anything with the hair-traightening business, it might prove a temptation to play my work down to Burroughs, then do something on it himself.
check	V;IMP;2	check	Please check on that for me.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	The Saturday Evening Post photographer has been here and took a lot of pictures.
catch	V;PST;SG;3	caught	He caught me in my dungarees working outdoors, and naturally insisted on photographing me like that.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	He told me that Ben Hibbs likes the article very much and it will be in an early issue.
gather	V;PRS;SG;1	gather	I gather from reading the magazine that the SEP favors Taft, and so it probably falls right in with the plan.
seem	V	se	They never se m to miss a chance recently to mention him favorably.
get	V;NFIN	get	As soon as I can get some things in your hands to sell, I shall try to raise the cash for my newspaper devoted to those in domestic service.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I really believe that the idea is sound and can makke money. [...]
pay	V;PST;SG;1	paid	I paid up my sick and accident insurance for a year, $ 67.70, and spent so much getting the house wired and made more livable in other ways around the place, that I am almost broke already again.
buy	V;PST;SG;3	Bought	Bought an ice-box, and furniture too, so now, as badly as I hate to draw it, I need some cash to live on.
go	V;NFIN	go	And a pair of black slacks so that I can go downtown without messing up another was dress which takes up my time to launder.
pray	V;NFIN	pray	My best to you and yours, and pray that we make a lot of money this year, so that when/if I am told that I can buy this place, I can do it and build a comfortable little new house on it.
honor	V;PST;V.PTCP	honored	After visa snags, all-girl Afghan team honored for 'courageous achievement' at international robotics competition
award	V;PST;V.PTCP	awarded	A group of Afghan teenagers was awarded a silver medal for "courageous achievement" on Tuesday in the FIRST Global Challenge Robot Olympics in the Daughters of the American Revolution Constitution Hall in Washington D.C.
consist	V;PRS;SG;3	consists	The six - member Afghan team, which consists entirely of girls, had been denied visas to enter the United States until last week, drawing notice from critics of U.S. President Donald Trump's policies regarding Muslims traveling to the U.S.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	The President later personally took action to allow the team into the country.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to Afghan Ambassador to the United States Hamdullah Mohib, the participation of this team in this science competition shows progress in Afghanistan's response to nearly two decades of military conflict.
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	The first FIRST Global robotics competition was held this week in Washington D.C.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Image: United States Navy.
found	V;PST;V.PTCP	founded	The FIRST Global competition (For Inspiration and Recognition of Science and Technlogy) was founded by Dean Kamen to encourage interest in applied science, technology, engineering, and mathematics in young people.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	For the contest, the robots were required to recognize, pick up, carry, and deposit balls colored blue and orange to represent water and pollution, respectively.
arrange	V;PST;V.PTCP	arranged	Teams from different countries were arranged in groups of three, and groups faced off in pairs.
earn	V;PST;PL;3	earned	The group whose three robots collectively earned the most points won that match.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to the Afghan team's coach, Alireza Mehraban, in their first matchup, the Afghan team's robot, named "Better Idea of Afghan Girls," scored one or two points for the three-nation alliance with the Gabonese and Estonian teams.
win	V;PST;SG;3	won	This year, the European team won the competition overall, with the silver medal going to Poland and the bronze to Armenia.
find	V;PRS;1;PL	find	"We are so interested because we find a big chance to show the talent and ability of Afghans," team member Rodaba Noori told the press, "to show that Afghan women can make robots too and we can improve in this section — [the] robotics section.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We were so interested because we hadn't long or enough time to get ready for competition but we came to United States and now we are here because of Trump intervention."
arrive	V;PST;SG;3	arrived	The team arrived in Washington D.C. after many difficulties.
deny	V;PST;V.PTCP	denied	They were twice denied visas to enter the United States.
apply	V;PRS;V.PTCP	applying	Even applying for a visa required the entire team to travel about 500 miles (800 km) from Herat, their home, to the capital in Kabul, through territory controlled by the Taliban.
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	They made the trip twice and were turned down both times.
ask	V;PST;SG;3	asked	President Donald Trump personally asked the United States National Security Council to help the girls enter the country.
grant	V;PST;V.PTCP	granted	After some interplay between the State and Homeland Security Departments, the girls were granted "parole" status on the grounds that their entry would be a "significant public benefit."
draw	V;PST;V.PTCP	drawn	The President has drawn both praise for helping the girls enter the U.S. and criticism for his travel ban, which does not cover Afghanistan, and for the atmosphere of hostility toward Muslims that critics blame for denying them entry in the first place.
cite	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Citing	Citing privacy rules, U.S. officials have not said why the team was initially denied visas, but Afghan Ambassador Hamdullah Mohib says there were concerns they would attempt to remain in the United States or in Canada after the contest.
cover	V;PST;V.PTCP	covered	Afghanistan is not one of the nations covered by President Trump's travel ban, but teams from Sudan, Iran, Libya, and Yemen, which are covered, are among the 163 teams in the competition.
be	V;PST;PL;3	were	There were also two Syrian teams scheduled to compete, one national and one made up of refugees.
cover	V;PST;V.PTCP	covered	Syria and all refugees are covered by the ban.
list	V;PST;SG;3	listed	The Syrian refugee team listed their country as "Hope" on the backs of their shirts and named their robot "Robogee."
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	Teams from Morocco and Iran both had difficulties getting themselves or their robots across the U.S. border.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Team Estonia had to rebuild their robot entirely after the original disappeared in transit.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	"Seventeen years ago, this would not have been possible at all," said Ambassador Mohib.
represent	V;PRS;PL;3	represent	"They represent our aspirations and resilience despite having been brought up in a perpetual conflict.
prove	V;PRS;V.PTCP	proving	These girls will be proving to the world and the nation that nothing will prevent us from being an equal and active member of the international community."
send	V;PST;V.PTCP	sent	Every team in the contest was sent a collection of robot parts roughly four months in advance of the competition.
delay	V;PST;V.PTCP	delayed	The Afghan team's supplies were delayed, leaving them only two weeks left to assemble their robot and only one day to test it before packing it up to mail to the competition site.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The competition ended on Tuesday.
plan	V;PRS;SG;3	plans	This is the first FIRST Global competition, and the organization plans to hold the contest in a different country every year.
schedule	V;PST;V.PTCP	scheduled	Next year's competition is scheduled to take place in Mexico City.
rescue	V;PST;V.PTCP	rescued	Over 900 asylum seekers rescued off Indonesian coast
rescue	V;PST;PL;3	rescued	On Friday, fishermen rescued over 700 asylum seekers whose boat sank, and the Indonesian Navy reportedly saved 200 more after they were found swimming along the coast of Aceh, Indonesia.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Major general Fuad Basya, spokesman for the Indonesian military, said fisherman first noticed the people and a warship was deployed to retrieve them.
rescue	V;PST;V.PTCP	rescued	The rescued members included Bangladeshis and Rohingya, a stateless minority of Muslims from Myanmar.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Myanmar is mainly Buddhist and the United Nations rates the Rohingya among the world's most persecuted groups.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to ABC News, Basya also believes the asylum seekers found in the water may have left the boat on purpose to be rescued to avoid being sent away from Indonesia waters.
maintain	V;PST;V.PTCP	maintained	Malaysia and Indonesia have maintained a policy of turning away boats of migrants which, according to AFP, the Untied Nations and United States have both criticised.
rescue	V;PST;V.PTCP	rescued	One Rohingya, Muhammad Amin, the first boat rescued on Friday was turned around twice, toward Malaysia by Indonesian navy and then toward Indonesia by Malaysian navy.
discuss	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Discussing	Discussing his concern in a public statement, Malaysia's Prime Minister, Najib Razak said, "We are in contact with all relevant parties, with whom we share the desire to find a solution to this crisis".
crack	V;PST;V.PTCP	cracked	Thailand has recently cracked down on human trafficking, which has affected the routes by which people-smugglers transport migrants.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	The US State Department said John Kerry, the Secretary of State, contacted Thailand's foreign minister over temporary housing for the Rohingya out at sea.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Jeff Rathke, the State Department Spokesperson, said, "We urge the governments of the region to work together quickly, first and foremost, to save the lives of migrants now at sea who are in need of an immediate rescue effort".
ask	V;PST;SG;3	asked	Rathke also asked the governments of South East Asia not to turn away boats of people seeking asylum.
suggest	V;PRS;PL;3	suggest	Estimates suggest 8000 migrants may be currently at sea in the region.
inspire	V;PRS;SG;3	inspires	Texas student Ahmed Mohamed inspires social movement
inspire	V;PST;V.PTCP	inspired	News of police response to a boy's digital clock in Texas last week has inspired a global social movement in support of the youth.
arrest	V;PST;V.PTCP	arrested	Ahmed Mohamed, a 14-year-old boy from Irving, Texas, was arrested at his school by police after he brought a digital clock he had made to school.
report	V;PST;V.PTCP	reported	After news of the police response was reported, Mohamed received support online ranging from US President Barack Obama to Facebook creator Mark Zuckerberg.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	Yesterday Mohamed received VIP attention at Google headquarters in California at the Google Science Fair — and tomorrow he plans to meet international dignitaries at the United Nations headquarters in New York.
bring	V;PST;SG;3	brought	Mohamed brought the digital clock he made to school on September 14, and a teacher mistakenly thought it resembled a bomb.
arrest	V;PST;PL;3	arrested	Police arrested and questioned the student.
wear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wearing	Images of the boy wearing a NASA T-shirt and handcuffed by the police were quickly posted and reposted online.
observe	V;PST;SG;3	observed	The Deseret News observed that the hashtag on Twitter, #IStandWithAhmed, had become an international phenomenon.
note	V;PST;SG;3	noted	The Straits Times noted what began as individual messages to Mohamed had grown into a "support movement".
comment	V;PST;SG;3	commented	American academic and professor Marc Lamont Hill commented,
hope	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Hoping	"Hoping that the #istandwithahmed movement translates into a substantive national conversation on race, punishment, and incarceration."
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	The Twitter attention led to outreach from Google and Mohamed was invited to their Google Science Fair.
meet	V;NFIN	meet	Mohamed was a VIP guest at the Google Science Fair this weekend, and was able to meet finalists at the event held at Google's headquarters located in Mountain View, California.
meet	V;PST;SG;3	met	Co-founder of Google, Sergey Brin, personally met with Mohamed during his visit to Google.
travel	V;NFIN	travel	On Wednesday, Mohamed and his father are to travel to the United Nations.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	His father Mohamed El-Hassan Mohamed said he was taking his entire family to the U.N. headquarters in New York, because international dignitaries wanted to meet with his son and express their support.
hear	V;PST;SG;3	heard	Psychology major at University of Texas Arlington Amneh Jafari heard of the incident and wanted to issue her support to Mohamed.
choose	V;PST;SG;3	chose	She chose the hastag #IStandWithAhmed and posted to Twitter.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Jafari said she felt Mohamed had been subject to Islamophobia and her hashtag created greater awareness about this issue.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	The hashtag took on a life of its own, and was used in messages on Twitter over one million times by Thursday night.
tweet	V;PST;SG;3	tweeted	NASA tweeted support of Ahmed Mohamed via its account International Space Station
tweet	V;PST;SG;3	tweeted	US President Barack Obama tweeted using his @POTUS account:
want	V;NFIN;2	Want	Want to bring it to the White House?
inspire	V;NFIN	inspire	We should inspire more kids like you to like science.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	It's what makes America great."
give	V;PST;SG;3	gave	Twitter gave Mohamed the option to come to their company for an internship.
tweet	V;PST;SG;3	tweeted	Facebook co-founder Mark Zuckerberg tweeted to Mohamed and said the boy was welcome to come and tour the company.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Executive director of the Dallas chapter of the Council on American - Islamic Relations Alia Salem said Mohamed wishes to use his newfound fame and attention to spotlight social causes including the treatment of Muslims in the United States.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	University of Texas Arlington Muslim Student Association president Sehar Memon said the large amount of positive feedback for Mohamed had helped make his situation much better.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Memon also said she believed the social movement in reaction to Mohamed helped improve Muslim / non-Muslim.
assert	V;PST;V.PTCP	asserted	Both the Irving Independent School District and the Irving Police Department have asserted that the boy's religion and name were not a factor in the manner in which they approached the situation.
kill	V;PST;V.PTCP	killed	At least 107 killed in Mecca crane collapse
cause	V;PST;SG;3	caused	A storm yesterday caused a crane to fall into Mecca's Grand Mosque, killing 107 or more and wounding 238, according to Saudi Arabia's Civil Defense Authority.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Civil Defense Authority director General Suleiman al-Amr, in remarks to al-Ikhbariya television, said "All those who were wounded and the dead have been taken to hospital. There are no casualties left at the location."
occur	V;PST;SG;3	occurred	The accident, which occurred yesterday afternoon, was caused by strong winds and heavy rain that led the crane to fall.
release	V;PST;V.PTCP	released	Photos released of the scene showed numerous bloodied bodies and various scattered debris.
occur	V;PST;SG;3	occurred	The accident occurred at a time when the mosque was relatively uncrowded.
happen	V;PST;V.PTCP	happened	"Had it happened an hour later it would have been much worse," Khaled Al-Maeena, editor at large at the Saudi Gazette, said.
happen	V;PST;V.PTCP	happened	"Had it happened five hours earlier or four hours earlier, I think the death toll would have been more than a thousand."
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	The crane accident comes just ten days before the beginning of the Hajj, the annual pilgrimage to Mecca, Islam's holiest site.
undergo	V;PRS;V.PTCP	undergoing	As undergoing a pilgrimage to Mecca is a mandatory religious duty for all Muslims who physically and financially can, millions of pilgrims arrive in Mecca each year for the Hajj.
enlarge	V;NFIN	enlarge	Construction was underway at the mosque in order to enlarge the mosque and improve crowd control, which has been a recurring problem during Mecca's pilgrimages.
arrest	V;PST;V.PTCP	arrested	Scientology defector arrested after attempting to leave organization
reveal	V;PST;V.PTCP	revealed	Recent reports have revealed that a defector from the elite group within Scientology called the Sea Org was first persuaded to travel back to a management building for the organization in California, where he was interrogated by an attorney for Scientology and then arrested.
leave	V;PST;V.PTCP	left	Daniel Montalvo in September 2010, after having left the elite group within Scientology called the Sea Org.
hold	V;PRS;V.PTCP	holding	Montalvo is holding a copy of the book Blown for Good critical of Scientology, written by Marc Headley.
arrest	V;PST;V.PTCP	arrested	Daniel Montalvo, 19, a member of Scientology all his life, was arrested after leaving its elite group the Sea Org.
release	V;PST;V.PTCP	released	He was released on US$ 20,000 bail on Thursday.
charge	V;PST;V.PTCP	charged	Montalvo was charged with taking hard drives and a zip drive from the Scientology organization after leaving the Sea Org group.
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	The Sea Org requires members to sign a billion-year-contract, agreeing to work for the organization founded by science fiction writer L. Ron Hubbard for multiple lifetimes.
live	V;PRS;PL;3	live	Members of the Sea Org live a strict lifestyle – with little income, small diet provided and significant time invested in work per day.
read	V;PST;V.PTCP	read	Montalvo had read an article in the Scientology propaganda publication Freedom Magazine, which attacked high-ranking defectors from the organization including Tom Devocht and Mark Rathbun.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Montalvo had known Devocht personally, and came to the conclusion that the claims made about him in the Freedom article were lies.
motivate	V;PST;V.PTCP	motivated	Montalvo was subsequently motivated to seek out information about Scientology on the Internet, and came upon a blog written by Rathbun.
contact	V;PST;SG;3	contacted	Montalvo contacted former members of Scientology, who helped him leave the organization and get in touch with Devocht.
travel	V;PST;SG;3	traveled	He traveled to Florida to meet with Devocht, who had told Montalvo he would assist him with future employment and housing.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to Rathbun the Scientology property in question was returned by mail to the organization by Montalvo shortly after he left the Sea Org.
state	V;PST;V.PTCP	stated	Scientology officials have stated they have not received the materials.
call	V;PRS;V.PTCP	calling	After calling the Scientology organization by phone to attempt to speak with his mother, Montalvo was instead put through to a Scientology attorney, Kendrick Moxon.
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	Rathbun wrote, "Moxon proceeded to lie to Daniel that the hard drives were never returned, and made a convincing case (not difficult to do with a 19 year old who does not even know the three branches of government) that Daniel would be put behind iron bars for a good long time, UNLESS of course, he returned and cooperated with Moxon and routed out properly."
route	V;NFIN	route	To "route out" in Scientology jargon refers to the member who wishes to leave proceeding through a set of interrogative exit interviews while attached to the Scientology device called the E-meter.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to Rathbun, a detective for the Los Angeles Sheriff's office was provided with material from Scientology to build a case against Montalvo.
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	Rathbun stated,
pick	V;PST;V.PTCP	picked	"Daniel was picked up by an investigator in a black car with blacked out windows.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	Instead of being taken to a hotel, where he could route out as promised, he was taken to the Century City Towers.
deposit	V;PST;V.PTCP	deposited	He was deposited in an office on the 33rd floor where a pricy church lawyer interrogated him for two hours."
assert	V;PST;SG;3	asserted	Rathbun asserted that Scientology officials, "... plied the Sheriff's detective with a grand conspiracy theory, characterizing me as the 'anti-christ' of the church of Scientology. ... The next thing Daniel knows he's in a cell where he sat for the next 30 hours."
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	Steve Whitmore, a representative for the LA Sheriff's office, told LA Weekly in a statement that the case involving Montalvo was being dealt with as charges relating to grand theft.
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	Whitmore stated Montalvo left Scientology-related employment in Los Angeles on September 24.
go	V;PST;PL;3	went	On September 29, Scientology officials went to the East Los Angeles sheriff's station where they claimed Montalvo allegedly left the organization with computer hard drives.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	Whitmore told LA Weekly that on October 6 after traveling back from Florida, Montalvo "walked into lobby of the East L.A. station and turned himself in", and told the police, "everything he did".
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	Whitmore stated to LA Weekly that the alleged crime involved five hard drives each of $ 200 in value.
assist	V;PST;V.PTCP	assisted	Montalvo has been assisted by former members of Scientology, including musician Tiziano Lugli and actor Jason Beghe.
request	V;PST;SG;3	requested	Rathbun requested donations via a statement made on his blog, in order to help Montalvo with his legal defense financing.
describe	V;PST;SG;3	described	He described Montalvo as an individual, "facing the penitentiary in thanks for the following sins: a) Leaving a lifehood of slavery to get a taste of freedom and the world. b) Refusing - despite every provocation and incentive and threat - to frame three guys who went out of their ways (and dipped into their pockets) to help the kid achieve that freedom."
emerge	V;PRS;PL;3	emerge	New faces emerge as veteran politicians step down in Hong Kong legislative election
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	The Hong Kong legislative election was held on Sunday, with a record-breaking turnout of around 2 million voters and turnout rate of 58%.
show	V;PRS;PL;3	show	The results show the anti-establishment camp successfully maintained a majority in the geographical constituencies' seats thus veto power to any members' bills, and also a minority larger than a third to veto any bills featuring major political reforms.
emerge	V;PST;PL;3	emerged	Young and new faces emerged in the new council members, as veteran politicians stepped down in both the anti-establishment and pro-Beijing campus.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	Eddie Chu received the largest vote in any geographical constituency
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	In the New Territories West geographical constituency, nonpartisan pan-democrat Eddie Chu was elected with 84,121 votes, the most given to any list in the geographical constituencies.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Other anti-establishment candidates elected in this district were localist camp's Cheng Chung-tai of Civic Passion, pan-democracy camp's Kwok Ka-ki and Andrew Wan of respectively Civic Party and the Democratic Party.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	The pro-Beijing camp candidates elected were New People Party's Michael Tien, DAB's Ben Chan and Leung Che-cheung, FTU's Alice Mak and pro-establishment nonpartisan Junius Ho.
defeat	V;PST;V.PTCP	defeated	Two pan-democratic veteran politicians Lee Cheuk-yan of the Labour Party and Frederick Fung of ADPL were defeated in this constituency, with Junius Ho defeating Lee Cheuk-yan by around five thousand votes.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	In the New Territories East geographical constituency, pan-democracy camp councillors Alvin Yeung from the Civic Party, Fernando Cheung of the Labour Party, Raymond Chan of People Power, and Leung Kwok-hung of LSD were successfully elected.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Pan-democrat Lam Cheuk-ting from the Democratic Party and localist group Youngspiration's Baggio Leung were successfully elected newcomers.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	For the Pro-Beijing camp, Elizabeth Quat of DAB was elected with 58,825 votes, the highest in the constituency.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Other pro-Beijing candidates elected in this constituency were Gary Chan of DAB and Eunice Yung of New People's Party.
fail	V;PST;SG;3	failed	Pan-democrat Gary Fan of the Neo Democrats failed to get re-elected in this constituency, and resigned from his leadership in his party due to its election defeat.
re-elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	re-elected	In the Hong Kong Island geographical constituency, pro-Beijing New People Party's Regina Ip was re-elected with 60,760 votes.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Other pro-Beijing elected were FTU's Kwok Wai-keung and DAB's Horace Cheung.
earn	V;PST;SG;3	earned	Demosisto's Nathan Law earned a surprise victory as second place in the constituency with 50,818 votes, and becomes the youngest Legislative Council member in Hong Kong's history.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	The other two anti-establishment camp elects were Hui Chi-fung from the Democratic Party and Tanya Chan of the Civic Party, both considered to be in the pan-democracy camp.
lose	V;PST;SG;3	lost	Pan-democrat veteran Cyd Ho from the Labour Party lost her seat in this constituency.
re-elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	re-elected	In the Kowloon West geographical constituency, pro-Beijing councillors Ann Chiang of DAB and Priscilla Leung of BPA were successfully re-elected, as well as pan-democratic councillors Claudia Mo of the Civic Party and Wong Pik-wan of the Democratic Party.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	The rest of the candidates elected in this constituency were young and relatively new activists from the localist camp, Lau Siu-lai and Youngspiration's Yau Wai-ching.
defeat	V;PST;SG;3	defeated	Yau Wai-ching defeated another localist camp candidate, veteran politician Raymond Wong of the Proletariat Political Institute, by 424 votes.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	In the Kowloon East geographical constituency, pro-Beijing district councillor Wilson Or was elected with 51,516 votes, the highest in this constituency.
re-elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	re-elected	Nonpartisan Paul Tse and Wong Kwok-kin of the FTU were successfully re-elected.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Pan-democracy camp councillor Wu Chi-wai of the Democratic Party was elected.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Civic Party's Jeremy Tam was elected for the first time.
defeat	V;PST;V.PTCP	defeated	Localist group Civic Passion's Wong Yeung-tat was defeated in this district, and resigned his leadership in Civic Passion.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	know	In the District Council (Second) functional constituency, commonly know as "Super District Council" seats, pan-democracy camp's Kwong Chun-yu of the Democratic Party received 491,667 votes, the highest in this constituency.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Other pan-democrats elected were Leung Yiu-chung of NWSC and James To of the Democratic Party.
elect	V;PST;V.PTCP	elected	Pro-Beijing DAB's Starry Lee and Holden Chow were successfully elected in this constituency, while pro-Beijing veteran politician Wong Kwok-hing of FTU was defeated by 10,694 votes by James To.
gain	V;PST;SG;3	gained	In traditional functional constituencies, the pan-democracy camp gained a seat from the pro-Beijing camp.
defeat	V;PST;SG;3	defeated	Shiu Ka-chun of the Social Welfare constituency defeated their opponents, hence the pan-democrats' seats in the functional constituencies increased from 9 to 10.
claim	V;NFIN	claim	Also, an independent who does not claim to belong to either camp, Edward Yiu of the Architectural, Surveying, Planning and Landscape constituency, defeated his opponents and was elected.
vote	V;PST;V.PTCP	voted	The turnout of this election was the highest in the history of Legislative Council elections, with around 2 million voters voted and a turnout rate of 58%.
close	V;PST;PL;3	closed	At a polling station in Taikoo of the Hong Kong Island constituency, after the polls closed at 22:30, with the long queues outside the station the last ballot there was cast at 2:30 the next day, four hours after the last person was allowed to join the queue.
elect	V;PRS;SG;3	elects	The election elects 70 members of the Legislative Council.
celebrate	V;PST;V.PTCP	celebrated	Pop culture celebrated at Fan Expo Canada 2016 in Toronto
open	V;PRS;V.PTCP	opening	The far end of part of the convention floor, shortly after opening Friday.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	The 22nd Fan Expo Canada took place at the Metro Toronto Convention Centre this weekend, attracting thousands to the event, one of the most popular in North America.
cross	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Crossing	Crossing between the genres of science fiction, horror, anime, fantasy and comics, the event sprawled throughout all corners of the facility.
offer	V;PST;PL;3	offered	Hundreds of vendors offered products new and old, joined by celebrity guests like Stan Lee, Mark Hamill, and Margaret Atwood.
don	V;PST;PL;3	donned	Various local fan organizations were there in a charitable capacity, and thousands of fans donned costumes, many of which they themselves created.
launch	V;NFIN	launch	Atwood, a Booker Prize-winner, was there to launch her graphic novel Angel Catbird, with illustrator Johnnie Christmas.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	Lee was making his final Canadian convention appearance, part of a larger farewell tour.
include	V;PST;SG;3	included	Hamill's schedule included a panel with Kevin Conroy, the voice of Batman in Batman: The Animated Series, in which the Star Wars lead played the Joker.
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	Ubisoft, Microsoft, and PlayStation VR all had large areas set up for attendees to try their latest games.
drop	V;PST;V.PTCP	dropped	In the 20-minute PlayStation VR demonstration of Batman: Arkham VR, players are dropped down a secret elevator in Bruce Wayne's manor, to the Bat Cave, to suit up.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	From there, the demonstration took players to investigate the murder of Nightwing.
run	V;PST;SG;3	ran	The Northern Arena Esports Championship ran throughout the weekend, facing off teams of video gamers in a packed hall.
attend	V;PST;V.PTCP	attended	Even in early rounds, the event was well attended, with the stands full of gamers, watching the matches with live colour commentary.
see	V;PST;PL;3	saw	The finals saw US team Cloud9 and Immortals, from Brazil, face off in Counter-Strike: Global Offensive.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	Both unbeaten teams going in, the Brazillian team won the championship and $ 50,000.
delay	V;PST;V.PTCP	delayed	Gameplay was delayed during the "third map" (third round) of the finals, when one Immortals player was caught with their headset off.
deduct	V;PST;V.PTCP	deducted	After nearly half an hour, the team was deducted a point, leading to a sudden but temporary rush of success by the US team.
return	V;PST;SG;3	returned	The Canadian garrison of 501st Legion returned with their setup of photo-ops, with proceeds to the Make-A-Wish Foundation.
collect	V;PRS;SG;3	collects	Volunteer organization Comic Corps of Canada collects donations of comics, to distribute to children and youth in the hospital.
collect	V;PRS;V.PTCP	collecting	Along with collecting comics on-site, the group was fundraising through raffles and button sales.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	Other charitable fan-based groups included The Ontario Ghostbusters, Doctor Who Society of Canada, and Klingon Assault Group.
pose	V;PRS;PL;3	pose	Brampton Spider-Gwen and Symbiote Spidey pose at the Comic Corps booth.
share	V;PRS;PL;3	share	Successful Stormtroopers share a high-five.
offer	V;PST;PL;3	offered	The thousands of cosplay outfits on parade around the various halls offered a tremendous cross-section of genres and eras of pop culture.
involve	V;PST;SG;3	involved	The majority of outfits involved at least some elements made from scratch, a fantastic display of craftsmanship.
speak	V;PST;SG;3	spoke	Of the thousands, Wikinews spoke with a cosplayer going by the name "The Katherinator".
wait	V;PRS;V.PTCP	waiting	She was outside the building in the gardens, waiting for a photographer she had arranged to shoot her new Epic Mickey costumed character-style outfit.
carve	V;PST;V.PTCP	carved	With shoes as wide as a forearm, and a rigid torso of carved foam, she remained exuberant even when caught in the traffic between halls, many hours into wearing the outfit.
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	Three of Digimon's "Digi-destined", including cosplayer Jessica Anderson.
begin	V;PRS;SG;3	begins	New Zealand begins process to consider changing national flag design
announce	V;PST;SG;3	announced	On Tuesday, the New Zealand government announced the start of a public process to suggest designs for a new national flag, and determine whether their citizens would prefer a different national flag over the current one.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	The current New Zealand flag is partially based on the United Kingdom's flag;
plan	V;PST;V.PTCP	planned	The government's Flag Consideration Project has planned a number of conferences and roadshows as part of this process, with the first meeting set to take place in Christchurch on May 16.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to the New Zealand Herald, Emeritus Professor John Burrows, the chairman of the project's panel of twelve, said New Zealand's flag has never before been open to public choice.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Professor Burrows also said resources and kits would be accessible for schools and communities," For example, schools can run their own flag discussions and referendums to mirror the formal process as part of their own learning exercise".
encourage	V;PST;V.PTCP	encouraged	People were encouraged to submit their designs online at www.flag.govt.nz and suggest what the flag should mean on www.standfor.co.nz.
engrave	V;PST;V.PTCP	engraved	Names of participants would be engraved, at their option, on a flag pole monument to be built in the nation's capital, Wellington.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	New Zealand's Prime Minister John Key said he believes redesigning the flag now has a "strong rationale".
promote	V;PST;SG;3	promoted	Mr Key promoted the campaign for a unique New Zealand flag on Waitangi Day - February 6 - this year.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Of the public process, he said, "In the end I'll have one vote in each referendum just like every other New Zealander on the electoral roll".
intend	V;PRS;SG;3	intends	The New Zealand government intends to hold two referendums to reach a verdict on the flag, at an estimated cost of NZ $ 26 million, although a recent poll found only a quarter of citizens favoured changing the flag.
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	The first referendum is to be held from November 20 to December 11, selecting a single new flag design out of about four finalists.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Voters would then choose between the new flag and their current flag early in 2016.
attack	V;PRS;PL;3	attack	Hackers attack Church of Scientology website
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	Wikinews has learned that according to an Internet posting made just over 24 hours ago, the Church of Scientology's website is being attacked by hackers, causing the site to shut down.
launch	V;PST;V.PTCP	launched	The attack was launched on Wednesday by a user labelled "Anonymous", on the website "Insurgency Wiki", a spinoff of 4chan.
explain	V;PRS;SG;3	explains	The "History" section of the site explains, in a satirical fashion, that the incident was prompted by the Church of Scientology's attempts to remove a promotional video featuring Scientologist Tom Cruise from YouTube.
comply	V;PRS;V.PTCP	complying	Though YouTube is complying with the Church of Scientology's requests to take down the video, other sites such as Gawker.com have stated that they will keep hosting the video.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Writing	Writing in a blog post, Matthew Ingram of The Globe and Mail dubbed the ongoing conflict involving the Church of Scientology's attempts to remove the Cruise video from the Internet: "Scientology vs. the Internet, part XVII".
characterize	V;PST;SG;3	characterized	He characterized the conflict between the Church of Scientology and anonymous posters of the Cruise video as "another small skirmish in a war that Scientology has been waging for almost 15 years, since the early days of newsgroups such as alt.religion.scientology, which posted internal church documents in 1994.
file	V;PST;V.PTCP	filed	Lawsuits have been filed, mailing lists have been shut down, homes of discussion group participants have been raided and their computers seized -- an all-out war."
prompt	V;PST;V.PTCP	Prompted	"Prompted I think by the Tom Cruise video, a new obsession is taking hold on the internet. An insurgency against The Church of Scientology." Anonymous blogger, "Anon declares war on Church of Scientology"
admit	V;PST;SG;3	admitted	One poster admitted to being a part of the effort, writing in a blog post "I have myself, as per instructions, loaded up Gigaloader and started bombarding the Scientology homepage.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	s	Theres [sic] something in the hilarious anarchy of the net that produces these 'events' every now and again."
write	V;PST;SG;3	wrote	The poster wrote that "Prompted I think by the Tom Cruise video, a new obsession is taking hold on the internet. An insurgency against The Church of Scientology."
email	V;PST;SG;3	emailed	"Someone emailed me earlier today talking about a tool a group’s been using to attack the scientology website.
create	V;PST;V.PTCP	created	It’s an interesting tool, created to overload/create malformed strings and crash a website’s database," said the post by an unknown author on pigmy.
report	V;PST;PL;3	reported	Some individuals reported that when they are able to reach the site, all they get is a message stating, "The word scientology means search for truth ...".
load	V;PRS;SG;3	loads	As of 15:11 GMT the site was accessible again, but only loads at relatively slow speeds, and by the end of the day Saturday the site was not loading at all.
theorize	V;PST;PL;3	theorized	Posts on the message board for the Scientology-critic site Operation Clambake from Friday theorized that a denial-of-service attack had occurred, and wrote that as of Friday the Scientology.org site was either not loading at all, or loading very slowly.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	writing	Critics of Scientology at the Internet newsgroup alt.religion.scientology were critical of the attacks to the Church of Scientology website, with one poster writing "How can people look at both sides if one side is gone?"
increase	V;PST;V.PTCP	increased	Traffic to the Scientology website had already increased 18-fold prior to the attack, following increased attention after the Tom Cruise video appeared on the Internet.
come	V;PST;PL;3	came	At that time, one in three visits to the site came from BBC News, and the website increased to number 3 in the company Hitwise UK's Lifestyle-Religion category.
wait	V;PRS;SG;3	waits	Microsoft waits for death of Internet Explorer 6
announce	V;PST;SG;3	announced	With development of Microsoft's Internet Explorer 9 (IE9) browser almost complete, Microsoft announced on Friday that it is time for IE6 users to move away from the antiquated 10-year-old browser.
replace	V;PST;V.PTCP	replaced	Microsoft has replaced it with three newer versions.
launch	V;PST;V.PTCP	launched	On a new website, "The Internet Explorer 6 Countdown", Microsoft has launched an aggressive campaign to persuade users to stop using IE6 and update to a newer IE.
decrease	V;NFIN	decrease	Its goal is to decrease IE6 users to less than one percent.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	"[Ten] years ago, a browser was born.
say	V;NFIN	say	Now that we’re in 2011, in an era of modern web standards, it’s time to say goodbye," Microsoft says on "The Internet Explorer 6 Countdown".
slip	V;PRS;V.PTCP	slipping	Internet Explorer's market share is slipping.
account	V;PST;PL;3	accounted	IE's various versions accounted for 70 percent of the market in 2009; this has dropped to approximately 56 percent today.
increase	V;PRS;V.PTCP	increasing	Mozilla Firefox, its main rival, has been actively increasing its market share while the recently released Google Chrome is also quickly gaining users.
conclude	V;PST;SG;3	concluded	PC World's recent analysis of its own web traffic statistics concluded, "Usually, Internet Explorer is the #2 browser after Firefox, but over the past 30 days it's been #3, a couple of points behind Safari - and nearly tied with Chrome.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	But if it isn't, and Chrome continues to surge, IE could fall to fourth place."
face	V;PRS;PL;3	face	Web developers face big problems with IE6.
design	V;PRS;PL;3	design	As they design their web pages for the newer browsers with advanced web technology and geared to the newest web core markup language HTML 5, they are forced to accommodate older out-of-date technology to support IE6 users.
mention	V;PST;V.PTCP	mentioned	Also, not mentioned on Microsoft's "The Internet Explorer 6 Countdown" site, are the numerous security vulnerabilities putting the browser at risk of being hacked.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	The Internet security firm Secunia.com, has said that as of March 4, 2011, IE6 has 277 vulnerabilities; 39 percent of these are rated "highly critical".
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Microsoft says that the latest versions of Internet Explorer provide the user with enhanced protection from these attacks and threats.
measure	V;PST;V.PTCP	measured	Microsoft's Internet Explorer 6 is one of the most successful software products ever as measured by its durability and the massive numbers of people who have used it.
ship	V;PST;SG;3	shipped	IE6 shipped with Windows XP, nearly a decade ago.
outlive	V;PST;V.PTCP	outlived	But it has outlived its usefulness, and Microsoft no longer wants to support it.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	Australian woman claims Church of Scientology imprisoned her for twelve years
claim	V;PST;V.PTCP	claimed	Valeska Paris, an Australian woman and former Scientologist, has claimed that the Church of Scientology imprisoned her for twelve years aboard the Scientology - owned cruise ship, Freewinds.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Paris was born in Switzerland but her parents moved her to the United Kingdom when she was six so they could be closer to the church's headquarters.
join	V;PST;SG;3	joined	She joined the Sea Org aged 14.
tell	V;PST;V.PTCP	told	In 1995, at age seventeen, she was told to disconnect from her mother.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	Paris claims she was first sent to the ship aged eighteen for two weeks as a teenager but ended up spending twelve years there.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	Paris also claims that her passport was taken away from her and she was forced to work in the engine room.
haul	V;PST;V.PTCP	hauled	I was basically hauled in and told that my mum had attacked the church and that I needed to disconnect from her because she was suppressive. Valeska Paris
haul	V;PST;V.PTCP	hauled	I was basically hauled in and told that my mum had attacked the church and that I needed to disconnect from her because she was suppressive.
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	Her mother had spoken out against Scientology on French television after her ex-husband and Paris's father, Albert Jaquier, committed suicide after he lost his fortune to Scientology.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	Paris claims that she was kept at Flag Land Base in Clearwater, Florida until she was eighteen and then transferred to the Freewinds.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	On board the Freewinds, Paris claims that she was working from 6:00am to midnight and due to lack of sleep ended up being a walking zombie.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	She also claims that in 1997 and 1998, she was tasked with removal of asbestos from the ship.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	In 2007, Paris left the ship after a confrontation with the ship's management.
send	V;PST;V.PTCP	sent	She was eventually sent to Rehabilitation Project Force in Sydney, which the Church describes as a religious retreat, but critics charge is a forced work camp.
meet	V;PST;SG;3	met	Here, she met her future husband and subsequently left the church.
deny	V;PST;SG;3	denied	In a statement to the Australian Broadcasting Corporation, the Church denied these allegations:
force	V;PST;V.PTCP	forced	She certainly wasn't 'forced' to be there.
force	V;PST;V.PTCP	forced	She was also never forced to perform labour in the engine room.
claim	V;PST;PL;3	claimed	They also claimed Paris was a volunteer and claim she has a vested interest for misrepresenting her experiences with the Church.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	The Church also claims that Paris not having her passport was maritime procedure.
put	V;PRS;SG;3	puts	North Korea puts military in 'quasi-state of war'
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to Yonhap news agency, quoting North Korea's main news agency Korean Central News Agency (KCNA), the country's military has been placed on high alert and has been told to prepare a "quasi-state of war".
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	The report comes after North and South Korea exchanged artillery fire yesterday.
make	V;PST;PL;3	made	"War maniacs of the South Korean puppet military made another grave provocation to the DPRK in the central western sector of the front on Thursday afternoon.
perpetrate	V;PST;PL;3	perpetrated	They perpetrated such reckless action as firing 36 shells at KPA civil police posts under the absurd pretext that the KPA fired one shell at the south side.
hit	V;PST;PL;3	hit	Six shells of them hit the area near KPA civil police posts 542 and 543 and other 15 shells fell near KPA civil police posts 250 and 251", said KCNA.
deserve	V;PRS;SG;3	deserves	"Foolhardy provocation deserves harsh punishment", it added; and, later, "Kim Jong-un issued an order of the supreme commander of the Korean People's Army [KPA] that the front-line large combined units of the KPA should enter a wartime state to be fully battle ready to launch surprise operations".
fire	V;PST;SG;3	fired	Yesterday, North Korea fired artillery rounds aimed at a loudspeaker in South Korea that broadcasts anti-Pyongyang propaganda into the North.
dispatch	V;PST;V.PTCP	dispatched	"Commanders of the Korean People's Army were hastily dispatched to the front-line troops to command military operations to destroy psychological warfare tools if the enemy does not stop the propaganda broadcast within 48 hours and prepare against the enemy's possible counteractions", added KCNA.
report	V;PST;V.PTCP	reported	No injuries or deaths were reported in the attacks from either side, but South Korea evacuated about 80 people in the town of Yeoncheon after at least one shell landed near the area.
retaliate	V;PST;SG;3	retaliated	The South retaliated by firing dozens of rounds of artillery into the north.
start	V;PST;V.PTCP	started	South Korea had recently started to broadcast propaganda from loudspeakers after an 11-year lull.
begin	V;PST;PL;3	began	The broadcasts began on August 10 and South Korea says the broadcasts will continue.
state	V;PST;SG;3	stated	After the exchange of fire, North Korea stated it would attack any loudspeakers broadcasting propaganda and would respond, militarily, within 48 hours if the broadcasts do not stop.
put	V;PST;V.PTCP	put	Both countries have put their militaries on high levels of alert.
light	V;PRS;SG;3	lights	Lantern Festival in Taipei lights up the night
crowd	V;PST;PL;3	crowded	Taipei, Taiwan — The Taipei City Lantern Festival was off to a bright start on Saturday Night as hundreds of thousands of people crowded onto the Chiang Kai Shek Memorial near downtown Taipei to witness city mayor Hau Lung-bin and other dignitaries ceremoniously flip the switch to light hundreds of colorful lantern scenes in and around the 240,000 square meter memorial park.
rotate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	rotating	The centerpiece of this rock concert-like performance is a 15 meter high rotating pig equipped with a fog machine, laser light show and theme music.
revolve	V;PRS;SG;3	revolves	The theme of this years lantern festival revolves around the 12 year Chinese zodiac cycles year of the pig.
symbolize	V;PRS;SG;3	symbolizes	Its round shape symbolizes wealth and good fortune and is believed to bring good luck.
start	V;PST;V.PTCP	Started	Started in 1990, the Taipei lantern festival continues a 2000 year old tradition of light related festivals marking the end of the Chinese New Year celebrations.
grow	V;PRS;SG;3	grows	This particular event grows in popularity every year.
attend	V;PST;PL;3	attended	Last Year over 4 million visitors attended the event and this year the city expects about 6 million visitors according to the event website.
fill	V;PRS;PL;3	fill	Colorful lantern scenes not only fill the central square but also the surrounding 2 km circumference of the memorial.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Each section has a theme.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	One side of the memorial is scenes made by student groups while other areas represent the counties of Taiwan.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is even a section of lanterns sponsored by foreign countries.
crowd	V;PST;V.PTCP	crowded	As with all new year celebrations in Taiwan, the lantern festival is crowded.
flock	V;PRS;PL;3	flock	People flock to see the lantern scenes and enjoy the festival atmosphere as they carry lanterns, given away by the city and bought from street vendors at the event.
turn	V;PRS;PL;3	turn	The many light toys such a swords, devil ears, and lanterns turn the milling crowd into a unique light show that can be observed by climbing the steps of the 70 meter memorial hall on the grounds.
swamp	V;PST;V.PTCP	swamped	Local public transportation hubs are often swamped by the event.
do	V;PRS;PL;3	do	The station staff at Chiang Kai Shek Memorial Hall Station do an excellent job of crowd control but lines for the subway can still be hours long and very exhausting.
cause	V;PST;SG;3	caused	A sudden rain storm on Sunday night (March 4) caused a mass exodus of people to head for the station prompting lines that backed up into the street.
commute	V;PRS;V.PTCP	commuting	The commuting crowds can be avoided however by walking from NTU Hospital Station or Shandao Station.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Both of these stops are only about a 20 minute walk from the event and have no crowds at all.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you really want to avoid the crowds, definitely avoid the weekends.
wait	V;IMP;2	Wait	Wait until the fourth or fifth day of the festival when the crowds will have dwindled.
visit	V;PST;SG;1	visited	I visited the festival on Saturday night, the opening night, and there was a sea of people in the square.
go	V;PST;SG;1	went	I went back the next Tuesday night and there was almost no one there, just a few stray photographers taking advantage of the unimpeded views of the lanterns.
run	V;PRS;SG;3	runs	The festival runs through March 11 this year.
streamline	V;PST;V.PTCP	streamlined	Reports: 'streamlined' UK asylum process to replace interview with questionnaire for five nationalities
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The United Kingdom Home Office today is to begin issuing questionnaires in place of official interviews to determine refugee status for some 12,000 asylee hopefuls from Afghanistan, Eritrea, Libya, Syria and Yemen who applied before last summer.
clear	V;NFIN	clear	The forms are part of Prime Minister Rishi Sunak's effort to clear the nation's 92,000 asylum claim- 'legacy backlog' by year end.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	While standard security, criminal, and health checks will continue, officials will scrap the face-to-face interview step to "streamline" processing for five nationalities whose claim acceptance rate is already above 95%.
report	V;PRS;SG;3	reports	The document, which media reports range from ten to 32 pages with 40 to 50 questions, "must be completed in English", the government recommending "online translation tools" if needed.
return	V;PST;V.PTCP	returned	It also must be returned within twenty working days.
leak	V;PST;V.PTCP	leaked	A leaked internal letter seen by Sky News says "failure to return the questionnaire without reasonable explanation may result in an individual's asylum claim being withdrawn in line with the published policy", but the government will provide a reminder after the deadline and consider extensions.
tell	V;PRS;PL;3	tell	Officials tell BBC News every claim will be assessed meritocratically, with the i reporting no one will be refused "based solely on the response of the questionnaire".
back	V;PST;V.PTCP	backed	A similar fast-track system backed by the British Red Cross in September 2021 for 3,000 Afghan refugees was reportedly rejected by Prime Minister Boris Johnson over fears it would amount to amnesty, which the Home Office today rejects, and strain the UK's welfare and housing systems.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Labour's Shadow Home Secretary Yvette Cooper said her party has supported the idea "for months", and said it was "damning that the Home Office isn't doing this already [...] including for safe countries like Albania" after the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees "recommended it two years ago."
continue	V;PST;SG;3	continued	She continued: "Labour has a common-sense plan to fast-track cases, get return agreements in place so unsuccessful claims can be quickly and safely returned, and take much stronger action against the criminal gangs driving dangerous small boat crossings."
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	Figures to be published today show a record 45,756 migrants crossing the Channel last year drove outstanding asylum claims over 150,000, also a record.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Sunak has made tackling migration one of his main priorities, with laws banning illegal migrants from claiming asylum in the UK expected next month.
read	V;PRS;SG;3	reads	The Home Office letter reads "we may extend the use of the questionnaire as a means to gather additional information from other claimants awaiting an asylum decision in due course", but no current plans to expand the scheme exist.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	On Wednesday, Home Secretary Suella Braverman told GB News: "It's clear that we have an unsustainable situation in towns and cities around our country [...] because of the overwhelming numbers of people arriving here illegally and our legal duties to accommodate them".
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	But media have said the questionnaires attract criticism both for making it easier for some asylum seekers to obtain the right to live and work in the UK, and for presenting a burden on those who may not speak English or have access to help or legal counsel.
cite	V;PST;SG;3	cited	The Guardian cited examples of complicated questions with over fifty words, including: "Were you subject to human trafficking (the recruitment, transportation, transfer, harbouring or receipt of people through force, fraud or deception, with the aim of exploiting them for profit) or modern slavery (severe exploitation of other people for personal or commercial gain) during your journey to or after you arrived in the UK?"
quote	V;PST;SG;3	quoted	It quoted critics like immigration barrister Colin Yeo, and Joint Council for the Welfare of Immigrants campaigner Caitlin Boswell, with Boswell stating: "People fleeing desperate circumstances clearly need this government to make quicker and fairer asylum decisions, but this latest move from government is clumsy, unthinking and could put people’s safety at risk.
jeopardise	V;PST;V.PTCP	jeopardised	"No one’s right to refuge should be jeopardised because they weren't able to fill in an unwieldy form in a language they don’t speak."
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	After the leak, CEO of the Refugee Council Enver Solomon told Sky News any process "must be well thought out": "Moves to reduce the backlog are welcome but the answer is not yet more bureaucratic hurdles and threats of applications being withdrawn.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	living	"After living in worry and uncertainty for months and even years without hearing anything about their claims, it cannot then be fair or reasonable to expect people to complete a lengthy form only in English in a matter of weeks especially for those who don't have access to legal advice and don't speak English."
defend	V;PST;SG;3	defended	During Prime Minister's Questions Wednesday, Sunak defended the government's record whilst promising a "formal update" from Braverman is forthcoming: "The Home Secretary and I are working intensely and as quickly as possible to bring forward that legislation [...] in the meantime our deals with Albania and France are already yielding benefits [...] we want a system whereby if someone arrives in our country illegally they will not be able to stay."
win	V;PRS;PL;3	win	FIFA World Cup 2018 day five: Sweden, Belgium, England win three points
defeat	V;PST;SG;3	defeated	Yesterday, day five of the 2018 FIFA football World Cup, Sweden defeated South Korea 1 – 0; Belgium defeated debutants Panama 3 – 0; and Tunisia lost 1 – 2 to England, with a late goal from English captain Harry Kane.
face	V;PST;SG;3	faced	In the first game of the day, Sweden faced South Korea at the Nizhny Novgorod Stadium.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Sweden had higher ball possession in the game.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	Korea's Kim Shin-Wook was shown a yellow card early in the game.
miss	V;PST;SG;3	missed	In the 20th minute, Marcus Berg missed a chance to put Sweden in the lead.
substitute	V;PST;SG;3	substituted	Korea substituted Park Joo-Ho in the 28th minute of the game due to injury and was replaced by Kim Min-Woo.
open	V;NFIN	open	Despite their attempts, neither side could open the scoring in the first half as it ended 0 – 0.
book	V;PST;V.PTCP	booked	In the second half, Hwang Hee-Chan was booked for a bad foul.
concede	V;PST;SG;3	conceded	In the 62nd minute Kim Min-Woo conceded a penalty which the match referee Joel Aguilar awarded Sweden after confirming with the video assistant referee.
score	V;PST;SG;3	scored	Swedish captain Andreas Granqvist scored from the penalty spot, putting Sweden 1 – 0 up.
go	V;PST;SG;3	went	In the injury time, Hwang Hee-Chan's header went wide to the left, missing a chance to equalise for Korea.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The match ended with Sweden winning three points.
win	V;PST;SG;3	won	Swedish captain Andreas Granqvist won the Budweiser Man of the Match award.
open	V;PRS;V.PTCP	opening	This was Sweden's first victory in the opening match of the World Cup since 1958.
face	V;PST;SG;3	faced	In the first game of Group G, Belgium faced Panama at the Fisht Stadium in Sochi.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Belgium had greater ball possession in the match.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	Belgium started the game with attacking style with Romelu Lukaku, Yannick Carrasco, and Dries Mertens making early attempts to put Belgium into the lead against debutans Panama.
fail	V;PST;SG;3	failed	Dries Mertens's attempt in the 18th minute failed.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The first half ended with 0 – 0 on the scoreboard.
score	V;PST;SG;3	scored	Just two minutes in the second half, Dries Mertens scored a goal from a volley, putting the Belgians in the lead.
save	V;PST;V.PTCP	saved	Panama's attempt in the 54th minute, by Michael Murillo, was saved by Thibaut Courtois.
double	V;PST;SG;3	doubled	In the 69th minute, Romelu Lukaku doubled the lead for Belgium, scoring a header from Kevin De Bruyne's assist.
score	V;PST;SG;3	scored	Six minutes later, Lukaku scored a third goal for Belgium, this time from Eden Hazard's assist.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	In the 90th minute, Belgium made a change, as Nacer Chadli replaced Axel Witsel.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	The second half ended with the Belgians winning three points.
book	V;PST;V.PTCP	booked	Five Panamanian players were booked in the match.
win	V;PST;SG;3	won	Lukaku won the Budweiser Man of the Match award.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	After the match, Hernan Dario Gomez, Panama coach, said, "I think we have learned a lot from today's game.
