accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to the Saudi Interior Ministry over 345 Muslim pilgrims have been killed in a stampede during the annual Hajj pilgrimage near Mecca, Saudi Arabia.
happen	V;PST;SG;3	happened	The stampede at Islam's most holy site happened at Jamarat Bridge, during an event where pebbles are thrown at a pillar to represent the stoning of Satan as part of the final rites of the Hajj.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	The stampede began when luggage from a bus tripped pilgrims at the eastern end of the wall, causing a bottleneck.
trip	V;PST;V.PTCP	tripped	Those who were tripped were then crushed by the wave of people behind them.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	Muslims are required to make a pilgrimage to Mecca during their lifetimes, if physically or financially possible, as it is one of the five pillars of the Islamic faith.
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	Saudi officials had already provided safeguards by installing an oval wall with padded edges to protect pilgrams from a crush, installed security cameras and placed over 60,000 security personnel in the area.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	This is not the first time deadly stampedes have taken place during the Hajj, with the deadliest stampede during the 1991 Hajj, in which 1,426 pilgrims were trampled.
kill	V;PST;V.PTCP	killed	Last week, at least 76 people were killed when the Al-Ghaza Hotel near the Masjid Al-Haram (Grand Mosque) collasped, the cause of which is unknown.
ask	V;PRS;PL;3	ask	Local municipalities in Italy ask taxes from religious schools
start	V;PST;V.PTCP	started	In the past few days in Italy, several municipalities have started asking religious schools to pay taxes for property and local services, despite the resistance of the Catholic Church.
result	V;PRS;SG;3	results	The request of the municipalities results from the sentence of the Italian Supreme Court of Cassation on July 8, recognising as legitimate the request of the Municipality of Livorno asking religious schools to pay property taxes.
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	Requests have come from the Municipality of Bogliasco, next to Genova, and from the deputies of Movimento 5 Stelle (M5S) of the regional counsel of Lombardy.
receive	V;PST;SG;3	received	The first case is of the mayor Luca Pastorino that in last years received several refusals of his requests for payment because of the religious nursery school and retirement home.
ask	V;PRS;SG;3	asks	In the second case, the M5S party asks to the regional government to assure the local administrations of the region regularly apply the sentence of the Supreme Court.
rule	V;PST;V.PTCP	ruled	The case on which the Court has ruled was of the religious schools Santo Spirito and Immacolata in Livorno, Tuscany, to pay over 422,000 euros in arrears for the period from 2004 to 2009.
advance	V;PST;V.PTCP	advanced	The request was advanced by the Municipality of Livorno in 2010.
reason	V;PST;SG;3	reasoned	The city reasoned, "because the users of the private schools pay a frequency fee, this kind of activity is considered as a commercial one" ((it)).
change	V;PST;V.PTCP	changed	In 2014 the Italian municipal tax discipline has changed from the ICI system to the IMU system by the Monti government.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	A mean cost per student criterion is used to tax only the schools that receive a fee higher than the mean cost per student fixed by the State.
request	V;PST;V.PTCP	requested	The new law is not retroactive, so the taxes requested in arrears from 2006 to 2009 are under the ICI system.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Minister of Education Stefania Giannini said a "more general reflection" ((it)) is needed.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Claudio De Vincenti, undersecretary to the prime minister, said "a discussion table will be opened with the non-profit associations, religious association included" ((it)).
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	Undersecretary for Education Mr. Toccafondi says "many schools will increase their fees or they will quit. Then the State will have to find new resources to build new structures and manage them" ((it)).
react	V;PST;V.PTCP	reacted	Also the president of the Lombardy region, Roberto Maroni, has reacted by proposing some regional counter-measures to finance private schools.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	The secretary general of CEI (Italian Episcopal Conference), Nunzio Galantino, has called the sentence "dangerous" ((it)) and "ideological" ((it)):
face	V;PRS;1;PL	face	"We face a dangerous sentence.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	Who takes the decisions, do it with less ideology.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	Because I have the clear sensation that with this way of thinking, they wait the praise of some ideologized supporters.
understand	V;NFIN	understand	Indeed, they don't understand what kind of good service private schools held" ((it)).
concern	V;PST;V.PTCP	concerned	Italian secularist associations are concerned the Government will modify the law in order to maintain an exception for religious schools.
describe	V;PRS;PL;3	describe	The secularist magazine MicroMega describe the court's judgement as historic.
launch	V;PST;V.PTCP	launched	The Union of Rationalist Atheists and Agnostics (UAAR) has launched a petition which now has more than 11,000 signatures, asking the government to respect and execute the sentence of the Supreme Court.
encourage	V;PRS;V.PTCP	encouraging	It is also encouraging citizens to ask for application of the law in their local municipalities.
spread	V;PST;V.PTCP	spread	Warhol's photo legacy spread by university exhibits
donate	V;PST;V.PTCP	donated	Portrait shot of Dennis Hopper, famous for his role in the 1969 film Easy Rider, amongst the Warhol Polaroids donated to USI by the Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts.
open	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Opening	Opening night, January 23, 2014, of the Andy Warhol exhibit of Polaroids and screen prints at the University of Southern Indiana.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Evansville, Indiana, United States -
mark	V;PST;SG;3	marked	This past week marked the opening night of an Andy Warhol exhibit at the University of Southern Indiana.
cultivate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	cultivating	USI's art gallery, like 189 other educational galleries and museums around the country, is a recipient of a major Warhol donor program, and this program is cultivating new interest in Warhol's photographic legacy.
attend	V;PST;PL;3	attended	Wikinews reporters attended the opening and spoke to donors, exhibit organizers and patrons.
celebrate	V;PST;SG;3	celebrated	The USI art gallery celebrated the Thursday opening with its display of Warhol's Polaroids, gelatin silver prints and several colored screen prints.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	USI's exhibit, which is located in Evansville, Indiana, is to run from January 23 through March 9.
base	V;PRS;SG;3	bases	The McCutchan Art Center / Pace Galleries at USI bases its exhibit around roughly 100 Polaroids selected from its collection.
donate	V;PST;V.PTCP	donated	The Polaroids were all donated by the Andy Warhol Photographic Legacy Program, according to Kristen Wilkins, assistant professor of photography and curator of the exhibit.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	The Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts made two donations to USI Art Collections, in 2007 and a second recently.
express	V;PST;SG;3	expressed	Kathryn Waters, director of the gallery, expressed interest in further donations from the foundation in the future.
seed	V;PST;V.PTCP	seeded	Since 2007 the Andy Warhol Photographic Legacy Program has seeded university art galleries throughout the United States with over 28,000 Andy Warhol photographs and other artifacts.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	The program takes a decentralized approach to Warhol's photography collection and encourages university art galleries to regularly disseminate and educate audiences about Warhol's artistic vision, especially in the area of photography.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Wikinews provides additional video, audio and photographs so our readers may learn more.
observe	V;PST;SG;3	observed	Wilkins observed that the 2007 starting date of the donation program, which is part of the Andy Warhol Foundation for the Visual Arts, coincided with the 20th anniversary of Andy Warhol's death in 1987.
receive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	receiving	USI was not alone in receiving a donation.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	K.C. Maurer, chief financial officer and treasurer at the Andy Warhol Foundation, said 500 institutions received the initial invitation and currently 190 universities have accepted one or more donations.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Institutional recipients, said Mauer, are required to exhibit their donated Warhol photographs every ten years as one stipulation.
hold	V;PRS;V.PTCP	holding	While USI is holding its exhibit, there are also Warhol Polaroid exhibits at the Tang Teaching Museum and Art Gallery at Skidmore College in Saratoga Springs, New York and an Edward Steichen and Andy Warhol exhibit at the Mary & Leigh Block Museum of Art at Northwestern University in Evanston, Illinois.
receive	V;PST;V.PTCP	received	All have received Polaroids from the foundation.
reach	V;NFIN	reach	University exhibits can reach out and attract large audiences.
see	V;PST;SG;3	saw	For example, the Weatherspoon Art Museum at the University of North Carolina at Greensboro saw attendance levels reach 11,000 visitors when it exhibited its Warhol collection in 2010, according to curator Elaine Gustafon.
combine	V;PRS;V.PTCP	combining	That exhibit was part of a collaboration combining the collections from Duke University, located in Durham, North Carolina, and University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, which also were recipients of donated items from the Andy Warhol Photographic Legacy Program.
donate	V;PST;V.PTCP	donated	Each collection donated by the Andy Warhol Photographic Legacy Program holds Polaroids of well-known celebrities.
include	V;PST;SG;3	included	The successful UNC Greensboro exhibit included Polaroids of author Truman Capote and singer-songwriter Carly Simon.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	"I think America's obsession with celebrity culture is as strong today as it was when Warhol was living", said Gustafon.
intrigue	V;PST;V.PTCP	intrigued	"People are still intrigued by how stars live, dress and socialize, since it is so different from most people's every day lives."
explain	V;PST;SG;3	explained	Wilkins explained Warhol's obsession with celebrities began when he first collected head shots as a kid and continued as a passion throughout his life.
hang	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hanging	"He's hanging out with the celebrities, and has kind of become the same sort of celebrity he was interested in documenting earlier in his career", Wilkins said.
include	V;PRS;SG;3	includes	The exhibit at USI includes Polaroids of actor Dennis Hopper; musician Nick Rhodes of Duran Duran; publishers Jann Wenner of Rolling Stone Magazine and Carlo De Benedetti of Italy's la Repubblica; disco club owner Steve Rubell of Studio 54; photographers Nat Finkelstein, Christopher Makos and Felice Quinto; and athletes Vitas Gerulaitis (tennis) and Jack Nicklaus (golf).
observe	V;PST;SG;3	observed	Wikinews observed the USI exhibit identifies and features Polaroids of fashion designer Halston, a former resident of Evansville.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	University collections across the United States also include Polaroids of "unknowns" who have not yet had their fifteen minutes of fame.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Cynthia Thompson, curator and director of exhibits at the University of Arkansas, Fayetteville, said, "These images serve as documentation of people in his every day life and art - one which many of us enjoy a glimpse into."
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	Warhol was close to important touchstones of the 1960s, including art, music, consumer culture, fashion, and celebrity worship, which were all buzzwords and images Wikinews observed at USI's opening exhibit.
think	V;PST;SG;3	thought	"Pop art was about what popular American culture really thought was important", Kathryn Waters said.
do	V;PST;SG;3	did	"That's why he did the Campbell Soup cans or the Marilyn pictures, these iconic products of American culture whether they be in film, video or actually products we consumed.
know	V;PRS;1;PL	know	Which as we all know in 2014, has only increased probably a thousand fold."
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	"I think everybody knows Andy Warhol's name, even non-art people, that's a name they might know because he was such a personality", Water said.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Hilary Braysmith, USI associate professor of art history, said, "I think his photography is equally influential as his graphic works, his more famous pictures of Marilyn.
paint	V;PRS;V.PTCP	painting	In terms of the evolution of photography and experimentation, like painting on them or the celebrity fascination, I think he was really ground-breaking in that regard."
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	The Polaroid format is not what made Warhol famous, however, he is in the company of other well-known photographers who used the camera, such as Ansel Adams, Chuck Close, Walker Evans, Robert Mapplethorpe, and Helmut Newton.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Wilkins said," [Warhol] liked the way photo booths and the Polaroid's front flash looked".
explain	V;PST;SG;3	explained	She explained how Warhol's adoption of the Polaroid camera revealed his process.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	According	According to Wilkins, Warhol was able to reproduce the Polaroid photograph and create an enlargement of it, which he then could use to commit the image to the silk screen medium by applying paint or manipulating them further.
exhibit	V;PST;V.PTCP	exhibited	One of the silk screens exhibited at USI this time was the Annie Oakley screen print called "Cowboys and Indians" from 1987.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Wilkins also said Warhol was both an artist and a businessperson.
commercialize	V;NFIN	commercialize	"As a way to commercialize his work, he would make a blue Marilyn and a pink Marilyn and a yellow Marilyn, and then you could pick your favorite color and buy that.
want	V;PST;SG;3	wanted	"He wanted to be rich and famous and he made lots of choices to go that way", Wilkins said.
admit	V;PST;SG;3	admitted	Kiara Perkins, a second year USI art major, admitted she was willing to skip class Thursday night to attend the opening exhibit but then circumstances allowed for her to attend the exhibit.
want	V;NFIN	want	Why did she so badly want to attend?
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	He said, "Andy Warhol was the center of the Zeitgeist for the 20th century and everything since.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Allton said he had only seen the Silver Clouds installation before in film.
blow	V;PST;V.PTCP	blown	The Silver Clouds installation were silver balloons blown up with helium, and those balloons filled one of the smaller rooms in the gallery.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	"I thought that in real life it was really kind of magical," Allton said.
smack	V;PST;SG;1	smacked	"I smacked them around."
recreate	V;PST;V.PTCP	recreated	Elements of the Zeitgeist were also playfully recreated on USI's opening night.
point	V;PST;SG;3	pointed	In her opening remarks for attendees, Waters pointed out those features to attendees, noting the touches of the Warhol Factory, or the studio where he worked, that were present around them.
point	V;PST;SG;3	pointed	She pointed to the refreshment table with Campbell's Soup served with "electric" Kool Aid and tables adorned with colorful gumball "pills".
observe	V;PST;SG;3	observed	The big hit of the evening, Wikinews observed from the long line, was the Polaroid-room where attendees could wear a Warhol-like wig or don crazy glasses and have their own Polaroid taken.
display	V;PST;V.PTCP	displayed	The Polaroids were ready in an instant and immediately displayed at the entry of the exhibit.
become	V;PST;PL;3	became	Exhibit goers then became part of the very exhibit they had wanted to attend.
observe	V;PST;SG;3	observed	In fact, many people Wikinews observed took out their mobiles as they left for the evening and used their own phone cameras to make one further record of the moment - a photo of a photo.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	Perhaps they had learned an important lesson from the Warhol exhibit that cultural events like these were ripe for use and reuse.
call	V;NFIN	call	We might even call these exit instant snap shots, the self selfie.
analyse	V;PRS;PL;3	analyse	Scientists analyse effects of global warming, atmospheric ozone on crops
analyse	V;PST;V.PTCP	analysed	A research team from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology and Colorado State University of the US and the University of Sheffield of the UK has analysed effects of global warming and ozone pollution over 2000 - 2050 on the worldwide production of wheat, rice, maize and soybean.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	The study was published in journal Nature Climate Change yesterday.
find	V;PST;PL;3	found	The scientists found reduction of crop yields by 2050 exceeded 10% of 2000 levels, substantially decreasing food security, in all cases examined.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	Several scenarios were considered because of uncertainty of future levels of ozone pollution.
estimate	V;PST;PL;3	estimated	They estimated by 2050, increasing population and changing diet would increase world food needs by 50 percent.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	As coauthor Colette Heald told The Huffington Post, "The climate projections are quite consistent [...]
lead	V;PRS;V.PTCP	leading	the future of ozone pollution is very different [...] leading to either offsetting or reinforcing effects [of climate change] on crops".
increase	V;NFIN	increase	By 2050, undernourishment would increase by either 49 percent or by 27 percent, depending on the scenario.
focus	V;PRS;SG;3	focuses	The study focuses on ozone-temperature covariation: ground-level ozone increases with temperatures.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	Heald said although temperature and ozone are separately known to impact crop yields, "nobody has looked at these together".
depend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Depending	Depending on region and crops, the yields may be primarily sensitive to ozone - in the case of wheat - or heat - in the case of maize - alone, providing a local estimation of relative benefits of climate change adaptation versus ozone regulation.
note	V;PRS;SG;3	notes	The U.S. Department of Agriculture notes, "Ground-level ozone causes more damage to plants than all other air pollutants combined", highlighting the importance of air quality for agriculture.
publish	V;PST;V.PTCP	published	Results of NCLAN studies, published in a paper by AS Heagle in 1989, show dicot species, such as soybean, cotton, and peanut, lose more yield from ozone than do monocot species such as sorghum, field corn, and winter wheat.
find	V;PST;PL;3	found	The researchers found that ozone pollution caused 46 percent of previously heat-attributed damage to soybean crops.
include	V;NFIN	include	The model does not include the effect of rising carbon dioxide concentration, which has complex and potentially offsetting impacts on global food supply.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency says some crops may have higher yields with increased levels of carbon dioxide.
increase	V;PRS;SG;3	increases	However, global warming also increases probability of extreme crops-damaging weather events such as floods, droughts, and extreme temperatures.
affect	V;PRS;SG;3	affects	Climate change affects distribution of weeds, pests, and diseases.
note	V;PST;SG;3	noted	Heald noted the findings show pollution reduction is also important.
improve	V;NFIN	improve	"An air-quality cleanup would improve crop yields [...]
understand	V;PRS;1;PL	understand	Ozone is something that we understand the causes of, and the steps that need to be taken to improve air quality."
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	As Heald told The Huffington Post, US surface ozone has dropped partly due to the Clean Air Act.
drive	V;PST;V.PTCP	driven	"Despite an increase in vehicle miles driven and energy consumption, surface ozone has declined by 25 percent on average across the U.S. from 1980 to 2012 [...]
depend	V;NFIN	depend	However, the future of ozone air quality in the U.S. and around the world will depend on local emissions, the use of pollution control technology, regulations, and air quality policy."
support	V;PST;V.PTCP	supported	The study was supported by the Croucher Foundation, US National Science Foundation, and US National Park Service.
rule	V;PRS;SG;3	rules	Greek court rules worship of ancient Greek deities is legal
rule	V;PST;V.PTCP	ruled	Greek court has ruled that worshippers of the ancient Greek religion may now formally associate and worship at archeological sites.
ban	V;PST;V.PTCP	banned	Prior to the ruling, the religion was banned from conducting public worship at archeological sites by the Greek Ministry of Culture.
estimate	V;PRS;SG;3	estimates	The Greek Orthodox Church estimates that number is closer to 40,000.
use	V;PRS;PL;3	use	Many neo-pagan religions, such as Wicca, use aspects of ancient Greek religions in their practice; Hellenic polytheism instead focuses exclusively on the ancient religions, as far as the fragmentary nature of the surviving source material allows.
say	V;NFIN	say	Would you say there has to be a negative consequence to the thing you can't stop?
think	V;IMP;2	think	No, because think of functional alcoholics, right?
manage	V;NFIN	manage	Somebody can manage to be a teacher.
manage	V;NFIN	manage	Somebody can manage to be a cop.
manage	V;NFIN	manage	Somebody can manage to be the mayor of Toronto.
hit	V;PRS;SG;3	hits	And somehow the blowback never hits them.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	Yeah, they've lost control over use, right?
manage	V;PRS;V.PTCP	managing	Even though they're managing to sort of stickhandle their way through life.
forget	V;PST;SG;1	forgot	Oh, but one of the things I forgot is one of the things is a negative impact on your health as well.
go	V;NFIN	go	Well, you can go for a long, long, say, alcoholism.
go	V;NFIN	go	You can go a decade or more before you actually suffer significant health consequences from heavy, heavy alcohol use.
like	V;NFIN	like	So what I'd like to find out by the time this episode is over is whether I'm addicted to sugar or I just really like it.
guess	V;PRS;SG;1	guess	Well, yeah, I guess --
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	Have you lost control over your consumption of sugar?
guess	V;PRS;SG;1	guess	I guess I have to define --
exhibit	V;PST;V.PTCP	exhibited	Has he ever exhibited any control whatsoever?
guess	V;PRS;SG;1	guess	Yeah, I guess I have to define what that means.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	What control means.
keep	V;NFIN	keep	Do you keep using despite wanting to stop?
have	V;NFIN	have	Then you may have an addiction to sugar.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	So I've got a list of a few specific addictions that we're probably covering.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Well, one of them we probably won't, because I think we're going to have an episode specifically on that.
consume	V;PRS;SG;3	consumes	But drug abuse, of course, is a pattern use of a substance drug in which the user consumes the substance in amounts or with methods which are harmful to themselves or others.
add	V;PRS;SG;3	adds	This adds in the harm aspect of it.
devise	V;PST;V.PTCP	devised	Sexual addiction is a conceptual model devised in order to provide a scientific explanation for sexual urges, behaviors or thoughts that appear extreme in frequency or feel out of one's control in terms of being a literal addiction to sexual activity.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's some --
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	And again, it's probably just getting into what do you call addiction.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	This is obviously something that you want and it can be causing problems.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	So I think it gets covered here.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Problem gambling, I think we might have a gambling episode, so I'm not going to touch too much on this.
