know	V;PRS;1;PL	know	We know that this is a virus of a type called coronavirus, which makes it part of the family of viruses that include the common cold, SARS and MERS.
distinguish	V;PRS;SG;3	distinguishes	Um, now, what distinguishes this new virus technically called SARS-CoV-2?
call	V;PRS;SG;1	call	Now, I usually just call it Covid-19, like most reports do.
distinguish	V;PRS;SG;3	distinguishes	But what distinguishes it is that it is highly infectious and highly virulent.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	Now, highly infectious means it is easily passed from one person to another.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	Highly virulent means it can cause death easily.
compare	V;PST;V.PTCP	compared	It has been compared in this respect to the 1918 flu pandemic, which we estimated infected about 500 million people worldwide.
hold	V;PRS;SG;3	holds	Now, that was about one third of the population at that time, so that would be, if the same proportion holds true, that would be over 2 billion people, um, in 2020.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	I've seen estimates go as high as 100 million.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	Now, you know, why don't we know a precise number?
assign	V;PRS;V.PTCP	assigning	Well, assigning cause of death is not a precise science.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	Um, so you know that -- but that gives you an idea of what that could be, uh, so we could be talking about, uh, again, keeping the proportion the same, about 200 million people dying if it was the same as 1918.
think	V;NFIN	think	Now, I don't think it's going to be quite that bad, uh, because medical science has advanced.
help	V;NFIN	help	Um so what can help here?
crawl	V;PST;V.PTCP	crawled	Thankfully, the antivaxxers have crawled back under whatever rocks they live under for the time being.
appear	V;PRS;SG;3	appears	The good news is that the coronavirus appears to be one that does not mutate a lot, which means the vaccine is likely to be more effective than the flu vaccine, which is kind of guesswork year by year and that is because the influenza virus changes every year.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	Now, scientists are working on a vaccine right now in a crash program.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	Uh, and again, there's some good news on that front.
sequence	V;NFIN	sequence	First, Chinese scientists were able to DNA sequence the virus pretty quickly.
put	V;PRS;SG;3	puts	That is one of those advances in medical technology that puts us in a better position than we were in 1918.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	We've learned how to do rapid DNA sequencing.
find	V;PST;PL;3	found	They found that this virus shares 80 to 90% of its DNA with the SARS virus, which is one of the reasons why it's named SARS-CoV-2.
have	V;NFIN	have	Uh, unfortunately, we don't have a SARS vaccine, uh, because that outbreak died off before work was completed.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	Um, and you know why that was?
clamp	V;PST;PL;3	clamped	Because they clamped down and forced people to stay home, and that's what stopped the disease.
start	V;PST;PL;3	started	Um, now, the fact that they started on it means that there's work there that we can build on.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	But in general, most researchers have said it will take as much as 18 months, even with a crash program.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	Uh, and I've seen a few people say that even 18 months is ridiculously optimistic.
take	V;PRS;PL;3	take	Usually vaccines take a lot longer.
find	V;PRS;SG;2	find	Uh, the -- you find a candidate and then first thing you do is, is animal studies uh, both for safety and for efficacy.
cause	V;PRS;V.PTCP	causing	Uh, for example, one of the early candidates for a SARS vaccine was actually causing harm to the animals who received it.
go	V;NFIN	go	Uh, well, you don't go forward with that.
figure	V;PST;PL;3	figured	Uh, they figured out -- they think they figured out what caused that problem and reformulated it in a way that they don't have that problem anymore.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	Um, but, you know, if the animal trials are promising, you can move on to human trials.
test	V;NFIN	test	Uh, stage one is to test with a small number of healthy volunteers looking specifically to test safety.
want	V;NFIN	want	You don't want to administer a vaccine that kills everyone or even a significant number of people.
pass	V;PRS;SG;3	passes	Now, if the candidate passes that test, stage two is to administer the vaccine to a few hundred people in an area where the disease is rampant.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	If it looks like it is working and no safety issues have arisen, a final trial can involve several thousand people.
pass	V;PRS;SG;3	passes	If it passes that, it can be approved for medical use and move into production.
add	V;PRS;SG;3	adds	Now that's not the end of it because production adds even more delays.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	It takes a long time.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	according	Uh, according to Ronald Klain, who was the Obama administration's Ebola czar and, you know, that's what first got people in our federal government seriously thinking about this, at least until Trump came along, and what Klain says is" developing and testing the vaccine, as hard as it is, isn't the hardest part.
manufacture	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Manufacturing	Manufacturing hundreds of millions of doses and getting people to take the vaccine will be harder and take longer."
read	V;PRS;SG;1	read	Now, when I read that, the first thing that jumps into my mind is hundreds of millions.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	Uh, that's just, you know, the population of the United States.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	Well, you know, if that's all you're concerned with, fine.
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	But I think people all over the world are going to want this.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	Um, so that means we need to be talking about billions.
accelerate	V;PST;V.PTCP	accelerated	Now, this process can be accelerated to some degree, but not by as much as you might think.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Doing	Doing the research to find a candidate that might work will take time.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	You know, frequently it starts, um, basically in Petri dishes where you put in, uh samples of the virus and then add a candidate and, you know, see if it has any effect and stuff like that.
address	V;NFIN	address	It is my special pleasure to address the fifty-ninth session of the General Assembly of the United Nations.
enable	V;PST;V.PTCP	enabled	This universal forum has enabled the peoples of the world to coordinate and harmonize their efforts in preserving peace and security, in achieving prosperity and upholding the values of human civilization.
like	V;NFIN	like	I would like to congratulate you, Mr. President, on being elected to preside over this august body and to express my confidence in your successful leadership.
like	V;NFIN	like	I would also like, on behalf of Albania, to convey to the Secretary-General our highest appreciation for his leading contribution to the fulfilment of the historical mission of the United Nations and to the aspirations of our peoples for a better world in which only peace, democracy and economic prosperity reign.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	This session of the General Assembly takes place in a milestone year for Europe.
vitalize	V;PST;SG;3	vitalized	Only a few months ago, a historic event vitalized the dream of a more united and stronger Europe.
share	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sharing	Ten European countries, sharing a common aspiration for integration, were admitted into the European Union.
engage	V;PST;V.PTCP	engaged	The Albanian Government and society are engaged in an all-round European integration process, and they are carrying out necessary reforms with the necessary political will and drive.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	Albania is working to strengthen the democratic institutions and capacities of its central and local government with a view to bringing its legislation and conditions into line with European Union standards, progressively achieving concrete and measurable results, as previously defined.
determine	V;PST;V.PTCP	Determined	Determined to make our contribution to regional and global security, the Albanian Government considers the country's integration into NATO to be one of its major objectives.
commend	V;PST;SG;3	commended	The NATO Istanbul Summit last June commended the progress made by my country in this regard and encouraged the deepening of reforms.
welcome	V;PST;SG;3	welcomed	It welcomed Albania's contribution to regional stability and cooperation and committed to assess our further progress at the next NATO summit, which would hopefully mark the start of negotiations for our full membership in the alliance.
believe	V;PRS;1;PL	believe	We believe that the commitments set up in the framework of the Adriatic Charter are also contributing to meet NATO standards, to the benefit of regional stability and security.
remain	V;PRS;1;PL	remain	We remain truly convinced that the respect and protection of human rights and freedoms are the main pillars of a pluralist democratic society.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	The Government of the Republic of Albania will continue to constantly demonstrate its commitment to a full implementation of the standards enshrined in the United Nations and Council of Europe conventions, as well as the relevant documents of regional organizations, such as the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe, in which we are playing a very active role.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	We will continue to do so in the future as well.
adopt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	adopting	By adopting contemporary legislation and by becoming a party to other recently adopted international instruments on human rights, Albania has associated itself with countries that are enforcing a number of national strategies and programmes focusing on specific population groups, especially women, children, persons in need and minorities, among others.
start	V;PST;V.PTCP	started	In recent years, the Albanian Government has started to periodically submit reports to the committees of the six main United Nations human rights treaties and is taking their recommendations into serious consideration.
play	V;PRS;PL;3	play	It is our belief that these international mechanisms play a significant and important role as guardians of the common human values we have together adopted.
remain	V;PRS;SG;3	remains	Albania, as one of the 191 signatory countries of the Millennium Declaration, remains fully committed to implementation of the Millennium Development Goals, which have been properly reflected in a national strategy for social and economic development.
maintain	V;NFIN	maintain	The pattern of our sustained economic growth, which is at 6 to 7 per cent for the seventh consecutive year, is a good support base for the whole society to maintain higher standards of respect for human rights and to fight poverty and crime.
like	V;NFIN	like	I would like to take this opportunity to thank the United Nations specialized agencies for their valuable assistance in support of the implementation of these objectives nationwide and on a central and local level.
note	V;NFIN	note	The Albanian Government is pleased to note that an ever-healthier climate of confidence and relations of bilateral and multilateral cooperation are prevailing in the South-Eastern European region.
engage	V;PST;V.PTCP	engaged	The countries of the region are fully engaged in a process that will lead us clearly toward Euro-Atlantic integration and at the same time continually remove us from the extremist tendencies that caused a number of conflicts in the last decade.
guide	V;PST;V.PTCP	guided	The regional policy of my Government is guided by the motto: 'the more integrated in the region, the more integrated in Europe.'
implement	V;PRS;V.PTCP	implementing	In implementing this policy, Albania is cooperating as never before with all the countries of the region by making borders less relevant, opening up to one regional market the implementation of free trade agreements and free movement corridors, and stimulating foreign direct investment.
cooperate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	cooperating	We are especially cooperating with the common fight against organized crime and all kinds of illegal trafficking.
reinforce	V;PRS;V.PTCP	reinforcing	In the meantime, Albania is reinforcing its border management and control, guided by the European Union and NATO standards.
hold	V;NFIN	hold	My country will hold general parliamentary elections by the middle of next year.
commit	V;PST;V.PTCP	committed	We are committed to doing all that is required to ensure that the election process is fully compatible with international standards for free and fair elections.
commit	V;PST;V.PTCP	committed	To that end, we are committed to continuing to work with relevant specialized institutions on elections by fully implementing especially OSCE/Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights (ODIHR) recommendations.
strengthen	V;PRS;V.PTCP	strengthening	In the context of strengthening regional cooperation, my country is committed to a more enhanced partnership with all regional organizations and initiatives that serve the promotion of good neighbourly relations; the strengthening of regional peace, security and stability; ensuring political support for integration into Euro-Atlantic structures, and attracting financing for national and regional projects in priority fields such as energy, transportation and telecommunications.
face	V;PRS;V.PTCP	facing	On its path towards European integration, the South-East European region is still facing pending challenges, such as the future of Kosovo.
appreciate	V;PRS;SG;3	appreciates	Albania greatly appreciates the recent democratic and integration developments in Kosovo, thanks to the efforts of the respective peoples and the partnership of its self-governing institutions with the United Nations Interim Administration Mission in Kosovo (UNMIK).
allow	V;IMP;2	Allow	Allow me at this point to congratulate Mr. Soren Jessen-Petersen, Special Representative for Kosovo of the United Nations Secretary-General, in assuming this very important duty.
tour	V;PRS;SG;1	tour	When I tour around Taiwan, of course, I also find people taking action without even waiting for the ministries to take action.
change	V;PRS;SG;2	change	If you change the O to a zero, you get into them, considering environmental agency.
care	V;PRS;PL;3	care	This is what people care about, the air quality, PM2.5 in Taiwan.
install	V;PST;PL;3	installed	More than 2,000 people, actually, installed these in their balconies, in their schools and so on, to get a real-time sensor of their IoT devices but not just IoT.
upload	V;PRS;PL;3	upload	They also upload it to the public cloud, which then uploads it to a distributed ledger to make sure that nobody can modify those numbers after the fact.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	After that, we see this kind of innovation is very rare in Asia.
talk	V;PRS;SG;1	talk	When I talk to all the UN-related bodies, many other Asian countries tell me that they won't let this network grow to be 2,000 nodes strong.
try	V;NFIN	try	If it's 200 nodes strong, they will try to poach the leader to the government.
refuse	V;PRS;PL;3	refuse	If they refuse, then maybe, they get disappeared because they really challenge the legitimacy of the environmental agency.
report	V;PRS;SG;3	reports	If the environmental agency group reports one number and your neighbors report another number, of course, you're going to trust the one that you participated yourself, even though it's a lower quality sensor.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	Because of that, it's very rare in Taiwan because we have an expanding civic space.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	The government takes the approach of, "we can't beat them, let's join them".
manufacture	V;PRS;1;PL	manufacture	We manufacture low-cost, high-precision sensors for them, but we also see this as a map of digital gap in Taiwan, a digital divide.
set	V;PRS;1;PL	set	We set up points when the people don't have the resource to go to, indigenous places, places in the mountains.
talk	V;PRS;1;PL	talk	We also talk to the citizen scientists and they say they really want a point here to tell the air pollution from outside Taiwan or from inside Taiwan, domestically.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's no way the citizen scientists can install an AirBox there, even if they're very good at drones and so on.
stay	V;NFIN	stay	It's impossible to stay there forever.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We can because we have wind turbines.
install	V;NFIN	install	We can install those sensor networks as part of the complementary action.
download	V;PRS;SG;2	download	You just download, put it onto Raspberry Pi, and then, you can do it yourself, as people have done over the world.
change	V;NFIN	change	If you don't change the source code, it uploads to the Taiwan network by default, so we kind of have the numbers of all the atmosphere and meteorological data.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have a website dedicated for this, the Civil IoT project at CI.taiwan.gov.tw, that is basically, collective intelligence, meteorological, air quality, earthquake, and disaster prevention, and things like that.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have single websites like this, CI for Collective Intelligence, AI for AI Taiwan, SI for Social Innovation, Smart Taiwan for the Smart Taiwan plan, and also, Bio Taiwan.
say	V;PRS;SG;3	says	The medical industry says, "You really should call it biomed.taiwan because bio and med are different things."
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	Biomed Taiwan goes to the same website as Bio Taiwan.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	In any case, what we're doing is now is breaking across ministry and municipality and national government because this says nothing about the level of the government or the departments.
curate	V;PST;V.PTCP	curated	It is one, single message that is collaboratively curated by everybody.
solve	V;PST;1;PL	solved	Through this, we solved not just our local social and environmental issues through economic approaches but using the SDGs as a map to unite the efforts together.
say	V;PRS;V.PTCP	saying	Just by saying "Taiwan can help", we mean specifically, 17, 18, which is the availability of reliable data that people from across sectors, across jurisdictions, can trust the data.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	Then, based on the data, we encourage cross-sectoral partnerships.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	Then, based on that, we make open innovations that we, then, export, not as colonizers but, really, co-creators.
download	V;NFIN;2	download	Just download it on GitHub and then, we devise something together in a way that has free access to science and technology.
join	V;PST;SG;1	joined	When I joined the cabinet two years ago, based on these ideas -- with a contract -- the administration asked me for a job description, because they've never seen anything like this before.
write	V;PST;SG;1	wrote	Instead of a job description, I just wrote them a poem, a prayer, which I'm going to read to you as a conclusion.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	To me, it means the shift from IT or ICT to digital, the shift from the sectors separately as attack or an innovation, or whatever, into the humanity as a whole.
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	It goes like this.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	"When we see the Internet of Things, let's make it an Internet of Beings.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	When we see virtual reality, let's make it a shared reality.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	When we see machine learning, let's make it collaborative learning.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	When we see user experience, let's make it about human experience, and whenever we hear that a singularity is near, let's keep in mind and always remember that a plurality is here."
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you so much.
make	V;PST;1;PL	made	Long years ago we made a tryst with destiny, and now the time comes when we shall redeem our pledge, not wholly or in full measure, but very substantially.
sleep	V;PRS;SG;3	sleeps	At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	A moment comes, which comes but rarely in history, when we step out from the old to the new, when an age ends, and when the soul of a nation, long suppressed, finds utterance.
take	V;PRS;1;PL	take	It is fitting that at this solemn moment, we take the pledge of dedication to the service of India and her people and to the still larger cause of humanity.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	At the dawn of history, India started on her unending quest, and trackless centuries are filled with her striving and grandeur of her success and failures.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	Through good and ill fortune alike, she has never lost sight of that quest, forgotten the ideals which gave her strength.
end	V;PRS;1;PL	end	We end today a period of misfortunes and India discovers herself again.
celebrate	V;PRS;1;PL	celebrate	The achievement we celebrate today is but a step, an opening of opportunity to the greater triumphs and achievements that await us.
grasp	V;NFIN	grasp	Are we brave enough and wise enough to grasp this opportunity and accept the challenge of the future?
bring	V;PRS;PL;3	bring	Freedom and power bring responsibility.
rest	V;PRS;SG;3	rests	The responsibility rests upon this Assembly, a sovereign body representing the sovereign people of India.
endure	V;PST;V.PTCP	endured	Before the birth of freedom, we have endured all the pains of labour and our hearts are heavy with the memory of this sorrrow.
continue	V;PRS;PL;3	continue	Some of those pains continue even now.
beckon	V;PRS;SG;3	beckons	Nevertheless, the past is over and it is the future that beckons us now.
fulfill	V;NFIN	fulfill	That future is not one of ease or resting but of incessant striving so that we may fulfill the pledges we have so often taken and the one we shall take today.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	The service of India means, the service of the millions who suffer.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	It means the ending of poverty and ignorance and poverty and disease and inequality of opportunity.
wipe	V;NFIN	wipe	The ambition of the greatest men of our generation has been to wipe every tear from every eye.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	That may be beyond us, but as long as there are tears and suffering, so long our work will not be over.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	And so we have to labour and to work, and to work hard, to give reality to our dreams.
knit	V;PST;V.PTCP	knit	Those dreams are for India, but they are also for the world, for all the nations and peoples are too closely knit together today for any one of them to imagine that it can live apart.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	Peace is said to be indivisible, so is freedom, so is prosperity now, and also is disaster in this one world that can no longer be split into isolated fragments.
make	V;PRS;1;PL	make	To the people of India, whose representatives we are, we make an appeal to join us with faith and confidence in this great adventure.
blame	V;PRS;V.PTCP	blaming	This is no time for petty and destructive criticism, no time for illwill or blaming others.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have to build the noble mansion of free India where all her children may dwell.
appoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	appointed	The appointed day has come -the day appointed by destiny- and India stands forth again, after long slumber and struggle, awake, vital, free and independent.
cling	V;PRS;SG;3	clings	The past clings on to us still in some measure and we have to do much before we redeem the pledges we have so often taken.
begin	V;PRS;SG;3	begins	Yet the turning - point is past, and history begins anew for us, the history which we shall live and act and others will write about.
rise	V;PRS;SG;3	rises	A new star rises, the star of freedom in the East, a new hope comes into being, a vision long cherished materializes.
set	V;NFIN	set	May the star never set and that hope never be betrayed!
rejoice	V;PRS;1;PL	rejoice	We rejoice in that freedom, even though clouds surround us, and many of our people are sorrowstricken and difficult problems encompass us.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	brings	But freedom brings responsibilities and burdens and we have to face them in the spirit of a free and disciplined people.
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	On this day our first thoughts go to the architect of this freedom, the Father of our Nation, who, embodying the old spirit of India, held aloft the torch of freedom and lighted up the darkness that surrounded us.
stray	V;PST;V.PTCP	strayed	We have often been unworthy followers of his and have strayed from his message, but not only we but succeeding generations will remember this message and bear the imprint in their hearts of this great son of India, magnificent in his faith and strength and courage and humility.
allow	V;NFIN	allow	We shall never allow that torch of freedom to be blown out, however high the wind or stormy the tempest.
serve	V;PST;V.PTCP	served	Our next thoughts must be of the unknown volunteers and soldiers of freedom who, without praise or reward, have served India even unto death.
think	V;PRS;1;PL	think	We think also of our brothers and sisters who have been cut off from us by political boundaries and who unhappily can not share at present in the freedom that has come.
remain	V;NFIN	remain	They are of us and will remain of us whatever may happen, and we shall be sharers in their good [or] ill fortune alike.
beckon	V;PRS;SG;3	beckons	The future beckons to us.
go	V;NFIN	go	Whither do we go and what shall be our endeavour?
bring	V;NFIN	bring	To bring freedom and opportunity to the common man, to the peasants and workers of India; to fight and end poverty and ignorance and disease; to build up a prosperous, democratic and progressive nation, and to create social, economic and political institutions which will ensure justice and fullness of life to every man and woman.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have hard work ahead.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is no resting for any one of us till we redeem our pledge in full, till we make all the people of India what destiny intended them to be.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We are citizens of a great country on the verge of bold advance, and we have to live up to that high standard.
belong	V;NFIN	belong	All of us, to whatever religion we may belong, are equally the children of India with equal rights, privileges and obligations.
encourage	V;NFIN	encourage	We cannot encourage communalism or narrow - mindedness, for no nation can be great whose people are narrow in thought or in action.
send	V;PRS;1;PL	send	To the nations and peoples of the world we send greetings and pledge ourselves to cooperate with them in furthering peace, freedom and democracy.
love	V;PST;V.PTCP	loved	And to India, our much - loved motherland, the ancient, the eternal and the ever - new, we pay our reverent homage and we bind ourselves afresh to her service.
deliver	V;PST;SG;3	delivered	Washington, D.C.–Speaker Nancy Pelosi delivered remarks on the Floor of the House of Representatives in support of H. R. 1280, the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act of 2021.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you, Mr. Chair.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you, Madam Speaker.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	I thank the gentleman from New York, the distinguished Chair of the Judiciary Committee, for yielding and for his leadership in bringing this important legislation to the Floor.
commend	V;PRS;SG;1	commend	And I commend Congresswoman Karen Bass, Madam Chair, for your great leadership in this important legislation.
gasp	V;PST;SG;3	gasped	Madam Speaker, nearly one year ago, George Floyd gasped his last words, ‘I can't breathe,’ and ignited a nationwide reckoning on the racial injustice and police brutality in America.
take	V;PST;PL;3	took	Americans from every corner of the country took to the streets to peacefully protest violence against Black Americans, waving Black Lives Matter flags, chanting the names of the murdered, repeating George Floyd's dying words, ‘I can't breathe.’
turn	V;PST;PL;3	turned	They turned their agony into action, but tragically, despite these mass protests, the injustice, killings continued.
accept	V;NFIN	accept	Here, as Members of Congress and as Americans, we cannot accept this epidemic of injustice.
stay	V;NFIN	stay	We cannot stay silent when one–when our most vulnerable and historically marginalized communities, people of color, those living in poverty, Americans with disabilities are being targeted and sometimes killed.
pass	V;NFIN	pass	That is why, today, the House will again pass the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act and send it to the Senate and the President's desk so that it can finally become the law of the land.
salute	V;PRS;SG;1	salute	I salute Congresswoman Karen Bass who has been relentless, persistent and absolutely courageous in her leadership on this legislation.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you to the Congressional Black Caucus and its Chair, Joyce Beatty.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	And Judiciary Chair Jerry Nadler, thank you.
transform	V;PRS;SG;3	transforms	The George Floyd Justice in Policing Act fundamentally transforms the culture of policing with strong, unprecedented reforms.
erase	V;NFIN	erase	This legislation will not erase centuries of systemic racism and excessive policing in America.
bring	V;NFIN	bring	It will not bring back George Floyd, Breonna Taylor–say her name, Breonna Taylor–Ahmaud Arbery, or the countless other men and women who died or were senselessly injured.
make	V;NFIN	make	But it will make a tremendous–take a tremendous step toward–or to stop the violence, stem the suffering and start to build a healthier, better relationship between law enforcement and communities that they respect.
salute	V;PRS;PL;3	salute	All of us here salute and are proudly, profoundly grateful to our law enforcement heroes.
grow	V;PST;SG;1	grew	I grew up in a family, public service family.
have	V;PST;PL;3	had	And they had a motto about the police: ‘Be true to the men in blue.’
raise	V;PST;V.PTCP	raised	So, I was I was raised with that respect, with the – prayerfully that these people, our men and women, now, men and women in uniform, whether they're police or fire, but addressing police here, our first responders left home when they left to go to work, not knowing – their families not knowing if they would return home because they were risking their lives to save lives and to protect all of us.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	So, it’s with great, prayerful gratitude to most of the men and women in blue, that we sadly have to say that our appreciation for them cannot lapse into apathy or acceptance of actions that are fundamentally incompatible with the purpose of policing, the purpose of policing.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	Of course, we all – there's not anyone on our side of the aisle who has advocated any policy in this body to defund the police, contrary to misrepresentations that are coming forth.
salute	V;PRS;PL;3	salute	All of us here, again, salute and profoundly grateful for our law enforcement heroes.
write	V;PRS;SG;3	writes	As the National Organization of Black Law Enforcement Executives writes, ‘The passage of this act is paramount in achieving the fundamental principle of a police force that protects and serves every citizen of their community with fairness, accountability and transparency in their actions.’
ensure	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ensuring	The Democratic Congress, together with the Biden-Harris Administration, is committed to not only ensuring that this legislation becomes law, but to take further action to end violence and advance justice in America.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let us ensure that the passage of the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act is the first of many steps in this direction.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	To the family of George Floyd that came here when the bill was being reviewed by your committee, Mr Chairman – you remember that day.
come	V;PST;PL;3	came	We couldn't be in your committee room because of COVID, but they came and they asked me that day, they said, ‘Madam Speaker, will you name this bill for our brother?’
ask	V;PST;SG;3	asked	Philonise, his brother, asked that question.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	I said, ‘Only if you think it is worthy of your brother.’
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	I think of George Floyd at least once a day – sometimes more.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	And you know why?
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	Not just because of the sadness of it all, but I think of him because they tell us that in order to be safe from COVID, we must wash our hands for 20 seconds.
wash	V;PRS;V.PTCP	washing	As I'm washing my hands for 20 seconds, after about eight or nine seconds, I'm thinking
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	‘This is taking forever.
do	V;NFIN	do	I can't do this for 20 seconds.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	It takes too long.’
think	V;PRS;SG;1	think	And then I think of George Floyd.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	It's a long time, as Congresswoman Dean said, calling out for his mother, extending love to his family.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let us ensure that George's brother, Philonise, when he said, ‘George's name means something,’ and that, as his daughter, Gianna, said, ‘Daddy changed the world’ with this legislation.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let us take an important step in changing the world for George's family, for all communities of color, for all Americans, for the whole world.
urge	V;PRS;SG;1	urge	With that, Madam Speaker, I urge a strong, bipartisan vote on the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	I thank Karen Bass again, Madam Chair.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you so much, again.
yield	V;PRS;SG;1	yield	And I yield back the balance of my time.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The Charter of the United Nations sends a clear message to us all: put people first.
place	V;NFIN	place	The first words of the Charter, “We the peoples”, are a summons to place people at the centre of our work — every day, everywhere.
reward	V;PST;V.PTCP	rewarded	Those rights are not a favour to be rewarded or withheld; they are an endowment for simply being human.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	Throughout the first half of my mandate, I have had the good fortune to meet people around the world, not in gilded meeting rooms but where they live, work and dream.
listen	V;PST;V.PTCP	listened	And I have listened.
hear	V;PST;V.PTCP	heard	I have heard families in the South Pacific who fear their lives being swept away by rising seas; young refugees in the Middle East yearning for a return to school and home; Ebola survivors in North Kivu struggling to rebuild their lives; women demanding equality and opportunity; people of all beliefs and traditions who suffer simply because of who they are; and so many others.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	living	We are living in a world of disquiet.
fear	V;PRS;PL;3	fear	A great many people fear getting trampled, thwarted, left behind.
take	V;PRS;PL;3	take	Machines take their jobs, traffickers take their dignity, demagogues take their rights, warlords take their lives, fossil fuels take their futures.
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	And yet people believe in the spirit and ideas that bring us to this Hall.
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	They believe in the United Nations.
believe	V;NFIN	believe	But do they believe in us?
believe	V;NFIN	believe	Do they believe that, as leaders, we will put people first?
deliver	V;NFIN	deliver	We the leaders must deliver for we the peoples.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	People have the right to live in peace.
speak	V;PST;SG;1	spoke	One year ago in this Hall, I spoke of winds of hope despite the chaos and confusion of our world (see A/73/PV.6).
continue	V;PST;V.PTCP	continued	Since then, some of those currents have continued to move in promising directions.
unfold	V;PST;PL;3	unfolded	Against the expectations of many, elections unfolded peacefully in Madagascar, the Maldives and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, to name just a few places.
resolve	V;PST;PL;3	resolved	Greece and the Republic of North Macedonia resolved their decades-long name dispute.
bring	V;PST;V.PTCP	brought	Political dialogue in the Sudan and the peace process in the Central African Republic have brought renewed hope.
seek	V;PST;V.PTCP	sought	A long-sought step forward has just been taken on the political path out of the tragedy in Syria, in line with Security Council resolution 2254 (2015).
announce	V;PST;SG;1	announced	As I announced yesterday, an agreement has been reached with all parties for a credible, balanced and inclusive Syrian-owned and Syrian-led constitutional committee.
leave	V;PST;SG;3	left	My Special Envoy just left Damascus after finalizing the last details with the Government and the opposition.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	The United Nations looks forward to convening the committee in Geneva in the coming weeks.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	However, across the global landscape, we see conflicts persisting, terrorism spreading and the risk of a new arms race growing.
make	V;PRS;PL;3	make	Outside interferences, often in violation of Security Council resolutions, make peace processes more difficult.
remain	V;PRS;PL;3	remain	Many situations remain unresolved, from Yemen and Libya to Afghanistan and beyond.
threaten	V;PRS;SG;3	threatens	A succession of unilateral actions threatens to thwart a two-State solution between Israel and Palestine.
flee	V;PST;V.PTCP	fled	In Venezuela, 4 million people have fled the country — one of the largest displacements in the world.
heighten	V;PST;V.PTCP	heightened	Tensions are heightened in South Asia, where differences need to be addressed through dialogue.
face	V;PRS;V.PTCP	facing	Above all, we are facing the alarming possibility of armed conflict in the Gulf, the consequences of which the world can not afford.
lead	V;NFIN	lead	In a context where a minor miscalculation can lead to a major confrontation, we must do everything possible to push for reason and restraint.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	I hope for a future in which all the countries of the region can live in a state of mutual respect and cooperation, without interference in each other’s affairs.
hope	V;PRS;SG;1	hope	I hope equally that it will still be possible to preserve the progress on nuclear non-proliferation represented by the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action.
emphasize	V;PST;V.PTCP	emphasized	From the first day, I have emphasized prevention, mediation and a surge in diplomacy for peace in order to address the crises that we face.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider the lives that we can save by strengthening our investments to sustain peace around the world.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Across some of the most troubled corners of the world, some 100,000 United Nations peacekeepers protect civilians and promote peace.
strengthen	V;PRS;V.PTCP	strengthening	Through the Action for Peacekeeping initiative, we are strengthening our effectiveness and efficiency and we are renewing partnerships with troop- and police-contributing countries, host countries and regional organizations, such as the African Union and the European Union.
ease	V;PRS;V.PTCP	easing	I am also proud of the work of our humanitarian workers, easing suffering around the world.
channel	V;PST;V.PTCP	channelled	Fully half of all international relief aid is channelled through the United Nations, ensuring that millions receive protection, food, medicine, shelter, water and other life-saving forms of assistance.
lose	V;PST;V.PTCP	lost	This year alone, in brutal attacks and other circumstances, we have lost at least 80 peacekeepers, humanitarian workers and others, all of whom gave their lives serving the United Nations and trying to better the lives of others.
honour	V;PRS;SG;1	honour	I honour their service and their sacrifice.
bolster	V;PST;V.PTCP	bolstered	We have bolstered our counter-terrorism architecture and defined new strategies to tackle violent extremism and to address root causes, while respecting human rights.
put	V;PST;V.PTCP	put	I have put forward a new disarmament agenda to advance global peace.
extend	V;PST;V.PTCP	extended	In the near term, the New START Treaty must be extended.
work	V;NFIN	work	We must work to address the heightened threat posed by ballistic missiles and ensure a successful 2020 Review Conference of the Parties to the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons.
remain	V;PRS;SG;3	remains	The situation of the Korean peninsula remains uncertain.
support	V;PRS;SG;1	support	I fully support the efforts towards a new summit between the President of the United States and the leader of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	At this time of transition and dysfunction in global power relations, there is a new risk looming on the horizon that may not yet be large but it is real.
fear	V;PRS;SG;1	fear	I fear the possibility of a great fracture — the world splitting in two, with the two largest economies on Earth creating two separate and competing worlds, each with their own dominant currency, trade and financial rules, their own Internet and artificial intelligence capacities and their own zero-sum geopolitical and military strategies.
do	V;NFIN	do	We must do everything possible to avert the great fracture and maintain a universal system — a universal economy with universal respect for international law, a multipolar world with strong multilateral institutions.
need	V;PRS;SG;1	need	Mr. President, I need to catch my breath, so much has happened.
administer	V;PST;V.PTCP	administered	A few hours ago, on the West Front of this citadel of democracy, Joe Biden and KAMALA HARRIS were administered the oaths of Office to the Presidency and Vice Presidency of the United States.
turn	V;PST;V.PTCP	turned	We have turned the page to a new chapter in the history of our democracy, and I am full of hope.
challenge	V;NFIN	challenge	I would challenge anyone not to feel hopeful today after listening to Amanda Gorman, the amazing 22–year-old poet, wise far beyond her years, who told us that:
weather	V;PST;V.PTCP	weathered	Somehow we’ve weathered and witnessed a nation that isn’t broken but simply unfinished
speak	V;PST;SG;3	spoke	The hope she spoke about—the strong and happy hope— the hope we feel today, is a hope, of course, that is tempered by reality.
line	V;PRS;PL;3	line	The crowds that customarily line the National Mall were absent, a reminder that our Nation is still in the throes of a deadly pandemic.
take	V;PST;SG;3	took	The presence of thousands of National Guardsmen, police, and Secret Service was a reminder that 2 weeks ago, in this very room and on those very steps where President Biden took the oath, a mob of violent criminals tried to dismantle our democracy—our sacred democracy—brick by brick and would try again if they could.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	But, as President Biden said a few hours ago, today, democracy has prevailed, the will of the people was heeded, and the peaceful transfer of power fulfilled.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	It takes a lot more than a band of hooligans to bring our grand democracy down.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let it be a message to our friends and adversaries around the world that our democracy, though it has been tried and been tested, shall long endure.
let	V;IMP;2	let	And let it be a message to those terrorists who desecrated this temple of democracy that they will never prevail.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Joe Biden is now the 46th President of the United States.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	KAMALA HARRIS is now the 49th Vice President of the United States.
hold	V;NFIN	hold	But, of course, in more ways than one, she is not the 49th but the first—the first African-American woman, the first Asian-American woman, and the first woman, period, to hold the Office of Vice Presidency in our Nation’s history.
end	V;PST;V.PTCP	ended	Today, the threat to our democracy from the Presidency itself has ended, but the challenges we face as a nation remain.
warm	V;PRS;V.PTCP	warming	In the wake of violence and division, hatred and mistruth, in the shadow of disease and economic hardship, a warming planet and unequal society, we begin the work of the 117th Congress.
speak	V;PST;SG;3	spoke	In his inaugural address, President Biden spoke to this moment—a moment of great challenge—and told us two simple truths: One, that our responsibilities are numerous, and, two, it will take unity—unity of spirit, unity of purpose—to fulfill them.
hear	V;PST;1;PL	heard	President Biden, we heard you loud and clear.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have a lengthy agenda, and we need to get it done together.
point	V;PST;SG;3	pointed	President Biden pointed the way to our Nation’s recovery and renewal.
remind	V;PST;SG;3	reminded	He reminded us of who we are and where we need to go, but we must now turn the spirit of his words into action.
set	V;NFIN	set	The Senate must immediately set to work on the mission President Biden described—restoring the greatness and goodness of America.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is much to do, and we are ready to get to work.
upstage	V;NFIN	upstage	Now, not to upstage our new President and Vice President, but theirs has not been the only swearing in today.
welcome	V;PST;SG;3	welcomed	A few moments ago, the Senate welcomed three new Members to this Chamber.
call	V;NFIN	call	I can now happily and proudly call them Senator PADILLA, Senator OSSOFF, and Senator WARNOCK.
join	V;PRS;PL;3	join	They join Senators KELLY, HICKENLOOPER, and Luján as part of a six-Member class of Democratic Senators and a new Democratic Senate majority.
mention	V;PRS;SG;1	mention	I mention the historic nature of Vice President HARRIS’ ascendance, but let’s not forget that her successor, ALEX PADILLA, is the first Latino Senator to represent California; that RAFAEL WARNOCK, born while Georgia was represented in this Chamber by two staunch segregationists, is now the first African-American Senator Georgia has ever elected; and that JOHN OSSOFF is the first Jewish Senator from his State, sworn in today on a book of Hebrew Scripture once owned by the rabbi, who, decades ago, formed a bond between the Jewish and African-American communities of Georgia.
say	V;PST;SG;3	said	As President Biden said in his inaugural address, “don’t tell me things can’t change.”
turn	V;NFIN	turn	With the swearing in of these three Senators, the Senate will turn to Democratic control, for which I deeply thank my colleagues.
return	V;NFIN	return	We will return to Democratic control under the first New York-born majority leader in American history—a kid from Brooklyn, the son of an exterminator and a housewife, and a descendant of victims of the Holocaust.
tremble	V;PRS;PL;3	tremble	That I should be the leader of this new Senate majority is an awesome responsibility—awesome in the Biblical sense, as the angels that tremble in awe before God.
feel	V;PRS;SG;1	feel	Today, I feel the full weight of that responsibility, a sense of reverence and of awe at the trust placed in me.
intend	V;PRS;SG;1	intend	I intend to honor that trust with all of my energy and with joy.
change	V;PRS;SG;3	changes	And as the majority changes in the Senate, the Senate will do business differently.
address	V;NFIN	address	The Senate will address the challenges our country faces head on and without delay, not with timid solutions but with boldness and with courage.
tackle	V;NFIN	tackle	The Senate will tackle the perils of the moment, a once-in-a-generation health and economic crisis, and it will strive to make progress on generations-long struggle for racial justice, economic justice, equality of opportunity, and equality under the law.
make	V;IMP;2	make	And make no mistake, the Senate will forcefully, consistently, and urgently address the greatest threat to this country and to our planet: climate change.
legislate	V;NFIN	legislate	This Senate will legislate.
strive	V;NFIN	strive	And to my Republican colleagues, when and where we can, the Democratic majority will strive to make this important work bipartisan.
work	V;PRS;SG;3	works	The Senate works best when we work together.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have no choice.
face	V;PRS;1;PL	face	The challenges we face are great.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have no choice but to try to work together every day to reward the faith the American people have placed in us.
let	V;IMP;2	let	So let us begin.
yield	V;PRS;SG;1	yield	I yield the floor.
announce	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Announcing	Announcing Decision To Resign the Office of President of the United States
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	This is the 37th time I have spoken to you from this office, where so many decisions have been made that shaped the history of this Nation.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	Each time I have done so to discuss with you some matter that I believe affected the national interest.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	In all the decisions I have made in my public life, I have always tried to do what was best for the Nation.
feel	V;PST;V.PTCP	felt	Throughout the long and difficult period of Watergate, I have felt it was my duty to persevere, to make every possible effort to complete the term of office to which you elected me.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	In the past few days, however, it has become evident to me that I no longer have a strong enough political base in the Congress to justify continuing that effort.
be	V;PST;SG;3	was	As long as there was such a base, I felt strongly that it was necessary to see the constitutional process through to its conclusion, that to do otherwise would be unfaithful to the spirit of that deliberately difficult process and a dangerously destabilizing precedent for the future.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	But with the disappearance of that base, I now believe that the constitutional purpose has been served, and there is no longer a need for the process to be prolonged.
prefer	V;PST;V.PTCP	preferred	I would have preferred to carry through to the finish, whatever the personal agony it would have involved, and my family unanimously urged me to do so.
come	V;NFIN	come	But the interests of the Nation must always come before any personal considerations.
have	V;PST;V.PTCP	had	From the discussions I have had with Congressional and other leaders, I have concluded that because of the Watergate matter, I might not have the support of the Congress that I would consider necessary to back the very difficult decisions and carry out the duties of this office in the way the interests of the Nation will require.
leave	V;NFIN	leave	To leave office before my term is completed is abhorrent to every instinct in my body.
put	V;NFIN	put	But as President, I must put the interests of America first.
need	V;PRS;SG;3	needs	America needs a full-time President and a full-time Congress, particularly at this time with problems we face at home and abroad.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	To continue to fight through the months ahead for my personal vindication would almost totally absorb the time and attention of both the President and the Congress in a period when our entire focus should be on the great issues of peace abroad and prosperity without inflation at home.
resign	V;NFIN	resign	Therefore, I shall resign the Presidency effective at noon tomorrow.
swear	V;PST;V.PTCP	sworn	Vice President Ford will be sworn in as President at that hour in this office.
recall	V;PRS;SG;1	recall	As I recall the high hopes for America with which we began this second term, I feel a great sadness that I will not be here in this office working on your behalf to achieve those hopes in the next 2 1/2 years.
turn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	turning	But in turning over direction of the Government to Vice President Ford, I know, as I told the Nation when I nominated him for that office 10 months ago, that the leadership of America will be in good hands.
pass	V;PRS;V.PTCP	passing	In passing this office to the Vice President, I also do so with the profound sense of the weight of responsibility that will fall on his shoulders tomorrow and, therefore, of the understanding, the patience, the cooperation he will need from all Americans.
assume	V;PRS;SG;3	assumes	As he assumes that responsibility, he will deserve the help and the support of all of us.
look	V;PRS;1;PL	look	As we look to the future, the first essential is to begin healing the wounds of this Nation, to put the bitterness and divisions of the recent past behind us and to rediscover those shared ideals that lie at the heart of our strength and unity as a great and as a free people.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	By taking this action, I hope that I will have hastened the start of that process of healing which is so desperately needed in America.
regret	V;PRS;SG;1	regret	I regret deeply any injuries that may have been done in the course of the events that led to this decision.
say	V;NFIN	say	I would say only that if some of my judgments were wrong--and some were wrong--they were made in what I believed at the time to be the best interest of the Nation.
stand	V;PST;V.PTCP	stood	To those who have stood with me during these past difficult months--to my family, my friends, to many others who joined in supporting my cause because they believed it was right--I will be eternally grateful for your support.
feel	V;PST;V.PTCP	felt	And to those who have not felt able to give me your support, let me say I leave with no bitterness toward those who have opposed me, because all of us, in the final analysis, have been concerned with the good of the country, however our judgments might differ.
let	V;IMP;2	let	So, let us all now join together in affirming that common commitment and in helping our new President succeed for the benefit of all Americans.
leave	V;NFIN	leave	I shall leave this office with regret at not completing my term, but with gratitude for the privilege of serving as your President for the past 5 1/2 years.
represent	V;PRS;PL;3	represent	They have been a time of achievement in which we can all be proud, achievements that represent the shared efforts of the Administration, the Congress, and the people.
require	V;NFIN	require	But the challenges ahead are equally great, and they, too, will require the support and the efforts of the Congress and the people working in cooperation with the new Administration.
end	V;PST;V.PTCP	ended	We have ended America's longest war, but in the work of securing a lasting peace in the world, the goals ahead are even more far-reaching and more difficult.
complete	V;NFIN	complete	We must complete a structure of peace so that it will be said of this generation, our generation of Americans, by the people of all nations, not only that we ended one war but that we prevented future wars.
unlock	V;PST;V.PTCP	unlocked	We have unlocked the doors that for a quarter of a century stood between the United States and the People's Republic of China.
ensure	V;NFIN	ensure	We must now ensure that the one quarter of the world's people who live in the People's Republic of China will be and remain not our enemies, but our friends.
come	V;NFIN	come	I am proud to come here to Philadelphia and join my fellow Democrats.
produce	V;PST;PL;3	produced	In 1960, the Democrats of this city produced a margin in the presidential race that was in this city three times as large as it was in the whole United States.
introduce	V;PST;V.PTCP	introduced	So I am proud to be back here again, and I am very happy to be introduced by your distinguished chairman, Bill Green.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	When he became chairman of this city committee, there were 300,000 more Republicans registered in the city of Philadelphia than Democrats, and it is a source of satisfaction to me that tonight there are 260,000 more Democrats registered.
understand	V;NFIN	understand	I can understand why some Republicans may not like it, but, as a Democrat, as one who believes in the Democratic Party, as one who believes that the Democratic Party has meant progress for this city, this State, and this country, I am proud to be here in Philadelphia.
come	V;NFIN	come	I do not come from Philadelphia, and I would not interfere, but I am hopeful and confident that when we come to the Army - Navy game in a month from now, we will be greeted by the new mayor of Philadelphia, Mayor Tale.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	I haven't given a political speech for about 3 years, so I am a little out of practice, but I am gradually getting back into it.
follow	V;PRS;SG;3	follows	And I am glad to be here with a mayor who follows two other distinguished mayors of this city, who carries on their tradition - Joe Clark, your United States Senator who was a great mayor of Philadelphia, and Dick Dilworth, who followed him, who was a great mayor of Philadelphia.
carry	V;PRS;SG;3	carries	That is the tradition of honest, progressive democratic government that Mayor Tate carries on.
run	V;PRS;PL;3	run	And I am glad to be here with Judge Hoffman and Senator Mahady who also run this fall in the State of Pennsylvania.
expect	V;PRS;SG;3	expects	Everyone expects things of Presidents, but I am not sure that they realize how much depends upon the Members of the House and the Members of the Senate who must make the final judgment on what kind of laws a President must execute.
support	V;PST;V.PTCP	supported	The Congressmen from this city as well as the Senator from this State - Bill Barrett and Bob Nix and Herman Toll and Jim Byrne and Chairman Green - have, with Joe Clark supported legislation month in and month out that benefited this city and this State and this country and, what is more, has helped make the United States meet its responsibilities around the globe.
do	V;PRS;SG;2	do	So what you do in this city counts all across the country, and Philadelphia has sent the right men to the House of Representatives and to the Senate of the United States.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	And I am also proud to be here with your former Governor who is now working for us in Washington as our adviser on fair housing, Governor Dave Lawrence, of the State of Pennsylvania.
speak	V;PST;SG;1	spoke	Three years ago tomorrow night, I spoke in this hall in the closing days of the 1960 campaign, and I asked the people of this city to give us their support to help this country move again.
give	V;PST;PL;3	gave	The people of Philadelphia gave that support, and the support I received from this city and this State was, as it was in the 1960 convention, the key to our victory across the country.
express	V;NFIN	express	I am back in Philadelphia to express my thanks for that support and also to express appreciation for the help we received from those Democrats in this city and State, and to report to you on the progress that this country has made on the goals that were outlined 3 years ago.
promise	V;NFIN	promise	I did not promise on that October night that life would be easy in the Great Republic.
say	V;NFIN	say	I did not say we would not have new pressures and new problems.
speak	V;NFIN	speak	Nor did I speak of swift solutions in 100 days in office.
talk	V;PST;SG;1	talked	I talked instead about the kind of America that I wanted for my family and for your family and all those who are citizens of this country in these difficult and changing years, the kind of America in which I believed, not as a Democrat or as a candidate, but as a citizen.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	Today, in many ways, the world looks very different, and the revolutionary change of pace is even more rapid than it has been in the past.
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	But there has been no change in my concept of the goals which this country must strive for if it is to meet its responsibilities to its people and those who depend upon it.
believe	V;PRS;SG;1	believe	I still believe in the kind of America which I described in this hall 3 years ago, and I am still determined that this Nation shall continue to strive to meet those goals.
report	V;NFIN	report	And I am gratified to be able to report some progress in the last 33 1/2 months.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	I said, first of all, that I believed in America where work was available to those who were willing and able to work, where the waste of idle men and machines could be avoided, and where greater economic growth could provide the new jobs and the new markets that our growing Nation needed.
achieve	V;PST;V.PTCP	achieved	That goal has not been fully achieved.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are still too many men and women, particularly young men and women, unable to find work.
prevent	V;PRS;PL;3	prevent	And our high wartime tax rates still prevent our economy from growing as fully and as freely as it must.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	But one fact is that 2 1/2 million people more are working in the United States than were working 33 months ago.
cut	V;PST;V.PTCP	cut	The rate of unemployment and idle capacity has been cut, and our economy of the United States will shortly pass the $ 600 billion mark, for a record rise--for a record rise in 3 years of $ 100 billion--the largest peacetime rise in the history of the United States.
obtain	V;NFIN	obtain	And if we can obtain the early passage of an effective tax cut which the House of Representatives has already passed--and which the Ways and Means Committee, on which Bill Green serves, wrote--we shall be sailing this country next year on the longest and strongest peacetime expansion of our economy in the history of the United States.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	It gives me great pleasure to be here today as the Chief Guest during the launch of School of Open – Africa.
like	V;NFIN	like	I would like to begin by sincerely thanking Creative Commons Africa community and under the able coordination of Alex Gakuru and Tobias Schonwetter, and the global Creative Commons Community for inviting me to preside over this launch.
note	V;NFIN	note	I am happy to note the enthusiasm demonstrated by School of Open Africa in transforming education along Sustainable Development Goals proposed for post-2015 (Goal No. 4: “Ensure inclusive and equitable quality education and promote life-long learning opportunities for all”) and in line with the Kenya Vision 2030 which seeks to transform Kenya into a middle-income country that offers high quality of life to all citizens by the year 2030.
note	V;NFIN	note	I am happy to note how much School of Open Africa has grown in Kenya and embraced in countries like Senegal, Nigeria, and South Africa among other African countries in the last few years.
inform	V;PST;V.PTCP	informed	I am informed that School of Open by Creative Commons is highly reputed around the world for addressing universal access to education.
award	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Awarding	Awarding CopyrightX certificates / CC BY / Phillip Ranja
license	V;PST;V.PTCP	licensed	Kenya is a signatory to the UNESCO’s 2012 Paris Declaration on Open Education Resources licensed under Creative Commons open licenses.
improve	V;PRS;SG;3	improves	The use of open education resources improves the quality of teaching and learning, including by accelerating student comprehension and by providing more opportunities for the realisation of universal access to education.
reside	V;PRS;PL;3	reside	Open Educational Resources (OER) are teaching, learning, and research materials in any medium that reside in the public domain or have been released under an open license that permits their free use and re-purposing by others.
undertake	V;PST;V.PTCP	undertaken	Fully aware of the role of education in a country’s development agenda, I am sure that the new initiatives being undertaken by School of Open Africa, the Creative Commons and UNESCO are making their contribution towards the social, economic, and political pillars which are the three fundamental cornerstones of our country, and indeed for our great continent.
implement	V;PST;V.PTCP	implemented	Ladies and gentlemen, as you may be aware, the Country’s development blue print is being implemented through successive five- year Medium Term Plans (MTPs) that will finally enable the country to achieve the long-term goals.
transform	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Transforming	We are now in the second medium term plan cycle (2013 - 2017) whose theme is “Transforming Kenya: Pathways to Devolution, Socio-economic Development, Equity and National Unity”.
roll	V;PST;SG;3	rolled	As you may be aware, the ICT Authority rolled out the National ICT Master plan that will set the pace for progression of the country in ICT for the next five years.
roll	V;PST;V.PTCP	rolled	The Master plan – once fully rolled out – will completely transform government processes, services and management, and make information access and service delivery more efficient.
pilot	V;NFIN	pilot	Again, the Master plan, with the flagship projects to pilot its implementation, will steer the march towards the digital future that will transform the country to a regional technical hub, raise the country’s competitiveness and align the country in line with Vision 2030’s ICT goals.
launch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	launching	By launching the Kenya ICT Master Plan, the government revealed its commitment towards the enhancement of access to quality education and training through ICT in line with the Millennium Development Goals (MDGs).
review	V;PRS;V.PTCP	reviewing	We are reviewing the National ICT Policy Guidelines to ensure alignment with proposed Sustainable Development Goals.
have	V;NFIN	have	As a country, we are also privileged to have a National ICT Policy whose goal is to create a prosperous ICT - driven Kenyan society.
assure	V;PST;V.PTCP	assured	With a well mainstreamed ICT society, we are assured of better livelihoods of Kenyans attainable through the availability of accessible, efficient, reliable and affordable ICT services.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	ICT provides a platform that enables the realization of these goals.
emphasize	V;NFIN	emphasize	I must emphasize that Creative Commons through the School of Open Africa has provided a good example of innovative use of ICT in education that resonates well with the Kenya National ICT Master Plan.
place	V;PRS;SG;3	places	The integration of ICT into educational programmes places both the teaching staff and students at the forefront in the utilization of ICT for the enhancement of lives.
note	V;PRS;SG;1	note	I note with great pleasure the freedom to re-purpose offered by openly licensed educational resources, the convenience online access to learners as alternative courses delivery and certification methods.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	At this juncture, ladies and gentlemen, I thank William Fisher III, Professor of Intellectual Property and his staff at the Berkman Centre for Internet and Society at Harvard Law School for providing a free copyright law course taught to graduands present today to receive their certificates.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	I also thank Michael Murungi (then CEO, National Council for Law Reporting or “Kenya Law”) and Alex Gakuru for successfully conducting the course in Nairobi.
congratulate	V;NFIN	congratulate	I must congratulate the former students and ask to make the very best use of the copyright law knowledge they acquired while also challenging all universities represented here to consider emulating the highly successfully CopyrightX initiative.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	As the government continues to work on modalities of ensuring universal access to education and increasing the internet penetration in all parts of the country, we are pleased to witness this mode of study that will definitely translate to affordable education.
couple	V;PST;V.PTCP	coupled	Open Education Resources coupled with innovative use of ICT in education will accelerate realization of a modern Kenya that will be a knowledge-based economy.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	By using Open Educational Resources, OER, School of Open is opening up to many students who would have otherwise missed the opportunity of accessing education, especially in the marginalized areas which could not adequately access quality education.
motivate	V;PST;V.PTCP	motivated	Ongoing, voluntary, and self- motivated pursuit of knowledge for either personal or professional reasons is one of the characteristics of the 21st Century.
promote	V;PRS;V.PTCP	promoting	One of the major ways of promoting life-long learning is the continuous use of ICT innovations in education.
congratulate	V;PRS;SG;1	congratulate	I congratulate School of Open teams across Africa for the innovative and transformative mode of teaching and learning that we are launching today.
emulate	V;NFIN	emulate	This African initiative is a worthy model for other regions of the world to emulate.
conclude	V;PRS;SG;1	conclude	As I conclude I take this opportunity to applaud UNESCO’s efforts and contribution in the development and growth of the country through this noble initiative that enables the primary, secondary and universities to optimize the use of Information and Communication Technology (ICT) in learning.
acknowledge	V;PRS;SG;1	acknowledge	I acknowledge the generous financial support from the Hewlett Foundation and SOO Africa teams support by Google.
declare	V;NFIN	declare	With those remarks, it is now my pleasure to declare the School of Open Africa officially opened.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
preview	V;NFIN	Preview	Remarks by President Biden and Vice President Harris in a Briefing to Preview the First Images from the James Webb Space Telescope
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
talk	V;PRS;1;PL	talk	President Joe Biden and I talk often about our mutual passion for everything you all do.
join	V;NFIN	join	This is a very exciting moment to join you for the unveiling of the work that you have been laboring on for decades.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	So, to our President, Joe Biden, thank you for your leadership around all of these issues.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you also to our colleagues from across the White House and NASA and everyone else joining us from home.
know	V;PRS;SG;1	know	So, yes, as Chair of the National Space Council, I know that today represents an exciting new chapter in the exploration of our universe.
look	V;PST;V.PTCP	looked	From the beginning of history, humans have looked up to the night sky with wonder.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	And thanks to dedicated people who have been working for decades in engineering and on scientific marvels, we can look to the sky with new understanding.
launch	V;PST;SG;3	launched	When NASA launched the Hubble Space Telescope in 1990, we were able to see the stars unobstructed by the Earth’s atmosphere and understand the universe in ways we could have never imagined even a few decades earlier.
enter	V;PRS;1;PL	enter	And now we enter a new phase of scientific discovery.
build	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Building	Building on the legacy of Hubble, the James Webb Space Telescope allows us to see deeper into space than ever before and in stunning clarity.
enhance	V;NFIN	enhance	It will enhance what we know about the origins of our universe, our solar system, and possibly life itself.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	This was made possible by partnership among nations.
build	V;NFIN	build	And it is an example of how the scientific endeavor can build upon the international rules and norms that govern our cooperation in space.
see	V;NFIN	see	And the images we will see today are a testament to the amazing work done by the thousands of workers across our nation who dedicated years to this project.
embark	V;PST;PL;3	embarked	They embarked on this complex endeavor for the benefit of humankind, and in the process, accelerated American innovation, strengthened partnerships with our allies, and will undoubtedly inspire generations to look to the heavens with excitement and ambition.
introduce	V;NFIN	introduce	With that, it is my great honor to introduce a leader who has always believed in the power of American innovation and international cooperation to achieve the remarkable: our President, the President of the United States, Joe Biden.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	And I was going to say “good afternoon,” but we’re starting this meeting late because I was engaged in preparing for a trip to the Middle East.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	And thank you, Vice President Harris, Chair of the National Space Council.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	And thank you, my dear friend and our outstanding NASA Administrator and the guy—the only guy here who’s been in space, Bill Nelson.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you very much for what you’re doing.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	doing	And—and, Dr. Nelson, you’re doing a great job leading this Office of Science and Technology Policy.
launch	V;PST;SG;3	launched	Six and a half months ago, a rocket launched from Earth carrying the world’s newest, most powerful deep-space telescope on a journey one million miles into the cosmos—first of all, that blows my mind: a million miles into the cosmos—along the way unfolding itself, deploying a mirror 21 feet wide, a sunshield the size of a tennis court, and 250,000 tiny shutters, each one smaller than a grain of sand.
put	V;PST;V.PTCP	Put	Put together, it’s a new window into the history of our universe.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	And today, we’re going to get a glimpse of the first light to shine through that window—light from other worlds, orbiting stars far beyond our own.
read	V;PST;SG;1	read	It’s astounding to me when I read this and saw the—I mean, it really is.
want	V;NFIN	want	It’s—it’s—anyway, I don’t want to—I’ll see what they think when we see this.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	But light where stars were born and from where they die.
document	V;PST;V.PTCP	documented	The oldest documented light in the history of the universe from over 13 billion—let me say that again: 13 billion years ago.
fathom	V;NFIN	fathom	It’s hard to even fathom.
share	V;PST;V.PTCP	shared	Tomorrow, when this image is shared with the world, it will be a historic moment for science and technology, for astronomy and space exploration, for America and all of humanity.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	You know, as an international collaboration, this telescope embodies how America leads the world not by the example of our power, but the power of our example.
symbolize	V;PRS;SG;3	symbolizes	A partnership with others, it symbolizes the relentless spirit of American ingenuity.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	And it shows what we can achieve, what more we can discover, not just about distant places, but about our very own planet and climate, like NASA’s Earth System Observatory that we launched last year.
invest	V;NFIN	invest	That’s why the federal government must invest—must invest in science and technology, more than we have in the past.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	These images are going to remind the world that America can do big things.
remind	V;NFIN	remind	And they’ll remind the American people, especially our children, that there’s nothing beyond our capacity—nothing beyond our capacity.
see	V;NFIN	see	We can see possibilities no one has ever seen before.
go	V;NFIN	go	We can go places no one has ever gone before.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	You know, you’ve—you’ve heard me say it over and over again.
define	V;PST;V.PTCP	defined	America is defined by one single word: possibilities.
want	V;PRS;SG;1	want	I want to thank the team at NASA for once again showing that that’s who we are—that’s who we are as a nation: a nation of possibilities.
let	V;IMP;2	let	And now let’s take a look at the very first image from this miraculous telescope.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	thank	So nice, thank you very much.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
have	V;NFIN	have	And it's an honor to have everybody here.
be	V;PST;V.PTCP	been	There's been no crowd like this.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	And, I can tell you, some of the candidates, they went in.
know	V;NFIN	know	They didn't know the air-conditioner didn't work.
sweat	V;PST;PL;3	sweated	They sweated like dogs.
know	V;NFIN	know	They didn't know the room was too big, because they didn't have anybody there.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	How are they going to beat ISIS?
think	V;NFIN	think	I don't think it's gonna happen.
have	V;NFIN	have	We don't have victories anymore.
use	V;PST;1;PL	used	We used to have victories, but we don't have them.
see	V;PST;SG;3	saw	When was the last time anybody saw us beating, let's say, China in a trade deal?
kill	V;PRS;PL;3	kill	They kill us.
beat	V;PRS;SG;1	beat	I beat China all the time.
want	V;PRS;1;PL	want	We want Trump.
want	V;PRS;1;PL	want	We want Trump.
beat	V;NFIN	beat	When did we beat Japan at anything?
send	V;PRS;PL;3	send	They send their cars over by the millions, and what do we do?
see	V;PST;SG;2	saw	When was the last time you saw a Chevrolet in Tokyo?
exist	V;NFIN	exist	It doesn't exist, folks.
beat	V;PRS;PL;3	beat	They beat us all the time.
beat	V;NFIN	beat	When do we beat Mexico at the border?
laugh	V;PRS;V.PTCP	laughing	They're laughing at us, at our stupidity.
beat	V;PRS;V.PTCP	beating	And now they are beating us economically.
believe	V;IMP;2	believe	They are not our friend, believe me.
kill	V;PRS;V.PTCP	killing	But they're killing us economically.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	The U.S. has become a dumping ground for everybody else's problems.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you.
send	V;PRS;SG;3	sends	When Mexico sends its people, they're not sending their best.
send	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sending	They're not sending you.
send	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sending	They're not sending you.
send	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sending	They're sending people that have lots of problems, and they're bringing those problems with us.
bring	V;PRS;V.PTCP	bringing	They're bringing drugs.
bring	V;PRS;V.PTCP	bringing	They're bringing crime.
assume	V;PRS;SG;1	assume	And some, I assume, are good people.
speak	V;PRS;SG;1	speak	But I speak to border guards and they tell us what we're getting.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	And it only makes common sense.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	It only makes common sense.
send	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sending	They're sending us not the right people.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	It's coming from more than Mexico.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	It's coming from all over South and Latin America, and it's coming probably — probably — from the Middle East.
know	V;NFIN	know	But we don't know.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	Because we have no protection and we have no competence, we don't know what's happening.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	got	And it's got to stop and it's got to stop fast.
eat	V;PRS;V.PTCP	eating	Islamic terrorism is eating up large portions of the Middle East.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	They've become rich.
build	V;PST;PL;3	built	They just built a hotel in Syria.
believe	V;NFIN	believe	Can you believe this?
build	V;PST;PL;3	built	They built a hotel.
have	V;PRS;SG;1	have	When I have to build a hotel, I pay interest.
have	V;NFIN	have	They don't have to pay interest, because they took the oil that, when we left Iraq, I said we should've taken.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	So now ISIS has the oil, and what they don't have, Iran has.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	And in 19 — and I will tell you this, and I said it very strongly, years ago, I said — and I love the military, and I want to have the strongest military that we've ever had, and we need it more now than ever.
say	V;PST;SG;1	said	But I said, "Don't hit Iraq," because you're going to totally destabilize the Middle East.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	Iran is going to take over the Middle East, Iran and somebody else will get the oil, and it turned out that Iran is now taking over Iraq.
think	V;IMP;2	Think	Think of it.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	Iran is taking over Iraq, and they're taking it over big league.
spend	V;PST;1;PL	spent	We spent $2 trillion in Iraq, $2 trillion.
lose	V;PST;1;PL	lost	We lost thousands of lives, thousands in Iraq.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have wounded soldiers, who I love, I love — they're great — all over the place, thousands and thousands of wounded soldiers.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	And we have nothing.
go	V;NFIN	go	We can't even go there.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	We have nothing.
give	V;PRS;1;PL	give	And every time we give Iraq equipment, the first time a bullet goes off in the air, they leave it.
read	V;PST;SG;1	read	Last week, I read 2,300 Humvees — these are big vehicles — were left behind for the enemy.
say	V;NFIN	say	You would say maybe two, maybe four?
run	V;PST;PL;3	ran	2,300 sophisticated vehicles, they ran, and the enemy took them.
need	V;PRS;1;PL	need	We need Trump now.
need	V;PRS;1;PL	need	We need Trump now.
announce	V;PST;V.PTCP	announced	Last quarter, it was just announced our gross domestic product — a sign of strength, right?
hear	V;PST;SG;3	heard	Whoever heard of this?
think	V;IMP;2	think	But think of it, GDP below zero, horrible labor participation rate.
believe	V;IMP;2	believe	Don't believe the 5.6.
believe	V;IMP;2	believe	Don't believe it.
get	V;NFIN	get	A lot of people up there can't get jobs.
get	V;NFIN	get	They can't get jobs, because there are no jobs, because China has our jobs and Mexico has our jobs.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	They all have jobs.
talk	V;PRS;SG;3	talks	But the real number, the real number is anywhere from 18 to 19 and maybe even 21 percent, and nobody talks about it, because it's a statistic that's full of nonsense.
want	V;PRS;1;PL	want	We want Trump now.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	Our enemies are getting stronger and stronger by the way, and we as a country are getting weaker.
work	V;NFIN	work	Even our nuclear arsenal doesn't work.
come	V;PST;SG;3	came	It came out recently they have equipment that is 30 years old.
know	V;NFIN	know	They don't know if it worked.
think	V;PST;SG;1	thought	And I thought it was horrible when it was broadcast on television, because boy, does that send signals to Putin and all of the other people that look at us and they say,
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	"That is a group of people, and that is a nation that truly has no clue.
know	V;NFIN	know	They don't know what they're doing.
know	V;NFIN	know	They don't know what they're doing."
migrate	V;PST;PL;3	migrated	Many Americans who migrated to Texas at the invitation of the Mexican government did not completely shed their identity or loyalty to the United States.
bring	V;PST;PL;3	brought	They brought American traditions and expectations with them (including, for many, the right to own slaves).
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	For instance, the majority of these new settlers were Protestant, and though they were not required to attend the Catholic mass, Mexico's prohibition on the public practice of other religions upset them and they routinely ignored it.
accustome	V;PST;V.PTCP	Accustomed	Accustomed to representative democracy, jury trials, and the defendant’s right to appear before a judge, the Anglo-American settlers in Texas also disliked the Mexican legal system, which provided for an initial hearing by an alcalde, an administrator who often combined the duties of mayor, judge, and law enforcement officer.
send	V;PST;SG;3	sent	The alcalde sent a written record of the proceeding to a judge in Saltillo, the state capital, who decided the outcome.
resent	V;PST;PL;3	resented	Settlers also resented that at most two Texas representatives were allowed in the state legislature.
bring	V;PST;V.PTCP	brought	Most American settlers were from southern states, and many had brought slaves with them.
try	V;PST;SG;3	tried	Mexico tried to accommodate them by maintaining the fiction that the slaves were indentured servants.
distrust	V;PST;PL;3	distrusted	But American slaveholders in Texas distrusted the Mexican government and wanted Texas to be a new U.S. slave state.
prevail	V;PRS;V.PTCP	prevailing	The dislike of most for Roman Catholicism (the prevailing religion of Mexico) and a widely held belief in American racial superiority led them generally to regard Mexicans as dishonest, ignorant, and backward.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	This 1833 map shows the extent of land grants made by Mexico to American settlers in Texas.
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	Nearly all are in the eastern portion of the state, one factor that led to war with Mexico in 1846.
inspire	V;PST;SG;3	inspired	Belief in their own superiority inspired some Texans to try to undermine the power of the Mexican government.
attempt	V;PST;SG;3	attempted	When empresario Haden Edwards attempted to evict people who had settled his land grant before he gained title to it, the Mexican government nullified its agreement with him.
outrage	V;PST;V.PTCP	Outraged	Outraged, Edwards and a small party of men took prisoner the alcalde of Nacogdoches.
march	V;PST;SG;3	marched	The Mexican army marched to the town, and Edwards and his troops then declared the formation of the Republic of Fredonia between the Sabine and Rio Grande Rivers.
demonstrate	V;NFIN	demonstrate	To demonstrate loyalty to their adopted country, a force led by Stephen Austin hastened to Nacogdoches to support the Mexican army.
collapse	V;PST;SG;3	collapsed	Edwards’s revolt collapsed, and the revolutionaries fled Texas.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	The growing presence of American settlers in Texas, their reluctance to abide by Mexican law, and their desire for independence caused the Mexican government to grow wary.
forbid	V;PST;SG;3	forbade	In 1830, it forbade future U.S. immigration and increased its military presence in Texas.
continue	V;PST;PL;3	continued	Settlers continued to stream illegally across the long border; by 1835, after immigration resumed, there were twenty thousand Anglo-Americans in Texas.
gather	V;PST;PL;3	gathered	Fifty-five delegates from the Anglo-American settlements gathered in 1831 to demand the suspension of customs duties, the resumption of immigration from the United States, better protection from Indian tribes, the granting of promised land titles, and the creation of an independent state of Texas separate from Coahuila.
order	V;PST;SG;3	Ordered	Ordered to disband, the delegates reconvened in early April 1833 to write a constitution for an independent Texas.
agree	V;PST;SG;3	agreed	Surprisingly, General Antonio Lopez de Santa Anna, Mexico’s new president, agreed to all demands, except the call for statehood.
make	V;PST;SG;3	made	Coahuila y Texas made provisions for jury trials, increased Texas’s representation in the state legislature, and removed restrictions on commerce.
depict	V;PRS;SG;3	depicts	This portrait of General Antonio Lopez de Santa Anna depicts the Mexican president and general in full military regalia.
quash	V;PST;V.PTCP	quashed	Texans' hopes for independence were quashed in 1834, however, when Santa Anna dismissed the Mexican Congress and abolished all state governments, including that of Coahuila y Texas.
renege	V;PRS;V.PTCP	reneging	In January 1835, reneging on earlier promises, he dispatched troops to the town of Anahuac to collect customs duties.
march	V;PST;PL;3	marched	Lawyer and soldier William B. Travis and a small force marched on Anahuac in June, and the fort surrendered.
meet	V;PST;PL;3	met	On October 2, Anglo-American forces met Mexican troops at the town of Gonzales; the Mexican troops fled and the Americans moved on to take San Antonio.
vote	V;PST;PL;3	voted	Now more cautious, delegates to the Consultation of 1835 at San Felipe de Austin voted against declaring independence, instead drafting a statement, which became known as the Declaration of Causes, promising continued loyalty if Mexico returned to a constitutional form of government.
select	V;PST;PL;3	selected	They selected Henry Smith, leader of the Independence Party, as governor of Texas and placed Sam Houston, a former soldier who had been a congressman and governor of Tennessee, in charge of its small military force.
meet	V;PST;PL;3	met	The Consultation delegates met again in March 1836.
declare	V;PST;PL;3	declared	They declared their independence from Mexico and drafted a constitution calling for an American-style judicial system and an elected president and legislature.
establish	V;PST;PL;3	established	Significantly, they also established that slavery would not be prohibited in Texas.
support	V;PST;PL;3	supported	Many wealthy Tejanos supported the push for independence, hoping for liberal governmental reforms and economic benefits.
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember the Alamo!
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Mexico had no intention of losing its northern province.
besiege	V;PST;V.PTCP	besieged	Santa Anna and his army of four thousand had besieged San Antonio in February 1836.
outnumber	V;PST;V.PTCP	outnumbered	Hopelessly outnumbered, its two hundred defenders, under Travis, fought fiercely from their refuge in an old mission known as the Alamo.
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	After ten days, however, the mission was taken and all but a few of the defenders were dead, including Travis and James Bowie, the famed frontiersman who was also a land speculator and slave trader.
include	V;PRS;V.PTCP	including	A few male survivors, possibly including the frontier legend and former Tennessee congressman Davy Crockett, were led outside the walls and executed.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	The few women and children inside the mission were allowed to leave with the only adult male survivor, a slave owned by Travis who was then freed by the Mexican Army.
terrify	V;PST;V.PTCP	Terrified	Terrified, they fled.
paint	V;PST;V.PTCP	painted	The Fall of the Alamo, painted by Theodore Gentilz fewer than ten years after this pivotal moment in the Texas Revolution, depicts the 1836 assault on the Alamo complex.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider this scenario.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	A student comes to class one day, and the instructor says, “I’ve decided that you’re all a bunch of failures and I’m flunking the entire class.”
imagine	V;IMP;2	Imagine	Imagine then that the instructor simply stands there after that announcement, blinking calmly as the class erupts in protest.
imagine	V;IMP;2	imagine	Now imagine that same scenario with one very slight difference.
announce	V;PRS;SG;3	announces	The instructor announces, “I’ve decided that you’re all a bunch of failures and I’m flunking the entire class.”
erupt	V;PRS;SG;3	erupts	Then, as the class erupts in protest, the instructor calmly blinks one eye, leaving the other eye open.
take	V;NFIN	take	Would you take this woman seriously?
wink	V;PRS;V.PTCP	winking	In American culture, winking, related to the normal biological function of blinking, takes on special meaning in social interactions.
happen	V;PST;SG;3	happened	What just happened there?
blink	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Blinking	Blinking is a biological compulsion common to humans everywhere.
blink	V;PRS;PL;3	blink	Humans blink to keep eyes hydrated and clear of debris.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Humans are born knowing how to blink; nobody has to teach us.
blink	V;PRS;PL;3	blink	On average, humans blink 15 to 20 times every minute.
realize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	realizing	Without realizing it, people are necessarily blinking throughout every conversation, every social interaction, every activity during the day.
talk	V;PRS;1;PL	talk	The people we talk to and interact with are also blinking constantly, so often that everyone is accustomed to ignoring it.
blink	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Blinking	Blinking does not affect the perceived meaning of speech or actions.
blink	V;PRS;SG;3	blinks	But if someone deliberately blinks one eye, leaving the other one open, that’s a completely different matter.
leave	V;PRS;V.PTCP	leaving	In fact, leaving one eye open makes a blink a wink.
wink	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Winking	Winking is not a biological necessity.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Humans are not born knowing to how to wink, and it takes some practice to learn how to do it.
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	Because it requires deliberate effort and people are not constantly doing it, winking can acquire special meaning in social interactions.
indicate	V;PRS;SG;3	indicates	In American culture (and many others), a wink often indicates that someone is joking around and that whatever they’ve just said or done should not be taken seriously.
mean	V;NFIN	mean	Of course, a wink can mean different things in different societies.
mean	V;NFIN	mean	Moreover, a wink can mean different things in the same society.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	If someone on a date takes their companion’s hand and gives a cute little wink, the person may have reason to hope the winker is not just joking around.
use	V;PST;SG;3	used	American cultural anthropologist Clifford Geertz (1973) used the example of winking to illustrate two important aspects of culture.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	First, culture is learned.
bear	V;PST;V.PTCP	born	Innate human behaviors—that is, behaviors that people are born with—are biological, not cultural.
blink	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Blinking	Blinking is biological.
acquire	V;PST;V.PTCP	Acquired	Acquired human behaviors—that is, behaviors that people are taught—are cultural.
wink	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Winking	Winking is cultural.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	This means that cultural behaviors are not genetically inherited from generation to generation but must be passed down from older members of a society to younger members.
recall	V;NFIN	recall	This process, as you’ll recall from What is Anthropology? is called enculturation.
learn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	learning	Some aspects of enculturation are deliberate and systematic, such as learning the rules of written punctuation in a language.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	At some point in an English speaker’s childhood, someone explicitly told them the difference between a question mark and an exclamation point.
learn	V;PST;PL;3	learned	Most likely, they learned this distinction in school, a fundamental institution of enculturation in many societies.
provide	V;PRS;V.PTCP	providing	Religious institutions are another common force of enculturation, providing explicit instruction in cultural rules of morality and social interaction.
teach	V;PRS;PL;3	teach	Extracurricular activities such as sports, dance, and music lessons also teach children cultural rules and norms.
convey	V;PST;V.PTCP	conveyed	While a great deal of very important cultural content is deliberately conveyed in these systematic contexts, the greater part of culture is acquired unconsciously by happenstance—that is, nobody planned to teach it, and no one made an effort to consciously try to learn it.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	growing	By virtue of growing up in a culture, children learn what certain actions and objects mean, how their society operates, and what the rules are for appropriate behavior.
Go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Going	Going back to the cultural notion of home, did anyone ever explain to you why your childhood home was structured in a certain way?
point	V;NFIN	point	Did anyone ever point out the cultural assumptions about gender and family built into your house?
imagine	V;IMP;2	imagine	Now, imagine that you were taken away from your parents as a baby and adopted by a family far away, with a very different way of life situated in a very different environment.
raise	V;PST;V.PTCP	raised	With your adoptive family, you might have been raised in a very different kind of home.
grow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Growing	Growing up, your everyday habits, activities, and expectations would have been shaped by the setup of that home.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Living	Living in that house, you would have wordlessly absorbed a set of assumptions about family, gender, work, leisure, hospitality, and property.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	And all of it would seem quite natural to you.
pass	V;PST;V.PTCP	passed	Many forms of culture are passed down through a combination of deliberate and unconscious processes.
tell	V;PST;SG;3	told	Perhaps when you were a child, someone told you what a wink was and showed you how to accomplish one; or perhaps you just witnessed a few winks, figured out what they meant from their contexts, and then learned how to accomplish one through trial and error.
point	V;PST;SG;3	pointed	Geertz pointed out that there are two important aspects to winking: the meaning and the action.
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	As both are learned, both are cultural.
standardize	V;PST;V.PTCP	standardized	But perhaps more importantly, both the standardized action of winking and the assumed meaning of this action are commonly known among members of a group.
share	V;PST;V.PTCP	shared	That is, culture is shared.
regard	V;PST;V.PTCP	regarded	Arguably, beauty is regarded as the keyword in Western art tradition since antiquity.
browse	V;PRS;1;PL	browse	If we browse Chinese classical writings on art criticism, a striking difference manifests — there the category of mei or beauty does not have a dominant presence.
list	V;PST;V.PTCP	listed	For instance, among the 24 aesthetic categories of poetry listed by Sikong Tu 司空图 (837–908) in his celebrated treatise (Sikong n.d.), only three or four categories can be regarded as related to the Western notion of beauty.
accord	V;PRS;V.PTCP	according	Moreover, according to Xu Hong’s (c. 1580–1660) Xishan Qinkuang 谿山琴况 (c. 1640), the most influential treatise on the aesthetics of guqin (Chinese zither) music, the category of li (pretty, beautiful) is merely the 10th of the 24 aesthetic categories, and the top six were harmony, tranquillity, clarity, profundity, antiquity, subtlety. [38]
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	In Chinese paintings, prominence was given to the categories such as shen (spiritual) and miao (subtle) before mei.
find	V;PRS;SG;3	finds	Overall, one also finds that the comparison between the beautiful and the sublime — which defined the influential aesthetics of Edmund Burke (1729–1797) and Immanuel Kant, and consequently became an iconic discourse in Western aesthetics — is also missing in the Chinese history of aesthetics.
conclude	V;NFIN	conclude	To conclude, in a cross-cultural comparative context, it is fair to say that an inquiry on what makes an artwork beautiful may turn out to be even less productive than in the contemporary artworld.
consider	V;PRS;SG;3	considers	The Hegelian definition of aesthetics as the philosophy of art that considers the issue of artistic beauty as its core concern is no more enchanting in our time.
agree	V;NFIN	agree	Many would agree that the question “what makes an artwork beautiful” plays a minor role in contemporary aesthetics and philosophy of art, where the question has been updated to “what makes an artwork meaningful or valuable?”
contribute	V;NFIN	contribute	Two factors might contribute to this situation.
coincide	V;PRS;SG;3	coincides	Firstly, it is not a coincidence that the loss of interest in the inquiry into artistic beauty coincides with a sea change in the artworld, transformed by new art movements as well as struggles in defining art.
define	V;PRS;V.PTCP	defining	Also, when the representationalism, the expression theories, formalism, neoformalism, aesthetic theories in defining art all fail to offer an account that is capable of coping with the dynamic scenes of the artworld, and when the anti-essentialist movement in philosophical aesthetics then paves a way to functionalism, institutionalism and various versions of historicism, the question of artistic beauty as such not only loses its limelight, but also is, to some extent, abandoned for its strong Hegelian colour.
become	V;PRS;SG;3	becomes	Now if “art” itself becomes an open concept or a socially, historically, culturally sensitive notion, then whatever makes an artwork beautiful is unlikely to be something homogeneous and unitary.
experience	V;PST;SG;3	experienced	Secondly, the inquiry into “beauty” experienced a shift of focus from the emphasis on aesthetic properties of the objects to the subjective experience involved in aesthetic appreciation.
classify	V;PST;V.PTCP	classified	The contemporary inquiry into beauty in general is often classified in light of the framework of realism vs non-realism.
regard	V;PRS;SG;3	regards	Realism regards artistic beauty as a property of artworks which is independent of the subject, while non-realism holds that artistic beauty is not an independent property of artworks.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	This shift has a long historical preparation, contributed especially by the British empiricists such as Francis Hutcheson (1694–1746) and David Hume (1711–1776).
think	V;PRS;SG;3	thinks	Hutcheson thinks that beauty lies in both the object and the subject, and artistic beauty comes from the quality of “uniformity amidst variety” in the object and the involvement of an “internal sense” in the subject. [39]
argue	V;PRS;SG;3	argues	Hume argues that “beauty is no quality in things themselves,” but a “sentiment” in “the mind that contemplates them” and “to seek the real beauty … is … fruitless.” [40]
seek	V;PRS;PL;3	seek	In contemporary aesthetics, from the study of empathy to Freud’s libido, to the evolutionary theory of beauty, to the focus on perception in aesthetic experience, more and more philosophers seek to answer the question of what makes an artwork beautiful in light of human physiology and psychology.
regard	V;PRS;SG;3	regards	For instance, the evolutionary theory regards artistic beauty as “fitness signal” that displays desirable personal qualities that strengthen reproductive advantage, and our pleasant feeling caused by something beautiful, like sexual pleasure, is engraved in our minds by the process of evolution, assisting us to make the “the most adaptive decisions for survival and reproduction.” [41]
excite	V;PRS;PL;3	excite	39. “The Figures which excite in us the Ideas of Beauty, seem to be those in which there is Uniformity amidst Variety” (§ II, p. 28).
receive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	receiving	“The internal Sense is, a passive Power of receiving Ideas of Beauty from all Objects in which there is Uniformity amidst Variety” (§ VI, 67).
exist	V;PRS;SG;3	exists	40. “Beauty is no quality in things themselves: it exists merely in the mind which contemplates them; and each mind perceives a different beauty … every individual ought to acquiesce in his own sentiment, without pretending to regulate those of others.
seek	V;NFIN	seek	To seek the real beauty, or real deformity, is as fruitless an inquiry, as to pretend to ascertain the real sweet or real bitter” (Hume 1998, 136–137).
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	41. Denis Dutton (2010) provides such an account in his TED talk, “A Darwinian Theory of Beauty.”
describe	V;NFIN;2	Describe	Describe cognition
distinguish	V;NFIN;2	Distinguish	Distinguish concepts and prototypes
explain	V;NFIN;2	Explain	Explain the difference between natural and artificial concepts
imagine	V;IMP;2	Imagine	Imagine all of your thoughts as if they were physical entities, swirling rapidly inside your mind.
move	V;NFIN	move	How is it possible that the brain is able to move from one thought to the next in an organized, orderly fashion?
perceive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	perceiving	The brain is endlessly perceiving, processing, planning, organizing, and remembering—it is always active.
notice	V;NFIN	notice	Yet, you don’t notice most of your brain’s activity as you move throughout your daily routine.
involve	V;PST;V.PTCP	involved	This is only one facet of the complex processes involved in cognition.
put	V;PST;V.PTCP	put	Simply put, cognition is thinking, and it encompasses the processes associated with perception, knowledge, problem solving, judgment, language, and memory.
study	V;PRS;PL;3	study	Scientists who study cognition are searching for ways to understand how we integrate, organize, and utilize our conscious cognitive experiences without being aware of all of the unconscious work that our brains are doing (for example, Kahneman, 2011).
wake	V;PRS;V.PTCP	waking	Upon waking each morning, you begin thinking—contemplating the tasks that you must complete that day.
run	V;NFIN	run	In what order should you run your errands?
go	V;NFIN	go	Should you go to the bank, the cleaners, or the grocery store first?
get	V;NFIN	get	Can you get these things done before you head to class or will they need to wait until school is done?
experience	V;PST;V.PTCP	experienced	Exceptionally complex, cognition is an essential feature of human consciousness, yet not all aspects of cognition are consciously experienced.
dedicate	V;PST;V.PTCP	dedicated	Cognitive psychology is the field of psychology dedicated to examining how people think.
attempt	V;PRS;SG;3	attempts	It attempts to explain how and why we think the way we do by studying the interactions among human thinking, emotion, creativity, language, and problem solving, in addition to other cognitive processes.
strive	V;PRS;PL;3	strive	Cognitive psychologists strive to determine and measure different types of intelligence, why some people are better at problem solving than others, and how emotional intelligence affects success in the workplace, among countless other topics.
focus	V;PRS;PL;3	focus	They also sometimes focus on how we organize thoughts and information gathered from our environments into meaningful categories of thought, which will be discussed later.
handle	V;PRS;V.PTCP	handling	The human nervous system is capable of handling endless streams of information.
serve	V;PRS;PL;3	serve	The senses serve as the interface between the mind and the external environment, receiving stimuli and translating it into nervous impulses that are transmitted to the brain.
process	V;PRS;SG;3	processes	The brain then processes this information and uses the relevant pieces to create thoughts, which can then be expressed through language or stored in memory for future use.
make	V;NFIN	make	To make this process more complex, the brain does not gather information from external environments only.
form	V;PST;V.PTCP	formed	When thoughts are formed, the brain also pulls information from emotions and memories (Figure 7.2).
receive	V;PST;V.PTCP	received	Sensations and information are received by our brains, filtered through emotions and memories, and processed to become thoughts.
organize	V;NFIN	organize	In order to organize this staggering amount of information, the brain has developed a file cabinet of sorts in the mind.
store	V;PST;V.PTCP	stored	The different files stored in the file cabinet are called concepts.
generate	V;PST;V.PTCP	generated	Concepts are, in many ways, big ideas that are generated by observing details, and categorizing and combining these details into cognitive structures.
use	V;PRS;SG;2	use	You use concepts to see the relationships among the different elements of your experiences and to keep the information in your mind organized and accessible.
inform	V;PST;V.PTCP	informed	Concepts are informed by our semantic memory (you will learn more about semantic memory in a later chapter) and are present in every aspect of our lives; however, one of the easiest places to notice concepts is inside a classroom, where they are discussed explicitly.
study	V;PRS;SG;2	study	When you study United States history, for example, you learn about more than just individual events that have happened in America’s past.
absorb	V;PRS;SG;2	absorb	You absorb a large quantity of information by listening to and participating in discussions, examining maps, and reading first-hand accounts of people’s lives.
analyze	V;PRS;SG;3	analyzes	Your brain analyzes these details and develops an overall understanding of American history.
gather	V;PRS;SG;3	gathers	In the process, your brain gathers details that inform and refine your understanding of related concepts like democracy, power, and freedom.
agree	V;PST;V.PTCP	agreed	Some concepts, like tolerance, are agreed upon by many people, because they have been used in various ways over many years.
touch	V;PRS;PL;3	touch	In this way, concepts touch every aspect of our lives, from our many daily routines to the guiding principles behind the way governments function.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Another technique used by your brain to organize information is the identification of prototypes for the concepts you have developed.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	Or your prototype could be Mohandas Gandhi, sometimes called Mahatma Gandhi (“Mahatma” is an honorific title) (Figure 7.3).
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	In 1930, Mohandas Gandhi led a group in peaceful protest against a British tax on salt in India.
serve	V;PST;SG;3	served	Mohandas Gandhi served as a nonviolent force for independence for India while simultaneously demanding that Buddhist, Hindu, Muslim, and Christian leaders—both Indian and British—collaborate peacefully.
prevent	V;PRS;V.PTCP	preventing	Although he was not always successful in preventing violence around him, his life provides a steadfast example of the civil disobedience prototype (Constitutional Rights Foundation, 2013).
make	V;NFIN	make	Just as concepts can be abstract or concrete, we can make a distinction between concepts that are functions of our direct experience with the world and those that are more artificial in nature.
create	V;PRS;PL;3	create	Within your industry of interest or area of study, what are the challenges that create frustration?
turn	V;PST;V.PTCP	turned	How can these be turned into opportunities?
discuss	V;PST;1;PL	discussed	Earlier in this chapter, we discussed Evernote, a company that focuses on expanding our memories by storing and organizing information.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let’s look at some other examples of entrepreneurial endeavors in specific industries to help you plan your own venture in your own industry.
harvest	V;PRS;V.PTCP	harvesting	In the agriculture industry, insects, weeds, weather conditions, and the challenges of harvesting crops are all ripe for entrepreneurial activities.
affect	V;PST;V.PTCP	affected	The move toward organic produce has also affected this industry.
invent	V;NFIN	invent	From an entrepreneurial perspective, what products could you invent to support both organic farming and the problems of insects that damage or destroy crops?
use	V;NFIN	use	The old method was to use chemical sprays to kill the insects, but today, the growing demand for organic foods and increased awareness of the impact of chemical sprays on our environment are changing this scenario.
solve	V;NFIN	solve	One new idea to solve this problem combines a vacuum cleaner with an agriculture product.
watch	V;IMP;2	Watch	Watch this video on the creation of a crop vacuum that sucks up insects and bugs to learn more.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	A bug vacuum is an example of how using divergent thinking contributed to the solution of removing bugs from crops without using chemicals.
create	V;PRS;V.PTCP	creating	In the group activity of creating divergent ideas, this idea may not have been received well.
come	V;PST;V.PTCP	come	However, in the incubation stage, the idea must have come forward as a viable solution.
face	V;PRS;PL;3	face	Entrepreneurs frequently face the challenge of pressure to conform to established habits and patterns within industries.
include	V;PRS;SG;3	includes	Often, the entrepreneurial mindset includes futuristic ideas that shake up the normal, conventional processes that are grounded in experience over time.
try	V;PST;V.PTCP	Tried	Tried-and-tested processes and products that have a proven history of success can be a formidable obstacle to new ideas.
appear	V;NFIN	appear	A new idea may even appear as impossible or outlandish, perhaps even an embarrassment to the steady and predictable practices established within an industry.
create	V;NFIN	create	This can create a dilemma: Do we try something new and unproven that lacks documented research?
disregard	V;NFIN	disregard	Sometimes, we must disregard our past successes and research to be open to new possibilities for success and failure.
include	V;PRS;SG;3	includes	An entrepreneurial mindset includes creativity, problem-solving skills, and a propensity to innovation.23
support	V;PRS;SG;3	supports	Open-mindedness is one characteristic that supports creativity, problem solving, and innovation.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Taking	Taking the time to explore new ideas, dream, reflect, and view situations from a new perspective contribute to the entrepreneurial mindset.
lead	V;NFIN	lead	Some innovations can lead to disruptions within the industry, or even create a new industry.
present	V;PST;V.PTCP	presented	The innovator’s dilemma was presented by Clayton Christensen to explain disruptive technology, which are technologies that, once introduced, displace established patterns, processes, and systems previously accepted as normal or accepted.
threaten	V;PRS;SG;3	threatens	One example of a disruptive technology is Airbnb, a company that threatens the established hotel industry by connecting personal resources to people who desire those resources.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	If you have a spare bedroom that you aren’t using, why not sell that space to someone who wants and needs the space?
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	Airbnb has become a significant threat to the established hotel industry’s business model of building large hotels and renting rooms within those hotels to their customers.
reconfigure	V;PST;V.PTCP	reconfigured	Airbnb has reconfigured that model, and since its 2008 launch, 150 million travelers have taken advantage of 3 million Airbnb listings in more than 191 countries.
raise	V;PST;V.PTCP	raised	Airbnb has raised more than $3 billion (plus a $1 billion credit line) and is considering selling stocks to support significant expansion.
compare	V;IMP;2	Compare	Compare this market value to Hilton’s market capitalization of $19 billion and Marriott’s of $35 billion.
worry	V;PST;V.PTCP	worried	If you were the CEO of Hilton or Marriott, would you be worried?
recognize	V;PST;SG;3	recognized	The hotel industry recognized Airbnb as a threat, and in 2016, began a campaign to create legislation to rein in Airbnb’s growth and popularity.
play	V;PRS;V.PTCP	playing	From the hotel industry’s perspective, Airbnb is not playing by the same rules.
create	V;PRS;V.PTCP	creating	This is the definition of disruptive technology, the focus on creating a new idea or process that negates or challenges established process or products.24
result	V;PRS;PL;3	result	Sometimes disruptive technologies result from not listening to customers.
know	V;NFIN	know	Customers don’t always know what they want.
need	V;NFIN	need	Customer groups might need to be redefined by the entrepreneurial team on the basis of better models, knowing when to invest in developing lower-performance products that promise lower margins while still satisfying the need, and knowing when to pursue small markets at the expense of larger or established markets.
occur	V;PRS;PL;3	occur	Basically, disruptive technologies occur through identifying new and valuable processes and products.
recognize	V;PST;PL;3	recognized	The founders of Airbnb recognized that some people have unused resources, bedrooms, that other people need.
apply	V;NFIN	apply	We can apply this idea to other unused resources such as vehicles and motor homes.
see	V;PRS;1;PL	see	We see this model reproduced in short-term car rental and bike-sharing programs.
need	V;PST;V.PTCP	Needed	Emma Fleck. “Needed: Entrepreneurial Mindset.” Central Penn Business Journal, 34(12), 10. http://pageturnpro2.com.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com/Publications/201803/15/83956/PDF/131668002208352000_CPBJ033018WEB.pdf
combat	V;NFIN	Combat	Katie Benner. “Inside the Hotel Industry’s Plan to Combat Airbnb.” New York Times. April 16, 2017. https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/16/technology/inside-the-hotel-industrys-plan-to-combat-airbnb.html
be	V;PRS;PL;3	Are	Are there Moral Facts?
cover	V;PST;V.PTCP	covered	So far, we have covered how evolutionary ethics relates to the question what we ought to do and why we are moral in the first place.
concern	V;PRS;PL;3	concern	A related set of questions about ethics are metaethical questions, which concern the meaning of moral terms, our ways of gaining knowledge of moral facts, and the nature of the moral facts.
think	V;PRS;V.PTCP	thinking	In other words, apart from thinking about how we ought to live, and where our moral sense comes from, we can also ask what it means to affirm a particular answer to that question, how we could come to know the answer, and whether the answer would be a matter of fact or more like an opinion.
start	V;PRS;PL;3	start	Many books and articles in moral philosophy start with the observation that moral judgments seem to be objectively true and the assumption that this is how non-philosophers also think about morality.
write	V;PRS;SG;3	writes	For example, Michael Smith writes:
seem	V;PRS;1;PL	seem	We seem to think moral questions have correct answers; that the correct answers are made correct by objective moral facts; that moral facts are wholly determined by circumstances; and that, by engaging in moral conversation and argument, we can discover what these objective facts are.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	But as we have seen above, evolutionary theory explains why we would think that moral judgments are objective.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	The philosopher Michael Ruse takes this to show that the apparent objectivity of morality is something like an “illusion” foisted on us by our genes (Ruse [1986] 1998, 253).
show	V;NFIN	show	Of course, even if Ruse is correct, this does not show that moral judgments are not objective, but it can make us think whether the objectivity of morality really has to be explained, as many philosophers assume.
argue	V;PST;V.PTCP	argued	Ruse has also argued that an evolutionary account of morality suggests that there are no moral facts in the first place.
want	V;PRS;1;PL	want	Anything we want to explain about morality can be explained without mentioning moral facts, or so Ruse argues.
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	If moral facts are explanatorily redundant, indeed, moral realists (those who believe there are mind-independent, objective facts about morality, as discussed in Chapter 1) need to say why we should still suppose that moral facts do exist.
lead	V;PRS;SG;3	leads	Interestingly, Ruse’s methodological naturalism leads him to embrace a view that goes against the metaphysical naturalist position that moral facts exist.
argue	V;PRS;SG;3	argues	Relatedly, Sharon Street, for example, argues that evolutionary explanations of morality show that moral realism is probably false.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The kind of moral realism that Street has in mind is the view that moral properties exist as objective features of the world (to wit, whether stealing is wrong is independent of whether anyone thinks or feels that stealing is wrong).
start	V;PRS;SG;3	starts	Street starts with the premise that our moral judgments are influenced by evolutionary forces.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	This fact, Street claims, gives rise to a dilemma for moral realists concerning the relation of the evolutionary forces that influenced our moral judgments and the moral facts claimed to exist by moral realists.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	If there is no relation between the evolutionary forces and the moral facts, then it would be an astonishing coincidence if many of our moral judgments were true.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	In light of the coincidence, we have no reason to assume that our moral judgments are true—an unpalatable conclusion for realists.
claim	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Claiming	Claiming that there is a relation, however, is empirically dubious, according to Street.
mirror	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Mirroring	Mirroring Ruse’s claim, moral facts could be purged from what we assume to exist because they do not perform an important explanatory function.
reject	V;NFIN	reject	So, we should reject moral realism.
illustrate	V;PRS;SG;3	illustrates	Street’s argument illustrates how evolutionary theory can be used to make a case about which metaethical theories we should adopt.
rely	V;PRS;PL;3	rely	Both Ruse’s and Street’s arguments rely on the idea that we should only accept that something exists if it plays an indispensable explanatory role and there are means to resist these arguments. [12]
present	V;PST;V.PTCP	presented	Both arguments, as presented here, rely on the correctness of the evolutionary explanation of morality they convey.
turn	V;PRS;1;PL	turn	In the next section, we turn to the relevance of evolutionary ethics for moral epistemology.
have	V;NFIN	have	Can We have Justified Moral Beliefs?
interpret	V;PST;V.PTCP	interpreted	The evolutionary argument by Sharon Street can also be interpreted as an argument about moral justification and moral knowledge.
suppose	V;IMP;2	Suppose	Suppose that moral realists assume that there is no relation between our moral judgments and the moral facts.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	It would seem like an incredible coincidence if the realists were right that our moral judgments are nonetheless true.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are just too many possible moral truths and too many ways in which evolution could have “pushed” our moral beliefs.
claim	V;PRS;SG;3	claims	Street claims that such a coincidence would be too much to believe.
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	But what is the problem, exactly, with having beliefs that are only coincidentally true?
depend	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Depending	Depending on how we understand “coincidence,” my belief that there is a bird outside my window is coincidentally true because had I looked a little later, the bird would have flown away already.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	Accidentally true beliefs do not seem problematic in every case.
show	V;NFIN	show	The question for proponents of Street’s argument is to show why the evolutionary influence on human moral beliefs makes for a particularly problematic case of coincidence.
argue	V;PRS;SG;3	argues	The philosopher Richard Joyce argues that the problem has to do with the sensitivity of our moral beliefs to the moral facts (Joyce 2006).
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	Given	Given that our moral beliefs are influenced by evolutionary forces, and not by the moral facts, our moral beliefs would be the same even if the moral facts would change.
hold	V;NFIN	hold	But because we should not hold on to such insensitive beliefs, evolutionary explanations of morality show that our moral beliefs are unjustified (because they are not sensitive).
reveal	V;PRS;PL;3	reveal	It is not clear, however, whether and why evolutionary explanations of morality reveal something about our moral beliefs that is particularly troubling from an epistemological perspective.
depend	V;PRS;SG;3	depends	The impact of evolutionary ethics on epistemological questions depends on these deeper questions about epistemology. [13]
provide	V;PRS;PL;3	provide	Evolutionary explanations of morality, however, provide us with a useful starting point for thinking about these questions.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See Klenk (2019).
help	V;PST;V.PTCP	helped	Evolutionary ethics has helped us to get a much clearer sense of where the human moral sense is coming from.
reveal	V;PRS;V.PTCP	revealing	While this is a far cry from revealing to us what we ought to do, the research program seems full of promise nonetheless.
get	V;PRS;1;PL	get	Once we get into the details, we can see that it also raises deep theoretical questions about evolutionary explanations, the relation of descriptive and normative claims, the epistemology of justification and truth, and the general viability of naturalism in ethics.
leave	V;PST;V.PTCP	left	Much left to wonder, then, but with some ideas about where to get our answers.
motivate	V;PST;V.PTCP	Motivated	2.2 The Motivated Learner
consider	V;NFIN	consider	Questions to consider:
affect	V;NFIN	affect	How do different types of motivation affect my learning?
apply	V;NFIN	apply	How can I apply the Uses and Gratification Theory to make decisions about my learning?
prevent	V;NFIN	prevent	How do I prevent negative bias from hindering learning?
continue	V;NFIN	continue	In this section, you will continue to increase your ability as an informed learner.
explore	V;NFIN	explore	Here you will explore how much of an influence motivation has on learning, as well as how to use motivation to purposefully take an active role in any learning activity.
attempt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	attempting	Rather than passively attempting to absorb new information, you will learn how to make conscious decisions about the methods of learning you will use (based on what you intend to do with the information), how you will select and use learning materials that are appropriate for your needs, and how persistent you will be in the learning activity.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are three main motivation concepts that have been found to directly relate to learning.
prove	V;PST;V.PTCP	proven	Each of these has been proven to mean the difference between success and failure.
find	V;NFIN	find	You will find that each of these is a strong tool that will enable you to engage with learning material in a way that not only suits your needs, but also gives you ownership over your own learning processes.
cover	V;NFIN	cover	While much of this chapter will cover very specific aspects about the act of learning, in this section, we will present different information that may at first seem unrelated.
consider	V;NFIN	consider	Some people would consider it more of a personal outlook than a learning practice, and yet it has a significant influence on the ability to learn.
talk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	talking	What we are talking about here is called grit or resilience.
define	V;PST;V.PTCP	defined	Grit can be defined as personal perseverance toward a task or goal.
think	V;PST;V.PTCP	thought	In learning, it can be thought of as a trait that drives a person to keep trying until they succeed.
tie	V;PST;V.PTCP	tied	It is not tied to talent or ability, but is simply a tendency to not give up until something is finished or accomplished.
race	V;PRS;SG;3	races	U.S. Army veteran and captain of the U.S. Invictus team, Will Reynolds, races to the finish line.
show	V;PST;SG;3	showed	The study showed that grit and perseverance were better predictors of academic success and achievement than talent or IQ.
define	V;PST;V.PTCP	defined	This personality trait was defined as “grit” by the psychologist Angela Duckworth. 1
find	V;PST;PL;3	found	In a 2007 study Duckworth and colleagues found that individuals with high grit were able to maintain motivation in learning tasks despite failures.
examine	V;PST;SG;3	examined	The study examined a cross section of learning environments, such as GPA scores in Ivy League universities, dropout rates at West Point, rankings in the National Spelling Bee, and general educational attainment for adults.
show	V;PST;PL;3	showed	What the results showed was that grit and perseverance were better predictors of academic success and achievement than talent or IQ.
apply	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Applying	Applying Grit
dismiss	V;NFIN	dismiss	The concept of grit is an easy one to dismiss as something taken for granted.
have	V;PRS;1;PL	have	In our culture, we have a number of sayings and aphorisms that capture the essence of grit: “If at first you do not succeed, try, try again,” or the famous quote by Thomas Edison: “Genius is one percent inspiration, ninety-nine percent perspiration.”
understand	V;PRS;1;PL	understand	The problem is we all understand the concept, but actually applying it takes work.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	trying	If the task we are trying to complete is a difficult one, it can take a lot of work.
apply	V;PRS;V.PTCP	applying	The first step in applying grit is to adopt an attitude that looks directly to the end goal as the only acceptable outcome.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	With this attitude comes an acceptance that you may not succeed on the first attempt — or the nineteenth attempt.
fail	V;PST;V.PTCP	Failed	Failed attempts are viewed as merely part of the process and seen as a very useful way to gain knowledge that moves you toward success.
study	V;PRS;V.PTCP	studying	An example of this would be studying for an exam.
study	V;PRS;V.PTCP	studying	In your first attempt at studying you simply reread the chapters of your textbook covered in the exam.
find	V;PRS;SG;2	find	You find that while this reinforces some of the knowledge you have gained, it does not ensure you have all the information you will need to do well on the test.
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	You know that if you simply read the chapters yet again, there is no guarantee you are going to be any more successful.
determine	V;PRS;SG;2	determine	You determine that you need to find a different approach.
achieve	V;NFIN	achieve	In other words, your first attempt was not a complete failure, but it did not achieve the end goal, so you try again with a different method.
copy	V;PRS;PL;2	copy	On your second try, you copy down all of the main points onto a piece of paper using the section headlines from the chapters.
come	V;PRS;SG;2	come	After a short break you come back to your list and write down a summary of what you know about each item on your list.
accomplish	V;PRS;SG;3	accomplishes	This accomplishes two things: first, you are able to immediately spot areas where you need to learn more, and second, you can check your summaries against the text to make certain what you know is correct and adequate.
achieve	V;PST;V.PTCP	achieved	In this example, while you may not have yet achieved complete success, you will have learned what you need to do next.
study	V;PRS;SG;2	study	In true grit fashion, for your next try, you study those items on your list where you found you needed a bit more information, and then you go through your list again.
write	V;NFIN	write	This time you are able to write down summaries of all the important points, and you are confident you have the knowledge you need to do well on the exam.
stop	V;NFIN	stop	After this, you still do not stop, but instead you change your approach to use other methods that keep what you have learned fresh in your mind.
locate	V;NFIN;2	Locate	Locate on a map the major American civilizations before the arrival of the Spanish
discuss	V;NFIN;2	Discuss	Discuss the cultural achievements of these civilizations
discuss	V;NFIN;2	Discuss	Discuss the differences and similarities between lifestyles, religious practices, and customs among the native peoples
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	Some scholars believe that between nine and fifteen thousand years ago, a land bridge existed between Asia and North America that we now call Beringia.
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	The first inhabitants of what would be named the Americas migrated across this bridge in search of food.
melt	V;PST;PL;3	melted	When the glaciers melted, water engulfed Beringia, and the Bering Strait was formed.
come	V;PST;PL;3	came	Later settlers came by boat across the narrow strait.
share	V;PRS;PL;3	share	(The fact that Asians and American Indians share genetic markers on a Y chromosome lends credibility to this migration theory.)
move	V;PRS;V.PTCP	moving	Continually moving southward, the settlers eventually populated both North and South America, creating unique cultures that ranged from the highly complex and urban Aztec civilization in what is now Mexico City to the woodland tribes of eastern North America.
suggest	V;PRS;SG;3	suggests	Recent research along the west coast of South America suggests that migrant populations may have traveled down this coast by water as well as by land.
believe	V;PRS;PL;3	believe	Researchers believe that about ten thousand years ago, humans also began the domestication of plants and animals, adding agriculture as a means of sustenance to hunting and gathering techniques.
bring	V;PST;SG;3	brought	With this agricultural revolution, and the more abundant and reliable food supplies it brought, populations grew and people were able to develop a more settled way of life, building permanent settlements.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	This map shows the extent of the major civilizations of the Western Hemisphere.
develop	V;PST;PL;3	developed	In South America, early civilizations developed along the coast because the high Andes and the inhospitable Amazon Basin made the interior of the continent less favorable for settlement.
stretch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	stretching	Mesoamerica is the geographic area stretching from north of Panama up to the desert of central Mexico.
mark	V;PST;V.PTCP	marked	Although marked by great topographic, linguistic, and cultural diversity, this region cradled a number of civilizations with similar characteristics.
possess	V;PST;PL;3	possessed	Mesoamericans were polytheistic; their gods possessed both male and female traits and demanded blood sacrifices of enemies taken in battle or ritual bloodletting.
domesticate	V;PST;V.PTCP	domesticated	Corn, or maize, domesticated by 5000 BCE, formed the basis of their diet.
develop	V;PST;PL;3	developed	They developed a mathematical system, built huge edifices, and devised a calendar that accurately predicted eclipses and solstices and that priest-astronomers used to direct the planting and harvesting of crops.
create	V;PST;PL;3	created	Most important for our knowledge of these peoples, they created the only known written language in the Western Hemisphere; researchers have made much progress in interpreting the inscriptions on their temples and pyramids.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Though the area had no overarching political structure, trade over long distances helped diffuse culture.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Weapons made of obsidian, jewelry crafted from jade, feathers woven into clothing and ornaments, and cacao beans that were whipped into a chocolate drink formed the basis of commerce.
flourish	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Flourishing	Flourishing along the hot Gulf Coast of Mexico from about 1200 to about 400 BCE, the Olmec produced a number of major works of art, architecture, pottery, and sculpture.
build	V;PST;PL;3	built	The Olmec built aqueducts to transport water into their cities and irrigate their fields.
grow	V;PST;PL;3	grew	They grew maize, squash, beans, and tomatoes.
bre	V;PST;PL;3	bred	They also bred small domesticated dogs which, along with fish, provided their protein.
know	V;PRS;SG;3	knows	Although no one knows what happened to the Olmec after about 400 BCE, in part because the jungle reclaimed many of their cities, their culture was the base upon which the Maya and the Aztec built.
worship	V;PST;SG;3	worshipped	It was the Olmec who worshipped a rain god, a maize god, and the feathered serpent so important in the future pantheons of the Aztecs (who called him Quetzalcoatl) and the Maya (to whom he was Kukulkan).
develop	V;PST;PL;3	developed	The Olmec also developed a system of trade throughout Mesoamerica, giving rise to an elite class.
carve	V;PST;PL;3	carved	The Olmec carved heads from giant boulders that ranged from four to eleven feet in height and could weigh up to fifty tons.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	All these figures have flat noses, slightly crossed eyes, and large lips.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	These physical features can be seen today in some of the peoples indigenous to the area.
rise	V;PST;SG;3	rose	After the decline of the Olmec, a city rose in the fertile central highlands of Mesoamerica.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	One of the largest population centers in pre-Columbian America and home to more than 100,000 people at its height in about 500 CE, Teotihuacan was located about thirty miles northeast of modern Mexico City.
debate	V;PST;V.PTCP	debated	The ethnicity of this settlement’s inhabitants is debated; some scholars believe it was a multiethnic city.
allow	V;PST;PL;3	allowed	Large-scale agriculture and the resultant abundance of food allowed time for people to develop special trades and skills other than farming.
construct	V;PST;PL;3	constructed	Builders constructed over twenty-two hundred apartment compounds for multiple families, as well as more than a hundred temples.
uncover	V;PST;V.PTCP	uncovered	Near the Temple of the Feathered Serpent, graves have been uncovered that suggest humans were sacrificed for religious purposes.
extend	V;PST;SG;3	extended	The city was also the center for trade, which extended to settlements on Mesoamerica’s Gulf Coast.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	The Maya were one Mesoamerican culture that had strong ties to Teotihuacan.
flourish	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Flourishing	Flourishing from roughly 2000 BCE to 900 CE in what is now Mexico, Belize, Honduras, and Guatemala, the Maya perfected the calendar and written language the Olmec had begun.
devise	V;PST;PL;3	devised	They devised a written mathematical system to record crop yields and the size of the population, and to assist in trade.
surround	V;PST;V.PTCP	Surrounded	Surrounded by farms relying on primitive agriculture, they built the city-states of Copan, Tikal, and Chichen Itza along their major trade routes, as well as temples, statues of gods, pyramids, and astronomical observatories (Figure 1.5).
last	V;PST;SG;3	lasted	However, because of poor soil and a drought that lasted nearly two centuries, their civilization declined by about 900 CE and they abandoned their large population centers.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	El Castillo, located at Chichen Itza in the eastern Yucatán peninsula, served as a temple for the god Kukulkan.
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	contains	Each side contains ninety-one steps to the top.
count	V;PRS;V.PTCP	counting	When counting the top platform, the total number of stairs is three hundred and sixty-five, the number of days in a year.
find	V;PST;PL;3	found	The Spanish found little organized resistance among the weakened Maya upon their arrival in the 1520s.
find	V;NFIN	find	However, they did find Mayan history, in the form of glyphs, or pictures representing words, recorded in folding books called codices (the singular is codex).
fear	V;PST;SG;3	feared	In 1562, Bishop Diego de Landa, who feared the converted natives had reverted to their traditional religious practices, collected and burned every codex he could find.
survive	V;PRS;PL;3	survive	Today only a few survive.
link	V;PRS;V.PTCP	linking	an ancient land bridge linking Asia and North America
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	1. Which of the following Indian peoples built homes in cliff dwellings that still exist?
develop	V;PST;SG;3	developed	2. Which culture developed the first writing system in the Western Hemisphere?
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Despite how much humans have in common, cultural differences are far more prevalent than cultural universals.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	For example, while all cultures have language, analysis of particular language structures and conversational etiquette reveal tremendous differences.
stand	V;NFIN	stand	In some Middle Eastern cultures, it is common to stand close to others in conversation.
keep	V;PRS;PL;3	keep	North Americans keep more distance and maintain a large “personal space.”
eat	V;PRS;V.PTCP	eating	Even something as simple as eating and drinking varies greatly from culture to culture.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	If your professor comes into an early morning class holding a mug of liquid, what do you assume she is drinking?
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	In the United States, it’s most likely filled with coffee, not Earl Grey tea, a favorite in England, or Yak Butter tea, a staple in Tibet.
vary	V;PRS;PL;3	vary	The way cuisines vary across cultures fascinates many people.
pride	V;PRS;PL;3	pride	Some travelers pride themselves on their willingness to try unfamiliar foods, like celebrated food writer Anthony Bourdain, while others return home expressing gratitude for their native culture’s fare.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Often, people in the United States express disgust at other cultures’ cuisine and think that it’s gross to eat meat from a dog or guinea pig, for example, while they don’t question their own habit of eating cows or pigs.
evaluate	V;PRS;V.PTCP	evaluating	Such attitudes are an example of ethnocentrism, or evaluating and judging another culture based on how it compares to one’s own cultural norms.
describe	V;PST;SG;3	described	Ethnocentrism, as sociologist William Graham Sumner (1906) described the term, involves a belief or attitude that one’s own culture is better than all others.
tend	V;PRS;PL;3	tend	For example, Americans tend to say that people from England drive on the “wrong” side of the road, rather than on the “other” side.
find	V;NFIN	find	Someone from a country where dog meat is standard fare might find it off - putting to see a dog in a French restaurant — not on the menu, but as a pet and patron’s companion.
refer	V;PRS;V.PTCP	referring	A good example of ethnocentrism is referring to parts of Asia as the "Far East."
question	V;NFIN	question	One might question, "Far east of where?"
connect	V;PRS;SG;3	connects	A high level of appreciation for one’s own culture can be healthy; a shared sense of community pride, for example, connects people in a society.
lead	V;NFIN	lead	But ethnocentrism can lead to disdain or dislike for other cultures and could cause misunderstanding and conflict.
travel	V;PRS;PL;3	travel	People with the best intentions sometimes travel to a society to “help” its people, because they see them as uneducated or backward-essentially inferior.
begin	V;PST;V.PTCP	begun	Europe’s colonial expansion, begun in the sixteenth century, was often accompanied by a severe cultural imperialism.
view	V;PST;PL;3	viewed	European colonizers often viewed the people in the lands they colonized as uncultured savages who were in need of European governance, dress, religion, and other cultural practices.
include	V;NFIN	include	A more modern example of cultural imperialism may include the work of international aid agencies who introduce agricultural methods and plant species from developed countries while overlooking indigenous varieties and agricultural approaches that are better suited to the particular region.
confront	V;PST;V.PTCP	confronted	Ethnocentrism can be so strong that when confronted with all of the differences of a new culture, one may experience disorientation and frustration.
call	V;PRS;1;PL	call	In sociology, we call this culture shock.
find	V;NFIN	find	A traveler from Chicago might find the nightly silence of rural Montana unsettling, not peaceful.
annoy	V;PST;V.PTCP	annoyed	An exchange student from China might be annoyed by the constant interruptions in class as other students ask questions—a practice that is considered rude in China.
excite	V;PST;V.PTCP	excited	Perhaps the Chicago traveler was initially captivated with Montana’s quiet beauty and the Chinese student was originally excited to see a U.S.-style classroom firsthand.
experience	V;PRS;PL;3	experience	But as they experience unanticipated differences from their own culture, their excitement gives way to discomfort and doubts about how to behave appropriately in the new situation.
learn	V;PRS;PL;3	learn	Eventually, as people learn more about a culture, they recover from culture shock.
appear	V;NFIN	appear	Culture shock may appear because people aren’t always expecting cultural differences.
discover	V;PST;SG;3	discovered	Anthropologist Ken Barger (1971) discovered this when he conducted a participatory observation in an Inuit community in the Canadian Arctic.
hesitate	V;PST;SG;3	hesitated	Originally from Indiana, Barger hesitated when invited to join a local snowshoe race.
know	V;PST;SG;3	knew	He knew he’d never hold his own against these experts.
finish	V;PST;SG;3	finished	Sure enough, he finished last, to his mortification.
congratulate	V;PST;PL;3	congratulated	But the tribal members congratulated him, saying, “You really tried!”
learn	V;PST;V.PTCP	learned	In Barger’s own culture, he had learned to value victory.
win	V;PRS;V.PTCP	winning	To the Inuit people, winning was enjoyable, but their culture valued survival skills essential to their environment: how hard someone tried could mean the difference between life and death.
participate	V;PST;SG;3	participated	Over the course of his stay, Barger participated in caribou hunts, learned how to take shelter in winter storms, and sometimes went days with little or no food to share among tribal members.
try	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Trying	Trying hard and working together, two nonmaterial values, were indeed much more important than winning.
learn	V;PST;SG;3	learned	During his time with the Inuit tribe, Barger learned to engage in cultural relativism.
assess	V;PRS;V.PTCP	assessing	Cultural relativism is the practice of assessing a culture by its own standards rather than viewing it through the lens of one’s own culture.
practice	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Practicing	Practicing cultural relativism requires an open mind and a willingness to consider, and even adapt to, new values and norms.
embrace	V;PRS;V.PTCP	embracing	However, indiscriminately embracing everything about a new culture is not always possible.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Even the most culturally relativist people from egalitarian societies—ones in which women have political rights and control over their own bodies—would question whether the widespread practice of female genital mutilation in countries such as Ethiopia and Sudan should be accepted as a part of cultural tradition.
attempt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	attempting	Sociologists attempting to engage in cultural relativism, then, may struggle to reconcile aspects of their own culture with aspects of a culture that they are studying.
attempt	V;PRS;PL;3	attempt	Sometimes when people attempt to rectify feelings of ethnocentrism and develop cultural relativism, they swing too far to the other end of the spectrum.
refer	V;PRS;SG;3	refers	Xenocentrism is the opposite of ethnocentrism, and refers to the belief that another culture is superior to one’s own.
pronounce	V;PST;V.PTCP	pronounced	(The Greek root word xeno, pronounced “ZEE-no,” means “stranger” or “foreign guest.”)
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	An exchange student who goes home after a semester abroad or a sociologist who returns from the field may find it difficult to associate with the values of their own culture after having experienced what they deem a more upright or nobler way of living.
describe	V;NFIN;2	Describe	Describe Einstein’s view of gravity as the warping of spacetime in the presence of massive objects
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand that Newton’s concept of the gravitational force between two massive objects and Einstein’s concept of warped spacetime are different explanations for the same observed accelerations of one massive object in the presence of another massive object
bend	V;PST;V.PTCP	bent	Is light actually bent from its straight-line path by the mass of Earth?
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	How can light, which has no mass, be affected by gravity?
prefer	V;PST;SG;3	preferred	Einstein preferred to think that it is space and time that are affected by the presence of a large mass; light beams, and everything else that travels through space and time, then find their paths affected.
follow	V;PRS;SG;3	follows	Light always follows the shortest path—but that path may not always be straight.
say	V;IMP;2	Say	Say you want to fly from Chicago to Rome.
go	V;NFIN	go	Since an airplane can’t go through the solid body of the Earth, the shortest distance is not a straight line but the arc of a great circle.
show	V;NFIN	show	To show what Einstein’s insight really means, let’s first consider how we locate an event in space and time.
imagine	V;IMP;2	imagine	For example, imagine you have to describe to worried school officials the fire that broke out in your room when your roommate tried cooking shish kebabs in the fireplace.
explain	V;PRS;SG;2	explain	You explain that your dorm is at 6400 College Avenue, a street that runs in the left-right direction on a map of your town; you are on the fifth floor, which tells where you are in the up-down direction; and you are the sixth room back from the elevator, which tells where you are in the forward-backward direction.
explain	V;PRS;SG;2	explain	Then you explain that the fire broke out at 6:23 p.m. (but was soon brought under control), which specifies the event in time.
pinpoint	V;PST;V.PTCP	pinpointed	Any event in the universe, whether nearby or far away, can be pinpointed using the three dimensions of space and the one dimension of time.
consider	V;PST;SG;3	considered	Newton considered space and time to be completely independent, and that continued to be the accepted view until the beginning of the twentieth century.
show	V;PST;SG;3	showed	But Einstein showed that there is an intimate connection between space and time, and that only by considering the two together—in what we call spacetime—can we build up a correct picture of the physical world.
examine	V;PRS;1;PL	examine	We examine spacetime a bit more closely in the next subsection.
curve	V;PRS;SG;3	curves	The gist of Einstein’s general theory is that the presence of matter curves or warps the fabric of spacetime.
identify	V;PST;V.PTCP	identified	This curving of spacetime is identified with gravity.
enter	V;PRS;SG;3	enters	When something else—a beam of light, an electron, or the starship Enterprise—enters such a region of distorted spacetime, its path will be different from what it would have been in the absence of the matter.
summarize	V;PST;SG;3	summarized	As American physicist John Wheeler summarized it: “Matter tells spacetime how to curve; spacetime tells matter how to move.”
depend	V;PRS;SG;3	depends	The amount of distortion in spacetime depends on the mass of material that is involved and on how concentrated and compact it is.
read	V;PRS;V.PTCP	reading	Terrestrial objects, such as the book you are reading, have far too little mass to introduce any significant distortion.
build	V;PRS;V.PTCP	building	Newton’s view of gravity is just fine for building bridges, skyscrapers, or amusement park rides.
have	V;NFIN	have	General relativity does, however, have some practical applications.
tell	V;NFIN	tell	The GPS (Global Positioning System) in every smartphone can tell you where you are within 5 to 10 meters only because the effects of general and special relativity on the GPS satellites in orbit around the Earth are taken into account.
produce	V;PRS;PL;3	produce	Unlike a book or your roommate, stars produce measurable distortions in spacetime.
produce	V;PRS;SG;3	produces	A white dwarf, with its stronger surface gravity, produces more distortion just above its surface than does a red giant with the same mass.
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	So, you see, we are eventually going to talk about collapsing stars again, but not before discussing Einstein’s ideas (and the evidence for them) in more detail.
understand	V;NFIN	understand	How can we understand the distortion of spacetime by the presence of some (significant) amount of mass?
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let’s try the following analogy.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	You may have seen maps of New York City that squeeze the full three dimensions of this towering metropolis onto a flat sheet of paper and still have enough information so tourists will not get lost.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let’s do something similar with diagrams of spacetime.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	Figure 24.7, for example, shows the progress of a motorist driving east on a stretch of road in Kansas where the countryside is absolutely flat.
travel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	traveling	Since our motorist is traveling only in the east-west direction and the terrain is flat, we can ignore the other two dimensions of space.
elapse	V;PST;V.PTCP	elapsed	The amount of time elapsed since he left home is shown on the y-axis, and the distance traveled eastward is shown on the x-axis.
drive	V;PST;SG;3	drove	From A to B he drove at a uniform speed; unfortunately, it was too fast a uniform speed and a police car spotted him.
stop	V;PST;SG;3	stopped	From B to C he stopped to receive his ticket and made no progress through space, only through time.
drive	V;PST;SG;3	drove	From C to D he drove more slowly because the police car was behind him.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	This diagram shows the progress of a motorist traveling east across the flat Kansas landscape.
travel	V;PST;V.PTCP	traveled	Distance traveled is plotted along the horizontal axis.
elapse	V;PST;V.PTCP	elapsed	The time elapsed since the motorist left the starting point is plotted along the vertical axis.
let	V;IMP;2	let	Now let’s try illustrating the distortions of spacetime in two dimensions.
use	V;NFIN	use	In this case, we will (in our imaginations) use a rubber sheet that can stretch or warp if we put objects on it.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let’s imagine stretching our rubber sheet taut on four posts.
complete	V;NFIN	complete	To complete the analogy, we need something that normally travels in a straight line (as light does).
suppose	V;IMP;2	Suppose	Suppose we have an extremely intelligent ant—a friend of the comic book superhero Ant-Man, perhaps—that has been trained to walk in a straight line.
begin	V;PRS;1;PL	begin	We begin with just the rubber sheet and the ant, simulating empty space with no mass in it.
put	V;PRS;1;PL	put	We put the ant on one side of the sheet and it walks in a beautiful straight line over to the other side (Figure 24.8).
put	V;PRS;1;PL	put	We next put a small grain of sand on the rubber sheet.
distort	V;NFIN	distort	The sand does distort the sheet a tiny bit, but this is not a distortion that we or the ant can measure.
send	V;PRS;1;PL	send	If we send the ant so it goes close to, but not on top of, the sand grain, it has little trouble continuing to walk in a straight line.
grab	V;PRS;1;PL	grab	Now we grab something with a little more mass—say, a small pebble.
bend	V;PRS;SG;3	bends	It bends or distorts the sheet just a bit around its position.
send	V;PRS;1;PL	send	If we send the ant into this region, it finds its path slightly altered by the distortion of the sheet.
follow	V;PRS;1;PL	follow	The distortion is not large, but if we follow the ant’s path carefully, we notice it deviating slightly from a straight line.
get	V;PRS;SG;3	gets	The effect gets more noticeable as we increase the mass of the object that we put on the sheet.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let’s say we now use a massive paperweight.
distort	V;PRS;SG;3	distorts	Such a heavy object distorts or warps the rubber sheet very effectively, putting a good sag in it.
see	V;NFIN	see	From our point of view, we can see that the sheet near the paperweight is no longer straight.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	The median, M, is called both the second quartile and the 50th percentile.
calculate	V;NFIN	calculate	To calculate quartiles and percentiles, you must order the data from smallest to largest.
divide	V;PRS;PL;3	divide	Quartiles divide ordered data into quarters.
divide	V;PRS;PL;3	divide	Percentiles divide ordered data into hundredths.
recall	V;IMP;2	Recall	Recall that a percent means one-hundredth.
mean	V;PRS;PL;3	mean	So, percentiles mean the data is divided into 100 sections.
score	V;NFIN	score	To score in the 90th percentile of an exam does not mean, necessarily, that you received 90 percent on a test.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	It means that 90 percent of test scores are the same as or less than your score and that 10 percent of the test scores are the same as or greater than your test score.
compare	V;PRS;V.PTCP	comparing	Percentiles are useful for comparing values.
use	V;PRS;PL;3	use	For this reason, universities and colleges use percentiles extensively.
use	V;PRS;PL;3	use	One instance in which colleges and universities use percentiles is when SAT results are used to determine a minimum testing score that will be used as an acceptance factor.
suppose	V;IMP;2	suppose	For example, suppose Duke accepts SAT scores at or above the 75th percentile.
translate	V;PRS;SG;3	translates	That translates into a score of at least 1220.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Percentiles are mostly used with very large populations.
say	V;NFIN	say	Therefore, if you were to say that 90 percent of the test scores are less, and not the same or less, than your score, it would be acceptable because removing one particular data value is not significant.
measure	V;PRS;SG;3	measures	The median is a number that measures the center of the data.
think	V;NFIN	think	You can think of the median as the middle value, but it does not actually have to be one of the observed values.
separate	V;PRS;SG;3	separates	It is a number that separates ordered data into halves.
consider	V;IMP;2	consider	For example, consider the following data:
order	V;PST;V.PTCP	Ordered	Ordered from smallest to largest:
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	When a data set has an even number of data values, the median is equal to the average of the two middle values when the data are arranged in ascending order (least to greatest).
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	When a data set has an odd number of data values, the median is equal to the middle value when the data are arranged in ascending order.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Since there are 14 observations (an even number of data values), the median is between the seventh value, 6.8, and the eighth value, 7.2.
find	V;NFIN	find	To find the median, add the two values together and divide by two.
separate	V;PRS;PL;3	separate	Quartiles are numbers that separate the data into quarters.
find	V;NFIN	find	To find the quartiles, first find the median, or second, quartile.
get	V;NFIN	get	To get the idea, consider the same data set:
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The data set has an even number of values (14 data values), so the median will be the average of the two middle values (the average of 6.8 and 7.2), which is calculated as (6.8+7.2)/2 and equals 7.
divide	V;PRS;1;PL	divide	The first quartile is the median of the lower half of the data, so if we divide the data into seven values in the lower half and seven values in the upper half, we can see that we have an odd number of values in the lower half.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using the same procedure, we can see that the median of the upper half, or the third quartile (Q3) will be the middle value of the upper half, or 9.
illustrate	V;PST;V.PTCP	illustrated	The quartiles are illustrated below:
indicate	V;PRS;SG;3	indicates	The interquartile range is a number that indicates the spread of the middle half, or the middle 50 percent of the data.
calculate	V;PST;V.PTCP	calculated	The IQR for this data set is calculated as 9 minus 2, or 7.
help	V;NFIN	help	The IQR can help to determine potential outliers.
suspect	V;PST;V.PTCP	suspected	A value is suspected to be a potential outlier if it is less than 1.5 × IQR below the first quartile or more than 1.5 × IQR above the third quartile.
require	V;PRS;PL;3	require	Potential outliers always require further investigation.
understand	V;PRS;V.PTCP	understanding	These special data points may be errors or some kind of abnormality, or they may be a key to understanding the data.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	For the following 13 real estate prices, calculate the IQR and determine if any prices are potential outliers.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try It 2.15
calculate	V;IMP;2	calculate	For the 11 salaries, calculate the IQR and determine if any salaries are outliers.
follow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	following	The following salaries are in dollars.
see	V;PST;SG;2	saw	In the example above, you just saw the calculation of the median, first quartile, and third quartile.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	The five number summary is used to create a box plot.
