blend	V;PST;V.PTCP	blended	And then the Chardonnay-Pinot Noir, it is a blended wine of 60% Chardonnay and 40% Pinot Noir, so it's more like a dry rosé style of wine with lots of strawberry flavors, hints of yellow apple, and it's 2018 vintage, which is unwooded.
pick	V;PRS;PL;2	pick	And then with the Chardonnay, Chardonnay is naturally high-acidic, but not so much aromatic on the nose, so you pick up lots of citrus fruits, limes, lemons on the palate, and this is 2017 vintage, also unwooded.
enjoy	V;NFIN	enjoy	So these are the type of wine that you can enjoy on their own and also with your light meals, because they are made to open now, drink now wines.
ask	V;NFIN	ask	Can I ask you, with your white wines, how – how long – when's the best time to drink them?
drink	V;NFIN;2	drink	It's open now drink now wines, as I said they are lifestyle wines, easy drinking, everyday drinking styles.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	But if you want to keep them, I would say 5 years in the cellar.
keep	V;NFIN	keep	Yes you can keep them in your cellar, and then the red wines, six years to seven years, not more than that.
spend	V;PST;PL;3	spent	And then with the red wines from the Signature they only spent eight months in the French oak.
get	V;NFIN	get	So the reason behind that shorter period of time is for you to get more of the original flavors from the grapes, not so much of the secondary flavor since they are more level entry wines.
grow	V;PST;V.PTCP	grown	So the Pinotage is a uniquely grown South African grape.
discover	V;PST;V.PTCP	discovered	It was discovered in the year of 1925 by a professor called Abraham Perold in the University of Stellenbosch.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	So it's a high grade grape that is made from Pinot Noir and the Hermitage.
take	V;PST;SG;2	took	You took a stem of both, cross-breeded them.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	The Hermitage is known as the Cinsault today.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	So the Pinotage is very aromatic, has lots of plum fruits, red fruit flavors.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	This is a 2017 vintage and then the Shiraz, it's known for its biasing characteristics, that what you pick up on the palette on the nose to the palate, spicy peppery flavor, has lots of great fruits and this is 2017 vintage.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	And then the Cab, the Cabernet is known for its thick uh skin and it's known for when it's blended, it always acts as a backbone to the wine.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	So since this one had not much of the oak barrel you pick up the original black currant fruits, pencil shavings aromas, has lots of black cherries as well.
wait	V;NFIN	wait	So, so with th- with these people, it's better to wait for these as well, even though they're your everyday –
say	V;NFIN	say	Would it be better to like say –
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember, while it gets better with age, so, these also.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you!
enjoy	V;IMP;2	Enjoy	Enjoy!
bring	V;NFIN	bring	I will bring the cheese platter.
thank	V;PRS;SG;1	Thank	Thank you!
hear	V;PST;SG;2	heard	So you heard it people: whatever she said.
make	V;NFIN	make	To make it a real wine tasting, we also got a cheese platter.
get	V;PST;SG;3	Got	Got some cheese!
smell	V;PRS;V.PTCP	smelling	I'm just smelling the wine.
see	V;PST;1;PL	saw	So we just saw a wine tasting, and got to say it was really good.
give	V;PST;PL;3	gave	They gave us a little wine.
feel	V;PRS;SG;1	feel	I feel like my sunburn adds nicely to the effect.
spit	V;PRS;SG;1	spit	I actually spit it, all of it, in here.
drink	V;PST;SG;1	drank	Yeah, I drank all of it.
see	V;PRS;PL;2	see	But you see all of these glasses?
drink	V;PST;SG;1	drank	I drank all of them.
drink	V;PST;SG;2	drank	No you actually drank a few sips –
lie	V;PRS;V.PTCP	lying	He's lying, you drank – you drank most of them.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	I've been called out!
say	V;IMP;2	say	Just say your favorite wine.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	Got	Got it.
have	V;NFIN	have	Crisp, fruity, fresh everyday wine, good to have with fish and maybe chicken.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Actually all of us didn't choose the most expensive one.
have	V;PST;1;PL	had	We had one of the very expensive ones, and actually none of us liked it the most.
like	V;PST;1;PL	liked	We all liked the inexpensive version of the Pinotage more.
know	V;PRS;PL;2	know	So, was a really good experience, and I'll, you know, gotta head home.
drive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	driving	He's driving, not me.
settle	V;PST;V.PTCP	settled	The Chatnam Islands (Rekohu in the indigenous Moriori language) are the eastern-most settled islands in New Zealand.
jog	V;PRS;SG;3	jogs	the International Date Line jogs eastward to keep them on the same calendar day as the rest of New Zealand.
lie	V;PRS;PL;3	lie	The islands lie 860 km east of Christchurch, in the middle of the "Roaring Forties".
steep	V;PST;V.PTCP	Steeped	Steeped in culture and history, these islands are on the very edge of civilisation.
experience	V;IMP;2	Experience	Experience Moriori culture, experience Rekohu.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
consist	V;PRS;PL;3	consist	The Chatham Islands consist of the main island, Chatham Island itself, with a population of 600, smaller Pitt Island with about 40 inhabitants, and a number of rocky outcrops.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	The islands are volcanic in origin and have a unique and sensitive habitat that supports many rare and endangered species.
descend	V;PST;V.PTCP	descended	The original people of the islands are the Moriori, who were descended from the Maori of mainland New Zealand, but who developed their own culture and variant of the language.
arrive	V;PST;PL;3	arrived	In 1835 Maori settlers from the mainland arrived, massacred and enslaved the Moriori.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
travel	V;NFIN	travel	The only way for visitors to travel to the Chathams is via scheduled or chartered air service.
fly	V;PRS;PL;3	fly	Air Chathams fly 5 days per week, from either Christchurch, Auckland or Wellington and, seasonally, from Napier.
base	V;PST;V.PTCP	based	The plane is based in the Chathams and flies to mainland New Zealand in the morning, then back to the Chathams in the afternoon.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	As part of New Zealand, there are no passport or visa requirements and no border controls on arrival from the mainland.
book	V;NFIN	book	You should book or ensure accommodation before you board a flight for the Chathams, as during the popular months of the tourism season, between October and April, accommodation can be severely limited.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are no same-day return flights, so once there you have to stay at least one night.
fly	V;PRS;V.PTCP	flying	Although flying to the Chathams and back is not cheap, it is worth it.
fly	V;PRS;PL;3	fly	Even the mainland school groups that fly there for school camps, and choose to stay at Kopinga Marae [1] get value for money by chartering a flight one way.
have	V;NFIN	have	Anyway, you don't have a choice on how you get there, unless you happen to own an aeroplane.
get	V;PRS;SG;2	get	However, when you get off that aeroplane, if you haven't pre-organised transport, you will have to walk, and it is a long way into town.
schedule	V;PST;V.PTCP	scheduled	The only scheduled shipping service to the Chatham Islands remains the Black Robin Freighters "Rangatira", an antiquated, slow and small coastal trading vessel.
service	V;PST;V.PTCP	serviced	This boat has serviced the islands for many years, but does not carry passengers.
appear	V;PRS;PL;3	appear	Freight charges on the Rangatira appear to be flexible and subject to change.
call	V;NFIN	call	Vessels can call at the Chathams and there are a number of "safe" harbours for shelter if the need arises.
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember to notify the local policeman, or Ministry of Fisheries officer if you are entering New Zealand via the Chathams.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want a wharf berthage then call the local harbour master as you near the Chathams and he will sort you out.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get around
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are several businesses on Chatham Island that will rent you a vehicle.
expect	V;IMP;2	expect	Do not expect the flashest and latest in rental cars though.
warrant	V;PST;V.PTCP	warranted	The cars "will" be warranted and safe, but they won't be exceptionally "flash".
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Chatham Motors have a range of nice rental vans available as well and they provide pretty good service for both visitors and locals.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	Unique tree carvings done by the peaceful Moriori people hundreds of years ago.
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	Set	Set adjacent to Henga Lodge is the magnificent Henga Scenic Reserve.
meander	V;NFIN	meander	Guests of the lodge can meander around either a short easy walk, or take the longer 2 hr return walk out onto the spectacular beach.
want	V;NFIN	want	One of only two National Historic Scenic Reserves in New Zealand (the other is the Waitangi Treaty Grounds), Hapupu is a special place, and somewhere that you will want to tell others about.
hold	V;PRS;SG;3	holds	Spiritually and culturally important to Moriori, it holds the last known concentration of momori-rakau, or tree carvings done by the ancestors of today's Moriori.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are many views on why they did these carvings, but whatever the reason, you will find them a very special attraction.
boast	V;PRS;SG;3	boasts	One of the most scenicly attractive ports in the whole of New Zealand, Port Hutt boasts an incredibly sheltered little harbour, with picturesque views and safe anchorage in all weather.
define	V;NFIN	define	If you could define a place that will remind you of your visit to the Chathams, then this very special place will be at the top of your list.
open	V;PST;V.PTCP	Opened	Opened in 2005, Kopinga is the official meeting place of Te Imi Moriori.
adorn	V;PST;V.PTCP	Adorned	Adorned with contemporary Moriori carvings and traditional taonga (treasures), the marae is a magnificent, tasteful and contemporary testament to the ancestors of Te Imi Moriori.
enjoy	V;IMP;2	Enjoy	Enjoy a guided tour of this beautiful and peaceful building.
utilise	V;PRS;PL;3	utilise	School-Camps At Kopinga Marae [3] also utilise this wonderful national treasure for hosting visiting school groups.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Tame Horomona Rehe was the last known full-blooded Moriori and he passed away in 1933.
live	V;PRS;PL;3	live	His own descendants and the descendants of other Moriori live on today.
come	V;IMP;2	Come	Come and have a look at this statue, erected in 1985 to commemorate his passing.
come	V;IMP;2	Come	Come and see Manakau, the property of his descendants, located on the eastern corner of Rekohu and blessed with the sun's first rays as they rise above the sea each day.
find	V;NFIN	find	You'll find world-class museums and cultural events, professional sports and amusement parks, and the most golf courses per capita in the United States.
Rate	V;PST;V.PTCP	Rated	Places Rated Almanac ranks the area second in recreational options out of 354 US metro areas.
rank	V;PRS;SG;3	ranks	Plus, this region ranks fifth in the nation in number of major cultural resources per one million residents.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
combine	V;PST;V.PTCP	combined	Cleveland is the urban center of Northeast Ohio, the 15th largest combined metropolitan area in the United States.
rank	V;PST;V.PTCP	ranked	Throughout the twentieth century, the City of Cleveland proper was ranked as one of the 10 largest cities in the U.S. (from 1890 until 1970 per US Census Bureau statistics).
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	Like most U.S. cities, Cleveland proper began to lose population to suburban areas in the 1960s and 1970s.
earn	V;PST;SG;3	earned	However, in the mid-1980s, Cleveland earned the nickname the "Comeback City" as the urban core experienced a dramatic revitalization process that continues today.
continue	V;PST;V.PTCP	continued	As its "comeback" has continued, the official moniker is now the New American City as Cleveland has rightfully earned the reputation as a model of effective public-private partnership for urban planning.
rise	V;PRS;SG;3	rises	Despite the common perception that Cleveland is an industrial town, just beyond the automotive and steel plants, a clean and beautiful downtown rises at the mouth of the Cuyahoga River on the southern shore of Lake Erie (often marvelled over by visitors who are surprised you can't see the other side, i.e., Canada).
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	Like other cities in the so-called "rust belt“, Cleveland has endured growing pains as it makes its transition from a manufacturing-based economy.
continue	V;PRS;SG;3	continues	While Cleveland continues to play a leading role in building the U.S. industrial base, it has also developed economic prowess in the fields of health care, law, finance, insurance, real estate development, and professional services.
realize	V;NFIN	realize	Another thing non-locals don't often realize is that Cleveland's long history of industrial wealth has left it chock full of cultural riches as well as the beginnings of a "sustainable city" movement.
serve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Serving	Serving as a global model for urban rebirth, Cleveland has been named one of the top 10 international visitor hotspots by Travel and Leisure magazine.
boast	V;PST;V.PTCP	boasted	For decades, the city has boasted of: a "Big Five" orchestra (The Cleveland Orchestra), the second largest performing arts center in the U.S. (Playhouse Square Center), a world-renowned art museum (The Cleveland Museum of Art), the nation's first health museum (HealthSpace Cleveland), R&D hub of the aerospace and aviation industry (the NASA Glenn Research & Visitors Center) and a number of other first-rate attractions (too many to mention here - read on).
add	V;PST;V.PTCP	added	During its "comeback" years, Cleveland has added: the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame and Museum, the Great Lakes Science Center with Omnimax theatre, and four new sports facilities in the downtown area - Progressive Field (Still known as "The Jake" after a recent corporate name change) for the Major League Baseball Indians, QuickenLoans Arena ("The Q") for the NBA Cavaliers, Cleveland Browns Stadium for the NFL Browns and the Wolstein Center for the Cleveland State University Vikings basketball team.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
see	V;IMP;2	see	For information specific to U.S. citizens see Americans in Cuba
list	V;PRS;SG;2	list	This visa, which is really little more than a piece of paper on which you list your vital statistics, costs between 15 - 25 CUC (or € 15 - 25), depending on where purchased.
purchase	V;PST;V.PTCP	purchased	It can be purchased at the Airport in Cuba on arrival, however it should be noted that many airlines will require a valid tourist visa card before boarding flights.
extend	V;PST;V.PTCP	extended	It is usually valid for 30 days and can be extended once for another 30 days at any immigration office in Cuba (for 25 CUC) - beyond this you would need a flight out of Cuba within the extended visa period.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	getting	Canadians are the exception, getting 90 days on arrival and can apply for a 90 day extension.
need	V;PRS;SG;3	needs	Your passport needs to be valid at least six months past the end of your planned return.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	Canadian passports must be valid for at least one month beyond the date of expected departure ([2]).
provide	V;PST;V.PTCP	provided	From Canada, the tourist card is normally provided on the flight.
purchase	V;PST;V.PTCP	purchased	It can also be purchased from most Latin American gateway airports if departing from there (Cancun: 250 MXN, Mexico City: USD 25).
note	V;IMP;2	note	Please note that if departing from Europe (this may apply to other countries), you will require to have the visa before boarding the plane.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Some times, the airline provides these at the airport, however check first that this is the case.
board	V;PRS;V.PTCP	boarding	Without a valid visa, boarding will be denied (the airline would otherwise get a $ 1,000 fine from the Cuban immigration authorities).
apply	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Applying	UK: Applying for the visa is a very simple process and can be done by post or in person at the Cuban embassy in London.
apply	V;PRS;V.PTCP	applying	When applying to the Cuban Consulate by post, there is a new charge introduced in 2011 which is a £ 25 for a non-personal transaction.
go	V;NFIN	go	If you cannot go to the Cuban Consulate you may consider using VisaCuba ([3]) because it may be cheaper.
cost	V;NFIN	cost	Through them it may cost £ 20 in total per person.
apply	V;PRS;SG;2	apply	If you apply in person to the Cuban Consulate, you get the visa straight away.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	It can also be done through online agencies as mentioned before although they may be slightly more expensive (normally £ 15 + £ 15 admin fee and additional postage).
obtain	V;NFIN	obtain	Germany: You can obtain the tourist card through the Cuban embassy in post.
offer	V;NFIN	offer	Travel agencies may often offer cheaper and quicker services though.
renew	V;PRS;PL;3	renew	Regular tourists who renew their 30 day visa are eligible to depart the country (to any destination) and return immediately enjoying a further 60 days (30 days plus a 30 day extension).
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	You are only allowed two consecutive stays in this manner.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to stay with friends or family in Cuba you have to go with your intended host within two days after arrival to a migration office and pay 40 CUC for a 30 days family visa.
stay	V;NFIN	stay	Citizens of Antigua and Barbuda (28 days), Barbados (28 days), Benin, Bosnia and Herzegovina, CIS (except Ukraine and Uzbekistan), Dominica, Grenada (60 days), Liechtenstein (90 days), Macedonia, Malaysia (90 days), Mongolia, Montenegro (90 days), Namibia, Singapore, Slovakia, Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Lucia, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, Serbia (90 days), Turkmenistan who can stay 30 days without visa.
claim	V;PRS;PL;3	claim	Aeromexico staff at Cancun airport claim that only citizens of China and Russia need no visa.
note	V;NFIN	note	It is important to note that there is also a departure tax of CUC 25, to be paid in cash when departing Cuba by airplane; this is not required for boat departures.
publicize	V;PST;V.PTCP	publicized	This tax is not well publicized but it is essential to remember it.
run	V;NFIN	run	You will run into significant difficulties if you do not have enough cash to pay this tax when leaving the country.
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	Cuban customs can be strict, though they sometimes go easy on tourists.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	View of the George Washington Bridge's long span and Manhattan, as seen while looking east from Fort Lee Historic Park
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	Fort Lee is located between the Paramus, NJ retail corridor and Upper Manhattan.
comprise	V;PST;V.PTCP	comprised	This town is comprised of a large residential community that includes Fort Lee natives, transplants from New York, and immigrants, especially from Korea.
represent	V;PST;V.PTCP	represented	This cultural diversity is represented through the vast variety of international restaurants and small business, and in particular, there is a strong Korean presence that can be seen in retail and dining storefronts.
pile	V;PST;V.PTCP	piled	Fort Lee is a small town but is piled high with apartment buildings.
situate	V;PST;V.PTCP	situated	Because Fort Lee is situated above the banks of the Hudson River, there are breathtaking views from almost any window facing the river.
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	built	Although it is highly built up, Fort Lee also houses many playing fields and recreational parks as well as bike and running trails.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	And although there is a great deal of vehicle traffic to and from the George Washington Bridge, which connects Fort Lee with Manhattan, one of the largest perks of this town is the walking access to many restaurants, convenience stores and mass transit, in the form of buses and vans that frequently run across the bridge and up and down major streets.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
border	V;PST;V.PTCP	bordered	Fort Lee is bordered by Edgewater, Englewood Cliffs, Cliffside Park and Leonia.
come	V;PRS;V.PTCP	coming	If coming from New York City, take the George Washington Bridge;
ask	V;IMP;2	Ask	Ask any bus or jitney driver leaving from the Port Authority George Washington Bus Station on 177 St. whether s/he can let you off in Fort Lee.
access	V;PST;V.PTCP	accessed	Fort Lee can also be accessed by boat or ferry from New York City via Edgewater and Weehawken docks.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get around
meet	V;PRS;PL;3	meet	Old and new meet in Fort Lee, as the Good Shepherd Episcopalian Church is dwarfed by a residential highrise immediately behind it
choose	V;PRS;PL;3	choose	Most people choose cars, but if headed to a central area, such as Main Street, it is best to drive there, park in a municipal parking lot, and walk around from there.
cluster	V;PST;V.PTCP	clustered	Since so much of the town’s retail area is clustered in 3 or 4 block radius, it would be beneficial to walk around, but might also be dangerous given the high traffic intersections.
walk	V;PRS;V.PTCP	walking	If walking use caution, and if parking, make sure to feed the meter with plenty of pocket change; the meter maids take their job very seriously.
situate	V;PST;V.PTCP	situated	Since fort lee is situated in such a convenient area for those commuting to and from the city, there is often high traffic and congestion on the roads.
plan	V;PRS;V.PTCP	planning	If you are planning on being somewhere at a certain time, one might suggest leaving a good 10 to 15 minutes to escape local traffic before hitting any major roadways.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	For local transportation or air-port / train station services, there are several local taxi companies that are reliable.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Fort Lee is known and named for the story of how General George Washington was forced to evacuate the area in November of 1776 and begin his famous retreat through New Jersey during the winter of 1776-77 in the protection of "Fort Lee".
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	The Visitors' Center provides information on the role of Fort Lee in the War.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	Audio-visual displays, detailed exhibits, gives visitors a sense of what this town was like centuries ago.
cross	V;PST;V.PTCP	crossed	The George Washington Bridge can be crossed by bicycle or on foot as well as in a motorized vehicle.
eat	V;PRS;V.PTCP	eating	Other than eating, or shopping, or shopping for food, there is not much else to do unless you work in Fort Lee.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Fort Lee also has a great community center open to all borough residents.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The community center has dance, yoga, and other exercise classes, offers open gym hours for basketball or other recreation.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	During the summer their outdoor theatre is used for performances and "movies in the park" every Friday night.
contact	V;IMP;2	contact	Jack Alter Fort Lee Community Center, 1355 Inwood Terrace, tel: 1 201-592-4699, contact for membership information, and upcoming events.
buy	V;IMP;2	Buy	Buy
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is an abundance of supermarkets, one the most famous and unique being Caffasso’s Fairway Market.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are also many nail salons, frozen yogurt shops, coffee shops and gas stations.
feature	V;PRS;SG;3	features	Main Street is the primary retail section of Fort Lee, and features stores such as Borders and liquor stores, and you can find a deli or drug store on every corner.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are tons of specialty boutiques and gourmet caterers, as well as clothing and sporting good retailers.
eat	V;IMP;2	Eat	Eat
offer	V;PRS;SG;3	offers	Fort Lee offers international cuisine and is famous for their pizzerias, delis and 24-hour diners, in addition to its Korean restaurants.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	They have a warm local atmosphere and taste great.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Guadeloupe, known as Gwadloup in the local creole, and sometimes known as the Butterfly Island (French: I'ile Papillon) on account of the shape of two of its major islands, is a group of islands in the eastern Caribbean, and is a French overseas department.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	It is located southeast of Puerto Rico.
compose	V;PST;V.PTCP	composed	Les Saintes: composed of Terre de Haut and Terre de Bas, one of the most beautiful bays.
enjoy	V;NFIN	enjoy	Gosier: maybe one of the most interesting places of Guadeloupe to enjoy nightlife.
enter	V;NFIN	enter	(You can enter most nightclubs with proper clothes, that is, no sneakers, no shorts)
go	V;PRS;SG;2	go	St François if you go at the eastern point of Guadeloupe, you will reach La Pointe des Chateaux, a scenery made of sand and rocks which have vaguely the shape of a castle.
look	V;NFIN	look	From there, you can look up at the islands La Désirade, Petite Terre, Marie Galante, Les Saintes, La Dominique but also have a perfect view of the islands Grande Terre and far away Basse Terre.
offer	V;PRS;SG;3	offers	L'Americano, bd Georges Mandel, 0590883899: bar / restaurant offers free salsa courses on Saturdays and live performances some days.
find	V;NFIN	find	You will find all kind of bars.
try	V;NFIN	try	You can try Club Med, 0590854950 fax: 0590854959 (for instance, others resort may propose this formula too) for a one day all inclusive (breakfast, buffet, bars, drinks, beach volley, windsurf, boat, gym, dance courses ...) for about € 46, so it may be a good deal (as it costs € 7 one hour of windsurf).
renown	V;PST;V.PTCP	renowned	Morne à l'eau, renowned for its amazing cemetery composed of burial places made of black and white tiles.
visit	V;NFIN	visit	Anse Bertrand, not far from there, you can visit La pointe de la Grande Vigie, northern point of mainland Guadeloupe.
go	V;NFIN	go	You can also go to Porte d'Enfer, a beautiful still stripe of sea between a scenery of reefs.
walk	V;NFIN;2	walk	From there, walk one hour along the cliff, and you will discover a Souffleur, kind of geyser due to the pressure of the sea.
see	V;NFIN	see	Abymes nothing special to see, but the weekend, there are 3 local nightclubs: L'instant, Caraibes and Latin Club.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	They are located at the same place.
do	V;NFIN	do	Baie-Mahault: the industrial and commercial zone of Guadeloupe, nothing special to do or see.
stand	V;PRS;SG;3	stands	Here stands the biggest shopping mall of the island.
find	V;NFIN	find	Not too far from there, you can find a local bar / nightclub named Bik Kreyol, Beau Soleil, 0590258046 or 0590920648 (Entrance € 5, Drinks € 3).
miss	V;IMP;2	miss	Don't miss the spectacular waterfalls in the jungle of Basse-Terre (Carbet Falls).
require	V;PRS;PL;3	require	Some are within 5 – 10 minutes walking distance from the nearest parking lot, some require at least 3 – 4 hours of hiking (those are, of course less frequented by other tourists and you might find yourself alone at a spectacular waterfall in the middle of nowhere - an amazing experience!).
offer	V;PRS;PL;3	offer	The local rum distilleries offer tours (check for opening times as they may very from season to season) which are certainly worth the while since rum production is a very integral part of Guadeloupe's economy.
sample	V;PRS;V.PTCP	sampling	And sampling the local rums is definitely worth the while.
get	V;NFIN	get	Even though they might not be the best way to get around the island, a ride on the bus is still an experience you should not miss.
conduct	V;PST;V.PTCP	conducted	Cheap, full of locals, conducted by fearless drivers, you can enjoy the beautiful Caribbean panorama to the sound of Guadeloupean zouk music.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Isfahan (Persian: اصفهان, also known as Esfahan) is a city in central Iran, south of Tehran and is the capital of Isfahan Province.
call	V;PRS;PL;3	call	The Persians call it "Nesf-e-Jahan", meaning "Half The World".
paint	V;PST;V.PTCP	painted	Due to its beautiful hand-painted tiling and magnificent public square, it is considered to be one of the most beautiful cities in the world.
note	V;PST;V.PTCP	noted	An ancient town and capital of Persia from 1598 to 1722, it was long noted for its fine carpets and silver filigree.
take	V;PRS;PL;3	take	Today, textile and steel mills take their place.
line	V;PST;V.PTCP	lined	Its architecture, tree-lined boulevards and relaxed pace make it one of the highlights of Iran.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
enjoy	V;PRS;SG;3	enjoys	The city enjoys a temperate climate and regular seasons.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	Isfahan is located on the main north-south and east-west routes crossing Iran.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The city has an Armenian Quarter called Jolfa (originally called New Julfa) established by Shah Abbas I in the 1600s after he destroyed the prosperous Armenian town of Julfa in Armenia and forced all of the Armenians to move to Persia, mostly to Isfahan.
allocate	V;PST;V.PTCP	allocated	They were allocated land and rebuilt a prosperous community that was a key part of an Armenian trade network extending from Singapore and India to Venice and Amsterdam.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Today the quarter still has many Armenian Churches, an Armenian school, shops and Armenian residents.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are daily flights to Tehran and Mashhad in Iran.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are also flights to Damascus, Dubai, Kuwait and Istanbul.
take	V;NFIN	take	From the Airport you can take cab for c. IRR200,000 to the city centre.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	Since most people are going there anyway, you can always ask people to share taxi.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Also known as shah square or imam square - 1602 (Meidan Emam).
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	contains	The square contains two mosques, a palace, and the bazaar.
surround	V;PST;V.PTCP	surrounded	The square is surrounded by buildings from the Safavid era.
picnic	V;NFIN	picnic	This a very popular place for locals to picnic on Friday and holiday evenings.
date	V;PRS;V.PTCP	dating	1596, dating from the Saffavid era, the avenue is the most historically famous in all of Persia.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	3 Imam Mosque (called Shah Mosque before the revolution), Naqsh-e Jahan Square, south side.
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	Built	Built during the Safavid period, it is an excellent example of Islamic architecture of Iran, and regarded as one of the masterpieces of Persian Architecture.
register	V;PST;V.PTCP	registered	It is registered as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	One of the architectural masterpieces of Safavid Iranian architecture, this mosque is considered to be the most beautiful in Iran.
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	Built	Built in 1602 by Shah Abbas I.= and designed by his chief architect, Sheikh Bahai.
design	V;PST;V.PTCP	designed	The mosque was designed to be a private mosque for the royal family and therefore it does not have any minarets.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a tunnel from the mosque to the Royal Palace, across the square.
start	V;PST;V.PTCP	Started	Started in AD842, this is the first Islamic building to adopt the four-courtyard layout of Sassanid palaces.
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	Built	Built by Shah Abbas II between 1656 and 1662.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	Located	Located on the site of a 10th century mosque.
cover	V;PST;V.PTCP	covered	The portal was covered in mud until it was discovered in 1956.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	It is forty-eight meters high and there are seven floors, each accessible by a difficult spiral staircase.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	In the sixth floor music room, deep circular niches are found in the walls, having not only aesthetic value, but also acoustic.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are floral, animal and bird motifs.
build	V;PST;V.PTCP	built	1669, reportedly built for residence purposes of the king's harem.
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	Set	Set within lush gardens which are free to roam if you don't want to go inside the building.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	1647: It is called Palace of forty columns, as there are many columns, and in Iranian, 40 means many.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Incidentally, there are twenty columns, and these are reflected in the pool in front, which might also account for its name.
hold	V;PRS;V.PTCP	holding	The function of this palace was for holding religious-national ceremonies and royal festivals and for receiving royal ambassadors and guests.
inscribe	V;PST;V.PTCP	inscribed	It's Persian Gardens is one of nine inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List.
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	Contains	Contains some spectacular battle murals.
pronounce	V;PST;V.PTCP	Pronounced	Łódź (Pronounced: Wootch) is Poland's third biggest city, and the capital of the Łódzkie Voivodship.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Unlike most other large Polish cities, which have long histories, Łódź was created almost from scratch during the 19th century textile industry boom to house textile mills, their owners and their workers, and rapidly grew to become an important industrial and commerce centre.
battle	V;PRS;V.PTCP	battling	In recent years, Łódź has been battling rising unemployment and youth migration resulting from the failing textile industry and is repositioning itself as a hub for design and creative industries.
owe	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Owing	Owing to its history, Łódź has an impressive collection of residential, commercial and industrial 19th-century architecture including some of Europe's largest factory buildings, now mostly repurposed as shopping centres or lofts.
stand	V;PRS;V.PTCP	standing	Recent local investment and a long-standing cultural scene ensures there is always something more to do than just sightseeing or shopping, with the city hosting some of the best museums and art galleries in the country.
make	V;PRS;PL;3	make	The close proximity of Warsaw and Łódź's location at the crossroads of the Polish motorway system make it an ideal destination for a day trip or longer city break.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
translate	V;PRS;SG;3	translates	The origin of the city's name is unknown, however, the word łódź translates to "boat" in English.
refer	V;PST;V.PTCP	referred	This is referred to in the emblem and when you hear the dwellers of Łódź (łodzianin (man) łodzianka (woman) łodzianie (plural)) calling themselves "the boat people".
establish	V;PST;V.PTCP	established	A settlement on the area of present-day Łódź was established in the 14th century and in 1423 it was granted a town charter, along with the right to have a marketplace.
start	V;PST;SG;3	started	However, the real development of Łódź started with the industrial era at the beginning of the 19th century when the city was chosen to be the heart of the rapidly-growing textile industry.
soar	V;PST;SG;3	soared	The population of Łódź soared from some 4,000 people in the 1830s and 40,000 in 1865 to over 300,000 inhabitants at the turn of the century, which was an unprecedented growth on a worldwide scale.
bring	V;PST;V.PTCP	brought	The rapid development of Łódź in the second half of the 19th century was brought about by the rise of enormous industrialist fortunes.
come	V;PST;PL;3	came	New inhabitants, craftsmen and merchants came to Łódź; markets and town fairs came to life.
obtain	V;PST;V.PTCP	obtained	The profits obtained from prosperous textile mills opened up practically unlimited possibilities for their owners.
become	V;PST;PL;3	became	The city residences became expressions of the riches and power of the local tycoons.
situate	V;PST;V.PTCP	situated	They were usually situated next to the owner's factory.
bring	V;PST;SG;3	brought	Ever since the industrial revolution brought a massive influx of workforce, Łódź had been a city almost equally divided between four cultures - Polish, Jewish, German and Russian.
inhabit	V;PST;V.PTCP	inhabited	The day before the outbreak of World War II, Łódź was inhabited by approximately 672,000 people, among whom 35% were of Jewish faith and some 15% were ethnic German.
Promise	V;PST;V.PTCP	Promised	Andrzej Wajda's movie, Promised Land based on a novel by the Nobel laureate Władysław Reymont, depicts those times and is a worthwhile introduction to the city's bustling history.
incorporate	V;PST;V.PTCP	incorporated	During the Nazi occupation, Łódź was incorporated directly into the Third Reich.
rename	V;PST;V.PTCP	renamed	The city was renamed to Litzmannstadt, and Piotrkowska Street was called Adolf-Hitler-Strasse.
wipe	V;PST;V.PTCP	wiped	The Jewish community was practically wiped out, and many others lost their lives.
destroy	V;PST;V.PTCP	destroyed	Although the city was not destroyed in the aftermath, the material losses were serious as the machinery, raw materials and finished goods were taken away by the fleeing Nazis.
leave	V;PST;PL;3	left	The day before the liberation, about 80,000 Germans left Łódź in panic.
estimate	V;PST;V.PTCP	estimated	At the beginning of 1945, the population was estimated at 300,000.
enjoy	V;PST;V.PTCP	enjoyed	Since the fall of communism in Poland, Łódź has not enjoyed the rapid economic growth cities like Kraków, Warsaw and Wrocław have experienced, and has had to endure the fall of much of its textile industry, rising unemployment and emigration (within Poland and abroad).
lead	V;PST;SG;3	led	This led to further dilapidation of the investment-starved city, and many of its streets and buildings show profound signs of neglect.
seem	V;NFIN;2	seem	At first glance, for a city of 760,000 and agglomeration of 1.428,000, there does not seem to be much to see.
change	V;PRS;V.PTCP	changing	However, the situation is rapidly changing now with Łódź campaigning and redesigning itself as an up-and-coming destination.
accomplish	V;NFIN	accomplish	To accomplish this, Łódź has been capitalizing on its film history - Łódź is jokingly referred to as the "HollyŁódź" of Poland and hosts several film festivals and many other cultural events.
have	V;NFIN	have	Łódź today may not have the glitz and glamour of its heyday; however, there is a certain charm to Łódź in its partially renovated façades and leitmotivs, not to mention its grand artistic traditions.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It has a population of about 750,000, and is the largest city in the Yucatán Peninsula.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
find	V;NFIN	find	You will find elegant hotels, restaurants and malls in the northern part of the city.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Downtown, there are hotels and restaurants to suit every budget.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	A large central market and numerous small shops are found all around the main plaza.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Mérida has a rich cultural life which also reflects its diversity.
hold	V;PST;V.PTCP	held	Many free concerts, performances and other events are held daily.
found	V;PST;V.PTCP	founded	The city was founded by the Spanish conquistadors in the 1540s on top of a centuries-old Maya city called T'ho.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	The palatial home of the family of conquistador leader Montejo can still be seen on the south side of the Zócalo or main square.
see	V;PST;V.PTCP	seen	Here and there bits of ancient Maya stonework can be seen reused in Spanish Colonial era buildings in the old part of town.
enjoy	V;PST;SG;3	enjoyed	From the later 1800s to the 1920s Mérida enjoyed prosperous boom times fueled by the henequen or sisal plant harvest, which made Yucatán the rope maker to the world.
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	Progressive Mérida had electric trams and street lights before Mexico City.
construct	V;PST;SG;3	constructed	The wealthy constructed the grand Pasejo Montejo avenue north of the old town, inspired by the Champs-Élysées in Paris.
end	V;PST;SG;3	ended	With the development of artificial twines the sisal boom ended, and Mérida slowed to a more sleepy provincial capital until development picked back up in the late 20th century.
vary	V;PRS;SG;3	varies	The daytime temperature varies; in January, it is about 24°C (75°F) and in June, about 35°C (95°F).
beat	V;NFIN	beat	To beat the heat, most people are busiest in the mornings.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	They have lunch and siesta, then go back to work for a few hours in the late afternoon.
drift	V;PRS;PL;3	drift	The cool breezes from the Gulf of Mexico drift into Mérida in the evenings and this is when many of the residents spend their time outdoors.
see	V;NFIN	see	You can see them visiting and talking as they stroll along the streets, sit in the plazas or dine in the many sidewalk restaurants.
take	V;NFIN	take	From Mérida, it is easy to take day trips to a vast array of destinations: archaeological sites, ecological parks, typical villages, caves, beaches, colonial missions and more.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take your time and really explore the Maya sites, walk for miles along the Gulf of Mexico, attend a village festival, photograph the wildlife, crawl through a cave or swim in a cenote.
maintain	V;PRS;SG;3	maintains	The city maintains several tourist information offices, all of which can offer helpful information on accommodation, free maps, and bus connections.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	All offices also have free copies of Yucatan Today, a helpful weekly Spanish-English publication listing all events in the city and the surrounding region.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Phoenix is the capital of the state of Arizona as well as the most populous city in the American Southwest and sixth largest city in the United States.
found	V;PST;V.PTCP	Founded	Founded in 1871, it has become the region's primary political, cultural, economic, and transportation center.
situate	V;PST;V.PTCP	situated	At an elevation of 1100 ft (335 m), it is situated in the biologically unique Sonoran Desert.
merge	V;PST;V.PTCP	merged	Over time it has merged with the neighboring cities of Scottsdale, Tempe, Glendale, Peoria, Chandler, and Gilbert to form the Greater Phoenix Metropolitan Area.
become	V;PRS;V.PTCP	becoming	Currently exurbs such as Apache Junction, Fountain Hills, Queen Creek, and Sun City are becoming part of this metropolitan area as well.
have	V;IMP;2	have	Phoenix is extremely hot and dry in the summertime, so always have sunscreen with you!
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
want	V;NFIN	want	Why would anybody want to start a city in the middle of a desert?
exploit	V;PST;V.PTCP	exploited	The Salt and Verde Rivers of central Arizona were exploited for large-scale agriculture by Native Americans as early as the 11th century.
encompass	V;PRS;SG;3	encompasses	The area that now encompasses Phoenix was a center of the Hohokam culture, which built large canal systems and a network of towns and villages, whose remains may be viewed in the city to this day.
discover	V;PST;PL;3	discovered	White settlers discovered the remnants of the Hohokam culture in the 19th century.
reflect	V;PRS;SG;3	reflects	The city's name reflects its history as a city "reborn from the ashes" of the previous settlement.
commence	V;PST;SG;3	commenced	European-American settlement of the area commenced in the 1860s, and in 1911 the completion of the first of several large reservoirs in the mountains north and east of Phoenix insured its success as a center for irrigation-based agriculture.
plant	V;PST;V.PTCP	planted	Many tens of thousands of acres were planted in citrus and cotton and other crops, and for many years, intensive, year-round irrigated agriculture formed the basis of the economy.
see	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeing	Recent years are seeing a revival, and trendy hotels, bars, shops and restaurants are making it a place to be again.
ensure	V;PST;PL;3	ensured	Warm and sunny winter weather also ensured a thriving tourism industry, and encouraged many Easterners and Midwesterners to relocate to Phoenix.
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	High-tech industry began to flourish after World War II, and since that time the growth of Phoenix has been explosive.
give	V;PST;V.PTCP	given	As a result, a population of just over 100,000 in 1950 has given way to a 2014 estimate of 1,537,058 (with the metro area estimated at 4,489,109).
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Phoenix has an arid climate with long, hot summers and very mild winters.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It has the highest average temperature of any metropolitan area in the States.
vary	V;PRS;SG;3	varies	The weather varies enormously from one season to the next.
freeze	V;NFIN	freeze	While it's not as cold as in the northern states during the winter, it does freeze sometimes, and temperatures in the 30s°F (that's around or slightly above 0°C) are not unheard of.
get	V;NFIN	get	On the hottest days, it can get up to 115°F (46°C) or more.
occur	V;PRS;PL;3	occur	Monsoon rains with lightning occur regularly from July to September during the late afternoon and evening, occasionally overnight also.
make	V;PRS;PL;3	make	In some neighborhoods, cicada insects make loud sounds from sunset to sunrise.
talk	V;IMP;2	Talk	Talk
speak	V;PST;V.PTCP	spoken	However, like much of the Southwest with a large Hispanic population, Spanish is very widely spoken in Phoenix.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Spanish is a language often used for day-to-day discourse in many places, although English is the language of preference, especially when dealing with businesses and government.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
number	V;PST;V.PTCP	numbered	Terminals are numbered from 2 - 4.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is no Terminal 1.
Unite	V;PST;V.PTCP	United	Terminal 2: Alaska (Gates 10 & 11), Great Lakes (Gates C & D), Spirit, Sun Country, United
go	V;PRS;SG;3	goes	Valley Metro #13 goes west to S 75th Ave along S Buckeye (via Greyhound depot) from Terminal 2.
take	V;NFIN	take	Likewise one can take the PHX skytrain over to the nearby 44th & Washington St Station to catch the #1 (west to the downtown Central Station and Priest & Washington in Tempe to the east along Washington St); #44 bus (north to the Desert Ridge Marriott Resort in Deer Valley along N 44th St & Tatum); and the light rail (east to Tempe & Mesa and to Midtown (along Central Ave) via downtown in the other direction).
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	Due to its remarkable biodiversity, with over a third of the local plant species found nowhere else, Socotra has been designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	The Island is new to tourism, so if you are looking for a luxurious vacation this is not the right destination.
take	V;PRS;SG;3	takes	Socotra takes pride in their eco-tourism and values their spectacular plant and wildlife that may sometimes be only found on this island.
show	V;PST;V.PTCP	shown	Great care is shown to both guests and the environment.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	However there are four hotels in Hadibo: Taj Socotra Hotel, Hafijj Hotel, Socotra Hotel, and Summer land Hotel.
provide	V;NFIN	provide	These hotels may not be the most luxerious, but they do provide air-conditionting, televisions, and refrigerators in all of the rooms.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Most of the rooms at these hotels have their own bathroom, but there are a few rooms at the Hafijj Hotel and Socotra Hotel that must share bathrooms.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	Fortunately, there is a restaurant in each of the hotels with simple yet delicious cuisine.
consider	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Considering	Considering Socotra is a fairly new eco-tourism destination, their facilities are decent.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	In addition, there is also another hotel at the airport as well as multiple guest houses around the island.
camp	V;PRS;V.PTCP	camping	The most popular lodging is actually camping on the beaches.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are several campsites that allow tourists to barbeque on the beach with beautiful mountain views in the background.
choose	V;NFIN	choose	Tourists can choose to purchase camping tour packages which include a driver or guide that will show tourists around the beaches as well as provide meals during their stay.
enjoy	V;NFIN	enjoy	It is a fantastic way to enjoy the beauty and wildlife of the island.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	A runway that provides fairly regular airline transportation was only constructed in 1999.
begin	V;PST;V.PTCP	begun	Like everything else, infrastructure and transportation has only just begun in recent years.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are also plans for new harbor facilities that will allow fisherman to sell and catch more easily and make importing food and fuel simpler.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	In addition, there are also two information centers located at the airport and Hadibo.
go	V;NFIN	go	When to go: Climate is an important consideration.
depend	V;PRS;SG;3	depends	And this depends on your interest.
go	V;NFIN	go	Nature enthusiasts should go from early October to late April.
occur	V;PRS;SG;3	occurs	The monsoon occurs in July till mid-August (during July in the south east and south west of the island, wind surfing activity can be arranged).
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are flights from Sana'a via Al Mukalla.
offer	V;PRS;SG;3	offers	Yemenia Airlines offers one flight per week on Thursday morning.
offer	V;PRS;SG;3	offers	Felix Airways offers two flights per week on Wednesday afternoon and Saturday morning.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are also flights from Sharjah via Al Mukalla.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get around
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a public bus from the airport to Hadibo and from Hadibo to Qalansia.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	Beside these two buses, there is no public transport, but car rental with driver is available.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a lot of walking and hiking along with camel back rides available.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	Socotra is considered the jewel of biodiversity in the Arabian Sea.
characterize	V;PST;V.PTCP	characterized	Although the region is small, it is characterized by unique land and marine biodiversity.
dominate	V;PST;V.PTCP	dominated	The interior of the island is dominated by the beautiful and green Hagghier mountains and limestone plateau.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	While on the outer coastline, breathtaking natural beaches have pure white sand dunes and palm trees.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	Socotra Island is very rich and is home to many rare spectacular species of reefs, fish, birds, plants, and trees which are not found anywhere else in the world.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	The limestone plateau and the Hagghier Mountains are the richest areas for endemic plant species, but endemics are found throughout the island in every type of vegetation.
exist	V;PRS;SG;3	exists	One of the most famous plants is the dragon's blood tree “dracaena cinnabara” which exists only on Socotra and nowhere else on the Earth.
get	V;PST;SG;3	got	The tree got its name because if any damage is made to the bark then a dark red liquid oozes out.
thrive	V;PRS;SG;3	thrives	The dragon's blood tree thrives in Socotra's arid climate
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is also the Desert Rose (adenium obesium) which looks like a blooming elephant leg.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	Also found in Socotra's landscape is the ever-strange and extremely rare Cucumber Tree.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	In addition there are over 120 species of birds and about 190 species of butterflies.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is so much beauty all around the island, it is difficult not the appreciate to magnificent scenery and wildlife.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	Sydfynske Øhav, the South Funen Archipelago, is located south of the Danish island of Funen, comprising 55 islands and islets, at the mouth of the Baltic Sea, with a bit more than 20,000 inhabitants in total.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	It is one of Denmark's most scenic areas, and should be a top destination of anyone taking the time to explore Denmark outside the capital.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
form	V;PST;V.PTCP	formed	The islands were formed during the last ice age, by ice sliding over the area from the south, only the ancient hilltops was spared from submersion, and forms the many islands and islets peaking above the sea everywhere.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The area has a milder climate than the country's average, and because of this, is home to flora and fauna not seen anywhere else in Denmark.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are strong local movements to convert the area into a National Park.
designate	V;PST;V.PTCP	designated	The area is one of the most diverse natural environments in the country, and is designated as an international wildlife protection area, as it is home of several rare and protected species.
migrate	V;PRS;SG;3	migrates	If you are interested ornithology the many shallow waters around the islands and islets is a treat with thousands of birds and colonies, one of the more notable is the Arctic Tern, which migrates to Antarctica and back (nearly 40,000 kilometers) every year.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	This is the longest regular migration by any known animal.
find	V;PRS;SG;2	find	You also find Shore Crabs, Beach Otters and - rare for Denmark - Seals in the shallow waters and beaches.
find	V;NFIN	find	On dry land, up on the many islands you can find badgers, stoats and several types of deer.
find	V;PST;V.PTCP	found	While possibly the most exciting animals are found beneath the water: the Harbour Porpoise, Denmark's only resident whale population.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
connect	V;PST;V.PTCP	connected	Tåsinge, Langeland and a few of the smaller islands are connected with bridges, but to most of the islands transportation is done with tiny ferries ploughing through the island sea several times per day.
reach	V;PST;V.PTCP	reach	The main entry points are Svendborg which is easily reach both by highway, bus and train - and Faaborg, which is best reached by bus from Odense.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	From Langeland there are ferries to some other islands.
list	V;PST;V.PTCP	listed	For easy reference ferries are listed under the individual islands.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	From Spodsbjerg on Langeland there is a ferry connection across the great belt to Tårs on Lolland.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get around
bring	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Bringing	Bringing a car to the islands serves little point but to rob your wallet of your hard earned cash, and you will be much better served by bringing or renting a bike in Svendborg or Faaborg, or just simply walk since the islands are generally small enough to make this feasible.
connect	V;PST;V.PTCP	connected	While some islands are connected by the same ferry, you will generally have to go back to the mainland to pass between the islands, if you rely on public transportation.
visit	V;NFIN	visit	However the best way to visit is really by boat, and there are plenty of options to do this.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are several mapped routes in the area, but remember to be realistic about your fitness, as some of these routes take you a fair bit away from land.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	Several rental places also gives you the option of trained guide, which can both provide information about the sights you visit, and make sure you are safe.
rent	V;NFIN	rent	It is also possible to rent Smack Dinghies with sails to ease the strain, or larger cattle barges if you are in a group at Øhavet's smakkecenter on Strynø.
walk	V;IMP;2	Walk	Walk
connect	V;PST;V.PTCP	connected	The archipelago trail (da: Øhavsstien) is a 220 km hiking trail connected by ferries that circles the entire area.
complete	V;PST;V.PTCP	Completed	Completed in 2008 the trail is well marked with regular signs set on wooden poles.
allow	V;IMP;2	Allow	Allow 4 - 7 days for the whole trail depending on experience and your desired pace, but it is also possible do to day trips along the route.
arrange	V;NFIN	arrange	The local tourist offices in Svendborg and Faaborg can arrange baggage forwarding between your chosen camps or accommodations during peak season.
prearrange	V	Pre	Pre arranged package tours are also available from Travel Heels in Taasinge or Vagabond Tours in Tranekær, in cooperation with the local tourist authority.
choose	V;PRS;SG;2	choose	If you choose to do the trek individually be mindful that substantial part of the trails crosses private property, so be considerate.
break	V;PST;V.PTCP	broken	Pamphlets with maps, tips and points of interest in English, broken up into 7 parts is available at the tourist offices as well and as pdf files on the introductory link, it is sometimes also available at parking lots along the route, but these are only restocked a couple of times throughout the year, so you may be out of luck.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See
list	V;PST;V.PTCP	listed	Islands in the archipelago listed in east to west order; note that there are many more islands and islets in the area, but these are the ones with ferry traffic that are possible to visit without making special arrangements.
close	V;PST;V.PTCP	closed	Most other islands are closed during much of the year for the protection of local wildlife.
permit	V;PST;V.PTCP	permitted	For the remainder of the year, short stays are permitted, which is interpreted by the authorities as a day or a night.
allow	V;PST;V.PTCP	allowed	While you are normally not allowed to set up a tent, you are permitted to sleep on the beach in a sleeping bag, if necessary under a primitive cover like a tarpaulin.
order	V;PRS;V.PTCP	ordering	Ferry from Marstal on Ærø, 25 minutes, 1 departure per day (but ordering additional departures are possible), 70 DKK.
love	V;PRS;V.PTCP	loving	Birkholm Is only 1km² and home of a stubborn but loving 8 people, making it one of the smallest populated islands in the country, how long the island can sustain a full year population remains to be seen, but permanent life on the island will probably be a thing of the past in a not too distant future.
tower	V;PRS;V.PTCP	towering	Non the less it is a pretty, but incredibly flat place, with the highest point towering a proud 1,8 meters over the sea!
remain	V;PRS;V.PTCP	remaining	some fine old farm houses which the remaining population does a commendable job trying to maintain, since many of them are now empty and the rest used as vacation home for "Danish foreigners".
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are some nice beaches and good fishing once you've toured the island.
run	V;NFIN	run	If you are a hunter, you might be lucky to run into the yearly Pheasant or hare hunts - you can even rest assured your "evil deeds" does some good, as it supports life on the island.
gallivant	V;PRS;V.PTCP	gallivanting	Thais are a polite people and, while remarkably tolerant of foreigners gallivanting on their beaches and with their women, youll find that you will get more respect if you in turn treat them and their customs with respect.
show	V;PRS;V.PTCP	showing	Ronald McDonald showing how it's done
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	The traditional greeting known as the wai, where you press your hands together as is in prayer and bow slightly, is derived from the Hindu cultural influence from India, and still widely practised.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	Among Thais, there are strict rules of hierarchy that dictate how and when the wai should be given.
salute	V;PRS;PL;3	salute	In brief, inferiors salute superiors first.
wai	V;NFIN	wai	You should not wai service people or street vendors.
go	V;PRS;PL;3	go	The higher your hands go, the more respectful you are.
see	V;NFIN	see	You will also often see Thais doing a wai as they walk past temples and spirit houses.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	As a foreign visitor, you are not expected to know how to wai, nor to reciprocate when wai'd to;
cause	V;NFIN	cause	while you're unlikely to cause offense if you do, you may well look slightly strange.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	If somebody makes a wai to you, a slight bow alone is more than sufficient for ordinary occasions, and for business, most Thais will shake hands with foreigners instead of waiing anyway.
find	V;NFIN	find	You will find that dressing appropriately means that you are shown more respect in return.
translate	V;PRS;SG;3	translates	This translates in many ways, even sometimes lowering initial offering prices at markets.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	While some allowance is made for the differing customs of foreigners, Thais respond more positively to well-dressed Westerners.
avoid	V;NFIN	avoid	Except at the beach or at sacred sites normal western dress is acceptable for both men and women, except that you should avoid clothing showing a lot of skin.
have	V;NFIN	have	Pants are preferable to shorts, blouses should have capped sleeves, and if tank tops are worn, the straps should be thick (i.e., not spaghetti straps).
wear	V;PRS;PL;3	wear	Thai men generally wear pants, and most Thais view an adult man wearing shorts as fairly ridiculous;
wear	V;PST;V.PTCP	worn	shorts are primarily worn by laborers and schoolchildren.
wear	V;PST;V.PTCP	worn	Men's shorts should be knee length or more, if worn at all.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Taking	Taking off one's shoes at temples and private homes is mandatory etiquette, and this may even be requested at some shops.
wear	V;IMP;2	Wear	Wear shoes that slip on and off easily.
travel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	traveling	Flip-flops, hiking sandals, and clog-type shoes are usually a good pragmatic choice for traveling in Thailand;
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	only in the most top-end establishments are shoes required.
play	V;NFIN	play	It is best to play it safe with wats and other sacred sites in Thailand;
cover	V;NFIN	cover	your dress should be unambiguously modest and cover your entire torso and most of your limbs.
recommend	V;PRS;PL;3	recommend	Many recommend that women wear only full length dresses and skirts;
make	V;NFIN	make	you should make sure that your clothing covers at least your shoulders and your knees and some places may require that you wear ankle-length pants or skirts and long sleeved tops.
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	The rules are even more strict for foreign visitors, so even if you see a local in shorts it's not OK for everyone.
advise	V;PST;V.PTCP	advised	At beaches which are primarily Thai visitors however, this is not advised.
expect	V;PST;V.PTCP	expected	Monks are an integral part of Buddhism in Thailand, and Thai men are generally expected to spend a certain amount of time living as a monk at least once in their lifetime.
mean	V;PST;V.PTCP	meant	Buddhist monks are meant to avoid sexual temptations, and in particular they do not touch women or take things from women's hands.
make	V;NFIN	make	Women should make every effort to make way for monks on the street and give them room so they do not have to make contact with you.
avoid	V;NFIN	avoid	Women should avoid offering anything to a monk with their hands.
place	V;PST;V.PTCP	placed	Objects or donations should be placed in front of a monk so he can pick it up, or place it on a special cloth he carries with him.
aid	V;PST;V.PTCP	aided	Monks will sometimes be aided by a layman who will accept things from women merit-makers on their behalf.
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	Theravada Buddhist monks are also supposed to avoid material temptations and as such, are not allowed to touch money, so offering money to a monk is considered to be a sign of disrespect in most Theravada Buddhist cultures.
wish	V;NFIN	wish	Therefore, should you wish to donate to a monk, you should only offer food, and put your monetary donation in the appropriate donation box at the temple.
accept	V;PRS;PL;3	accept	Those monks that accept money are almost always fakes.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	It is also called “T-town” by the locals and has been called the "Oil Capital of the World".
have	V;PST;SG;3	had	The city had about 391,000 people and the metro area had about 937,000 people as of 2010 from the US Census Bureau.
Combine	V;PST;V.PTCP	Combined	The Tulsa Bartlesville Combined area had 988,000 in the 2010 census.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
lie	V;PRS;SG;3	lies	Tulsa lies in northeastern Oklahoma, at the convergence of the Great Plains and the Ozark Plateau, and receives an average of 40 inches of precipitation each year, both of which account for its abundant beautiful rolling green terrain.
break	V;PRS;SG;3	breaks	As a result, Tulsa breaks the Oklahoma stereotype of being nothing but a flat, arid dust bowl.
get	V;NFIN	get	Summers can be very warm and with the cold wind across the plains it can get very cold in the winter, but it does not last long.
consider	V;PST;V.PTCP	considered	The winters are considered to be very mild.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is not much snow, just a few inches each year, typically, although in 2007 and again in 2008 there were rather large "ice storms".
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Tulsa has over 225 days of sunshine annually.
find	V;NFIN	find	In Tulsa you will find old west charm as well as a cosmopolitan atmosphere.
find	V;NFIN	find	You will find the people of Tulsa love their city and they have that southern charm, so they are willing to help you find your way around.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Tulsa has one of the largest concentrations of Art Deco in the nation, having been a booming city during the 1920s when the architecture was first built by rich oil barons who built stately mansions and turned the Downtown area into a treasure trove of art.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	It has major airlines with direct flights to major cities in the U.S.
lay	V;PST;V.PTCP	laid	It is a small but nice airport with sofas and chairs all around and a very laid-back feel.
integrate	V;PST;V.PTCP	integrated	The car rental area and parking is well integrated.
offer	V;PRS;SG;3	offers	The airport offers free short term parking (first half-hour free).
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	Richard L. Jones, Jr. Airport (IATA: RVS) (often called the "Riverside Airport") is south of downtown and is a general aviation airport.
drive	V;PRS;PL;3	drive	Most Tulsans drive almost everywhere, although bus, bike, and pedestrian routes are starting to catch on.
span	V;PRS;SG;3	spans	The world's former largest McDonalds spans the roadway near Vinita, OK.
come	V;IMP;2	come	For the slow scenic route from Northeast or Southwest come in on old Route 66.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get around
place	V;PST;V.PTCP	placed	Thanks to urban planning, the major city streets are placed in a grid layout.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	That makes it much easier to find places than in cities where streets go every which way.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Several freeways and bypasses can be used to easily get around the Tulsa Metro area: I-244, I-44, US 169 (Mingo Valley Expressway, aka "Pearl Harbor Memorial Expressway"), US 75, Hwy 51 (Broken Arrow Expressway, The "B.A."), Creek Turnpike.
plan	V;PST;V.PTCP	planned	The streets and avenues are planned on a 1 mile by 1 mile grid system, with the main arterials running on each mile.
name	V;PST;V.PTCP	named	In the core of the city, named avenues run north / south and are named after US cities, generally in repeating alphabetical order (for example, Winston - Yale - Allegheny - Braden).
take	V;PST;V.PTCP	taken	In the mid-town area the names are taken from colleges and college towns.
divide	V;PST;V.PTCP	divided	North / South is divided by Admiral Blvd.
number	V;PST;V.PTCP	numbered	In the parts of the city farther from downtown, north-south streets are numbered.
recognize	V;NFIN	recognize	It is important to recognize that the specific format of the north-south numbered street names is North / South 145th East / West Avenue.
run	V;PRS;PL;3	run	Numbered streets run East / West with Main Street and the Arkansas River as the dividing line.
watch	V;IMP;2	Watch	Watch out for Place, Street, Avenue designation, e.g. 47th Place, 47th Street, or Florence Place, Florence Avenue.
recognize	V;NFIN	recognize	It is important to recognize that the specific format of the east-west numbered street names is West / East 71st Street North / South.
intersect	V;PRS;PL;3	intersect	In some parts of the city, numbered streets intersect, so the distinction is important.
intersect	V;NFIN	intersect	Although rare, one east-west numbered street may even intersect with a street of the same number running north-south.
plat	V;PST;V.PTCP	platted	Downtown streets were originally platted parallel to the Frisco railroad tracks.
expand	V;PST;SG;3	expanded	When Tulsa expanded beyond the bounds of its original plat, the expanded areas were platted in alignment with the points of the compass.
twist	V;PST;V.PTCP	twisted	Thus the "twisted" area down-town represents the original extent of Tulsa ca 1907.
provide	V;PRS;SG;3	provides	Tulsa Transit provides bus service for the Tulsa Metro area.
serve	V;PST;V.PTCP	served	Cities served are Tulsa, Sand Springs, Sapulpa, Jenks, and Broken Arrow.
run	V;NFIN	run	They do not run that often, especially to the outer towns like Broken Arrow.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Tulsa has an extensive interconnected paved bike trail system.
follow	V;PRS;SG;3	follows	Rivertrail follows the Arkansas River from downtown Tulsa south to the suburbs.
run	V;PRS;SG;3	runs	The Katy Trail runs west to Sand Springs.
begin	V;PRS;SG;3	begins	The Osage Trail is a rails-to-trails route that begins at the OSU-Tulsa campus and travels north 15 miles to Skiatook.
connect	V;PRS;SG;3	connects	The Creek Trail connects Rivertrail and continues east through Broken Arrow to the NSU-Broken Arrow campus.
find	V;NFIN	find	Riders accustomed to flat terrain may find Tulsa's rolling land to be a bit more challenging, particularly during the heat of summer.
look	V;PRS;V.PTCP	looking	If you are looking for a good workout, the Creek Turnpike Trail follows the land's original contours.
seek	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeking	Rivertrail is probably be best choice for the rider seeking an easy route.
locate	V;PST;V.PTCP	located	Four bike loan depots, located along Rivertrail, allow riders to borrow a bike for free for up to twenty-four hours.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	Tulsa has an active bicycling community.
date	V;PRS;SG;3	dates	York is an ancient cathedral city with a history that dates back to before Roman times.
situate	V;PST;V.PTCP	situated	It is situated in Yorkshire, North Yorkshire, England with some of the best preserved historical buildings and structures in Europe.
rank	V;PST;V.PTCP	ranked	York is frequently ranked (often vying with Manchester) the second most visited city in England after London.
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	York was known as Eboracum by the Romans, who founded the fortress city on the River Ouse in the year 71.
become	V;PST;SG;3	became	York quickly became one of the most important cities in Roman Britain, and after 211 became the capital of the province Britannia Inferior.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	Constantine the Great - later responsible for making Christianity the official religion of the Roman Empire - was first proclaimed Emperor in the city.
capture	V;PST;V.PTCP	Captured	Captured by the Vikings 866, the city quickly took on a new identity as Jorvik (pronounced "Yor-vik") and experienced a major urban revival as a centre of Viking trade and settlement in northern England.
reveal	V;PST;PL;3	revealed	The Coppergate excavations of the 1970s revealed much of this Viking past.
see	V;NFIN	see	York is a fairly small city - four days is enough to see the major sights although York is a city that reveals its charms to explorers with curiosity and patience.
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	York is known as England's "City of Festivals "as there are regular cultural festivals every year.
bring	V;NFIN	bring	York is full of magic and a wonderful place to bring children!
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get in
arrive	V;NFIN	arrive	Most travellers will arrive in York by road (car or bus) or rail from other parts of the UK or an airport.
take	V;NFIN	take	From the south, the quickest route is probably to take the M1 northbound to junction 32, then the M18 eastbound to junction 2, the A1M northbound to junction 44 and finally the A64 eastbound to the York Outer Ring Road (A1237).
take	V;NFIN	take	Alternatively, you can take the M1 all the way to the A64, but the upper reaches of the M1 around Sheffield and Leeds can get very congested, especially in the rush hours.
provide	V;PRS;PL;3	provide	From the west, the A59 and the M62 provide connections from Liverpool and Manchester, and from the north the A1 and the A19 link York with Tyneside, Northumberland and south-eastern Scotland.
drive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Driving	Driving into the city centre itself is something to avoid.
serve	V;PRS;V.PTCP	serving	Traffic congestion on the main arterial roads serving the city (especially the A19 on Bootham and the Inner Ring Road) can get very bad, especially during the rush hours and on Saturday mornings.
accommodate	V;NFIN	accommodate	The remodelling of some roads near bottleneck junctions to accommodate bicycle lanes has made traffic jams even worse still in recent years, and parking in the city centre is very expensive.
visit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	visiting	If you are just visiting York for the day, using a Park and Ride [1] costs a lot less than trying to park in or near the city centre, and there are five sites dotted around the Outer Ring Road.
leave	V;PRS;PL;3	leave	However, the last buses from the city centre leave at around 8 pm in the evenings and you are not allowed to leave your car in a Park and Ride overnight.
stay	V;PRS;V.PTCP	staying	Therefore, if you are staying overnight in York and arriving by car, make sure that your hotel offers parking before you book.
visit	V;PRS;V.PTCP	visiting	If you are only visiting for the day but staying until late evening, you will need to use a city centre car park.
detect	V;NFIN	Detect	How to Detect Arrogant People
seem	V;NFIN	seem	Do you always seem to cross paths with people who are stuck on themselves, intolerant of people different from them, rude or downright arrogant?
help	V;NFIN	help	These people can be a great source of potential pain, and this article is here to help you sort the arrogant from the not-so-arrogant.
pay	V;IMP;2	Pay	Pay attention to their conversations.
eavesdrop	V;IMP;2	eavesdrop	Don't eavesdrop, but when they're talking to you or to those around you, listen to them.
get	V;NFIN	get	Do they get mad or irritated if the centre of attention moves to someone else?
feel	V;PRS;V.PTCP	feeling	Arrogance and smugness is often a reflection of limited life experience, and feeling concerned that those with greater life experience "have got something over them".
seek	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeking	Rather than seeking to find out more through questions and learning (actions viewed by them as showing vulnerability), arrogant people tend to generalize from their limited, narrow life experiences and try to impose their small worldview on others.
cause	V;NFIN	cause	Jealousy of your achievements or seeming lifestyle can cause another person to feel smug or arrogant about something they think they do better than you or own / have that you don't.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Arrogant people have an extremely strong need to look good.
make	V;PRS;SG;2	make	When you make them look bad - even if it is the slightest offense - they will usually be very mad at you.
happen	V;PRS;SG;3	happens	This happens when you question (or at least seem to question) their appearance, intelligence, athletic abilities, or anything else relating to their self-image.
challenge	V;IMP;2	Challenge	Challenge their worldview.
upset	V;PST;V.PTCP	upset	If they get upset, gauge their anger.
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	If it's minimal, they may be simply having a bad day.
enrage	V;PST;V.PTCP	enraged	But if they're enraged, then they may see you as questioning their 'perfect little world.'
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	And having one of those is usually indicative of arrogance.
realize	V;PRS;PL;3	realize	At some point or another, most people realize that the world doesn't revolve around them.
counteract	V;PRS;PL;3	counteract	Arrogant people counteract this by creating an atmosphere that revolves around them, and get angry if they're reminded of the real world.
frighten	V;PRS;SG;3	frightens	Ambiguity frightens arrogant people because it suggests imperfection, change and lack of certainty (realities we all must contend with as best we can).
accept	V;PRS;V.PTCP	accepting	As such, instead of accepting that the world behaves randomly and at times totally averse to one's preferences, the arrogant person seeks to control everything and everyone, which of course, is an impossible mission.
hurt	V;PRS;SG;3	hurts	Reality hurts when it intrudes; as such, an arrogant person is less likely than other people to self-reflect or analyse, thereby not seeing their own imperfections.
give	V;NFIN	give	They may also give themselves undue credit for positive achievements instead of acknowledging the input of others or of circumstances.
learn	V;IMP;2	Learn	Learn the quality of their friendships.
have	V;PRS;V.PTCP	having	Don't be nosy or gossipy, but if they are happy with someone one day and hateful with them the next, that's a sign of them having a lot of fair weather friends.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Prideful people have a strong need to look good, and being self-sufficient is an effective way to do that.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	Since being a good friend to someone usually means helping them, they often can't stand the thought of a good friendship.
understand	V;NFIN	understand	Ironically, arrogant people often can't understand why they don't have any reliable and supportive friends.
treat	V;NFIN	treat	How do they treat those not like themselves?
treat	V;NFIN	treat	In other words, how do they treat those with different beliefs, cultural backgrounds and ways of seeing the world?
avoid	V;NFIN	avoid	If it's inherently negative, then they're either over-zealous, ignorant of other people or what to avoid those that contradict their fantasy land that caters to them and them only.
determine	V;IMP;2	Determine	Determine this based on their general personality and the people they're interacting with.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Many times prideful people have a serious 'my-way's-the-only-way' attitude.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take note of how they act, talk, and use their social status.
have	V;NFIN	have	Do they have a general sense of 'coolness?'
act	V;NFIN	act	Do they act like they own the place, or act like the 'big dog?'
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Many arrogant people have a false charm that no one seems to see through.
show	V;NFIN	show	But the arrogant person is usually more than happy to show their cruel side to those that they don't like.
ignore	V;NFIN	ignore	When they are cruel, their friends will usually ignore it or not do anything to stop it since they're afraid that they'll be treated badly by their 'friend.'
mention	V;IMP;2	Mention	Mention people you know that they don't like.
mean	V;PST;V.PTCP	meant	This isn't meant to begin a conflict, but to gauge their rivalries, annoyances and enmities.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	If their condemnation seems to be reasonable, they probably aren't hubristic.
see	V;PRS;PL;3	see	For the most part, arrogant people see people that they don't like as threats to their perfect little world.
hate	V;PRS;PL;3	hate	The more they hate someone, the more dangerous that person is to their fantasy land.
ask	V;IMP;2	Ask	Ask around to see what they've been saying about you.
say	V;PRS;V.PTCP	saying	If they have been saying bad things about you, they may simply not like you.
talk	V;PRS;PL;3	talk	If they're nice to your face, but talk bad about you behind your back like it's their favourite hobby, then they probably have a problem with pride.
know	V;PRS;PL;3	know	Arrogant people often subconsciously know that they don't have any good friends.
compensate	V;PRS;PL;3	compensate	They compensate for this by creating the impression that they have a lot of friends - they have a 'quantity, not quality' mentality.
insult	V;PRS;PL;3	insult	Then they simply insult their trophy friends when they aren't looking.
dance	V;NFIN	Dance	How to Ballet Dance
begin	V;PST;SG;3	began	Ballet began in royal courts in the early 1600's, and early forms of this elegant and sophisticated art involved long skirts and wooden clogs.
dance	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Dancing	Dancing ballet is extremely popular throughout the world, and studying ballet can help develop strong bodies, spatial and temporal awareness, and improve coordination.
study	V;PRS;PL;3	study	People who study ballet also retain flexibility throughout their adult lives, making this technique the basis of training for all types of dance.
dance	V;PRS;V.PTCP	dancing	While dancing ballet takes dedication and requires serious training, you can learn the basics to prepare yourself for further study.
learn	V;IMP;2	Learn	Learn to get ready for practicing, the basic positions, and some of the first techniques you're likely to encounter in ballet.
get	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Getting	Part 1 Getting Ready to Dance
stretch	V;IMP;2	Stretch	Stretch your muscles thoroughly.
stretch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Stretching	Stretching is important to loosen muscles, strengthen muscles and to elongate your posture.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	It is vital that it is done at the beginning of each ballet session, including before a performance.
take	V;PRS;V.PTCP	taking	When taking on ballet, it is important to stretch daily for at least 15 - 30 minutes, to give the muscles ample opportunity to warm up, reducing the risk of injury.
stretch	V;NFIN	stretch	You should also stretch to "wind down" at the end of ballet dancing.
wear	V;IMP;2	wear	Always wear ballet slippers.
cut	V;PRS;PL;3	cut	Properly-fitted ballet shoes should be snug, but not so tight they cut off blood flow and create numbness in the feet.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are different styles and types of shoes, so ask your ballet teacher or a salesman at the store for advice, given your intentions for dancing.
buy	V;IMP;2	buy	Don't buy shoes to grow into, because your feet will seem flexed when you pointe and will look flat.
fit	V;NFIN	fit	You should fit them to where the drawstring is tied slightly loose.
cut	V;NFIN	cut	If your drawstring is longer than your pinkie then you should cut it off till it is roughly the size of your fingernail.
complete	V;PRS;SG;3	completes	The drawstring just completes the fit.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	It isn't made to tighten overly large shoes.
buy	V;NFIN	buy	If you can't buy ballet shoes, that it is okay.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use socks without the stickies on the bottom, so you can turn!
wear	V;IMP;2	Wear	Wear comfortable and snug-fitting athletic clothes.
wear	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wearing	The most important thing is that you are comfortable, and that you're not wearing baggy or loose fitting clothing so you can make sure you're forming and moving correctly in the mirror.
sign	V;PST;V.PTCP	signed	If you're signed up for a class, check with your instructor to find out if there is a dress code at the school.
have	V;NFIN	have	Some schools may have students wear the same thing and others may just require any type of leotard and tights and sometimes ballet skirts.
require	V;PRS;PL;3	require	Mostly they require tight fitting clothing so they can see that your muscles are working properly and so on.
find	V;IMP;2	Find	Find an appropriate space to practice.
learn	V;PRS;V.PTCP	learning	Ballet is less about learning the moves and more about perfecting them.
require	V;PST;V.PTCP	required	The moves themselves are relatively straightforward, but the positing, the timing, and the elegance required takes a lifetime of practice.
practice	V;NFIN	practice	For this reason, it's always better to practice ballet in a ballet studio under the guidance of a good instructor, who will be able to correct your positioning and make sure that you're dancing properly.
equip	V;PST;V.PTCP	equipped	A dancing studio is equipped with mirrors for correcting your positioning and seeing what you're doing accurately, and a barre for practicing. [1]
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to practice at home, make sure you've got enough open space to move freely, preferably on a hard wood floor.
replace	V;NFIN	replace	The back of a chair can replace the need for a bar.
position	V;IMP;2	Position	Position a large mirror so you can check your positioning and see what you're doing.
grow	V;NFIN	Grow	How to Grow Basil
care	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Caring	Three Methods: Starting Seeds Caring for Basil Harvesting and Using Basil
grow	V;NFIN	grow	Basil is easy to grow, and transforms ordinary meals into culinary treasures!
taste	V;PRS;SG;3	tastes	Fresh basil not only tastes better than dried, it actually tastes different, almost as if it were not the same herb.
outline	V;PRS;SG;3	outlines	This article outlines how to plant and harvest basil.
choose	V;IMP;2	Choose	1 Choose the kind of basil you wish to grow.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	Basil comes in many different varieties, each of which have a unique flavor and smell.
read	V;IMP;2	Read	Read up on different types of basil and pick out one - or several - that appeal to you, then order the seeds or buy them at a garden store.
smell	V;NFIN	smell	Cinnamon basil really does smell like the sweet spice, and it also has unusually beautiful and fragrant flowers.
contain	V;PRS;SG;3	contains	Lemon basil actually contains citral, an aromatic compound found in citrus fruit, and smells very lemony.
grow	V;PST;V.PTCP	grown	Purple basil is often grown for decoration, as well as its scent and flowers.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are perennial basils which come back year after year, like African Blue Basil (which has pretty blue veins on its leaves) and Thai Basil, while most other varieties are annuals, which you'll have to plant year after year.
grow	V;NFIN	grow	Globe and Greek basil are much more difficult to grow, but form pretty little bushes which stay well-contained.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	2 Start seed indoors four to six weeks before last frost.
need	V;PRS;SG;3	needs	Basil needs warm air and sun to do well, so it's often easiest to start the seeds indoors instead of risking that they'll get damaged by frost.
live	V;PRS;SG;2	live	If you live in a hot climate, you can start the seeds outside instead.
figure	V;NFIN	figure	To figure out when the last frost will be, consult an almanac or talk with other gardeners in your area.
prepare	V;IMP;2	Prepare	3 Prepare seed containers.
fill	V;IMP;2	Fill	Fill flats or individual seed containers with a mixture of equal parts perlite, vermiculite, and peat.
press	V;IMP;2	Press	Press the mixture slightly to eliminate air pockets.
dampen	V;IMP;2	Dampen	Dampen the mixture with water so it's ready to provide the right environment for the basil seeds to germinate.
plant	V;IMP;2	Plant	4 Plant the seeds.
drop	V;IMP;2	Drop	Drop one to two seeds into each container.
cover	V;IMP;2	Cover	Cover them lightly with soil.
cover	V;IMP;2	Cover	Cover containers with clear plastic kitchen wrap, so they stay moist.
leave	V;IMP;2	Leave	Leave the containers in a sunny window.
remove	V;IMP;2	remove	Twice daily, remove the plastic wrap and sprinkle the containers with more water.
remove	V;IMP;2	Remove	5 Remove the plastic wrap when the sprouts emerge.
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	When you see the first green tendrils push up through the soil, it's time to remove the plastic wrap.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep watering the sprouts twice daily, never letting the soil dry out.
become	V;PRS;PL;3	become	When the plants become a few inches tall and their leaves mature, it's time to transplant them to a larger container.
care	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Caring	Method 2 of 3: Caring for Basil
transplant	V;IMP;2	Transplant	1 Transplant the basil plants.
form	V;PST;V.PTCP	formed	Once two sets of leaves have formed, basil can be planted into the garden or permanent containers.
tolerate	V;NFIN	tolerate	Basil does not tolerate frost so don't plant too early.
put	V;NFIN	put	It's best to put basil somewhere where it will get a good deal of sunshine and have well-drained soil.
plant	V;NFIN	plant	To plant the basil in the garden, dig holes spaced at least 6 inches apart.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place the roots in the holes and place soil around the stems.
pat	V;IMP;2	Pat	Pat down soil around the plants to eliminate air pockets.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to plant the basil in a container, make sure it's large enough to accommodate the number of plants you're growing;
need	V;PRS;PL;3	need	they need to be planted 6 inches apart, since they grow quite large.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	2 Keep the soil damp, but not soaked.
do	V;PRS;SG;3	does	Basil does best in well-drained soil, and should not be subjected to standing water.
water	V;IMP;2	Water	Water the mature basil plants once a day, in the morning, so the water has time to soak in and evaporate rather than sitting on the plants overnight.
pinch	V;IMP;2	Pinch	3 Pinch off flower heads.
see	V;PRS;SG;2	see	When you see flower buds, pinch them and two pairs of leaves under them off.
bloom	V;PRS;V.PTCP	blooming	Flowers blooming create a hormone change which dramatically reduces the flavor of the leaves, as well as reducing the amount of foliage which grows.
call	V;PST;V.PTCP	called	This is called" bolting" and is more likely to happen when there's extra sunshine.
notice	V;NFIN	notice	You'll notice that if you leave the flowers, the plant will become lanky and the leaves won't be as full or tasty.
watch	V;IMP;2	Watch	4 Watch for pests and mold.
control	V;NFIN	control	the best way to control these pests is to pick them off the plants by hand.
show	V;PRS;PL;3	show	If your plants show signs of mold growth, they may not be getting adequate sunlight, or they may be too close together.
weed	V;IMP;2	Weed	Weed out the smaller plants to give the larger ones more space.
harvest	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Harvesting	Method 3 of 3: Harvesting and Using Basil
harvest	V;IMP;2	Harvest	1 Harvest and prune.
mature	V;PRS;SG;3	matures	As the plant matures, pinch off the top two pairs of leaves once a stalk reaches a reasonable height.
look	V;PRS;SG;2	look	If you look closely, at the base of every leaf are two tiny little leaves that will grow outwards if the stem growing between them is cut off.
cut	V;IMP;2	Cut	Cut close to those tiny leaves, but be sure not to damage them.
pinch	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Pinching	Pinching encourages the plant's energy to be directed into its stronger stems and leaves.
help	V;PRS;SG;3	helps	This helps the plants grow bushy.
pinch	V;IMP;2	pinch	Don't pinch on the lower part of the stem, or the basil plants will grow tall and reedy.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	You want them to get bushy, so pinch from the top.
enjoy	V;IMP;2	Enjoy	2 Enjoy the basil fresh.
rinse	V;IMP;2	Rinse	Rinse off the leaves and use the basil to make pesto or a Caprese salad with tomatoes and fresh mozzarella cheese.
store	V;IMP;2	Store	3 Store the basil in the refrigerator.
have	V;NFIN	have	You'll probably have more basil than you could possibly eat fresh, so plan on storing some in the fridge.
wash	V;IMP;2	Wash	Wash the leaves, dry them well, and wrap them in paper towels.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place them in a food storage container with an airtight lid.
freeze	V;IMP;2	Freeze	4 Freeze the basil.
freeze	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Freezing	Freezing the leaves whole doesn't work very well, but if you puree it first you can freeze it for months.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place the basil in a blender with a little water.
puree	V;IMP;2	Puree	Puree it until smooth, then put it in a food storage bag and freeze until you need it.
hypnotize	V;NFIN	Hypnotize	How to Hypnotize a Chicken
spend	V;PST;V.PTCP	spent	Anyone who's spent a lot of time on a farm with chickens is probably familiar with this trick.
hear	V;PST;V.PTCP	heard	Those who've never heard of this will be amazed to see a chicken lie perfectly still after these instructions are followed.
think	V;PRS;PL;3	think	Biologists think that fear causes this "hypnosis," which may be an attempt to "play dead" to fool predators.
hypnotize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Hypnotizing	Method One of Two: Hypnotizing a Chicken
hold	V;IMP;2	Hold	Hold the chicken down on a flat surface.
hold	V;IMP;2	Hold	Hold the chicken with one hand supporting the breast.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place it down so that its breast is carrying its weight and rests on the ground.
continue	V;IMP;2	Continue	Continue to hold its feet so the grand experiment can continue.
place	V;NFIN	place	You can place the chicken on its breast instead.
press	V;IMP;2	Press	Press gently down on its back, and gently move its legs back if it tries to stand up.
wiggle	V;IMP;2	Wiggle	Wiggle your finger.
hold	V;IMP;2	Hold	Hold the bird down gently with one hand.
put	V;IMP;2	Put	Put one finger on your other hand just in front of its beak tip, without touching it.
move	V;IMP;2	Move	Move the finger backward to about 4 inches (10 cm) away, then back again.
repeat	V;IMP;2	Repeat	Repeat until the chicken stops moving or squawking.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let go of its legs.
hypnotize	V;PST;V.PTCP	hypnotized	The chicken should be "hypnotized" and cease to struggle.
lie	V;NFIN	lie	It will lie there for anywhere from 30 seconds to several minutes.
draw	V;IMP;2	Draw	Draw a line in front of its beak instead.
hypnotize	V;PST;V.PTCP	hypnotized	If the chicken wasn't hypnotized, try this alternative.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using chalk, a stick, or your finger, draw a line on the ground 12 " (30 cm) long.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start near the chicken's beak and draw the line slowly outward, in front of its head.
wake	V;IMP;2	Wake	Wake it up by clapping.
let	V;NFIN	let	Be nice to your feathered friend and let it get back to its business.
clap	V;IMP;2	Clap	Clap your hands or give it a gentle shove until it jumps up and walks away.
reduce	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Reducing	Method Two of Two: Reducing Stress in the Chicken
understand	V;IMP;2	Understand	Understand the hypnotic effect.
call	V;PRS;PL;3	call	Researchers call this effect "tonic immobility".
become	V;PRS;SG;3	becomes	"When a chicken or another animal with this tendency becomes frightened, its heart rate lowers and it stops moving.
play	V;NFIN	play	This may be an attempt to play dead, discouraging predators that prefer live prey.
give	V;PRS;SG;3	gives	Unlike an opossum, the chicken gives an unconvincing act, as it continues to blink and breathe in an obvious way."
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep the chicken upright or on its side.
transport	V;NFIN	transport	While it's not uncommon to transport a chicken by its feet, this upside-down hold risks breaking the chicken's hip.
involve	V;PRS;PL;3	involve	Hypnosis techniques that involve laying a chicken on its back may "succeed" by cutting off its air supply.
cause	V;NFIN	cause	This can cause the chicken significant discomfort, leading to fainting or even, rarely, death.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep hypnosis short and infrequent.
cause	V;PRS;SG;3	causes	It's not completely clear how much stress this hypnosis causes.
suffer	V;NFIN	suffer	Even if it does, the chicken shouldn't suffer as long as you let it go soon afterward.
cause	V;NFIN	cause	Hours of stress, or regular exposure to stressful situations, can cause serious health problems.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get your chickens used to humans and novelty.
seem	V;PRS;SG;3	seems	Even prolonged eye contact seems to have an effect.
live	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Living	Living in stimulating environments with new objects may also help.
raise	V;PST;V.PTCP	raise	Hens raise in battery cages stay "hypnotized" longer, possibly because of greater fear.
watch	V;IMP;2	Watch	Watch for signs of stress.
stress	V;PST;V.PTCP	stressed	Abnormal feathering, constant preening, or delays in egg-laying are all signs of a stressed chicken.
hypnotize	V;PRS;V.PTCP	hypnotizing	While hypnotizing is unlikely to cause serious harm, any type of stress is more damaging to a chicken in this state.
make	V;NFIN	Make	How to Make Vegan Cupcakes
give	V;NFIN	give	No need to give up wicked cakes when you toss the eggs, butter, and milk!
use	V;NFIN	use	Here's a great vegan cupcake recipe to use as a base for whatever flavored icing you want to add to it.
guarantee	V;PST;V.PTCP	guaranteed	They're guaranteed to be light and fluffy and perfect for any occasion.
opt	V;PRS;SG;2	opt	Servings: 18 - 24 (fewer if you opt for larger-sized cupcakes)
raise	V;PRS;V.PTCP	raising	2 3/4 cups (345 g) self raising flour
make	V;PRS;SG;3	Makes	Makes approx: 20 - 24 cupcakes:
sift	V;PST;V.PTCP	sifted	275 g (2 cups) plain or all-purpose flour (sifted)
sift	V;PST;V.PTCP	sifted	100 g (3/4 cup) quality cocoa powder (sifted)
preheat	V;IMP;2	Preheat	Preheat the oven to 180oC/350oF.
prepare	V;IMP;2	Prepare	Prepare the muffin tray by adding cupcake holders/cases.
put	V;IMP;2	Put	Put to one side.
use	V;IMP;2	use	Alternatively, use a silicone cupcake baking sheet with molded holes.
put	V;IMP;2	Put	Put the cider vinegar into a small bowl and add the soy milk.
stir	V;IMP;2	Stir	Stir this well and then put to one side.
curdle	V;NFIN	curdle	The mixture will now curdle and this is what should be happening.
stir	V;IMP;2	Stir	Stir together the flour, sugar, baking powder, baking soda and salt.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want chocolate cupcakes, add cocoa (about 1/2 cup) to this stage.
stir	V;IMP;2	Stir	Stir the oil and vanilla extract into the bowl containing the soy milk and vinegar.
mix	V;IMP;2	Mix	Mix together.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add the wet ingredients to the dry ingredients and beat together (by hand or with an electric hand-held mixer).
fill	V;IMP;2	Fill	Fill the muffin pan.
fill	V;IMP;2	fill	Usually fill each paper cupcake holder with about 1/4 cup of batter.
prefer	V;PRS;SG;2	prefer	If you prefer larger cupcakes, then add a half a cup but make sure the muffin pan is larger and the cupcake holders are bigger.
bake	V;IMP;2	Bake	Bake for 18 - 20 minutes.
smell	V;NFIN	smell	If you can smell the cupcakes earlier, check to make sure they aren't browning earlier (this means they're more than ready).
turn	V;IMP;2	turn	If you aren't certain, turn off the oven earlier and let them sit for a few more minutes rather than bake them into rocks.
wait	V;IMP;2	Wait	Wait for the pan to cool down.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place cupcakes onto a wire rack to cool.
ice	V;IMP;2	Ice	Ice the cupcakes.
improve	V;PST;V.PTCP	improved	The flavor of icing or frosting can be improved by adding a little of your favorite juice to it instead of soy milk or water.
finish	V;PST;V.PTCP	Finished	Finished.
preheat	V;IMP;2	Preheat	Preheat the oven to 160oC/315oF.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	If using cupcake cases, arrange on a baking sheet now.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	If using a silicone cupcake sheet, simply have it ready.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add the flour, cocoa powder, baking soda and pinch of salt to the mixing bowl.
mix	V;IMP;2	Mix	Mix to combine.
pour	V;IMP;2	Pour	Pour the soy milk, vinegar, sugar, oil and vanilla into another bowl.
whisk	V;IMP;2	Whisk	Whisk together well.
pour	V;IMP;2	Pour	Pour the liquid mixture into the flour mixture.
stir	V;IMP;2	Stir	Stir to combine thoroughly.
pour	V;IMP;2	Pour	Pour into the cupcake cases or cupcake holes.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place into the oven.
bake	V;IMP;2	Bake	Bake for 20 to 25 minutes.
smell	V;PRS;PL;3	smell	If they smell strongly earlier, test with a wooden skewer; if it returns clean after sticking into a cupcake, they're ready.
remove	V;IMP;2	Remove	Remove from the oven.
transfer	V;IMP;2	Transfer	Transfer the cupcakes to a wire cooling rack as quickly as possible when cool enough to handle.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let cool completely on the rack.
frost	V;IMP;2	Frost	Frost as desired.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add standard frosting or make vegan butter cream.
add	V;NFIN	add	You can add pureed fruit for delicious flavor changes.
puree	V;PST;V.PTCP	pureed	One small container of pureed fruit is sufficient for flavoring (the little containers that children take to school in their lunchboxes.)
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try strawberry, apple, banana, apricot, peach etc.
prepare	V;PST;V.PTCP	prepared	The cupcakes will not be as light and fluffy, however, so be prepared for a heavier cupcake if you add fruit but the added moisture content is a definite plus.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	Instead of using vegan sugar, you can replace it with equal parts maple sugar or rice syrup
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	Instead of using vegetable oil, try substituting it for walnut or macadamia nut oils if they are available to you.
produce	V;PRS;SG;3	produces	Coconut oil also produces good results.
work	V;PRS;SG;3	works	And olive oil works fine if it's all that's left in the pantry.
replace	V;NFIN	replace	You can replace the plain soy milk with other flavors like chocolate or vanilla if you want to experiment with changing the flavor of the cupcakes.
experiment	V;IMP;2	Experiment	Experiment with the flavors of either recipe by replacing the vanilla extract with fragrant ingredients like neroli orange oil, rosewater, orange blossom water or coconut extract.
annoy	V;NFIN	Annoy	How to Annoy People in Elevators
annoy	V;NFIN	annoy	It’s easy to annoy people when you are cramped in such a small, tight space.
last	V;PRS;PL;3	last	Because elevator rides usually last less than a minute, make sure your pranks are quick and have a lot of punch.
annoy	V;PRS;V.PTCP	annoying	When annoying people on an elevator, keep your pranks lighthearted and fun as opposed to mean-spirited so that everyone leaving the elevator will have a pleasant story to tell.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Method 1 Using the Elevator to Annoy People
push	V;IMP;2	Push	Push all the buttons.
get	V;PRS;SG;2	get	When you get into the elevator, push the button for every floor.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	This makes everyone’s ride on the elevator longer, if only for a few seconds.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to take it a step further, look around questioningly at everyone in the elevator when no one gets off at the floors you pushed. [1]
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	When someone comes in, say, "I've got this," before you push all of the buttons.
push	V;NFIN	push	You can also push all the buttons as you reach the floor you are getting off at.
ask	V;PRS;SG;3	asks	If someone asks you why you pushed all the buttons, you can honestly say, "It was just a joke!"
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make a dinging noise at each floor.
reach	V;PRS;SG;3	reaches	Every time the elevator reaches a new floor, say “ding!” very loudly.
sing	V;NFIN	sing	You can even sing the word “ding” on each floor like notes in a scale, getting higher as you reach each new floor. [2]
make	V;NFIN	make	You can make other noises if you prefer, such as a bird cawing or an explosion sound every time a button is pushed.
talk	V;IMP;2	Talk	Talk to your reflection in the mirror.
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	Many elevators have mirrors on the walls.
carry	V;NFIN	carry	A great way to be mischievous in an elevator is to carry on a running commentary while you look at yourself in the mirror.
look	V;NFIN	look	You could look at yourself, turning to catch different angles and say “All right, all right, now we’re talking,” very loudly.
keep	V;NFIN	keep	You could also keep adjusting your shirt or your hair, keeping a running dialogue about what look is best for you.
stand	V;IMP;2	Stand	Stand in the corner of the elevator facing the wall.
say	V;IMP;2	say	Don't say anything the whole ride.
dance	V;IMP;2	Dance	Dance to the elevator music.
play	V;PRS;PL;3	play	Many elevators play music in the background, usually soft rock or smooth jazz.
play	V;PRS;V.PTCP	playing	If you’re in an elevator that is playing music, start to dance.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start just bobbing your head and tapping your feet, and then get your whole body into it.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take up as much space as you can to dance until everyone is staring at you. [3]
announce	V;IMP;2	Announce	Announce every floor.
make	V;IMP;2	make	At every floor, make a loud announcement telling everyone the floor number.
say	V;IMP;2	Say	Say something like “Everyone going to floor ten, get off now! No time to dilly dally!” [4]
pretend	V;NFIN	pretend	You can also pretend to conduct people onto the elevator, saying “All aboard the elevator train!”
exclaim	V;IMP;2	Exclaim	Exclaim that you've lost a beast.
go	V;PRS;V.PTCP	going	When the elevator is going from the bottom floor to the top, as soon as the doors close, exclaim that you lost your tarantula / snake / scorpion but confirm that it is somewhere in the elevator.
see	V;NFIN	see	Most people will probably see that this is a joke.
look	V;PRS;SG;3	looks	However, if somebody looks seriously alarmed or is starting to panic, let them know that you were not being serious.
build	V;IMP;2	Build	Build in or add something to the elevator.
build	V;IMP;2	build	For example, build a Lego city in the middle of the elevator.
lay	V;IMP;2	Lay	Lay a Twister mat on the floor and ask people if they want to play.
talk	V;IMP;2	Talk	Talk to people.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	When there are a lot of people, say, "You're probably wondering why I've gathered you here today."
flirt	V;NFIN	Flirt	How to Flirt Through Instant Messaging
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	So you want to flirt with a guy or gal on MSN, AIM, Facebook Chat, or any other instant messaging service and you don't want to look like a creep?
seek	V;PRS;V.PTCP	seeking	Congratulations — by seeking help, you've already demonstrated more self-awareness than the vast majority of online flirts.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See Step 1 below to start flirting smartly and respectfully.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start the conversation casually.
flirt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	flirting	As in the real world, the first step to flirting is to get over your cold feet and jump right in!
text	V;IMP;2	Text	Text the other person a brief message asking them about their day, asking them a specific question about work or school, or simply saying "Hi!".
flirt	V;PRS;V.PTCP	flirting	The hardest part of flirting is to break through your initial reluctance, so if you're having a hard time taking the plunge, just remind yourself that no matter how badly it goes, it'll be less stressful than an equivalent real-world encounter.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	's	There's no reason to be nervous when it comes to IM flirting —
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	if the person you want to talk to doesn't want to talk to you, s/he always has the option of simply not responding to your message, as, from your point of view, this is indistinguishable from him or her simply being away from the computer.
say	V;PST;V.PTCP	said	This being said, if you barely know someone, it can be a good idea to have an excuse for starting a conversation to avoid awkwardness.
ask	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Asking	Asking for help with a work or school-related problem is almost always a good bet, as is asking a question about something striking about the person.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	For instance, if the person has a band-related username, you might say: "Hey, cool name. Did you see those guys when they last came to town?"
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start small talk.
want	V;NFIN	want	After your initial greeting and exchange of pleasantries, you'll probably want to ask how the person's doing (just like you might in the real world).
ask	V;IMP;2	Ask	Ask him or her about work or school, his / her interests, or any recent trips, for instance.
ask	V;PRS;V.PTCP	asking	Rather than asking a question, you can also simply offer your comments on these things.
respond	V;PRS;SG;3	responds	When s/he responds, add your own comments or ask follow-up questions and proceed from there!
pry	V;IMP;2	pry	Don't pry into his or her personal life — keep things light, fun, and focused on worry-free subjects.
dwell	V;IMP;2	dwell	Don't dwell too long on small talk.
break	V;PRS;V.PTCP	breaking	A minute or two is great for breaking the ice, but much more can get boring fast.
open	V;PST;V.PTCP	opened	For example, after we've opened up by asking about this person's interest in the band in his / her username, it's logical and reasonable to ask about this person's musical likes and dislikes.
offer	V;NFIN	offer	You can even offer your own opinions and suggestions.
say	V;NFIN	say	For instance, you might say something like: "If you like those guys, you might want to check out this band called Manic Albatross - they're like the Beatles, only darker. What sorts of other bands do you like?"
joke	V;IMP;2	Joke	Joke.
like	V;PRS;SG;3	likes	Everyone likes a good sense of humor.
make	V;NFIN	make	In the immortal words of Marilyn Monroe, "If you can make a woman laugh, you can make her do anything" (don't worry, ladies — the same is true for men!)
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try to be playful and even slightly sarcastic as you respond to the statements of the person you're talking to.
ask	V;PST;V.PTCP	asked	For instance, if you're asked what you're up to, rather than saying, "looking for people to hit on on Facebook" you might find that it's a better idea to give a sarcastic answer like "writing the great American novel" or "drowning my sorrows".
have	V;PRS;PL;3	have	These responses have the added benefit of being natural launching points for conversations about your hobbies, like the writing you're doing on the side and the great bourbon you tasted the other day.
work	V;NFIN	work	In our example conversation, you might work a joke or two into your music-related small talk.
say	V;NFIN	say	For instance, you might say something like, "I don't know why every song on the radio nowadays has a Texas Flynn feature. Does he even have time to record between rehab stints?"
put	V;PST;V.PTCP	put	Simply put, confidence is sexy.
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	This is more true for real-life situations than it is online, but this mantra has some truth in the world of IM flirting as well.
want	V;NFIN	want	For instance, you'll want to avoid making lots of jokes at your own expense.
recur	V;PRS;V.PTCP	recurring	Just one is plenty — it shouldn't be a recurring theme throughout your conversation.
do	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Doing	Doing this too often can quickly turn an otherwise flirtatious conversation into one in which you appear needy and self-loathing.
say	V;NFIN	say	On the other hand, this isn't to say that you should necessarily make jokes at other people's expense, as this can make you seem mean and petty.
have	V;NFIN	have	Any sort of pointed or caustic remarks about yourself or someone else don't have a place in flirtatious conversations.
like	V;PRS;PL;3	like	People like flirting to be fun.
receive	V;PRS;V.PTCP	receiving	For most people, receiving compliments is only fun up to a point — getting more than one or two can make someone feel embarrassed and self-conscious.
make	V;NFIN	make	It can also make him or her question your motives, leading the person to believe that you may be trying to get something out of him or her.
woo	V;PRS;V.PTCP	wooing	Besides, the wooing power of flowery, gushing compliments is diminished (to say the least) when the compliments are displayed in a tiny box at the bottom of the screen alongside cartoon smiley faces.
rely	V;PRS;V.PTCP	relying	Instead of relying too heavily on compliments, instead, focus on having an engaging, genuine conversation.
follow	V;IMP;2	Follow	Follow the mantra "show, don't tell."
show	V;IMP;2	show	In other words, show that you're attracted to this person by giving him or her a great conversation, not by stating it outright.
make	V;NFIN	Make	How to Make a Glowstick
make	V;NFIN	make	To truly make a glowstick without breaking an already-made glowstick and shoving its contents into a tube (also known as cheating), you've gotta let your inner scientist out (along with a few dollars).
read	V;IMP;2	read	If you're still curious, read on.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Method One of Two: Using Luminol
put	V;IMP;2	Put	Put on protective glasses.
wear	V;IMP;2	wear	In addition, wear gloves to protect your skin.
wear	V;NFIN	wear	It's also a good idea to not wear your Sunday best.
throw	V;IMP;2	Throw	Throw on some old clothes or put a smock over clothes you want protected.
mean	V;PST;V.PTCP	meant	Some of this stuff is dangerous -- this experiment is not meant for children!
listen	V;IMP;2	Listen	Listen up, kids: You'll be working with a solution that's near a 12 on the pH scale.
mean	V;PRS;SG;3	means	That basically means don't swallow it, don't put it in your eyes, don't bathe in it, and don't really expose yourself to it directly at all.
get	V;PST;V.PTCP	Got	Got it?
move	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Moving	Moving on.
combine	V;IMP;2	Combine	Combine 50 milliliters of hydrogen peroxide and a liter of distilled water in a mixing bowl.
work	V;NFIN	work	A ceramic bowl will work best, but plastic works too.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use funnels, measuring tubes, and basters to keep everything well-measured and well away from you.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Hydrogen peroxide is used to replace the luminol's nitrogen atoms with oxygen.
happen	V;PRS;SG;3	happens	When that happens, all the substances create a rave and start partying and electrons fly everywhere and what results?
mix	V;IMP;2	Mix	Mix .2 grams of luminol, 4 grams of sodium carbonate, .4 grams of copper sulfate, .5 grams of ammonium carbonate and 1 liter (0.3 US gal) of distilled water in a second bowl.
touch	V;NFIN	touch	It is important not to touch the luminol.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use a funnel to make everything safe and easy.
float	V;NFIN	float	Unfortunately, these hazardous chemicals will not float freely in mid-air like this graphic suggests.
have	V;NFIN	have	Yep, unless you're a coroner or some sort of crazy spy / criminologist you probably don't have this stuff lying around the house (hopefully not ...).
set	V;PST;V.PTCP	set	If you're dead set on starting your own glowstick business (worse ideas exist), try websites like Alfa Aesar [1] or Sigma Aldrich [2] for supplies.
mix	V;IMP;2	Mix	Mix everything well.
use	V;IMP;2	use	Don't use your hands -- use a metal or plastic utensil of some sort.
clean	V;IMP;2	Clean	Clean the containers and dry them thoroughly.
use	V;NFIN	use	It's important to use sanitary, clean tubes for your glowsticks.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	The last thing you want is other substances interacting with the reactions you're depending on to make the substances glow.
set	V;IMP;2	Set	Set the correct lid next to each container.
enable	V;PRS;SG;3	enables	This enables you to seal the containers quickly after filling.
get	V;NFIN	get	It's not like the glow will get up and run away from you, but still.
combine	V;IMP;2	Combine	Combine equal amounts of the first and second solution in the container and close the bottles.
shake	V;IMP;2	Shake	Shake them up once the lids are on tightly.
turn	V;IMP;2	turn	Then turn off the lights!
glow	V;PRS;V.PTCP	glowing	If it's not already glowing, something went wrong.
watch	V;IMP;2	Watch	Watch as the chemical compound creates a colorful glow.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take your glowsticks to the party and charge your friends loads of money for them!
act	V;IMP;2	act	But act quickly ...
last	V;NFIN	last	the glow won't last very long.
crush	V;PST;V.PTCP	crushed	Expectations crushed?
create	V;PRS;SG;3	creates	The reaction that the luminol and hydrogen peroxide creates doesn't last long at all -- maybe a couple of minutes.
last	V;PRS;SG;3	lasts	For something that lasts hours, go to the next method (which is a lot easier to facilitate if you have access to a laboratory, but it's still worth mentioning).
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Method 2 of 2: Using Grammar
name	V;IMP;2	Name	Name your language.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	You have many names to choose from.
use	V;NFIN	use	You can even use a made up word from your made up language like 'Victory' or 'Great Language'.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start off with the highly frequently used words such as 'and' or 'I' or 'one' or 'the'.
recommend	V;PST;V.PTCP	recommend	It is recommend that you use short words since they are commonly used.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start thinking about the grammar rules.
make	V;NFIN;2	make	For example if the word Bird is 'Vogelaviatiolap' then why not make the word Birds 'Vogelaviatiolaps' since the suffix 's' is added at the end for most languages at plurals.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to add complication, you can add genders like most European languages like French and German.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	For example, if you want the word 'horse' to be a boy, you may want 'the horse' to be 'Mat Fereder', but a 'cat' to be a girl, you may want, 'the cat' to be 'Fet Kamaow'.
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider basing your language on an existing language.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	For example, in my made up language, 'Vogelaviatiolap' means Bird.
guess	V;PST;V.PTCP	guessed	If you haven't guessed, it comes from the following:
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	'Vogel' comes from German, which means bird
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	'aviatio' comes from English.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	'lap' comes from Onomatopoeia.
suppose	V;PST;V.PTCP	supposed	It's a completed term, but it is supposed to come from 'Flap!'
consider	V;IMP;2	Consider	Consider basing some of your words from other words you have invented.
invent	V;PST;SG;2	invented	For example, if you invented the word 'China' as 'Khinssa', 'Drink' as 'Bever', and the word 'Accident' as 'Casnondelibreaten', why not make the word 'tea' as 'Khincasnonbever' or 'Bevernondelibreatekin' or even 'Khinssacasnondelibreatenibever'!
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get inspiration from existing alphabets and words.
add	V;NFIN;2	add	Why not add some non Latin Characters such as ß?
make	V;NFIN	make	You can even make the whole language not based on the Latin Alphabet at all like Chinese!
take	V;NFIN	take	You can even take some words of languages, altering them or not.
make	V;NFIN	make	You can make the word 'pen' as 'penn' or even 'pen'.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using the dictionary can ensure you not missing a single word.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep track of all your
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember to write down your creations!
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use your language.
use	V;PST;V.PTCP	used	Get used to using it yourself, and spread it to others.
feel	V;PRS;SG;2	feel	Once you feel confident about your language, experiment and expand:
grab	V;IMP;2	Grab	Grab a book / novel and translate it to your own language.
teach	V;IMP;2	Teach	Teach your friends this new language.
understand	V;PRS;PL;3	understand	As soon as your friends understand your language, communicate to them with it.
make	V;PST;V.PTCP	made	Be fluent in your own made up language and start spreading this to your friends, family and strangers!
write	V;IMP;2	Write	Write your own poem / novel / story with your own made up language.
set	V;IMP;2	set	If you're really ambitious, set a goal of helping others become fluent in the language.
make	V;NFIN	make	One day, maybe you can make it the official language of a country!
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure that it is a language that while speaking, you don't get a literal knot in your tongue!
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember you should know how to write it.
write	V;PRS;1;PL	write	For example we write it from left to right, in Arabic it's right to left, Chinese is in columns, and so on.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure that you and your friend(s) all follow a systematic language system.
make	V;IMP;2	make	In other words, make sure that you follow the same guidelines.
use	V;IMP;2	use	Don't use random letters.
make	V;NFIN	make	It should make some sense, so that it will be easier to learn and speak with [Example: Don't use oh as e, hello as llo, and See ya as c yah).
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make sure you practice saying and spelling out many basic words in your language, English examples: is, who, when, of, why, if, what, where, can, may, etc.
start	V;PRS;V.PTCP	starting	When starting off, follow closely to a language you like.
make	V;NFIN	make	This will make grammar much easier to make.
avoid	V;NFIN	avoid	You must avoid simply copying the grammar rules, however, as this would technically turn your language into a code.
practice	V;IMP;2	Practice	Practice your language frequently so that you don't forget it!
forget	V;IMP;2	forget	Don't forget punctuation!
add	V;IMP;2	add	As a shortcut, and for a bit of interesting background, add connotation to various letters, preferably vowels.
do	V;NFIN	do	To do this, think of various words that begin with / have a lot of a certain vowel.
embolden	V;NFIN	embolden	For example: austere, acrimony, ebullient, embolden; in this case, the vowel A might carry with it a negative meaning, whereas E would be positive.
forget	V;PRS;SG;2	forget	Then, even if you forget your own words without the help of your dictionary, you could still make a good guess based on the composition of the letters.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try not to make your words sound like utter and complete gibberish just add a little extra than our regular English language.
want	V;NFIN	want	We don't want a knot in your tongue after speaking to your friends.
put	V;IMP;2	put	Just put marks over the letters or make up something right on the spot, you might have to take some time on this!
base	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Basing	Basing letters on objects (pictographs) is an easy way to start a writing system.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try this with a group of friends.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	It's much more fun when there are other people that can understand your language.
email	V;IMP;2	Email	Email or text your friends the language so that they won't be so confused.
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	making	When making a writing system, take a break about every five minutes and come back to it or all your letters will begin to look identical.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to make other languages, you can use this language as a model and change some letters or sounds to create a different language.
make	V;PRS;SG;3	makes	This makes your first language a proto-language — a language that branches off into a family of languages.
pack	V;NFIN	Pack	How to Pack Your Possessions When Moving
wait	V;NFIN	wait	Even if you can't wait to live in your new house, curb your enthusiasm and resist the urge to cram all your possessions any which way into boxes.
pack	V;IMP;2	Pack	Pack one room at a time if possible, and start with items that are least often used.
write	V;IMP;2	Write	Write down a list of everything you are packing, in particular if it's valuable.
take	V;IMP;2	take	Also, take note of which item is in which box.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use sturdy boxes.
fill	V;IMP;2	Fill	Fill the box to the brim and seal the lid.
stack	V;NFIN	stack	That way, you can easily stack boxes on top of one another.
tape	V;IMP;2	Tape	Tape the bottom of the box securely so that it doesn't give way.
fill	V;IMP;2	fill	Never fill a box until it is so heavy that you can't lift it safely.
spread	V;IMP;2	Spread	Spread the heavy items over a few boxes.
balance	V;IMP;2	Balance	Balance boxes with lighter items like towels or soft toys.
pack	V;IMP;2	Pack	Pack the lighter items on top.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use bubble wrap or newspapers when packing fragile items.
smudge	V;NFIN	smudge	However, newsprint may smudge so use newspaper selectively.
purchase	V;PST;V.PTCP	purchased	Clean paper can be purchased cheaply from moving supply stores.
mark	V;IMP;2	Mark	Mark boxes.
mark	V;PST;V.PTCP	marked	Boxes should be marked "Fragile" and "This side up" to prevent mishandling.
label	V;IMP;2	Label	Label each box clearly and which room it belongs to.
write	V;IMP;2	Write	Write on the sides of the box as well.
pack	V;IMP;2	Pack	Pack a separate box of essentials you may need for the first night at your new house.
include	V;IMP;2	Include	Include items like toiletries, toilet paper, bottled water, snacks and a change of clothes in case you can not unpack everything in time.
label	V;IMP;2	label	Don't label boxes with contents if the contents are valuable.
write	V;PRS;V.PTCP	writing	Instead of writing "Silver Service - Fragile" you should write something that makes it sound less theft inviting while showing the fragile nature of the contents.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use lots of it.
close	V;IMP;2	Close	Close box bottoms well.
get	V;IMP;2	Get	Get masking tape or packing tape and tape screws and other little things that came out of something back against it.
want	V;NFIN	want	You'll want to find the right screw reassemble something without having to pick it out of 100 others when you get there.
pack	V;IMP;2	pack	Don't pack one box full of books or paperwork - you might not be able to carry it at all.
fill	V;IMP;2	Fill	Fill it halfway with books, and fill it up with lighter stuff.
let	V;NFIN	let	National superstores (Walmart) will let you walk the store for boxes when they are stocking the stores between 11 pm and 1 am.
find	V;NFIN	find	You can find boxes of all sizes.
take	V;IMP;2	take	Just take a cart and roam the store.
check	V;NFIN	check	They do check the cart as you leave so don't try to sneak anything past them.
offer	V;PRS;PL;3	offer	U-haul and other nation companies offer box buy back services.
buy	V;PRS;SG;2	buy	If you buy too many boxes you can return the unused for a full refund.
re-use	V;NFIN	re-use	These boxes are also quite sturdy so you can re-use them or stash in your attic until you move again.
offer	V;PRS;PL;3	offer	Some national companies even offer free shipping and handling so you can have some delivered or go to the local location.
fill	V;IMP;2	Fill	Fill empty spaces with additional paper.
throw	V;IMP;2	Throw	Throw away or donate junk you don't want to take to your new place.
pack	V;IMP;2	pack	Don't pack it again!!!
set	V;IMP;2	Set	Set aside an area of the house where you can put all the packed boxes.
use	V;IMP;2	Use	Use a room that isn't used every day such as the formal dining or living room.
trip	V;PRS;V.PTCP	tripping	You won't be tripping over boxes and you can have a better idea of what you have and where things are.
pack	V;IMP;2	Pack	Pack books and heavy items into smaller boxes.
use	V;IMP;2	use	Digital Photos - use your camera to document the contents of boxes.
cope	V;NFIN	Cope	How to Cope With a Double Parachute Failure
malfunction	V;NFIN	malfunction	While skydiving, it is possible (yet extremely unlikely) that both your primary and reserve parachutes will malfunction, leaving you with no method of reducing your velocity.
occur	V;NFIN	occur	In the vast majority of cases, this will not occur; nevertheless, in this event these coping strategies may assist.
remain	V;IMP;2	Remain	Remain calm.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	This may seem obvious, but deep, even breathing (despite the rushing slipstream) and controlling your heart-rate are essential to your continued survival.
entreat	V;IMP;2	Entreat	Entreat assistance.
malfunction	V;PST;V.PTCP	malfunctioned	If both your primary and reserve chutes have malfunctioned, signal immediately to a fellow jumper who has not yet deployed their chute, waving your arms and signalling that your own is not functional.
skip	V;IMP;2	skip	If this is a solo jump, then skip to step 6.
prepare	V;IMP;2	Prepare	Prepare for deployment.
link	V;PRS;V.PTCP	linking	After linking arms with your fellow jumper, you will need to hook your arms through their chest strap, or through both sides of the front of their harness, as far as you can, then grab onto your own strap.
deploy	V;IMP;2	Deploy	Deploy.
multiply	V;NFIN	multiply	The G-forces will multiply your body weight, making it impossible to hold on; this is why hooking your arms through the harness is essential.
dislocate	V;NFIN	dislocate	It is likely that the shock will dislocate or break both your arms; nevertheless this is a small price to pay for your life.
prepare	V;IMP;2	Prepare	Prepare for impact.
open	V;PST;V.PTCP	opened	If your companion's canopy has successfully opened, then both your chances of survival have dramatically increased.
steer	V;PST;V.PTCP	steered	The canopy must be steered away from power lines and similar obstructions; try for a large body of water if you are confident your companion can save you from drowning.
constitute	V;PRS;SG;3	constitutes	If this is a solo jump, which constitutes the minority of skydivers, your only recourse is to ensure that at least one of your own chutes opens.
offer	V;PST;V.PTCP	offered	In this case, the excellent advice offered by your instructors on assisting chute opening may be of assistance to you.
become	V;PRS;SG;3	becomes	If it becomes apparent that you cannot open either of your two parachutes, or that for some other reason they are fatally non-functional, then the following steps may be of some minor assistance.
appeal	V;IMP;2	Appeal	Appeal.
wish	V;NFIN	wish	If you are of a religious turn of mind, you may wish at this point to offer a prayer to a god, goddess, protective spirit or guardian angel.
come	V;PRS;SG;3	comes	St Jude, the patron saint of Lost Causes, comes to mind.
help	V;NFIN	help	Objectively, this is not likely to help very much; but it may improve your emotional state.
have	V;NFIN	have	If you do not have any religious beliefs, or are for another reason unwilling to appeal to your patron deity, skip this step.
contemplate	V;IMP;2	Contemplate	Contemplate.
approach	V;PRS;SG;2	approach	As you approach the Earth at, or close to, terminal velocity, you may wish to contemplate your past existence, and the possibility of a future afterlife.
prove	V;NFIN	prove	A strategy that might prove helpful is to consider how fortunate you are that life has been good to you so far.
consider	V;PRS;V.PTCP	considering	Alternatively, if life has not been good to you so far, (and considering your current situation this seems more likely) consider how lucky you are that it will not be troubling you much longer.
expect	V;IMP;2	Expect	Expect impact.
have	V;NFIN	have	You are unlikely to have the time or ability to aim for a soft landing-spot, body of water, or other impact site that might improve your chances of survival.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a minuscule chance that your impact will result in non-life-threatening injuries; if this seems incredibly far-fetched, comfort yourself that double chute failure in modern times is also extremely unlikely, and that you have already beaten worse odds.
pack	V;PST;V.PTCP	packed	Your reserve chute should have been packed by a certified rigger and is moderately less likely to fail; therefore, it may be helpful to focus your efforts on opening your reserve parachute.
become	V;PST;V.PTCP	become	Some humans who have, for various reasons, become aware of their impending death, develop psychological coping strategies to deal with this; for instance, the Kubler-Ross phases of grief.
approach	V;PRS;SG;3	approaches	Since your own death approaches far more quickly, this is unlikely to be of any use to you.
attach	V;PST;V.PTCP	attached	Water landings: if you have successfully attached to a companion whose chute opens, keep in mind that both your arms may be broken or dislocated, and you may be unable to swim or tread water before your chute fills and drags you under.
land	V;PRS;SG;2	land	If, after total chute failure, you land on something soft, it is possible that instead of being killed outright, you may be permanently paralyzed due to spinal injury.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	If you have a strong aversion to this outcome, aiming head-downward before impact in the manner of an Olympic diver may ensure the destruction of your cranium and your immediate demise.
stop	V;NFIN	Stop	How to Stop Procrastinating
know	V;PRS;SG;2	know	If you're a chronic procrastinator, you know the pain and stress that comes with putting things off.
have	V;NFIN	have	Although you may have the desire to accomplish something, getting the motivation to do it is a different matter.
overcome	V;PRS;V.PTCP	overcoming	Fortunately, overcoming your procrastination is easy to do when you put your mind to it.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	Side note: If you have anything due right now, and you haven't finished it, leave this article and complete that work.
create	V;IMP;2	Create	Create a to-do list.
make	V;IMP;2	make	Yes, make yourself an old fashioned to-do list with check boxes and everything.
list	V;IMP;2	List	List everything, big and small, that you have to do for your entire day; break big activities into smaller bits if necessary.
work	V;PRS;SG;2	work	Then, as you work through your day, check off each of the items on your list.
feel	V;NFIN	feel	You will feel a growing sense of pride as you visually monitor your ever-diminishing list of projects.
focus	V;IMP;2	Focus	Focus your list on including the things you typically put off, not the things you are sure to do on a regular basis.
set	V;IMP;2	set	If necessary, set time-frames for your items to be done by.
list	V;IMP;2	list	For example, list “take the dog for a walk by 12:30” rather than simply “walk the dog.”
re-evaluate	V;IMP;2	Re-evaluate	Re-evaluate your list halfway through your day to rank your items based on highest priority.
tackle	V;IMP;2	tackle	Then, tackle the most important ones before looking back at the smaller things to do.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep a notebook by hand before you start your workday.
write	V;IMP;2	Write	Write every thought down that comes up during work.
do	V;NFIN	do	Every single to do or things you want to do at that specific moment.
do	V;IMP;2	do	Don't do it, put it on a list and do it later.
prevent	V;PRS;SG;3	prevents	This prevents you from getting into the "procrastination zone."
finish	V;IMP;2	Finish	Finish the hard stuff.
have	V;PRS;SG;2	have	When you have a looming project that's bogging you down and making you unproductive in other areas, tackle it first.
finish	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Finishing	Finishing the largest item on your list of things to do will make you feel extra productive and give you the boost to do other things you’ve been pushing to the side.
do	V;PST;V.PTCP	done	If your “big project” is something that can’t be done in one sitting, make a list of small parts of it that you can accomplish today.
worry	V;IMP;2	worry	Don’t worry about completing the entire thing, but take steps now so that doing so in the future is a breeze.
make	V;IMP;2	Make	Make an ultimate to-do list for this single project, and have it placed somewhere you will see it on a regular basis.
mark	V;PRS;SG;2	mark	As you mark things off, you will be motivated to continue doing so, and seeing it on a regular basis will remind you that your project needs to be done.
do	V;IMP;2	Do	Do two-minute tasks.
present	V;PST;V.PTCP	presented	Whenever you are presented with something that you don’t want to do or would consider putting off, ask yourself, “will this take me less than two minutes to finish?”
include	V;PRS;SG;3	includes	For many of us, this includes small chores, like taking out the trash or pulling a few weeds, but can include simple tasks in all areas of life.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	Anything that you want to put off but takes two minutes to do - do it.
force	V;IMP;2	force	Simply force yourself to use the next 120 seconds to be productive and do the duty you normally would push off for hours or days. [1].
create	V;IMP;2	Create	Create a timed work frenzy.
find	V;PRS;SG;2	find	If you find yourself being pulled off into the depths of daydreams, set a period of time to do nothing but work.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take ten minutes and remove all distractions - your phone, magazines, or thoughts of your attractive love - and go into a working frenzy.
force	V;IMP;2	Force	Force yourself to work productively for ten minutes, and then go back to whatever it was you were doing.
get	V;NFIN	get	Chances are, you’ll get into a groove and keep working at a high pace even when your frenzy time is out.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Working	Working with a timer is generally regarded by most experts as being one of the best ways to develop self-discipline and stop procrastination.
work	V;PRS;V.PTCP	working	The most famous method of working to strict time controls (known as time-boxing) involves creating a list of tasks.
assign	V;PST;V.PTCP	assigned	Each task is then assigned an exact amount of time to complete.
finish	V;NFIN	finish	If you don't finish the task in the allotted time, then you move on to the next one.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using this work arrangement, it forces you to take action, as you can't afford to waste any time. [2].
give	V;IMP;2	Give	Give yourself a break.
seem	V;NFIN	seem	If you can’t seem to focus and are working half-heartedly at your tasks, give yourself a brief break.
set	V;IMP;2	Set	Set a timer for ten minutes, and take a nap, read a book, or call your friend.
do	V;IMP;2	Do	Do whatever it is you’ve been daydreaming about so that the temptation is removed once you get back to work.
follow	V;NFIN	follow	Just be sure to follow through with your deadline rather than ignoring it when your alarm finally goes off.
play	V;NFIN	Play	How to Play Muggle Quidditch
write	V;PST;V.PTCP	written	In the Harry Potter series written by JK Rowling the main wizarding sport is Quidditch.
have	V;NFIN	have	However you don't have to have magical powers to play.
be	V;PRS;PL;3	are	There are many different ways of playing Quidditch, but the most commonly used set of rules are those given by the International Quidditch Association (which can be found here).
play	V;PST;V.PTCP	played	In the past Muggle Quidditch has mainly been played at colleges in the US, but over the last few years there has been a dramatic rise in the number of community teams.
spread	V;PST;V.PTCP	spread	Quidditch has also spread outside of America and is now played on 5 continents.
gather	V;IMP;2	Gather	Gather all needed materials and players (see Things You'll Need section below).
know	V;IMP;2	Know	Know that all players can play while riding a broom.
feel	V;IMP;2	feel	Brooms can be a hindrance however, so feel free to not use brooms.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place the quaffle and three bludgers in the middle of the field.
deflate	V;PST;V.PTCP	deflated	Ideally the quaffle and bludgers should be slightly deflated so that they are easier to throw and catch.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start the game.
start	V;PRS;PL;3	start	Both teams start from the edges and attempt to obtain the quaffle and bludgers.
follow	V;IMP;2	Follow	Follow your duty depending what type of player you are:
try	V;PRS;PL;3	try	Chasers try to score by throwing the quaffle into one of the three goal hoops.
try	V;PRS;PL;3	try	Beaters try to peg players with their bludger.
peg	V;PST;V.PTCP	pegged	If a player is pegged they have to stop what they're doing and pay a penalty; i.e. (drop the quaffle in the case of a chaser) and run back to and touch their goal post or alternatively sit down for 10 seconds.
guard	V;PRS;PL;3	guard	Keepers guard the goal posts at each end and attempt to block chasers' attempts at scoring.
hit	V;PST;V.PTCP	hit	If Keeper is close to their hoops they are safe from being hit by bludgers.
try	V;PRS;PL;3	try	Seekers try to tackle the snitch (a person) or obtain an object attached to the snitch such as a sock or flag.
establish	V;NFIN	establish	You can establish you own rules as to how a player catches the snitch.
play	V;NFIN	play	A common way to play is to make the snitch a person, and give the snitch a head start to run and hide in defined boundaries.
search	V;PRS;PL;3	search	Then the seekers search for the snitch, and attempt to tag the player.
adopt	V;PST;V.PTCP	adopted	Other methods, such as the method adopted in 2005, makes the snitch a tennis ball in a sock, hanging out of the running shorts of the snitch runner.
win	V;PRS;V.PTCP	winning	Regardless of method, the winning seeker wins 30 points for his or her team, unlike the books, in which the snitch is worth 150 points.
think	V;PST;PL;3	thought	The creators of Muggle Quidditch thought that it was worth too many points so changed the value of the snitch for muggle Quidditch.
run	V;PRS;SG;3	runs	The snitch, generally a track runner, runs around (usually the boundaries) trying to evade the seekers.
ensure	V;NFIN	ensure	The referee should ensure that rules are being followed.
keep	V;PRS;PL;3	keep	They also keep track of the score.
play	V;IMP;2	Play	Play the game!
score	V;NFIN	score	The object of the game is to score the most points and the game ends when the seeker snatches the snitch.
feel	V;IMP;2	Feel	Feel free to alter the game rules.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See the Tips section for suggestions.
play	V;NFIN	Play	How to Play a Real-Life Game of Quidditch
need	V;NFIN	need	You don't need flying broomsticks or magic balls to play this version of the game - it's tailor-made for muggles like us.
show	V;PRS;SG;3	shows	This video shows a more realistic approach to playing Quidditch.
work	V;PRS;PL;3	work	Alternatively the snitch can be a small yellow ball (tennis balls work great) that is hidden before the game starts by a bystander or referee.
establish	V;IMP;2	Establish	Establish boundaries and have the seekers look for the snitch.
try	V;NFIN	try	Beaters can try hitting the bludgers around in the air with a shortened hockey sticks or a short bat.
use	V;NFIN	use	They could also use regular-length hockey sticks to hit bludgers (perhaps wiffle balls) on the ground.
peg	V;NFIN	peg	The simplest way is to just peg players by throwing dodge balls.
play	V;NFIN	play	It may be easier to play without brooms (but also less fun!).
remember	V;IMP;2	Remember	Remember that the snitch runner, if you choose to use one, is not an actual player in the game, therefore, not bound by any of the rules.
desire	V;PST;V.PTCP	desired	If desired, the snitch is able to do whatever he or she wants to avoid capture.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See How to Be a Snitch in Quidditch for more information.
play	V;NFIN	play	You can play water quidditch in a pool.
apply	V;PRS;PL;3	apply	Almost all the same rules apply.
have	V;IMP;2	Have	Have someone throw something (the snitch) on a line into the pool at regular intervals.
use	V;NFIN	use	They can even use fake snitches.
buy	V;NFIN	buy	You can buy authentic looking brooms to make the game more realistic.
be	V;PRS;SG;3	is	There is a very large Quidditch community, have a look at the IQA website to find teams near you.
find	V;IMP;2	Find	Find a quarter or another small coin.
have	V;IMP;2	Have	Have both of the teams turn around and have the referee throw it into the grass or pitch while the teams aren't looking.
play	V;IMP;2	Play	Play while the seekers look for the snitch.
place	V;PRS;SG;3	places	Another variation is where the referee places the snitch somewhere in the field (in this case it's a small ball).
have	V;PRS;SG;3	has	The seeker has to find the snitch blindfolded, relying only on the coach's instructions, while the game is still on.
stay	V;IMP;2	Stay	Stay hydrated and exercise wisely.
fly	V;PRS;V.PTCP	flying	A ball flying in the air can hurt.
play	V;PRS;V.PTCP	playing	If you're playing quidditch you're probably just having fun so play nicely.
need	V;NFIN	Need	Things You'll Need
code	V;PST;V.PTCP	coded	Color coded shirts or capes for houses, black for referee
compose	V;PST;V.PTCP	composed	Each team is composed of:
prepare	V;NFIN	Prepare	How to Prepare Quinoa
know	V;PST;V.PTCP	known	Quinoa is known as the little rice of Peru.
treat	V;PST;PL;3	treated	The Incas treated the crop as sacred and referred to quinoa as "chisaya mama" or "mother of all grains." [1]
sow	V;NFIN	sow	By tradition, the Inca emperor would sow the first seeds of the season using "golden implements."
prepare	V;NFIN	prepare	It is much easier to prepare than rice and is rapidly gaining in popularity, especially among vegetarians who value its high protein levels.
cook	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Cooking	Method 1 Cooking On A Stove Top
rinse	V;IMP;2	Rinse	Rinse the grains of quinoa in water.
skip	V;NFIN	skip	You can skip this step if you have purchased pre-rinsed quinoa in a box.
rinse	V;NFIN	rinse	To rinse, place the measured grain in a strainer under running water for a few minutes.
remove	V;NFIN	remove	This is necessary to remove the excess saponins on the outside of the grains which impart a bitter flavor to quinoa if not removed.
toast	V;IMP;2	Toast	Toast quinoa in a saucepan (optional).
drizzle	V;IMP;2	Drizzle	Drizzle a bit of olive oil in a pan over medium-high heat.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add the quinoa and cook for about 1 minute.
bring	V;PRS;SG;3	brings	This brings out quinoa's nutty flavor.
cook	V;IMP;2	Cook	Cook the quinoa.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place two parts water or stock to one part of quinoa in a saucepan over medium-high heat and bring to a boil.
cover	V;IMP;2	Cover	Cover and reduce heat to low.
simmer	V;IMP;2	Simmer	Simmer the quinoa for about 15 minutes or until the grain becomes translucent and the white germ forms a visible spiral on the exterior of the quinoa grain.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try to ensure there is an al dente bite to the germ, in the same way as pasta.
continue	V;NFIN	continue	Quinoa will continue cooking for a bit even after you take it off the heat.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take the quinoa off the heat and let stand, covered, for 5 minutes.
give	V;NFIN	give	This will give it time to absorb any moisture that might still be in the pan.
uncover	V;IMP;2	Uncover	Uncover and fluff with a fork.
look	V;NFIN	look	The quinoa should look light and fluffy, and you should be able to see the germ separating from the seed.
serve	V;IMP;2	Serve	Serve.
serve	V;PST;V.PTCP	served	Freshly cooked quinoa should be served immediately to retain nutritional value and a good flavor.
serve	V;IMP;2	Serve	Serve with:
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	Stir-fry, using the quinoa as a substitute for rice.
think	V;NFIN	think	Virtually any other combination you can think of!
cook	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Cooking	Method 2 Cooking In a Rice Cooker
rinse	V;IMP;2	Rinse	Rinse 1 cup of quinoa in a fine mesh sieve under cold water.
buy	V;PST;SG;2	bought	If you bought packaged quinoa, this step isn't always necessary, but it's better to be safe than sorry.
pour	V;IMP;2	Pour	Pour quinoa into rice cooker.
toast	V;NFIN	toast	One option is to toast the quinoa before placing in the rice cooker.
see	V;IMP;2	See	See Step # 2 in Method One above for directions.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add 2 cups of liquid and 1/2 teaspoon of salt into the rice cooker.
work	V;PRS;PL;3	work	Water, chicken stock or broth, or vegetable stock all work.
cook	V;IMP;2	Cook	Cook for approximately 15 minutes.
have	V;NFIN	have	Some rice cooker will have different settings instead of a simple "cook" option.
try	V;IMP;2	Try	Try the "white rice" option if your rice cooker gives you several options.
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let stand for about 5 minutes.
fluff	V;IMP;2	Fluff	Fluff with a fork and serve.
cook	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Cooking	Method 3 Cooking In the Oven
preheat	V;IMP;2	Preheat	Preheat oven to 350 °F (177 °C).
arrange	V;IMP;2	Arrange	Arrange rack in the middle of the oven.
rinse	V;IMP;2	Rinse	Rinse quinoa thoroughly in a fine mesh seive under cold water.
heat	V;IMP;2	heat	In a medium saucepan, heat up 2 tablespoons vegetable oil over medium-low heat.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add onions, peppers, mushrooms, or any other type of desired vegetable or herb into the saucepan (optional).
cook	V;IMP;2	Cook	Cook onions until they are translucent, but not burned.
sweat	V;IMP;2	Sweat	Sweat the peppers or vegetables along with the onions.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add the quinoa and salt to the saucepan, stirring until completely incorporated.
take	V;NFIN	take	This should take no longer than 30 seconds.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add one cup of broth and one cup of water to the saucepan and bring to a boil over medium high heat.
reach	V;PST;V.PTCP	reached	Once a boil is reached, transfer quinoa to a 8-by-8-inch baking dish. [2]
spread	V;IMP;2	Spread	Spread out quinoa evenly and cover baking dish completely with aluminum foil.
cook	V;IMP;2	Cook	Cook the quinoa in the oven for roughly 20 minutes, or until most of the liquid is gone.
remove	V;IMP;2	Remove	Remove the aluminum foil from the baking dish, add cheese or other seasonings, and bake for an additional 5 minutes.
cook	V;PST;V.PTCP	cooked	After 5 minutes, the quinoa should be fully cooked.
serve	V;IMP;2	Serve	Serve and enjoy!
make	V;NFIN	Make	How to Make Skittles Vodka
skittle	V;PST;V.PTCP	Skittled	Bottles of "Skittled"
make	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Making	Making Skittles vodka is a fun way to add a splash of fruity flavor and color to regular vodka.
need	V;PRS;SG;2	need	All you need to do is place your favorite combination of Skittles candies into a glass or bottle of vodka and wait for it to infuse.
use	V;NFIN	use	You can then use your Skittles vodka to make brightly-colored cocktails or even serve it as a sugary shot!
find	V;IMP;2	Find	Find multiple methods for making Skittles vodka after the jump -- and soon you'll be tasting the rainbow!
separate	V;IMP;2	Separate	Separate the colors of Skittles that you want to flavor the vodka with.
choose	V;PRS;PL;3	choose	Many people choose to leave out the green, which is lime if you're using original Skittles, and purple, which is grape in the original style, as they can create a weird taste combination or a less than appealing color for the final product.
put	V;NFIN	put	However, you can put them in if you want.
mind	V;NFIN	mind	If you don't mind mixed flavors, you can just skip this step altogether.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add six Skittles to 25 ml of vodka.
keep	V;IMP;2	Keep	Keep swirling the mixture until the Skittles are almost completely dissolved.
note	V;IMP;2	Note	Note that the ratio of Skittles to vodka varies from recipe to recipe.
experiment	V;NFIN	experiment	You should experiment to find your preferred mix before making larger bottles.
filter	V;IMP;2	Filter	Filter out the remaining Skittle "insides".
decide	V;NFIN	decide	With single servings like this, you can decide which flavor(s) you like best and proceed to making bottles, if you desire.
skittle	V;PST;V.PTCP	Skittled	Method 2 of 3: Bottles of "Skittled" Vodka
pour	V;IMP;2	Pour	Pour out some vodka from a bottle to make room for Skittles.
separate	V;IMP;2	Separate	Separate the Skittles by color.
add	V;IMP;2	Add	Add the Skittles (20 - 25 per 350 ml / 11.8 fl oz).
let	V;IMP;2	Let	Let the mixture sit for a day or two.
shake	V;IMP;2	Shake	Shake occasionally so the flavour mixes well.
strain	V;IMP;2	Strain	Strain out the Skittles using a filter, such as a paper coffee filter or a clean t-shirt.
refrigerate	V;IMP;2	Refrigerate	Refrigerate overnight.
separate	V;IMP;2	Separate	Separate the colors from five bags of 200 g Skittles.
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	Using	Using 5 x 700 ml / 23.6 fl oz bottles, pour 300 ml / 10.1 fl oz of vodka into a jug and fill each bottle with skittles.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place the bottles in your dishwasher and put on an intensive cycle.
shake	V;IMP;2	shake	One third of of the way through the cycle, shake all bottles vigorously.
shake	V;IMP;2	shake	Two thirds of the way through the cycle, shake all bottles vigorously.
finish	V;PST;V.PTCP	finished	Once the cycle has finished, shake all bottles vigorously again.
place	V;IMP;2	place	Then place in the freezer for 2 hours.
take	V;IMP;2	Take	Take a sieve, line it with a piece of cheesecloth (muslin) or a coffee filter, place above a large jug and pour the vodka through slowly.
filter	V;PST;V.PTCP	filtered	Once the first bottle has been filtered, wash out the bottle and pour the Skittles vodka back in.
repeat	V;IMP;2	Repeat	Repeat the above two steps for all bottles, ensuring that everything is cleaned between uses to prevent color contamination.
place	V;IMP;2	Place	Place all of the bottles in the freezer until you are ready to drink.
worry	V;IMP;2	worry	Don't worry, the vodka won't freeze.
finish	V;PST;V.PTCP	finished	You are finished.
enjoy	V;IMP;2	Enjoy	Enjoy your tasty creation.
want	V;PRS;SG;2	want	If you want to make it more quickly, cut the skittles in half before putting them into vodka.
shake	V;IMP;2	Shake	Shake every 15 minutes.
dissolve	V;NFIN	dissolve	They'll dissolve in an hour instead of a day.
scoop	V;NFIN	scoop	Or you could just scoop them out when the color dissolves.
start	V;IMP;2	Start	Start with small amount of vodka and Skittles at first to experiment which combination of taste suits you best.
carry	V;NFIN	carry	You can carry this out with larger amount of vodka and Skittles once you have decided that you enjoy the taste.
add	V;PRS;PL;3	add	The lime green Skittles add a sourness to the drink, so if you want one that's sweet, leave the green ones out and add them to their own bottle for a sour lime vodka.
buy	V;NFIN	buy	Another approach is to buy five bottles of vodka and five bags of Skittles.
separate	V;IMP;2	separate	Then separate the candy and use one flavor for each bottle.
get	V;IMP;2	get	(Or get four bottles and an empty, to allow room for the skittles.)
use	V;PRS;V.PTCP	using	If using a t-shirt to strain, be sure fabric softener or a dryer sheet had not been used with it, lets you get some extra (unwanted) flavoring (and potentially toxic chemicals).
drink	V;IMP;2	Drink	Drink responsibly.
drink	V;IMP;2	drink	Never drink and drive.
know	V;IMP;2	Know	Know your limits, and do not drink to the extent of intoxication.
drink	V;IMP;2	drink	Do not drink if underage in your country.
need	V;NFIN	Need	Things You'll Need
require	V;PRS;SG;3	requires	Each handle of vodka (1.75 liters, about a half gallon) requires a large bag worth of Skittles, so if you plan on doing only one flavor for a flavorful vodka, you'll have to buy 5 large bags of Skittles.
hold	V;NFIN	hold	Large funnel or strainer to hold filter
